71,67,1,'6 x 7.5','1821','1821',14,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/10','0014470d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56126~100237','Depicts Craignethan Castle, as an equivalent of Tillietudlem. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 7; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 16, titled there \'Craignethan\' (see image), with artist given as Peter De Wint and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:31:25'),(107,'NTD0011',18,'[Bothwell Bridge]','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"96\"\n',71,67,1,'7 x 7','1821','1821',14,'vol. 11 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/11','0014472d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56160~100239','Depicts Bothwell Bridge, with contestants in battle on the bridge and in surrounding countryside. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 8; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 14, titled there \'Bothwell Brigg\' (see image), and with artist given as Peter de Wint, and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:30:19'),(108,'NTD0012',12,'[Heart of Mid-Lothian]','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"85\"\n',71,67,1,'6 x 7','1821','1821',14,'vol. 12 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/12','0007494d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54298~100128','Depicts the Old Edinburgh Tolbooth and side of St Giles Cathedral to the right. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 9; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), with title \'Heart of Mid-Lothian\' (see image) and artist given as Peter De Wint and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-08-14 07:52:05'),(109,'NTD0013',12,'[Grassmarket, Edinburgh]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"110\"\n',71,67,1,'6 x 8','1821','1821',14,'vol. 13 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/13','','Depicts Grassmarket and Edinburgh Castle. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821).',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:34:33'),(110,'NTD0014',12,'[St. Anthony\'s Chapel]','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',71,67,1,'6 x 7','1821','1821',14,'vol. 14 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/14','0007493d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54281~100127','Depicts St Anthony\'s Chapel (ruins), with background of Arthur\'s Seat, Edinburgh. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 10; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 18, titled there \'St Anthony\'s Chapel\' (see image) and with artist given as Peter De Wint and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:35:03'),(111,'NTD0015',6,'[Crichton Castle]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"245\"\n',71,67,1,'6.5 x 8','1821','1821',14,'vol. 15 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/15','0007400d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53317~100070','Depicts Chrichton Castle (ruins), as an equivalent of Ravenswood Castle. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 11; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), titled there \'Crichton Castle\' (see image) and with artist given as Peter De Wint and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:36:22'),(112,'NTD0016',16,'[Old Inverary]','--- \n- \"14\"\n',71,67,1,'6 x 8','1821','1821',14,'vol. 16 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1821/16','0007588d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55340~100190','Depicts old Inverary Castle. Illustration caption as given in \'Sixteen Engravings from Real Scenes Supposed to be Described in the Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1821). Vignette also on title-page of \'Novels and Tales of the Author of Waverley\' (2nd 8vo issue), 12 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1822), vol. 12; and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 24, titled there \'Old Inverary\' (see image), and with artist given as Peter De Wint and engraver as Charles Heath.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:36:54'),(113,'LIW0001',28,'Room at Abbotsford','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"103\"\n- \"172\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"50\"\n',83,34,3,'8 x 11.7','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 1','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts a loft at Abbotsford, including Scott\'s desk, where the early manuscript of Waverley was supposedly discovered, some armour and weaponry, books, and antlers. Accompanying quoted text (p.1) from 1829 \'General Preface\' to the Waverley Novels: \'I happened to want . . . manuscript presented itself\'(Magnum Opus edition, vol. 1, pp. xvii-xviii). Legend states published December 1831.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:31:31'),(114,'LIW0002',28,'Mirkwood Mere','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',7,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 2','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Mirkwood Mere at sunset, with ruined castle in the distance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 2): \'Late, when . . . world more fair.\' [12 lines verse; Ch. 5; EEWN 1, 25.19-30]. Legend states published June 1832.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:41:26'),(115,'LIW0003',28,'Doune Castle','--- - \"249\" - \"14\" - \"244\" - \"15\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',46,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 3','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Doune Castle, with river in the foreground, some grazing cattle, and two small figures in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 3): \'On the opposite . . . first rays of the sun\' [Ch. 38; EEWN 1, 147.20-22]. Legend states published April 1830.',NULL,NULL),(116,'LIW0004',28,'Stirling Castle','--- - \"250\" - \"14\" - \"143\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 4','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Stirling Castle, with several small figures. Accompanying quoted text: \'In about two hours time . . . waved in the evening sun\' [Ch. 39, EEWN 1, 202.7-9]. Legend states published April 1831.',NULL,NULL),(117,'LIW0005',28,'Linlithgow','--- - \"124\" - \"311\" - \"239\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 5','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the Palace of Linlithgow with lake and grazing sheep. Accompanying quoted text: \'They halted at Linlithgow, distinguished by its ancient palace\' [Ch. 39, EEWN 1, 203.24-25]. Legend states published February 1831.',NULL,NULL),(118,'LIW0006',28,'White Horse Inn','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"240\"\n',18,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 6','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the White Horse Inn, in a close off the Canongate, with several small figures and clothing hung out to dry. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene). Accompanying quoted text (p. 6) \'He reached the door . . . called the Canon-gate\' [Ch. 41; see EEWN 1, 210.6-7]. Compare the drawing by Skene in #SKE0004. Legend states published June 1831.',NULL,'2009-08-14 07:53:50'),(119,'LIW0007',28,'Penrith','--- - \"72\" - \"239\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',29,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 7','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the town of Penrith, with grazing sheep and mountains in the background. Accompanying quoted text (p. 7): \'If that hill were Benmore . . . scenery\' [Ch. 59; EEWN 1, 294.12-13]. Legend states published April 1830.',NULL,NULL),(120,'LIW0008',11,'Caerlaverock Castle','--- - \"108\" - \"14\" - \"15\" - \"124\" - \"245\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 8','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Caerlaverock Castle as an equivalent of the old Castle of Ellangowan, with grazing cattle. Accompanying quoted text (p. 8): \'A steep . . . in front of the old castle\' [Ch. 4; EEWN 2, 21.33-34]. Legend states published August 1830.',NULL,NULL),(121,'LIW0009',11,'Windermere','--- - \"124\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',107,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 9','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Lake Windermere, with mountains in the distance and a female figure in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 9): \'If India . . . more romantic recesses\' [Ch. 17; EEWN 2, 93.12-16]. Legend states published April 1830.',NULL,NULL),(122,'LIW0010',11,'Distant View of Skiddaw','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',29,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 10','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the mountain Skiddaw and the hill Saddleback, with a rainbow over a lake. Accompanying quoted text (p. 10): \'Let the reader conceive . . . Skiddaw and Saddleback are pre-eminent\' [Ch. 22; EEWN 2, 117.11-13]. Legend states published June 1830.',NULL,'2009-06-19 15:04:19'),(123,'LIW0011',11,'Waste of Cumberland','--- - \"117\" - \"49\" - \"15\" - \"20\" - \"116\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 11','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts a desolate border area called the Waste of Cumberland. Accompanying quoted text (p. 11): \'Brown  . . .  the Waste of Cumberland\' [Ch. 23; EEWN 2, 124.9-10]. Legend states published 1 May 1830.',NULL,NULL),(124,'LIW0012',11,'Frith of Forth','--- - \"140\" - \"8\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',94,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 12','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts a ship on choppy waters in the Firth of Forth, with Burntisland in the background. Accompanying quoted text (p. 12): \'But Mannering was chiefly delighted . . . between Edinburgh and the sea\' [Ch. 37; EEWN 2, 213.35-37]. Legend states published in October 1831.',NULL,NULL),(125,'LIW0013',11,'Solway Firth from Allonby','--- - \"140\" - \"8\" - \"29\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 13','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Solway Firth from the resort town of Allonby. Accompanying quoted text (p. 13): \'At length . . . upon the Scottish coast\' [Ch. 40; EEWN 2, 241.23-25]. Legend states published June 1830.',NULL,NULL),(126,'LIW0014',3,'North Queen\'s Ferry','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',94,34,3,'8.3 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 14','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the village of Queensferry on the Forth, with boats in a harbour. Accompanying quoted text (p. 14): \'So much time . . . tide was passed\' [Ch. 1; EEWN 3, 11.6-12]. Legend states published October 1830.',NULL,'2009-04-10 13:38:39'),(127,'LIW0015',3,'Home Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"82\" - \"17\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',78,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 15','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts Hume Castle, Berwickshire, with labourers harvesting grain in the fields below, and two children playing in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 15): \'The lights glanced . . . inland hills\' [Ch. 45; EEWN 3, 348.35-36]. Legend states published May 1831.',NULL,NULL),(128,'LIW0016',23,'London from Highgate','--- - \"322\" - \"30\" - \"26\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',7,34,3,'8.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 16','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts a view of London in the distance, with dome of St Paul\'s visible, from tree-lined Highgate as it would have looked at the time of the tale. Accompanying quoted text (p. 16): \'And when I looked . . . pleasures of cultured life\' [Ch. 3; EEWN 5, 23.16-18]. Legend states published July 1830.',NULL,NULL),(129,'LIW0017',23,'Glasgow Cathedral','--- - \"109\" - \"321\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',107,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 17','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the Glasgow Cathedral. Accompanying quoted text (p. 17): \'Situated in . . . solemnity of this scene\' [Ch. 19; EEWN 5, 156-57].',NULL,NULL),(130,'LIW0018',23,'Inch Cailleach','--- - \"241\" - \"124\" - \"47\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'7.8 x 11.7','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 18','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','','Depicts the island Inch Cailleach, in Loch Lomond. Accompanying quoted text (p. 18): \'I swear to ye . . . of Inch Cailleach\' [Ch. 23; See EEWN 5, 188.24-25]. Legend states published February 1831.',NULL,NULL),(131,'LIW0019',23,'Loch Ard','--- - \"124\" - \"72\" - \"15\" - \"47\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 19','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0014607d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56905~100283','Depicts Loch Ard, with Ben Lomond in the distance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 19): \'But the road now . . . is encircled\' [Ch. 30; EEWN 5, 252.1-6]. Legend states published May 1830.',NULL,NULL),(132,'LIW0020',23,'Ben Lomond','--- - \"72\" - \"47\" - \"244\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'8.3 x 11.6','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 20','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0014606d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56888~100282','Depicts Ben Lomond with sheep and pastoral scene in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 20): \'The lofty peak . . . on our right herd\' [Ch. 36; EEWN 5, 309.19-21].',NULL,NULL),(133,'LIW0021',5,'Manor Glen','--- \n- \"242\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 21','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007387d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53093~100057','Depicts Manor Glen, in Peeblesshire, as the home of David Richie, reputed original of the Black Dwarf. (Drawn from a scene by James Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 21): \'Ye\'ll hee heard of Canny Elshie the Black Dwarf\' [Ch. 1; EEWN 4a, 12.7]. Compare the drawing by Skene in #SKE0022. Legend states published April 1831.',NULL,'2009-05-25 12:27:18'),(134,'LIW0022',18,'Dunnottar Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',27,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 22','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0014464d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56025~100231','Depicts Dunnottar Castle, on the east cost of Kincardineshire, overlooking the North Sea. Accompanying quoted text (p. 22): \'These captives . . . overhangs the German ocean\' [\'Introduction to Old Mortality\', Magnum Opus edition, vol. 9, p. 223]. Legend states published May 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:21:51'),(135,'LIW0023',18,'Bothwell Bridge','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"244\"\n',83,35,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 23','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0014461d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55975~100228','Depicts Bothwell Bridge over the River Clyde with cattle being herded in near foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 23): \'There was a guard . . . narrow bridge of Bothwell\' [Ch. 31; EEWN 4b, 249.18-19]. Legend states published March 1831.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:32:36'),(136,'NTE0014',14,'Ivanhoe / The Trial of Rebecca','--- - \"6\" - \"87\" - \"286\" - \"188\" - \"88\" - \"178\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,110,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1824','1824',5,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0007509d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54550~100143','Depicts the Grand Master, on his elevated seat, staff in hand, condemning Rebecca, who turns away, with the figure of Higg on crutches to the side [Ch. 37]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of the Historical Romances of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824) (see image), and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 25. Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Abbey of St Mary, Kirkcaldy as in vol. 2 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(137,'NTE0015',17,'Monastery / Edward Glendinning Demanding an Account of his Brother of Sir Piercie Shafton','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"118\"\n- \"289\"\n- \"287\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"290\"\n',15,88,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1824','1824',5,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0014437d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55574~100204','Depicts Edward Glendinning, sword in hand, in front of the extravagantly attired Sir Piercie Shafton, in a room at Glendearg, with Father Eustace seated to the right [Ch. 26]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of the Historical Romances of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824) (see image), and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 29. Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Glendearg as in vol. 3 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:36:10'),(138,'NTE0016',1,'Abbot / Edward Graeme Rescued from the Water','--- - \"123\" - \"291\" - \"8\" - \"124\" - \"17\" - \"26\" - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',53,88,1,'9 x 7','1824','1824',5,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0007310d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52197~100005','Depicts Roland Graeme as a boy, in the Loch of Avenel, having been rescued by Wolf and being pulled into a small boat occupied by two men, with the Castle in the background [Ch. 1]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824) and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 31 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Loch Leven Castle as in vol. 5 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(139,'NTE0017',1,'Abbot / Roland Graeme Rescuing Henry Seyton','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"123\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"292\"\n- \"96\"\n',24,91,1,'9 x 7','1824','1824',5,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0007308d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52162~100003','Depicts Roland Graeme in full armour on horseback, at the Battle of Langside, pointing his lance towards enemies, with Henry Seyton, also in armour but without helmet, exhausted by the side of the horse [Ch. 37]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of the Historical Romances of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824) (see image), and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 33. Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Warwick Castle as in vol. 8 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:36:52'),(140,'NTE0018',15,'Kenilworth / The Countess Admiring the Decorations of the Earl','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"131\"\n- \"56\"\n- \"89\"\n',53,47,1,'9.5 x 7','1824','1824',5,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0007564d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54987~100169','Depicts Amy Robsart at Cumnor Place, on footstool beneath the seated Earl of Leicester, examining a chivalric collar worn by the Earl [Ch. 7]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of the Historical Romances of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824), and as in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 35 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Remains of the Gateway as in vol. 7 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:40:45'),(141,'LAD0042',9,'Chronicles of the Canongate (Second Series) / Page 209 Chap. 7 Vol. 2','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"211\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"339\"\n- \"178\"\n',67,74,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (31 December 1828), facing p. 661','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Simon Glover entering the bedroom in Henry Gow\'s house, with the reviving Catharine on a bed at which Henry kneels, and with Luckie Shoolbred in attendance [Ch. 19]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:34:42'),(142,'LAD0043',2,'Anne of Geierstein / Vol. 1 Page 13','--- - \"217\" - \"406\" - \"116\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',1,1,1,'8.5 x 8.5','1829','1829',27,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Arthur Philipson and his father pausing for reflection of their journey through the Alps. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(143,'LAD0044',2,'Anne of Geierstein / Vol. 2, p. 318','--- - \"223\" - \"217\" - \"155\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',86,86,1,'9 x 7','1829','1829',27,'vol. 10 (31 October 1829), facing p. 548','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Arthur Philipson kneeling before Anne of Geierstein in her apartment at Arnheim Castle, after she has told him he must leave [Ch. 19]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(144,'LAD0045',2,'Anne of Geierstein / Chap. 1st Page 56','--- - \"223\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',86,86,1,'9 x 7','1829','1829',27,'vol. 10 (30 November 1829), facing p. 601','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Anne of Geierstein balanced on a high cliff amongst surrounding Alpine scenery [Ch. 2]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(145,'LAD0046',2,'Anne of Geierstein / Chap. 8 Page 221 Vol. III','--- \n- \"216\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"217\"\n',86,86,1,'9 x 7','1829','1829',27,'vol. 10 (31 December 1829), facing p. 660','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts interview between Margaret of Anjou, dressed as nun, and Arthur Philipson, in the monastery at Mont Sainte Victoire [Ch. 31]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:42:18'),(146,'SKE0061',1,'Crookstown Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"245\"\n',92,1,2,'7.7 x 13.4','1831','1831',1,'leaf preceding p. 167','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Castle of Crookstone, in ruined state on rising ground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 164), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Not there . . . the lost--the murdered--/ The Abbot, Vol. XXI, p. 327.’ See Ch. 37; EEWN 10, 360. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1831.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:26:41'),(147,'SKE0062',1,'Dundranen [sic] Abbey','--- \n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.4','1831','1831',1,'facing p. 168','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Dundrennan Abbey, in ruins. Accompanying quoted text (p. 169), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'In this dejected mood . . . from the field of battle. / The Abbot, Vol. XXI, p. 343.\' See Ch. 38; EEWN 10, 368. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1831.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:27:05'),(148,'SKE0063',1,'Port Mary','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.4','1831','1831',1,'facing p. 170','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Port Mary, on the northern shore of Solway Firth. Accompanying quoted text (p. 171), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'May I remind your Majesty that the tide ebbs fast? . . . The Queen stood irresolute . . . quitting for ever. / The Abbot, Vol. XXI, pp. 349, 350.\' See Ch. 38; EEWN 10, 371. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1831.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:27:27'),(149,'ILA0001',1,'\'Stand not to look on a dying man, but haste to save the Queen.\'','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"292\"\n- \"178\"\n',25,47,1,'8 x 7','1824','1824',22,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson P. 3035','0007314d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52269~100009','Depicts Henry Seyton lying wounded on the ground in armour, with mounted soldiers with lances riding past in the middle distance, one of whom looks back at Seyton; a large tree is prominent, and a castle in the distance. [Ch. 37; EEWN 10, 364.25-26] Single print in the Corson collection (see image), with signs of erasure of original imprint near foot; publisher information above from Todd & Bowden 146Dd.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:28:14'),(150,'ILA0002',1,'Roland Introduced to Sir Halbert Glendinning','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"112\"\n- \"324\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"123\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"291\"\n',25,47,1,'10.2 x 7.8','1824','1824',22,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson P. 3020','0007315d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52287~100010','Depicts the Lady of Avenel (in white), introducing Roland Graeme as a boy to Sir Halbert, who is seated wearing armour, and pats Wolf on the head; Lilias stands in the doorway. [Ch. 3]. Single print in the Corson Collection; Todd & Bowden 146Dd records one further plate, \'Roland Graeme Offering His Services to the Queen\', not found in Corson Collection.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:36:57'),(151,'FIS0086',19,'Fenella','--- \n- \"157\"\n- \"84\"\n',53,19,3,'11.1 x 8.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 29, facing p. 32','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/29','','Portrait of Fenella Christian wearing a turban with a feather. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0027.',NULL,'2009-05-18 12:09:41'),(152,'FIS0087',19,'Peveril and Chiffinch / Peveril et Chiffinch','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"554\"\n- \"390\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"391\"\n- \"153\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 29, facing p. 184','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/29','0014505d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56348~100250','Depicts Julian Peveril pointing his pistol at Tom Chiffinch, who is also mounted, while Lance Outram menaces Monsieur Chaubert, who lies sprawled on the highway [Ch. 27]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 69] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:37:39'),(153,'FIS0088',19,'Trial of the Peverils. Oates giving evidence / Jugement des Peverils. Oates allant rendre temoignage','--- - \"392\" - \"188\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',47,78,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 30 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/30','0014504d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56331~100249','Depicts Titus Oates in the witness box at the trial of the Peverils, with four presiding judges on a balcony above [Ch. 41]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 68] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,NULL),(154,'FIS0089',19,'The Tower, 1670','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"322\"\n',83,34,3,'8.2 x 11.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 30, facing p. 111','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/30','','Depicts the Tower of London from the Thames. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0066.',NULL,'2009-05-18 12:11:25'),(155,'FIS0090',21,'Louis of France and the Count de Crevecoeur','--- - \"165\" - \"396\" - \"130\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',3,78,3,'8.2 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 31 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/31','0014515d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56516~100260','Depicts the Count of Crevecoeur having thrown down his gage in front of Louis XI, who sits on his throne, with a number of courtiers vying as to who shall pick the gage up [Ch. 8]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 70] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,NULL),(156,'FIS0091',21,'Tours','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',77,34,3,'7.8 x 11.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 31, facing p. 36','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/31','','Depicts harbour with boats in the city of Tours. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0067.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:03:30'),(157,'SSR0005',12,'West Bow Edinburgh / Heart of Mid-Lothian Vol. I, p. 219','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"354\"\n',18,21,3,'13.8 x 9.8','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 162','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007457d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54028~100112','Depicts the West Bow, in Edinburgh, with various citizens  conversing in the street [see Ch. 4]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition.',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:20:37'),(158,'SSR0006',1,'The Forth at Queen\'s Ferry / Abbot Vol. 1, p. 347','--- - \"8\" - \"140\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,62,3,'9.5 x 13.3','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 200','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007319d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52357~100014','Depicts sailing vessel (carrying Lord Lindesay in the story) in choppy sea approaching the north shore of the Firth of Forth, with settlements and possibly a lighthouse visible [Ch. 20]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition.',NULL,NULL),(159,'SSR0007',1,'Lochleven Castle (first view) / Abbot Vol. 1, p. 348','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"127\"\n- \"128\"\n',18,12,3,'9.8 x 13.5','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 202','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007316d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52304~100011','Depicts the arrival of Lord Lindesay and his party on the shore of Loch Leven, with a bugle being blown, and the Castle in the distance across the loch [Ch. 20]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition.',NULL,'2009-04-10 13:44:02'),(160,'SSR0008',1,'Lochleven Castle (second view) / Abbot Vol. 2, p. 287','--- - \"128\" - \"8\" - \"124\" - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,7,3,'9.7 x 13.4','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 214','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007382d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53008~100052','Depicts the escape of Mary in a rowing boat across Loch Leven, with splashes in the loch indicating shots from the distant Castle [Ch. 35]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Reproduced in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845), facing p. 61, with caption \'Escape of Mary\'.',NULL,NULL),(161,'SSR0009',28,'Stirling Castle / Waverley Vol. 2, p. 91','--- - \"250\" - \"259\" - \"53\" - \"49\" - \"14\" - \"246\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,82,3,'9.8 x 13.5','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 228','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0014643d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57257~100304','Depicts Balmawhapple, passing with his troop of Jacobite cavalry through the King\'s Park beneath Stirling Castle, and firing his pistol futilely at the Castle [Ch. 39]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Published later in \'Black\'s Picturesque Tour of Scotland\' (Edinburgh, 1840): see image.',NULL,NULL),(162,'SSR0010',23,'Crypt under Glasgow Cathedral / Rob Roy Vol. II, p. 41','--- - \"80\" - \"423\" - \"109\" - \"88\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,43,3,'9.8 x 13.5','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 244','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0014602d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56819~100278','Depicts congregation listening to a sermon, with Frank Osbaldistone, positioned under a pillar alongside (apparently) Andrew Fairservice, and turning round at having heard a mysterious voice behind him [Ch. 20]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Later published in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845): see image.',NULL,NULL),(163,'LIW0024',18,'Bothwell Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"244\" - \"29\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',80,34,3,'8.4 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 24','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0014460d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55957~100227','Depicts Bothwell Castle, overlooking the River Clyde, with anglers in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 24): It was on a delightful summer . . . ruins of Bothwell Castle\' [Ch. 37; EEWN 4b, 288.3-6]. Legend states published September 1830.',NULL,NULL),(164,'LIW0025',12,'Tolbooth','--- - \"85\" - \"251\" - \"109\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',71,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 25','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007456d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54010~100111','Depicts the old Edinburgh Tolbooth, with the side of St Giles Cathedral to the right, in the High Street. Accompanying quoted text (p. 25): \'He stood now . . . very middle of the High Street\' [Ch. 6; EEWN 6, 48.8-10]. Legend states published 1 August 1830.',NULL,NULL),(165,'LIW0026',12,'St. Anthony\'s Chapel','--- - \"109\" - \"245\" - \"129\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',7,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 26','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007459d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54062~100114','Depicts St Anthony\'s Chapel in ruins, overlooking the Firth of Forth near Salisbury Crags, outside Edinburgh. Accompanying quoted text (p. 26): \'It was situated . . . dedicated to St Anthony the eremite\' [Ch. 15; EEWN 6, 137.6-10]. Legend states published November 1830.',NULL,NULL),(166,'LIW0027',12,'Durham','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"109\"\n',84,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 27','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007461d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54097~100116','Depicts view of Durham, with the castle and cathedral, with grazing cattle in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 27): \'With a strong beast . . . advanced as far as Durham\' [Ch. 29; EEWN 6, 249.16-19]. Legend states published August 1830.',NULL,'2009-04-10 12:21:13'),(167,'LIW0028',12,'Newark Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"15\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',29,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 28','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007462d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54115~100117','Depicts Newark Castle in Nottinghamshire, with cattle grazing in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 28): \'At noon the hundred-armed Trent . . . lay before her\' [Ch. 30; EEWN 6, 256.33-35]. Legend states published June 1830.',NULL,NULL),(168,'LIW0029',12,'Dunbarton Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"143\" - \"244\" - \"8\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 29','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007463d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54132~100118','Depicts Dumbarton Castle on the River Clyde. Accompanying quoted text (p. 29): \'That\'s the old Castle of Dumbarton, the strongest place in Europe\' [Ch. 42; EEWN 6, 370.32-33]. Legend states published December 1830.',NULL,NULL),(169,'LIW0030',12,'Holy Loch','--- - \"124\" - \"72\" - \"20\" - \"49\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',46,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 30','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007466d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54183~100121','Depicts the Holy Loch in Argyllshire, with mountains and cottages in the background. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 30): \'The tower of Knockdunder . . . overhanging the Holy Loch\' [Ch. 45; EEWN 6, 393-94]. Legend states published 1 September 1830.',NULL,NULL),(170,'LIW0031',12,'Arran','--- - \"241\" - \"140\" - \"8\" - \"143\" - \"72\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',27,35,3,'8 x 11.8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 31','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007453d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53958~100108','Depicts mountains and cliffs on the Isle of Arran, with boats in the water in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 31): \'The hills of Dumbartonshire . . . thunder-splitten peaks of Arran\' [Ch. 46; EEWN 6, 399-400]. Legend states published April 1830.',NULL,NULL),(171,'LIW0032',6,'Coldingham','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',77,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 32','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007392d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53181~100062','Depicts the priory in Coldingham, with women, graves, and cattle in the yard. (Drawn from a sketch by James Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 32): \'Under the very arch . . . service of the church\' [Ch. 2, see EEWN 7a, 20.14-16]. Compare the drawing by Skene in #SKE0041. Legend states published September 1831.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:38:21'),(172,'LIW0033',6,'Fast Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"8\" - \"140\" - \"191\" - \"245\" - \"378\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 11.7','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 33','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007393d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53199~100063','Depicts Fast Castle near St Abb\'s Head, from the sea, with a boat struggling in fierce waters. Accompanying quoted text (p. 33): \'Yonder is Wolf\'s Crag . . . difficult to conceive\' [Ch. 7; EEWN 7a, 58.13-31]. Legend states published March 1831.',NULL,NULL),(173,'LIW0034',6,'Links of Eyemouth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',38,34,3,'8 x 11.8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 34','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007403d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53369~100073','Depicts a harbour with boats. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 34): \'The links by the seashore . . . swords our only weapons\' [Ch. 35; EEWN 7a, 265.18-19]. Compare drawing by Skene in #SKE0042. Legend states published May 1831.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:05:25'),(174,'LIW0035',16,'Dunstafnage','--- - \"14\" - \"239\" - \"49\" - \"15\" - \"116\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'7.8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 35','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007573d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55136~100178','Depicts Dunstafnage Castle in the distance, with animals grazing in the foreground, and horsemen and pedestrians travelling along road. Accompanying quoted text (p. 35): \'It was a gloomy . . . great height\' [Ch. 10; EEWN 7b, 77.34-35]. Legend states published January 1831.',NULL,NULL),(175,'LIW0036',16,'Pier at Inverary','--- - \"8\" - \"72\" - \"124\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',27,34,3,'8 x 11.8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 36','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007574d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55153~100179','Depicts boats in the harbour at Inverary. Accompanying quoted text (p. 36): \'The boat soon approached . . . little town of Inverary\' [Ch. 11; see EEWN 7b, 90.3-4]. Legend dated December 1830.',NULL,NULL),(176,'LIW0037',16,'Loch Awe and Ben Cruachan','--- - \"72\" - \"124\" - \"47\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',42,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 37','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007580d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55204~100182','Depicts Ben Cruachan and Loch Awe in the distance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 37): \'Ben Cruachan . . . rose high above the others\' [Ch. 17; EEWN7b, 141.14-16]. Legend dated November 1830.',NULL,NULL),(177,'LIW0038',16,'Inverlochy Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"72\" - \"47\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',84,34,3,'7.7 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 38','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007583d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55254~100185','Depicts Inverlochy Castle, surrounded by mountains. Accompanying quoted text (p. 38): \'The ancient castle of Inverlochy . . . some strength and consideration\' [Ch. 18; EEWN 7b, 147.22-24]. Legend dated December 1830.',NULL,NULL),(178,'LIW0039',14,'Wharncliffe','--- - \"30\" - \"49\" - \"116\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',29,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 39','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007512d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54601~100146','Depicts Warncliffe park, with cart and horse and three figures in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 39): \'The remains of . . . around Rotherham\' [Ch. 1; EEWN 8, 15.13-15]. Legend states published April 1831.',NULL,NULL),(179,'LIW0040',14,'Castle of Ashby','--- - \"14\" - \"245\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 1, facing p. 40','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007515d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54651~100149','Depicts the Castle of Ashby, partially in ruins. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 40): \'Prince John held . . . in the Castle of Ashby\' [Ch. 14; EEWN 8, 125.21]. Legend states published June 1831.',NULL,NULL),(180,'LIW0041',14,'Jorvaulx Abbey','--- - \"219\" - \"245\" - \"15\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',29,34,3,'7.5 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 41','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007514d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54634~100148','Depicts the remains of the Abbey at Jervaulx, near Middleham, with grazing cattle in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 41): \'Oh, it is such a rich . . . good fathers of Jorvaulx\' [Ch. 33; EEWN 8, 285.5-6]. Legend states published January 1831.',NULL,NULL),(181,'LIW0042',14,'Conisborough Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',29,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 42','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007544d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54686~100151','Depicts Conisborough Castle, in South Yorkshire, set in thick woodland. Accompanying quoted text (p. 42): \'The King attended by Ivanhoe . . . scenes in England\' [Ch. 41; EEWN 8, 368.11-15]. Legend states published December 1831.',NULL,NULL),(182,'LIW0043',14,'York Minster','--- - \"109\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',70,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 43','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007546d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54720~100153','Depicts York Minster, with the town walls in left foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 43): \'The nuptials of our hero . . . noble Minster of York\' [Ch. 44; EEWN 8, 347.41-42]. Legend states published March 1831.',NULL,NULL),(183,'LIW0044',17,'Old Bridge of Tweed','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"244\"\n',107,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 44','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0014459d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55939~100226','Depicts the old bridge over the River Tweed, with Eildon Hills in the background. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 44): \'This was, however . . . traced by the curious\' [Ch. 5; EEWN 9, 62.38-41]. Compare the drawing by Skene at #SKE0056. Legend states published June 1831.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:07:41'),(184,'LIW0045',17,'Cross of Melrose','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"354\"\n',83,35,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 45','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0014451d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55806~100218','Depicts the cross of Melrose, matching Kennaquhair in the story, with gypsies in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 45): \'In this order the procession . . . former monarch of Scotland\' [Ch. 37; EEWN 9, 343-45]. Legend states published August 1831.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:38:47'),(185,'LIW0046',1,'St. Mary\'s','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',77,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 46','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007307d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52139~100002','Depicts ruins of Melrose Abbey (interior) with cattle grazing. Accompanying quoted text (p. 46): \'An half-hour\'s walk . . . fury of the times\' [Ch. 13; EEWN 10, 96-97]. Legend states published September 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:29:50'),(186,'LIW0047',1,'Edinburgh Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"251\" - \"110\" - \"72\" - \"239\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',94,35,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 47','EUL, Corson H.MAR.1','0007322d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52409~100017','Depicts Edinburgh Castle, from Arthur\'s Seat. Accompanying quoted text (p. 47): \'See yonder is the old castle!\' [Ch. 17; EEWN 10, 132.23]. Legend states published November 1830.',NULL,NULL),(187,'LIW0048',1,'High Street, Edinburgh','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n',99,83,3,'8 x 11','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 48','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007324d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52444~100019','Depicts the High Street of Edinburgh, facing east, with two horsemen on causeway (one with hawk in hand) among assorted pedestrians. Accompanying quoted text (p. 48): \'The principal street of Edinburgh . . . specimens in Europe\' [Ch. 17; EEWN 10, 135.9-10]. Legend states published August 1831.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:31:05'),(188,'LIW0049',1,'Loch Leven','--- - \"124\" - \"14\" - \"128\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',44,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 49','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007323d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52426~100018','Depicts Loch Leven, with Loch Leven Castle in the distance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 49): \'The ancient castle . . . centre of the lake\' [Ch. 25; EEWN 10, 182-83]. Legend states published February 1831.',NULL,NULL),(189,'LIW0050',1,'Niddrie Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"30\" - \"245\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 50','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007313d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52251~100008','Depicts Niddrie Castle with surrounding trees. Accompanying quoted text (p. 50): \'Long before day-break . . . belonging to Lord Seyton\' [Ch. 36; EEWN 10, 345.38-40]. Legend states published December 1831.',NULL,NULL),(190,'LIW0051',1,'Castle of Crookstone','--- - \"14\" - \"245\" - \"244\" - \"15\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8.5 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 51','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007321d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52392~100016','Depicts the ruined Castle of Crookstone, along the river Cart in Renfrewshire. Accompanying quoted text (p. 56): \'This is the Castle of Crookstone . . . married to Darnley\' [Ch. 37; EEWN 10, 360-61]. Legend states published July 1831.',NULL,NULL),(191,'LIW0052',1,'Frith of Cumberland and Galloway','--- - \"140\" - \"8\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 52','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007312d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52233~100007','Depicts the Solway Firth, with a ship sailing in the distance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 52): \'The sails were hoisted . . . from those of Galloway\' [Ch. 38; EEWN 10, 372.7-9]. Legend states published July 1831.',NULL,NULL),(192,'LIW0053',15,'Warwick from the Kenilworth Road','--- - \"14\" - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',23,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 53','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Warwick from the Kenilworth Road, with the Castle, and churches with groups of trees in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 53): \'Two or three miles short of the fair town of Warwick\' [Ch. 24; EEWN 11, 248.17-18]. Legend states published May 1831.',NULL,NULL),(193,'LIW0054',15,'Warwick Castle','--- - \"14\" - \"244\" - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',7,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 54','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Warwick Castle, on the Avon River, surrounded by woodlands. Accompanying quoted text (p. 54): \'Warwick, within whose castle . . . uninjured by time\' [Ch. 25; EEWN 11, 253.1-3]. Legend states published May 1831.',NULL,NULL),(194,'LIW0055',15,'Kenilworth Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"245\"\n',29,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 55','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007553d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54838~100160','Depicts Kenilworth Castle (ruins) in the distance, surrounded by woodlands, with a plough and ploughman in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 55): \'At length the princely Castle appeared\' [Ch. 25; EEWN 11, 254.5]. Legend states published January 1831.',NULL,'2009-04-10 13:47:34'),(195,'LIW0056',20,'The Hill of Hoy','--- - \"72\" - \"140\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 56','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Hill of Hoy across the bay. (Drawn from a sketch by the Marchioness of Stafford) Accompanying quoted text (p. 56): \'But the love of the rock . . . Island of Hoy\' [Ch. 19; EEWN 12, 179.40-43]. Legend states published October 1830.',NULL,NULL),(196,'LIW0057',20,'Kirwall, Orkney','--- - \"8\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',27,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 57','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts large sailing vessels with smaller boats in the harbour at Kirkwall in Orkney. Accompanying quoted text (p. 57): \'The quay, with the shipping . . . vivacity to the scene\' [Ch. 31; EEWN 12, 291.14]. Legend states published January 1831.',NULL,NULL),(197,'LIW0058',20,'Stromness','--- - \"8\" - \"143\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 58','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the bay of Stromness, in Orkney. (Drawn from a sketch by the Marchioness of Stafford) Accompanying quoted text (p. 58): \'On the distant . . . in the roadstead\' [Ch. 33; EEWN 12, 312.13-15]',NULL,NULL),(198,'LIW0059',20,'St. Magnus','--- - \"109\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',79,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 59','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Cathedral of St Magnus, in Kirkwall, Orkney. (Drawn from a sketch by the Marchioness of Stafford) Accompanying quoted text (p. 59): \'The sisters were admiring the massive church of St Magnus\' [Ch. 36; EEWN 12, 331.18-19]. Legend states published October 1830.',NULL,NULL),(199,'LIW0060',10,'St. Cuthberts','--- - \"109\" - \"124\" - \"251\" - \"110\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 60','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the old church of St Cuthbert\'s, below the Edinburgh Castle. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 60): \'But there are . . . St Cuthbert\'s Kirk-yard\' [Ch. 3; EEWN 13, 45.16-17]. Legend states published November 1831.',NULL,NULL),(200,'LIW0061',10,'Whitehall','--- - \"8\" - \"311\" - \"244\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',83,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 61','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Palace of Whitehall on the River Thames. (Drawn from a sketch by Hollar) Accompanying quoted text (p. 61): \'At last Whitehall received our traveller\' [Ch. 5; EEWN 13, 65.19]. Legend states published February 1831.',NULL,NULL),(201,'LIW0062',10,'Heriot\'s Hospital','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"38\"\n',83,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 62','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Heriot\'s Hospital, Edinburgh, seen from Greyfriars Churchyard. Accompanying quoted text (p. 62): \'I think mine own estate . . . greater men\' [Ch. 29; EEWN 13, 329.27-28]. Legend states published December 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0082.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:10:25'),(202,'LIW0063',19,'Castle Rushin','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n',44,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 63','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Castle Rushen on the Isle of Man, with boats in the harbour. Accompanying quoted text (p. 63): \'He returned to Castle Rushin . . . night sate down\' [Ch. 14; EEWN 14, 150.31-32]. Legend states published March 1831.',NULL,'2009-05-12 16:41:01'),(203,'LIW0064',19,'Peel Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',44,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 64','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Peel Castle, on the Isle of Man, with stormy sea in channel. Accompanying quoted text (p. 64): \'Amidst all these ruins . . . Castle itself\' [Ch. 15; EEWN 14, 152.15]',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:39:27'),(204,'LIW0065',19,'Liverpool 1664','--- - \"140\" - \"8\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',5,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 65','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Liverpool from the sea, with sailing vessel and rowing boat resisting fierce waves. Accompanying quoted text (p. 65): \'Peveril had at length . . . quay of Liverpool\' [Ch. 20; EEWN 14, 204.18-19]. Legend states published October 1831.',NULL,NULL),(205,'LIW0066',19,'The Tower, 1670','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"322\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"244\"\n',83,34,3,'8 x 11.5','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 66','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Tower of London from the River Thames. Accompanying quoted text (p. 66): \'The tide carried them . . . well-known Traitors\' Gate\' [Ch. 36; EEWN 14, 388.19-25]. Legend states published February 1832. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0089.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:11:56'),(206,'LIW0067',21,'Tours','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',77,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 67','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Tours, on the Loire River. Accompanying quoted text (p. 67): \'About two miles onward lies the fine city of Tours\' [Ch. 3; EEWN 15, 42.4]. Legend states published August 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0091.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:12:18'),(207,'LIW0068',21,'Namur','--- - \"244\" - \"8\" - \"243\" - \"354\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',77,35,3,'8.3 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 68','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the city of Namur, Belgium, on the rivers Sambre and Meuse, with boatmen in the foreground. Legend states published October 1831.',NULL,NULL),(208,'LIW0069',21,'Liege','--- \n- \"354\"\n',77,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 69','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts a street market in the city of Liege. Legend states published July 1831.',NULL,'2009-04-10 13:49:33'),(209,'LIW0070',21,'Peronne','--- \n- \"354\"\n',15,34,3,'11.5 x 8.3','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 70','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts a square in the town of Peronne, France, with horse and cart in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 70): \'Thus travelling on . . . strong town of Peronne\' [Ch. 25; EEWN 15, 273.3-5]. Legend states published December 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0095.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:39:55'),(210,'LIW0071',24,'Nidpath Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',107,34,3,'7.5 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 71','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Neidpath Castle overlooking the river Tweed, in the Scottish borders. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying text (p. 71): \'A massive sqaure tower . . . embattled wall\' [Ch. 1; EEWN 16, 2.42-43].  Legend states published August 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels\': see #FIS0097.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:13:12'),(211,'LIW0072',22,'Dumfries from the South','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"239\"\n',21,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 72','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the town of Dumfries from the south, with sheep feeding in pasture in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 72): \'Of Dumfries . . . gallant river of Nith\' [Letter 3; EEWN 17, 17.39-41]. Legend states published October 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0101.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:13:34'),(212,'LIW0073',22,'Solway Sands','--- - \"8\" - \"140\" - \"49\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 73','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the coast along the Solway Firth, with a sailing vessel having landed, and a horse and cart alongside. Accompanying quoted text (p. 73): \'When I reached . . . low and setting sun\' [Letter 4; EEWN 17, 20.6-10]. Legend states published April 1831.',NULL,NULL),(213,'LIW0074',22,'Craigevar Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"67\"\n',18,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 74','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts a banquet in a room in Craigevar Castle. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 74): \'Away trots . . . Redgauntlet Castle\' [Letter 11; EEWN 17, 89.4]. Legend states published October 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0102.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:14:04'),(214,'LIW0075',22,'Lagg Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"242\"\n- \"245\"\n',83,34,3,'8 x 11.8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 75','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts ruined Lagg Castle in the Glen of Lagg, Dumfriesshire. (Drawn from a sketch by J. Skene) Accompanying quoted text (p. 75): \'They rode into the outer court-yard\' [Letter 11; EEWN 17, 95.41]. Legend states published August 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0104.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:14:21'),(215,'LIW0076',22,'Solway Frith','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"140\"\n',7,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 76','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the Solway Firth, at night, with boat and fishing equipment in the left foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 76): \'The moon shone broad . . . Solway Firth\' [Ch. 3; EEWN 17, 151.35-36]. Artist surname given as Barnet on engraved plate, but as Barret in list of plates.',NULL,'2009-04-10 11:14:57'),(216,'LIW0077',4,'Powis Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n',38,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 77','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','0007368d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52835~100042','Depicts Powis Castle in the distance, surrounded by woodland. (Drawn from a sketch by Lady Lucy Clive) Accompanying quoted text (p. 77): \'They were invited . . . name of Powys Castle\' [Ch. 1]. Legend states published September 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0107.',NULL,'2009-05-25 10:40:52'),(217,'LIW0078',29,'Woodstock from Blenheim','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"354\"\n',107,34,3,'8 x 12','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 78','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the town of Woodstock from Blenheim. Accompanying quoted text (p. 78): \'The preacher himself . . . streets of Woodstock\' [Ch. 2]. Legend states published November 1831.',NULL,'2009-04-10 13:51:05'),(218,'LIW0079',29,'Woodstock. From a Drawing in the collection of George III','--- \n- \"14\"\n',107,34,3,'7.5 x 11.8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 79','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts Woodstock Lodge in pastoral setting. Accompanying quoted text (p. 79): \'They stood accordingly in front of the old gothic building\' [Ch. 3]. Legend states published August 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0114.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:15:15'),(219,'LIW0080',29,'Round Tower, Windsor 1660','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"253\"\n',34,34,3,'11.8 x 8','1832','1832',10,'vol. 2, facing p. 80','EUL, Corson H.MAR.2','','Depicts the round tower at Windsor Castle, with the English banner. Accompanying quoted text (p. 80): \'At the ascent . . . wont to float\' [Ch. 8]. Legend states published July 1831. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0115.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:15:33'),(220,'NTD0017',14,'[Castle of Torquilstone]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"105\"\n',71,6,1,'7 x 6','1822','1822',4,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0007549d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54771~100156','Depicts Castle of  Torquilstone, with three armed figures in foreground. Also appears in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 1. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears, facing p. 26, though with Edward Finden as engraver (see image).',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:40:39'),(221,'NTD0018',17,'[Abbey of St Mary, Kirkcaldy]','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"219\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 8','1822','1822',4,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0014449d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55774~100216','Depicts large ecclesiastical building, with gateway in middleground. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears, facing p. 30, though with engraver given as Edward Finden, and no artist (see image). Also appears in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 2.',NULL,'2009-11-23 11:08:56'),(222,'NTD0019',17,'[Glendearg]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"382\"\n- \"117\"\n',71,6,1,'7.5 x 6','1822','1822',4,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0014450d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55790~100217','Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 28, though with engraver as Edward Finden (see image). Also appears in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 3.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:41:17'),(223,'NTD0020',17,'[Ruined Abbey?]','--- - \"256\" - \"219\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',71,6,1,'5.5 x 8','1822','1822',4,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','','Illustration title given as \'vignette of a ruined Abbey\' in Todd & Bowden 273A, but has several features of Melrose Abbey.',NULL,NULL),(224,'NTD0021',1,'[Lochleven Castle]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"128\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 8','1822','1822',4,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0007309d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52180~100004','Depicts Loch Leven Castle, on island in Loch, with mountains in far distance. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]) where same image appears facing p. 32, with engraver listed as Edward Finden, and no artist (see image).  Also features in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 4.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:41:53'),(225,'NTD0022',1,'[Battle Scene]','--- - \"96\" - \"103\" - \"323\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',71,6,1,'4.5 x 8','1822','1822',4,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','','Illustration title given as \'vignette of a battlefield\' in Todd & Bowden 273A, and possibly may be based on battles of Carberry Hill or Langside, where Queen Mary\'s forces were defeated.',NULL,NULL),(226,'NTD0023',15,'[Remains of the Gateway]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"245\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 8.5','1822','1822',4,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0007566d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55020~100171','Depicts ruined gateway at Kenilworth Castle, with trees growing amongst the ruins (see Ch. 25). Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 36, with engraver listed as Edward Finden, and no artist (see image). Also appears in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 6.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:42:30'),(227,'NTD0024',15,'[Warwick Castle]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 7.5','1822','1822',4,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1172.h-i','0007565d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55004~100170','Depicts Castle in middle distance, with trees and open verdure in foreground. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 34, with engraver listed as Edward Finden, and no artist (see image).  Also appears in \'Historical Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 8 vols (Edinburgh: Constable and Co., 1822), vol. 5.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:43:02'),(228,'ILN0001',18,'Old Mortality / \'He considered himself . . . of their forefathers.’ ','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"77\"\n- \"258\"\n',2,67,1,'9.5 x 8.5','1820','1820',7,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014465d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56042~100232','Depicts \'Old Mortality\' restoring Covenanting gravestones with hammer and chisel, greeted by a stranger (Pattieson) holding a cane and uplifting his hat. [Ch. 1; EEWN 4b, 10.6-8]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:33:09'),(229,'ILN0002',28,'Waverley / The Quarrel at Luckie Macleary\'s between the Baron of Bradwardine and the Laird of Balmawhapple','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"259\"\n- \"11\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"261\"\n- \"260\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"102\"\n',2,112,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts the Baron of Bradwardine at cross swords with the Laird of Balmawhapple, in Luckie Macleary\'s inn, with Waverley falling over the prostrate figure of the Laird of Killancureit, and the hostess entering, holding a plaid to throw over the weapons. [Ch. 11]',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:46:03'),(230,'ILN0003',28,'Waverley / The Conclusion of Flora\'s Song in the Glen of Glennaquoich','--- - \"102\" - \"60\" - \"262\" - \"36\" - \"242\" - \"41\" - \"96\" - \"26\" - \"103\" - \"47\" - \"225\" - \"74\" - \"\"',2,29,1,'10.5 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Flora MacIvor with her Harp and attendants, watched by Edward Waverley at her side, and interrupted by the sudden appearance of her brother Fergus and his greyhound. [Ch. 22]',NULL,NULL),(231,'ILN0004',11,'Guy Mannering / Meg Merrilies Predicting the Fall of the House of Ellangowan','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"263\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"264\"\n',2,19,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Godfrey Bertram and groom on horseback, with turbaned Meg Merrilies above them on bank, issuing her curse, with sapling bough in raised right hand [Ch. 8].',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:46:35'),(232,'ILN0005',11,'Guy Mannering / The Meeting of Meg Merrilies, Brown, and Dinmont','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"265\"\n',2,112,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Meg Merrilies offering warning to the seated Dandy Dinmont and Henry Bertram (as Brown) as they take refreshments in Tib Mumps\' alehouse [Ch. 22].',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:47:00'),(233,'ILN0006',3,'The Antiquary / The Antiquary Buying Fish from Mrs. Mucklebackit','--- - \"79\" - \"267\" - \"58\" - \"29\" - \"20\" - \"8\" - \"140\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',2,68,1,'8 x 10.5','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck bargaining over the price of fish with Maggie Mucklebackit, who is seated with a pail of fish at her feet. [Ch. 11]',NULL,NULL),(234,'ILN0007',3,'The Antiquary / The Laird of Monkbarns Arming himself on the Alarm of Invasion','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"78\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"96\"\n',2,112,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck at his house, sword in breeches, preparing himself at the news of French invasion, with his sister and female servants hurriedly assisting [Ch. 45].',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:22:11'),(235,'ILN0008',23,'Rob Roy / The Fight in the Public House at the Clachan of Aberfoil, Between Bailie Jarvie and Major Galbraith\'s Party','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"54\"\n',2,91,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 7]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Bailie Nicol Jarvie engaged in combat with party of three Highlanders, with (apparently) Frank Osbaldistone and Andrew Fairservice also engaged in the fray [Ch. 28].',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:22:32'),(236,'ILN0009',5,'The Black Dwarf / Isabella Vere\'s Midnight Visit to the Black Dwarf\'s Hut','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"268\"\n',2,29,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 8]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0007383d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53025~100053','Depicts Isabel Vere in the Black Dwarf\'s hut, with the latter interposing and clutching her hand, as she attempts to leave. [Ch. 16]',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:22:56'),(237,'ILN0010',18,'Old Mortality / Burley\'s Concealment in the Hay Loft at Milnwood','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"5\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"21\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"70\"\n',2,29,1,'11 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 9]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014468d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56093~100235','Depicts Balfour seated with broadsword in one hand and Bible in other, Morton having entered behind him, with a plateful of food in hand. [Ch. 6]',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:23:38'),(238,'ILN0011',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian / Breakfast Scene with captain Knockdunder at Knocktarlitie','--- - \"269\" - \"23\" - \"270\" - \"271\" - \"67\" - \"18\" - \"46\" - \"8\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',2,91,1,'10.5 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 10]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0007458d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54045~100113','Depicts breakfast at Knocktarlitie, with Reuben and Jeanie as guests, and Duncan Knock aggressively answering Dolly Dutton\'s queries about his dress. [Ch. 46]',NULL,NULL),(239,'ILN0012',6,'The Bride of Lammermoor / Lucy Ashton & the Master of Ravenswood Pledging their Love at the Mermaiden\'s Fountain','--- - \"86\" - \"2\" - \"155\" - \"30\" - \"352\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',2,91,1,'10.5 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 11]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0007401d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53334~100071','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood, with his hunting gun, and Lucy Ashton in a semi-embrace by the fountain in a woodland setting. [Ch. 20]',NULL,NULL),(240,'ILN0013',16,'A Legend of Montrose / Captain Dalgetty Consigning Gustavus to the Care of the Children of the Mist','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"96\"\n',2,47,1,'10.5 x 8','1820','1820',7,'[Plate 12]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0007579d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55187~100181','Depicts Dugald Dalgetty in armour on his horse, Gustavus, with Ranald MacEagh instructing a gillie to take charge of his horse [Ch. 14].',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:47:59'),(241,'NTE0001',28,'Waverley / Flora in the Glen of Glennaquoich','--- - \"60\" - \"274\" - \"36\" - \"242\" - \"47\" - \"41\" - \"46\" - \"74\" - \"225\" - \"351\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,47,1,'9.5 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0014651d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57392~100312','Depicts Flora MacIvor standing by a waterfall, with Cathleen to her side holding a harp [Ch. 22].  Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 1 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Craigcrook Castle, as in vol. 1 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(242,'NTE0002',28,'Waverley / Mac-Ivor Warned of his Fate by The Grey Spirit','--- - \"262\" - \"275\" - \"129\" - \"284\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,70,1,'9.5 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0014652d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57409~100313','Depicts Fergus MacIvor, hand on scabbard, on a moonlit night confronting the Bodach Glas (Grey Spirit), who stands at the end of a foot-bridge [Ch. 59]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).   Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 3 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Holyrood Palace, as in vol. 2 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(243,'NTE0003',11,'Guy Mannering / Meg Merrilies Compelling Dominie Sampson to Eat','--- - \"265\" - \"92\" - \"67\" - \"252\" - \"98\" - \"88\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,91,1,'9 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007420d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53660~100090','Depicts Meg Merrilies, spoon in hand, standing over cauldron, while Dominie Sampson cowers [Ch. 46]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page.  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 5 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Caerlaverock Castle, as in vol. 3 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(244,'NTE0004',3,'The Antiquary / The Antiquary Incensed at the Intrusion on his Sanctum Sanctorum','--- - \"79\" - \"58\" - \"276\" - \"66\" - \"103\" - \"163\" - \"57\" - \"225\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,77,1,'9 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007364d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52765~100038','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck entering his study with Lovel, and surprising the chambermaid Jenny Rintherout, while his niece, Maria McIntyre, looks on [Ch. 3]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 9 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Dundee, as in vol. 5 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(245,'NTE0005',3,'The Antiquary / Douterswivel Digging for Treasure in Misticot\'s Grave','--- - \"76\" - \"277\" - \"7\" - \"38\" - \"98\" - \"129\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,88,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007365d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52783~100039','Depicts Edie Ochiltree, staff in hand, standing over and directing Douterswivel, who stands in the grave digging with pickaxe [Ch. 25]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 7 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of the Abbey of Aberbrothick, as in vol. 6 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(246,'NTE0006',23,'Rob Roy / Francis Osbaldistone & Diana Vernon in the Library','--- - \"80\" - \"100\" - \"57\" - \"163\" - \"103\" - \"155\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,47,1,'9 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0014595d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56701~100271','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone leaning over the seated Diana Vernon, who looks at a manuscript in her hand, and has book on her lap [Ch. 16]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 11 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Inversnaid Fort, as in vol. 8 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(247,'NTE0007',5,'The Black Dwarf / The Black Dwarf in a Frenzy of Grief upon Beholding the Tomb of his Affianced Bride','--- - \"254\" - \"278\" - \"38\" - \"162\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,88,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007381d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52991~100051','Depicts the Black Dwarf looking away from the tomb of Mrs Vere of Ellieslaw, with a sculpted weeping cherub overlooking her marble figure [Ch. 17].   Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 13 (see image).  Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Craignethan, as in vol. 10 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(248,'NTE0008',18,'Old Mortality / King Charles the Second Saluting Lady Bellenden','--- - \"374\" - \"319\" - \"18\" - \"155\" - \"225\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,91,1,'9 x 7','1823','1823',15,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0014471d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56143~100238','Depicts Charles II, as a visitor at Tillietudlem, with doffed hat, holding the hand of and moving as if to kiss Lady Bellenden, who half turns her face away, with two female attendants behind the breakfast table in the background [Ch. 17]. Scott\'s original text refers to \'two buxom serving-wenches . . . elevated for the day into the capacity of waiting gentlewomen\' [EEWN 4b, 19.14-15].  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 15 (see image).  Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Bothwell Bridge, as in vol. 11 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(249,'NTE0009',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian / The Affecting Scene between Effie Deans and her Sister in the Tolbooth','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"280\"\n',60,88,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007455d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53993~100110','Depicts Jeanie Deans embracing her sister Effie in confinement in the Edinburgh Tolbooth, with Ratcliffe the jailer  holding a key by an open shutter [Ch. 26]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 19 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of the Heart of Mid-Lothian, with the Tolbooth in the middle ground, as in vol. 12 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath. A reengraving of this image appears in the Centenary edition of the Waverley Novels: see #CEN0013.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:50:07'),(250,'NTE0010',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian / The Interview of Jeanie Deans with the Queen','--- - \"281\" - \"23\" - \"282\" - \"283\" - \"18\" - \"246\" - \"30\" - \"146\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,47,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007446d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53845~100101','Depicts Jeanie Deans, with plaid over head and shoulders, petitioning Queen Caroline, in wooded parkland, with Lady Suffolk and the Duke of Argyle looking on [Ch. 38]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).   Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 17 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of St Anthony\'s Chapel, as in vol. 14 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath.',NULL,NULL),(251,'NTE0011',6,'The Bride of Lammermoor / The Ominous Incident at the Mermaiden\'s Fountain','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"191\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"86\"\n',60,47,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007390d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53146~100060','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood with arm round Lucy Ashton by the fountain, with dead raven and arrow at feet, as Ravenswood looks around for the marksman [Ch. 20]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 21 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Crichton Castle, as in vol. 15 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath. A reengraving of the present image appears in the Centenary edition of the Waverley Novels: see #CEN0015.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:34:58'),(252,'NTE0012',16,'A Legend of Montrose / Dalgetty and Ranald of the Mist Escaping through the Chapel','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"96\"\n',60,47,1,'9 x 6.5','1823','1823',15,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1177.f.18','0007581d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55222~100183','Depicts Dalgetty kneeling on a cushion and peering through a curtain in the Marquis of Argyle\'s private gallery in the chapel at Inverary, with Ranald MacEagh looking on [Ch. 14]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 23 (see image).   Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Old Inverary (forming with frontispiece one of several exterior/interior combinations to be found in this set), as in vol. 16 of 1821 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Charles Heath. A reengraving of the present image appears in the Centenary edition of the Waverley Novels: see #CEN0011.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:36:07'),(253,'NTE0013',14,'Ivanhoe / The Black Knight & the Clerk of Copmanhurst Carousing','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"285\"\n',99,91,1,'9 x 6.5','1824','1824',5,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','NLS, 1177.h.1','0007513d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54618~100147','Depicts Richard Coeur de Lion in black armour drinking at a table with Friar Tuck (as the Clerk) in his hermitage, with a dog at their feet, and torch behind them lighting the room [Ch. 16]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'A New Series of Illustrations of the Historical Romances of \"The Author of Waverley\"\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1824) (see image), and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 27. Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Castle of Torquilstone, as in vol. 1 of 1822 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-09-07 08:51:50'),(254,'NTE0019',20,'The Pirate / The Shipwreck of Cleveland','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"137\"\n',110,10,1,'9 x 7','1825','1825',13,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0014507d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56380~100252','Depicts Ulla Troil (Norna) attending to the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland, with Mordaunt Mertoun and Bryce the Pedlar looking on [Ch. 7]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears as a plate apparently belonging to \'Illustrations of The Pirate\' (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1825), and in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 37 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Roost of Sumburgh as in vol. 1 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-05-12 09:29:29'),(255,'NTE0020',10,'The Fortunes of Nigel / The Scene with Nigel, Trapbois, and Colepepper','--- - \"293\" - \"294\" - \"295\" - \"103\" - \"296\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',110,70,1,'9 x 7','1825','1825',13,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0007411d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53504~100081','Depicts Colepepper, about to draw his sword aggressively, with Trapbois imploring in the middle, and Nigel at other end of room holding his sword but pointing it away [Ch. 23]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 41 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Burgh-Westra as in vol. 2 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(256,'NTE0021',10,'The Fortunes of Nigel / Nigel Encountering the Murderers of Trapbois','--- - \"293\" - \"424\" - \"229\" - \"103\" - \"142\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',112,91,1,'9 x 6.5','1825','1825',13,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0007414d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53556~100084','Depicts Nigel rushing in through door and firing pistol at ruffians, one of whom falls, with the shocked Martha Trapbois appealing to him [Ch. 24]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 39 (see image).  Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Whitehall as in vol. 3 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(257,'NTE0022',19,'Peveril of the Peak / Sir Geoffrey Peveril Opposing Bridgenorth in his Pursuit of the Countess of Derby','--- - \"154\" - \"297\" - \"49\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',24,88,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1825','1825',13,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0014479d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56279~100246','Depicts Sir Geoffrey Peveril mounted on Black Hastings impeding the path of Major Bridgenorth, who rides a white stallion [Ch. 7] Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Todd & Bowden place this plate in vol. 5. Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 45 (see image). Present vol. also includes title-page vignette of Camlet Moat as in vol. 4 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(258,'NTE0023',19,'Peveril of the Peak / The Meeting of Peveril & Alice Interrupted by Bridgenorth','--- - \"152\" - \"153\" - \"154\" - \"155\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',112,70,1,'9.5 x 7','1825','1825',13,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0014478d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56262~100245','Depicts Alice Bridgenorth and Julian Peveril in a parlour at Black Fort in the Isle of Man, disturbed by the stern figure of Ralph Bridgenorth who has entered by the door [Ch. 13]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page).  Todd & Bowden place this plate in vol. 4. Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 43 (see image). Present vol. also includes title-page vignette of Holm-Peel as in vol. 7 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(259,'NTE0024',21,'Quentin Durward / Quentin Rescuing Isabelle at the Sack of Schonwaldt','--- - \"164\" - \"167\" - \"168\" - \"103\" - \"130\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',112,10,1,'9 x 7','1825','1825',13,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0014588d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56584~100264','Depicts Quentin Durward, in armour and feathered bonnet, rescuing kneeling Countess Isabelle, with Syndic Hermann Pavillon (also in armour) looking on [Ch. 21]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 51 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Castle of Peronne as in vol. 9 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,NULL),(260,'NTE0025',21,'Quentin Durward / Quentin Presenting to the Countess of Croye the Letter of her Aunt','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"96\"\n',15,88,1,'9 x 6.5','1825','1825',13,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.3','0014516d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56533~100261','Depicts Quentin Durward, on horseback in armour, tilting his lance with letter on its point towards the battlements of Peronne, close to its gateway [Ch. 36]. Imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London (the secondary publishers on title-page). Also appears in \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 47 (see image). Vol. also includes title-page vignette of Castle of Plessis-le-Tours as in vol. 8 of 1824 12mo issue, but with engraver given as Edward Finden.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:52:51'),(261,'NTE0026',24,'Saint Ronan\'s Well / Mowbray\'s Interview with his Sister','--- - \"298\" - \"177\" - \"34\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',50,67,1,'8.5 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts a distraught-looking Mowbray, clutching his hair with hand, confronting his seated sister Clara, with a vase of ornate flowers also prominent [Ch. 35]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'distant castle on a hill\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,NULL),(262,'NTE0027',22,'Redgauntlet / Meeting of Father Buonaventure with Allan Fairford, Miss Arthuret, & Miss Seraphina','--- \n- \"299\"\n- \"300\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"302\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"301\"\n',50,67,1,'8.5 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Charles Edward Stuart, dressed as a priest and wearing cockade, entertained by the Arthuret sisters in their apartment, with Alan Fairford looking on [Ch. 16]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'a footbridge\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:08:28'),(263,'NTE0028',22,'Redgauntlet / Wandering Willie, His Wife & Benjie Discovered by Darsie Latimer','--- \n- \"17\"\n- \"305\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"304\"\n- \"303\"\n',50,67,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1828','1828',21,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Wandering Willie with fiddle, his wife in coned hat, and Benjie (tankard in hand), seated before a cloth on the ground with bread on it, and Willie\'s fiddle case by his side [Letter 10]. Exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 110, 1828. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'two horsemen on a cliff\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:37:45'),(264,'NTE0029',4,'The Betrothed / Approach of Eveline to her Aunt the Ancient Lady of Baldringham','--- - \"306\" - \"190\" - \"26\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',50,67,1,'9.5 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Eveline Berenger presenting herself to the Lady of Baldringham, who is seated on a throne, staff in hand, with a large dog at her feet [Ch. 13]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'a castle on a hill\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,NULL),(265,'NTE0030',29,'Woodstock / Scene with the Pastor and Master Tomkins in the Church at Woodstock','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"307\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"206\"\n',50,67,1,'9 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 5 (frontispiece) [Misplaced: should be in vol. 6]','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts the minister Holdenough attempting to climb to the pulpit, book in hand, with Tomkins falling backwards having failed to stop him, and armed men also in the church [Ch. 1]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'a maiden by a spring\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,'2009-06-29 12:15:44'),(266,'NTE0031',26,'The Talisman / Sir Kenneth & the Emir at the Diamond of the Desert','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"247\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"199\"\n',50,67,1,'8.5 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 6 (frontispiece) [Misplaced: should be in vol. 5]','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Sir Kenneth and (the as yet unidentified) Saladin seated together by a spring, with a background of palm trees, and their swords on the ground [Ch. 2]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'palm trees\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.','2009-03-20 15:11:29','2009-04-10 14:09:14'),(267,'NTE0032',29,'Woodstock / Wildrake\'s Interview with Cromwell','--- - \"308\" - \"309\" - \"172\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',50,57,1,'9 x 7','1828','1828',21,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Oliver Cromwell, in room with Wildrake, standing before a large portrait of Charles I which appears to reflect his own image [Ch. 8]. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of \'a woman by a spring\' [Todd & Bowden], drawn by J. B. Kidd and engraved by W. H. Lizars, apparently not used in any other set.',NULL,NULL),(268,'NTE0033',13,'Highland Widow','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"208\"\n- \"207\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"96\"\n',41,60,1,'6.5 x 8','1833','1833',20,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Hamish MacTavish being handcuffed by soldiers, his mother Elspat having fainted on the floor of the hut [Ch. 11]. Matches #MAG0081, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of Hamish succumbing to the effects of the sleeping potion, matching Magnum vignette #MAG0082, though with James Johnstone again given as engraver.',NULL,'2009-05-19 14:15:57'),(269,'NTE0034',9,'Fair Maid of Perth','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"211\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"98\"\n',2,60,1,'6.5 x 9','1833','1833',20,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Catharine Glover leaning over and kissing Henry Gow, asleep in a chair, with her father Simon appearing at the door [Ch. 5]. Matches #MAG0083, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of the traveller surveying Perth, matching Magnum vignette #MAG0084, drawn by David Octavius Hill, though with James Johnstone again given as engraver.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:29:18'),(270,'NTE0035',2,'Anne of Geierstein','--- \n- \"216\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"217\"\n- \"218\"\n',69,60,1,'8 x 6.5','1833','1833',20,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Margaret of Anjou on the ramparts of the monastery at Mont Sainte Victoire, having thrown her sable feather and red rose into the wind, with Arthur Philipson looking on [Ch. 30]. Matches #MAG0087, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of a \'castle in the distance\', matching vol. 13 of 16mo issue, #NTD0046.',NULL,'2009-05-19 14:18:22'),(271,'NTE0036',8,'Count Robert of Paris','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"226\"\n',12,60,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1833','1833',20,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Count Robert of Paris seated on the Emperor\'s throne, with wolf-hound at his side [Ch. 9]. Matches #MAG0091, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of the sleeping figure of Hereward, matching Magnum vignette #MAG0092, drawn by John West, though with James Johnstone again given as engraver.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:29:58'),(272,'NTE0037',7,'Castle Dangerous','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"238\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"310\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"237\"\n',94,60,1,'6.5 x 9','1833','1833',20,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Sir Aymer de Valence, guided by old woman to the vaulted apartment of the sexton at Douglas Kirk, Lazarus Powheid, with the page Fabian looking on from the doorway [Ch. 9]. Matches #MAG0093, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of a river valley, probably Douglas Water, matching vol. 16 of 16mo issue, #NTD0049.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:58:13'),(273,'NTE0038',25,'The Surgeon\'s Daughter','--- \n- \"234\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"235\"\n- \"233\"\n- \"232\"\n- \"85\"\n',98,60,1,'6.5 x 8.5','1833','1833',20,'vol. 13 (frontispiece)','BL 12604.a.4','','Depicts Zilia de Moncada in her apartment, under the protection of Gideon Gray, being apprehended by an armed King\'s Messenger, with Gray and Mr Lawford intervening [Ch. 15]. Matches #MAG0095, but with different engraver. Vol. also contains title-page vignette illustration of a village lane with three old women, matching vol. 17 of 16mo issue, #NTD0050.',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:33:03'),(274,'NTD0025',20,'[Roost of Sumburgh]','--- \n- \"140\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 8','1824','1824',12,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0014509d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56414~100254','Depicts large rock, rising from sea, with fortified house at foot. [Compare Ch. 1: EEWN 12, 5]. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 40, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image).  Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 1. Similar but not identical to #CEN0026.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:59:07'),(275,'NTD0026',20,'[Burgh-Westra]','--- \n- \"311\"\n',71,6,1,'7 x 7','1824','1824',12,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0014508d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56397~100253','Depicts collection of buildings, forming a settlement, with man in foreground extending hand to another, with female and male groups on either side of them. Compare Ch. 12; EEWN 12, 109-110. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 38, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image).  Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 2.',NULL,'2009-06-25 15:59:25'),(276,'NTD0027',10,'[Whitehall]','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"146\"\n- \"96\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 8','1824','1824',12,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0007410d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53486~100080','Depicts man on foot presenting petition to another on horseback, with armed guards in attendance, against background of extensive building with twin towers. [Compare Ch. 3; EEWN 13, 47]. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 42, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image). Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 3.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:40:05'),(277,'NTD0028',10,'[Camlet Moat]','--- - \"30\" - \"229\" - \"142\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',71,6,1,'6.5 x 7.5','1824','1824',12,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0007415d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53574~100085','Depicts man and (seated) woman in forest glade, with page behind holding horse, and three figures behind hedge in the foreground, one of whom points a gun towards the man [Compare Ch. 36; EEWN 13, 392-93]. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 44, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image). Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 4.',NULL,NULL),(278,'NTD0029',19,'','--- - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',71,6,1,'6 x 8','1824','1824',12,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','','Depicts man and woman in wooded parkland, apparently conversing, with large house in background. Illustration title given as \'vignette of lady and gentleman conversing\' in Todd & Bowden 276A, though the figures there are very small.',NULL,NULL),(279,'NTD0030',19,'','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"116\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 7.5','1824','1824',12,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','','Depicts a roadside inn, with woman offering a stirrup cup to a rider on steps about to mount horse, with another rider already proceeding along the road. Probably based on incident in Ch. 21; EEWN 14, 216. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a wayside inn\' in Todd & Bowden 276A.',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:12:47'),(280,'NTD0031',19,'[Holm-Peel]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"140\"\n',71,6,1,'5 x 7.5','1824','1824',12,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0014480d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56297~100247','Depicts fortified island, with castle and church(es) on mount, with boats and boatmen in foreground. [Compare Ch. 15; EEWN 14, 151]. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 46, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image).  Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 5.',NULL,'2009-11-23 11:11:01'),(281,'NTD0032',21,'[Castle of Plessis-le-Tours]','--- \n- \"14\"\n',71,6,1,'6 x 7','1824','1824',12,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0014517d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56550~100262','Depicts extensive castle, with outer fortifications, and three small figures in foreground [Compare Ch. 3; EEWN 15, 39-40]. Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 48, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image). Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 7.',NULL,'2009-11-23 11:12:33'),(282,'NTD0033',21,'[Castle of Peronne]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n',71,6,1,'7 x 7.5','1824','1824',12,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG. 1172.i','0014518d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56567~100263','Depicts castle as entered by a water under an archway, with sailing ships and rowing boats around. [Compare Ch. 25; EEWN 15, 273.] Illustration title from \'The Waverley Album\' (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), where same image appears facing p. 50, with Alexander Nasmyth as artist and Edward Finden as engraver (see image). Also appears in \'Novels and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1824), vol. 6.',NULL,'2009-11-23 11:14:19'),(283,'NTD0034',24,'[View of Innerleithen?]','--- - \"354\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,67,1,'5.5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/1','','Depicts a grouping of houses, with surrounding trees, and hills in the distance. Illustration title given as \'vignette of two men approaching a mansion\' in Todd & Bowden 279A, though the two figures involved are very small.  Artist name absent, but supplied by \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 1, where the vignette also appears.',NULL,NULL),(284,'NTD0035',24,'[Innerleithen Church?]','--- - \"109\" - \"38\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,67,1,'5.5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/2','','Depicts a church, with graves in the foreground, and nearby houses, and hills in the distance. [Compare Ch. 1; \'the kirk of Saint Ronan\'s\'; EEWN 16, 4] Illustration title given as \'vignette of a churchyard and a graveyard\' in Todd & Bowden 279A. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol.2.',NULL,NULL),(285,'NTD0036',22,'','--- - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,67,1,'5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/3','','Depicts turreted castle of fortified house, in pastoral setting, with water to the front and side. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a castle\' in Todd & Bowden 279A.  Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 4, which contains The Betrothed.',NULL,NULL),(286,'NTD0037',22,'','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"140\"\n',35,67,1,'4.5 x 7','1827','1827',18,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/4','','Depicts cottage-like building with outhouse, close to a tree, alongside a small harbour, containing a sailing vessel and rowing boat, with large estuary in view. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 3.',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:13:23'),(287,'NTD0038',4,'[Castle of Garde Doloreuse?]','--- - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,67,1,'5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/5','','Depicts a castle with several towers in a hilly pastoral landscape, possibly representing a Welsh medieval castle.  Illustration title given as \'vignette of a castle\' in Todd & Bowden 279A. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 5.',NULL,NULL),(288,'NTD0039',4,'[Red Pool?]','--- - \"124\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',55,67,1,'5 x 7.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/6','','Depicts hills surrounding a lake, with a road leading toward this in the foreground. Possibly representing the Red Pool [Ch. 23] in the story. Illustration title given as \'vignette of distant lake\' in Todd & Bowden 279A.',NULL,NULL),(289,'NTD0040',26,'[Diamond of the Desert?]','--- \n- \"352\"\n',62,67,1,'5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/7','','Depicts a well, within a arched structure, with surrounding palm trees in an oasis setting. Likely to represent the Diamond of the Desert [Ch. 2] in the story. Illustration title given as \'vignette of an enclosed well\' in Todd & Bowden 279A. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 6. Lizars given only as engraver in 12mo, but features as both artist and engraver in 8vo issue.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:01:31'),(290,'NTD0041',29,'[Maypole of Woodstock?]','--- - \"33\" - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,67,1,'4.5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/8','','Depicts two small figures below a large tree, with a long pole before them, with buildings with towers and turrets in the distance. Likely to refer to Ch. 3, where Joseph Tomkins and Jocelin Joliffe pause before the King\'s Oak and the Maypole of Woodstock. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a castle in the distance\' in Todd & Bowden 279A.  Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Constable & Co., 1827), vol. 7.',NULL,NULL),(291,'NTD0042',29,'[Woodstock Lodge?]','--- - \"14\" - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',1,67,1,'5 x 6.5','1827','1827',18,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1827/9','','Depicts twin-towered building partly hidden in dense woodland. Possibly related to the description of in Woodstock Lodge in Ch. 2 (\'rising from the trees one or two ancient and venerable turrets\'). Illustration title given as \'vignette of a castle in the distance\' in Todd & Bowden 279A.',NULL,NULL),(292,'NTD0043',13,'[River Awe?]','--- - \"242\" - \"244\" - \"351\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,60,1,'6 x 8.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts river surrounded by mountains, with a long waterfall running down one of these in the distance. Probably relates to the description of the River Awe in first chapter of story (see EEWN 20, 72-73). Illustration title given as \'vignette of waterfall and stream\' in Todd & Bowden 282A. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 8.',NULL,NULL),(293,'NTD0044',9,'[Distant View of Perth]','--- \n- \"116\"\n- \"213\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"212\"\n',52,60,1,'8.5 x 6','1833','1833',19,'vol. 11 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a man horseback with sword and shield, riding towards a town and bridge in the distance, with a winding river on the plain below, and two pilgrim-like figures and stone cross nearby. Matches the view of the valley of the Tay outlined in Ch. 1 (EEWN 21, 12.27-30), though the figures in the illustration are historical. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a city\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.  Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 9. Also resembles vignette in Magnum Opus edition, vol. 42, #MAG0084, though engraver there differs.',NULL,'2009-05-19 14:23:31'),(294,'NTD0045',9,'[Two Boats]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"244\"\n',52,60,1,'6 x 8.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 12 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a rowing boat and sailing vessel close to each other on a river, with figures visible in each. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 10. Resembles the depiction of the barge of the Duke of Rothesay and Henshaw\'s boat [Ch. 30; EEWN 12, 323] in the vignette to vol. 43 of the Magnum Opus edition; see #MAG0086, though engraver there differs. Illustration title as given in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:02:06'),(295,'NTD0046',2,'','--- - \"14\" - \"244\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,60,1,'5 x 7.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 13 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a castle in pastoral setting, surrounded by trees and a stream, with a moat apparently formed from the stream. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 11. Illustration title given as \'vignette of castle in the distance\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,NULL),(296,'NTD0047',2,'','--- - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,60,1,'5 x 7.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 14 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a large lake, with sailing boasts visible, with distant mountains. Main title to volume is Count Robert of Paris, though volume contains later parts of Anne of Geierstein. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 12. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a lakeshore\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,NULL),(297,'NTD0048',8,'[Constantinople?]','--- - \"354\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,60,1,'5.5 x 7.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 15 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a walled city in the distance, with high buildings and towers, and framing palm trees. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 13. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a city in the distance\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,NULL),(298,'NTD0049',7,'[Douglas Water?]','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"244\"\n',35,60,1,'5.5 x 8','1833','1833',19,'vol. 16 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts a meandering river, with cottages to the side, and distant mountains. Probably relates to the account of Douglas Water in the  story [Ch. 1; EEWN 23b, 4]. Also appears in \'Tales and Romances of the Author of Waverley\' [8vo issue], 7 vols (Edinburgh: Robert Cadell, 1833), vol. 14. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a cottage and a stream\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:02:34'),(299,'NTD0050',25,'[Street of Middlemas?]','--- - \"354\" - \"20\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',35,60,1,'5.5 x 7.5','1833','1833',19,'vol. 17 (title-page vignette)','BL 12604.c.6','','Depicts the main street of a village, with the figures of three women approaching house with iron railing and gate. Probably relates to the scene in which three \'old dames\' of the village approach the house of Doctor Gray [Ch. 15; EEWN 20, 160-61]. Illustration title given as \'vignette of a village lane\' in Todd & Bowden 282A.',NULL,NULL),(300,'LAD0001',6,'The Bride of Lammermoor / (Tales of My Landlord, Third Series, Vol. 3, p. 132)','--- - \"86\" - \"2\" - \"312\" - \"155\" - \"30\" - \"191\" - \"352\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, no. 8 (August 1819), facing p. [339], forming frontispiece to number','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood with arm round Lucy Ashton, with dead raven and arrow at feet, with Edgar looking over shoulder at the marksman, Henry Ashton [Ch. 20]. Title/caption reference is to original edition, though the volume number should be 2.',NULL,NULL),(301,'LAD0002',16,'A Legend of Montrose / (Tales of My Landlord, Third Series, Vol. 4, p. 11)','--- - \"13\" - \"59\" - \"68\" - \"65\" - \"41\" - \"74\" - \"47\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, no. 9 (September 1819), facing p. [387], forming frontispiece to number','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Sir Duncan Campbell watching Annot Lyle playing her harp, across a table, with Menteith and Allan McAulay also observing [Ch. 9]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(302,'LAD0003',5,'The Black Dwarf / (Tales of My Landlord, First Series, Vol. 1, p. 106)','--- - \"268\" - \"254\" - \"313\" - \"162\" - \"49\" - \"264\" - \"50\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, no. 10 (October 1819), facing p. [435], forming frontispiece to number','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Isabel Vere on horseback, with two female companions, confronting the Black Dwarf, who stands outside his habitation situated under a large boulder [Ch. 5]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(303,'LAD0004',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian / (Tales of My Landlord, Second Series, Vol. 2, p. 264)','--- \n- \"22\"\n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"188\"\n',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, no. 11 (November 1819), facing p. 497','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Effie Deans at the bar in Court, bent over and clinging to her sister Jeanie\'s hand, with two armed guards standing behind, and David Deans and Dumbiedikes sitting alongside on a bench, close to a group of lawyers at the foot [Ch. 24]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:41:56'),(304,'LAD0005',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian / (Tales of My Landlord, Second Series, Vol. 3, p. 154)','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"104\"\n',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, no. 12 (December 1819), facing p. 542','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Jeanie Deans being led by Madge Wildfire, along the path to the church at Willingham, and old lady with stick peering at them [Ch. 32]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:42:18'),(305,'LAD0006',18,'Old Mortality / (Tales of My Landlord, First Series, Vol. 4, p. 311)','--- \n- \"5\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"70\"\n',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1819','1819',26,'vol. 50, Supplement, no. 13, for 1819, facing p. 597','BL, P.P.5141','','Depicts Balfour of Burley in the cavern at the Black Linn burning parchment in the fire, with Morton attempting to prevent him [Ch. 43]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:03:37'),(306,'LAD0007',14,'Ivanhoe / Vol. 1 p. 110','--- \n- \"51\"\n- \"315\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"116\"\n',106,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 1 (February 1820), facing p. 101','NLS, AB.3.79.56','0007498d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54369~100132','Depicts Ivanhoe disguised as the palmer, with staff in hand, standing in front of the startled figure of Isaac, who rises from his bed [Ch. 6]. Accompanying text (pp. 101-03) headed \'The Deliverance of Isaac the Jew: An Extract from the Romance of Ivanhoe\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 2972) (see image).',NULL,'2009-11-23 11:04:32'),(307,'LAD0008',14,'Ivanhoe / Vol. 2 p. 162','--- - \"87\" - \"316\" - \"85\" - \"204\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',106,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 3 (March 1820), facing p. 121','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Ulrica seated with spindle in hand, in her cell, with Rebecca in white dress and flowing headgear standing above her in a startled manner. Accompanying text (pp. 121-22) headed \'Rebecca with the Saxon Sybil; An Extract from the Romance of Ivanhoe\' [Ch. 24]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(308,'LAD0009',14,'Ivanhoe / Vol. 3 p. 19','--- - \"87\" - \"9\" - \"315\" - \"178\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',106,49,1,'10. x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 4 (April 1820), facing p. 182','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Bois-Guilbert in smoke-filled room carrying off Rebecca, who clutches at his face, while Ivanhoe in sick-bed protests to no avail. Accompanying text (pp. 182-83) headed \'Rebecca Carried Off by the Templar: An Extract from the Romance of Ivanhoe\' [Ch. 31]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(309,'LAD0010',17,'Monastery / Vol. 1','--- - \"121\" - \"317\" - \"103\" - \"284\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 5 (May 1820), facing p. 233','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, sword in hand, before the White Lady of Avenel in ravine [Ch. 12]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(310,'LAD0011',17,'Monastery / Vol. 2','--- - \"121\" - \"290\" - \"103\" - \"229\" - \"296\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 6 (June 1820), facing p. 324','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, turning away in anguish from the fallen figure of Sir Piercie Shafton, whose sword lies on the ground [Ch. 22]. Accompanying text (pp. 324-27) headed \'Rencontre of Halbert Glendinning and Sir Piercie Shafton\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(311,'LAD0012',17,'Monastery / Vol. 3','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"318\"\n- \"290\"\n',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 7 (July 1820), facing p. 392','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Mysie Happer, lamp in hand, at the bedside of Sir Piercie Shafton [Ch. 28]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:14:50'),(312,'LAD0013',1,'Abbot / Vol. 1, p. 69','--- - \"112\" - \"123\" - \"121\" - \"324\" - \"291\" - \"26\" - \"17\" - \"225\" - \"103\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 11 (November 1820), facing p. 616','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts the Lady of Avenel (seated), with Roland Graeme as a boy close by her, stretching her hand towards Sir Halbert (in armour), who pats Wolf on the head; Lilias looks on from over the lady\'s chair [Ch. 3]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(313,'LAD0014',1,'Abbot / Vol. 2, p. 305','--- \n- \"123\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"325\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7','1820','1820',27,'vol. 1, no. 12 (December 1820), facing p. 676','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Roland Graeme kneeling before and holding the hand of Catherine Seyton, who holds in her other hand a necklace with crucifix [Ch. 24]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:04:15'),(314,'LAD0015',1,'Abbot / Vol. 3, p. 335','--- - \"123\" - \"292\" - \"130\" - \"103\" - \"96\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7','1821','1821',27,'vol. 2, no. 1 (January 1821), facing p. 46','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Roland Graeme, in armour at the Battle of Langside, holding horse with one hand, and with the other that of Henry Seyton, who lies beneath a tree looking up at Graeme, with his sword on the ground [Ch. 37]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(315,'LAD0016',15,'Kenilworth / Vol. 1, p. 150','--- - \"56\" - \"89\" - \"130\" - \"131\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'10.5 x 7','1821','1821',27,'vol. 2, no. 5 (May 1821), facing p. 272','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Amy Robsart at Cumnor Place, at the feet of the seated earl of Leicester, examining a chivalric collar worn by the Earl [Ch. 7]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(316,'LAD0017',15,'Kenilworth / Vol. 2, p. 157','--- - \"56\" - \"25\" - \"326\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'11 x 7.5','1821','1821',27,'vol. 2, no. 5 (May 1821), facing p. 274','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts the Earl of Leicester opening curtain to reveal elderly man with beard (\'Alasco\') coming down stairway [Ch. 18]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(317,'LAD0018',15,'Kenilworth / Vol. 3, p. 342','--- - \"89\" - \"229\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',99,49,1,'11 x 7.5','1821','1821',27,'vol. 2, no. 6 (June 1821), facing p. 327','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts body of Amy Robsart lying on ground, with stone arches in background [Ch. 41]. Facing text headed \'The Death of the Countess of Leicester\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(318,'LAD0019',20,'Pirate / Vol. 1, p. 235','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"191\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"264\"\n- \"137\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1822','1822',27,'vol. 3, no. 2 (February 1822), facing p. 101','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Norna with staff in hand and her other arm raised, addressing Mordaunt Mertoun, who sits on a rock with fowling-piece in hand, against a backdrop of rocks and seabirds [Ch. 10]. Accompanying text (pp. 101-02) headed \'Meeting of Norna and Mordaunt; Illustrated by an Elegant Engraving\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:04:39'),(319,'LAD0020',20,'Pirate / Vol. 2, p. 129','--- \n- \"264\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1822','1822',27,'vol. 3, no. 3 (March 1822), facing p. 161','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts the seated figure of Norna by fireplace, staff in hand, pointing to the fire, with the two sisters standing and listening [Ch. 19]. Facing text headed \'The Interview of Norna with the Daughters of Magnus Troil\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition. Also appeared as a plate in ‘Illustrations of The Pirate’ (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1825): see #ILP0002.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:06:50'),(320,'LAD0021',20,'Pirate / Vol. 3, p. 149','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1822','1822',27,'vol. 3, no. 4 (April 1822), facing p. 216','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Cleveland in front of a large stone bowing towards Minna, who is accompanied by Brenda, with a ship and rowing boat in background [Ch. 40]. Accompanying text (pp. 216-17) headed \'The Final Interview with Minna\'. Title/caption reference (inaccurate) is to original edition. Also appeared as a plate in ‘Illustrations of The Pirate’ (London: Hurst, Robinson, & Co., 1825): see #ILP0003.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:07:12'),(321,'LAD0022',26,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vol. 4, p. 5)','--- - \"197\" - \"199\" - \"198\" - \"130\" - \"26\" - \"103\" - \"247\" - \"320\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',1,1,1,'11.5 x 8.5','1825','1825',27,'vol. 6 (30 September 1825), facing p. 543','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Kenneth in full armour with lance at side, hand on face, standing above wounded dog, with Saladin (as Arabian physician) seated behind [Ch. 14]. Accompanying text (pp. 542-44) headed \'The Prevalence of Love over Duty\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(322,'LAD0023',26,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vol. 3, Page 28)','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"247\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"199\"\n',112,24,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1825','1825',27,'vol. 6 (31 October 1825), facing p. 623','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Kenneth and (the as yet unidentified) Saladin resting at the oasis, weapons and armour lying on the ground, and three palm trees behind [Ch. 2]. Accompanying text (pp. 623-24) headed \'The Friendly Opponents; or the Interview between Sir Kenneth and the Emir\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-04-10 11:07:22'),(323,'LAD0024',4,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vol. 2, Page 154)','--- - \"190\" - \"328\" - \"49\" - \"142\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',112,24,1,'14 x 10','1825','1825',27,'vol. 6 (30 November 1825), facing p. 688','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Eveline Berenger being seized by Dawfyd the Freebooter, sword in hand,  with one of Dawfyd\'s men holding on to her horse [Ch. 23]. Accompanying text (pp. 623-24) headed \'The Abduction of the Lady Eveline\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(324,'LAD0025',4,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vide Page 274, Vol. 1)','--- - \"190\" - \"192\" - \"329\" - \"330\" - \"225\" - \"284\" - \"130\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',112,75,1,'11.5 x 8','1825','1825',27,'vol. 6 (31 December 1825), facing p. 744','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Eveline Berenger after fainting being rescued from the haunted chamber at Baldringham, carried by a soldier in full armour [Ch. 14]. Accompanying text (pp. 744-45) headed \'The Rescue, with an Illustrative Engraving\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(325,'LAD0026',4,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vol. 1, Page 158)','--- - \"190\" - \"192\" - \"331\" - \"193\" - \"14\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,117,1,'8.5 x 8.5','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (31 March 1826), facing p. 162','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Eveline Berenger on the ramparts of the Castle, spear held downwards, with other arm round the crouching figure of Rose Flammock, while Father Aldrovand and Wilkin Flammock sleep [Ch. 9]. Accompanying text (pp. 162-63) headed \'The Distress of the Lady Eveline, Reproduced in an Elegant Engraving\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(326,'LAD0027',4,'Tales of the Crusaders / (Vide Page 233)','--- - \"190\" - \"330\" - \"178\" - \"155\" - \"320\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,47,1,'8.5 x 8.5','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (30 April 1826), facing p. 200','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Eveline Berenger beside bed in chamber in the Garde Doloreuse, with Damian leaning upwards towards her [Ch. 28]. Accompanying text (pp. 200-01) headed \'The Wounded Knight With an Illustrative Engraving\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(327,'LAD0028',29,'Woodstock / Page 49 Chap. 3 Vol. 1','--- - \"200\" - \"201\" - \"206\" - \"246\" - \"188\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'10 x 9','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (31 May 1826), facing p. 274','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Sir Henry and Alice Lee, in the park of Woodstock, beside a garden bench, confronted by Joseph Tomkins, who has presented the official scroll which Sir Henry is reading [Ch. 2]. Title/caption reference is to original edition, though apparently with incorrect chapter.',NULL,NULL),(328,'LAD0029',29,'Woodstock / Page 163 Chap. 6 Vol. 2','--- - \"201\" - \"319\" - \"203\" - \"26\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'9 x 9','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (30 June 1826), facing p. 330','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Alice Lee menaced by old woman (actually Charles II), with the dog Bevis at her side defending her [Ch. 18]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(329,'LAD0030',29,'Woodstock / Page 42 Chap. 2 Vol. 3','--- - \"201\" - \"319\" - \"155\" - \"74\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'8.5 x 8','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (31 July 1826), facing p. 400','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Charles II holding the hand of the kneeling Alice Lee, the two figures framed by two high-backed chairs, against one of which is leaning a musical instrument [Ch. 26]. Accompanying text (p. 400) headed \'The Interview in which Charles II Discovers Himself to Alice\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(330,'LAD0031',29,'Woodstock / Page 111 Chap. 4 Vol. 3','--- - \"319\" - \"332\" - \"202\" - \"309\" - \"296\" - \"175\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'9 x 9','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (31 August 1826), facing p. 449','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Charles II on the point of duelling with Markham Everard and revealing his identity, with Doctor Rochecliffe standing behind, and Wildrake kneeling before Charles and holding his hand [Ch. 28]. Accompanying text (pp. 449-50) headed \'The Disguised Prince; With an Elegant Engraving\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(331,'LAD0032',29,'Woodstock / Page 160 Chap. 6 Vol. 3','--- - \"308\" - \"332\" - \"307\" - \"67\" - \"175\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'9 x 9','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (30 September 1826), facing p. 500','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Cromwell questioning  Markham Everard, at his supper-table, as to the whereabouts of Charles II, with Nehemiah Holdenough behind Everard\'s chair [Ch. 30]. Accompanying text (pp. 500-01) headed \'Cromwell\'s Search for a Fugitive Prince\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(332,'LAD0033',29,'Woodstock / Page 100 Chap. 4 Vol. 1','--- - \"200\" - \"201\" - \"30\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,102,1,'10.5 x 9','1826','1826',27,'vol. 7 (31 October 1826), facing p. 567','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Sir Henry Lee and his daughter Alice leaving Woodstock Lodge, itself visible in the background [Ch. 4]. Accompanying text (pp. 567-68) headed \'The Retreat of the Cavaliers\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(333,'LAD0034',13,'Chronicles of the Canongate / Page 247 Chap. 12 Vol. 1','--- - \"208\" - \"207\" - \"46\" - \"327\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,46,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1827','1827',27,'vol. 8 (30 November 1827), facing p. 508','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Elspat MacTavish, cup in hand, and her son turning away from her towards a bed, having taken the sleeping potion [Ch. 11] Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(334,'LAD0035',27,'Chronicles of the Canongate / Page 340 Chap. 14 Vol. 1','--- - \"333\" - \"334\" - \"229\" - \"188\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,46,1,'9 x 6.5','1827','1827',27,'vol. 8 (31 December 1827), facing p. 654','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Robin Oig McCombich viewing the body of Harry Wakefield, deposited on a table, with the Constable and others looking on [Ch. 13] Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(335,'LAD0036',9,'Chronicles of the Canongate','--- - \"116\" - \"213\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',1,1,1,'7 x 8.5','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts four travellers on horseback, with a castle and town in the background. Described as \'A Scene near Perth\' on p. 661 of vol. 9.',NULL,NULL),(336,'LAD0037',25,'Chronicles of the Canongate / Page 304 Chap. 13 Vol. 2','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"336\"\n- \"335\"\n',87,46,1,'8.5 x 6.5','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (31 January 1828), facing p. 44','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Madame Montreville in a feathered turban haranguing Richard Middlemas (as \'Sadoc\'), also wearing a turban, who turns away face in hands [Ch. 26] Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:07:43'),(337,'LAD0038',25,'Chronicles of the Canongate / Page 327 Chap. 14 Vol. 2','--- - \"337\" - \"38\" - \"103\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',87,46,1,'9 x 6.5','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (29 February 1828), facing p. 103','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Sadhu Sing, as a hermit, crouched besides the tomb of his intended bride, with tiger skull and sabre at his feet [Ch. 27]. Accompanying text (pp. 103-04) headed \'The Unfortunate Bridegroom\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(338,'LAD0039',9,'Chronicles of the Canongate (Second Series) / Page 126 Chap. 4 Vol. 1','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"211\"\n- \"209\"\n',67,81,1,'9.5 x 7','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (30 June 1828), facing p. 321','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Catharine Glover approaching Henry Gow as he sleeps in his chair, sword at feet, as her father looks on from a curtained doorway behind [Ch. 5]. Accompanying headed \'The Choice of a Valentine\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:08:41'),(339,'LAD0040',9,'Chronicles of the Canongate (Second Series) / Page 149 Chap. 6 Vol. 1','--- - \"209\" - \"210\" - \"211\" - \"155\" - \"18\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',67,74,1,'10 x 7','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (31 July 1828), facing p. 377','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts Henry Gow and Catharine Glover, alone, with Henry offering to give her gloves [Ch. 6]. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(340,'LAD0041',9,'Chronicles of the Canongate (Second Series) / Page 69 Chap. 2 Vol. 2','--- - \"338\" - \"210\" - \"214\" - \"46\" - \"288\" - \"103\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',67,74,1,'9.5 x 6.5','1828','1828',27,'vol. 9 (30 September 1828), facing p. 496','NLS, AB.3.79.56','','Depicts MacIan (Hector) in full Highland gear and feathered bonnet, addressing the seated Catharine Glover, with Father Clement and two armed Highlanders behind them [Ch. 14]. Accompanying text (pp. 496-97) headed \'The Fair Maid of Perth and the Highland Chief\'. Title/caption reference is to original edition.',NULL,NULL),(341,'ILI0001',14,'Page 34 / \"Do you dispute with me slave!\" / Vol. I.','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"547\"\n- \"40\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"101\"\n',106,47,1,'10 x 7','1820','1820',8,'title-page vignette','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007550d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54787~100157','Depicts Gurth and Wamba frightened by the rearing horse of Prior Aymer, who raises his staff threateningly after Gurth refuses to show the way to Cedric\'s home in the woods. [Ch. 2] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2970) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:16:02'),(342,'ILI0002',14,'Page 103 / Ivanhoe. / The Palmer\'s Introduction to Rowena. / Vol. I.','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"315\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"90\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 1]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007505d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54485~100139','Depicts Ivanhoe disguised as the palmer, staff in hand, bowing before the seated Rowena, surrounded by attendants, in her chamber at her father\'s mansion. [Ch. 6] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2971) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:26:38'),(343,'ILI0003',14,'Page 235 / Ivanhoe. / The Tournament. / Vol. I.','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"170\"\n- \"315\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"90\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 2]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007504d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54469~100138','Depicts Ivanhoe having collapsed at Rowena\'s feet, after being wounded at the tournament and unmasked, as other knights reach to assist him. [Ch. 12] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2979) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:30:27'),(344,'ILI0004',14,'Page 173 / Ivanhoe. / Fortitude of Rebecca. / Vol. II.','--- \n- \"9\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"105\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 3]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007500d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54405~100134','Depicts Rebecca fleeing the advances of Brian de Bois-Guilbert, whose hands are depicted just entering the scene, on the ramparts of Torquilstone Castle. [Ch. 24] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2993) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:33:57'),(345,'ILI0005',14,'Page 236 / Ivanhoe. / Wamba before Front-de-Boeuf. / Vol. II.','--- \n- \"9\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"355\"\n- \"101\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 4]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007503d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54453~100137','Depicts the interview between Wamba and Reginald Front-de-Boeuf, as Brian de Bois-Guilbert and others look on. [Ch. 26] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2982) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:36:10'),(346,'ILI0006',14,'Page 21. / Ivanhoe. / Rowena rescued from the flames. / Vol. III.','--- \n- \"356\"\n- \"40\"\n- \"90\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 5]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007501d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54421~100135','Depicts Rowena in her chamber clutching a crucifix, as smoke fills the room, with Cedric and Gurth entering through a door in the background to save her. [Ch. 31] Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 2983) (see image)',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:56:28'),(347,'ILI0007',14,'Page 337 / Ivanhoe. / Rebecca at the Stake','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"9\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"222\"\n- \"87\"\n',106,47,1,'12 x 8','1820','1820',8,'[Plate 6]','NYPL, NCC (Scott) (Westall R. Illustrations of Ivanhoe)','0007502d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54437~100136','Depicts Rebecca in white dress by black chair next to the stake where she is to be burned, speaking to Bois-Guilbert, who is on horseback. [Ch. 43] Also appears as a separate plate in the Corson Collection (P. 2987) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 11:59:02'),(348,'ILG0001',11,'Guy Mannering / \'As he was about . . . unexpectedly presented herself.’ ','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"263\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"264\"\n',106,47,1,'10 x 6.5','1821','1821',2,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Meg Merrilies standing on a high bank, with long sapling bough in hand, above Godfrey Bertram and his groom both on horseback. [Ch. 8; EEWN 2, 43.29-31]',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:17:00'),(349,'ILG0002',11,'Guy Mannering / The Dead Body of Kennedy Discovered on the Beach','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"340\"\n- \"229\"\n- \"140\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Frank Kennedy\'s body beneath the cliffs, discovered by seamen in a boat as it approaches close to the shore. [Ch. 9]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:44:06'),(350,'ILG0003',11,'Guy Mannering / Julia Mannering Serenaded from the Lake','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"8\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"74\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Julia Mannering on a balcony overlooking the Lake (Windermere), on a moonlit night, with Bertram (as \'Vanbeest Brown\') in a small boat below. [Ch. 16]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:44:31'),(351,'ILG0004',11,'Guy Mannering / The Meeting of Meg Merrilies, Brown, & Dinmont','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"265\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Meg Merrilies, hands upraised in amazement, standing above the seated Henry Bertram and Dandy Dinmont, at their meal in Tib Mumps\' ale-house. [Ch. 22]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:44:53'),(352,'ILG0005',11,'Guy Mannering / Meg Merrilies Attending Upon the Dying Smuggler','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"178\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Meg Merrilies seated and staring at the prostrate figure of the wounded smuggler in the ruined Kaim of Derncleugh. [Ch. 27]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:45:23'),(353,'ILG0006',11,'Guy Mannering / The Sudden Appearance of Meg Merrilies on Gibbie\'s-Knowe','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"358\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"265\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts Meg Merrilies, spear in hand, signalling Bertram and Dinmont to follow her, with Lucy Bertram and Julia Mannering holding on in alarm to the men. [Ch. 53]',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:45:50'),(354,'ILG0007',11,'Guy Mannering / The Death of Meg Merrilies','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"266\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"42\"\n- \"427\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',2,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','','Depicts the dying Meg Merrilies, arm raised, lying on the floor of the cave, as Dirk Hatteraick points his pistol at Bertram and Dinmont as they rush in on him, with Charles Hazlewood following behind. [Ch. 54]',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:10:40'),(355,'ILM0001',17,'\'Landed---landed! . . . swimming with me.’ [4 lines in verse]','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"341\"\n- \"244\"\n',106,47,1,'9 x 7','1821','1821',28,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014452d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55823~100219','Depicts Father Philip and bolting mule fleeing from the floating figure of the White Lady, who holds a book in her hand. [Ch. 5; EEWN 9, 66-67] Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 2997) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:01:08'),(356,'ILM0002',17,'The Monastery / The Lady of Avenel and Her Daughter in the Dwelling of Dame Elspeth','--- \n- \"111\"\n- \"112\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"113\"\n- \"114\"\n',106,47,3,'9.2 x 7.4','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014453d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55841~100220','Depicts the Lady of Avenel in Elspeth Glendinning\'s home at Glendearg, seated and holding the child Mary in her arms, with Martin and Tibb Tacket looking on, the former holding shepherd\'s crook. [Ch. 4] Legend uniquely in this series includes the wording \'Engraved on Steel by Chas. Heath\', and the illustration has denser quality of shading compared to the others. Todd & Bowden state that this plate is apparently \'the first in any production to be engraved on steel\' (p. 523; see also 453-55). Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 2995) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:06:52'),(357,'ILM0003',17,'The Monastery / Halbert Glendinning\'s First Invocation of the White Lady of Avenel','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"121\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014454d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55858~100221','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, sword in hand, facing the White Lady of Avenel in ravine. [Ch. 12] Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 2999) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:09:25'),(358,'ILM0004',17,'The Monastery / The Spirit of Avenel Appearing to Halbert the Night Previous to His Duel with Sir Piercie Shafton','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"289\"\n- \"121\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014455d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55874~100222','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, sword pointing downwards, confronting the floating figure of the White Lady of Avenel, who points with an extended arm towards him, while his brother Edward leans upwards from his bed [Ch. 20]. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3000) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:11:24'),(359,'ILM0005',17,'The Monastery / Warden Reproving Julian Avenel for His Injustice to Catherine','--- \n- \"342\"\n- \"343\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"119\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014456d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55890~100223','Depicts Henry Warden, with white hair, pointing his arm upwards accusingly, while Julian Avenel sits on a throne-like chair, with the imploring figure of Catherine bending at his side; two soldiers are also visible behind [Ch. 25]. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3001) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:14:08'),(360,'ILM0006',17,'The Monastery / Mysie Happer\'s Visit to Sir Piercie Shafton','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"318\"\n- \"290\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014457d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55907~100224','Depicts Mysie Happer lamp in hand, at the bedside of Sir Piercie Shafton. [Ch. 28]. Similar scene to, but distinct from, #LAD0012. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3005) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:17:16'),(361,'ILM0007',17,'The Monastery / Catherine Discovered by Halbert Lamenting Over the Body of Julian Avenel','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"342\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"343\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"96\"\n',106,47,1,'9.5 x 7.5','1821','1821',28,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.ALL','0014458d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55923~100225','Depicts the body of Julian Avenel on the battlefield, with Catherine leaning over him with a child in arms, and Halbert Glendinning in semi-armour looking on, with soldiers in the background [Ch. 36]. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3006) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:19:07'),(362,'ILK0001',15,'\'Upstarted of a sudden . . . cudgel in his hand.’','--- \n- \"31\"\n- \"344\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"89\"\n',60,91,1,'9 x 7','1821','1821',9,'title-page vignette','NLS, F.4.e.25','0007552d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54821~100159','Depicts Anthony Foster with a cudgel threatening Laurence Goldthred, who is on horseback, and interrupting the intended conversation with Amy Robsart, who appears at a window of Cumnor Place [Ch. 2; EEWN 11, 15.36-38]. Also appears as separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3040) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:20:34'),(363,'ILK0002',15,'Kenilworth / The Countess in the Withdrawing Room Attended by Janet','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"32\"\n- \"89\"\n',60,47,1,'10 x 8','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 1]','NLS, F.4.e.25','0007551d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54804~100158','Depicts an elaborately-attired Amy Robsart in the withdrawing room at Cumnor Place, attended to by the more plainly-dressed Janet Foster [Ch. 6]. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3041) (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:21:51'),(364,'ILK0003',15,'Kenilworth / Wayland Smith Relating the History of His Life','--- - \"95\" - \"345\" - \"346\" - \"353\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,88,1,'10.5 x 8.5','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 2]','NLS, F.4.e.25','','Depicts Wayland Smith in his forge, with Tressilian, whose sword is pointed downward, listening, while Sludge (\'Flibbertigibbet\') crouches at Smith\'s feet looking at him intently. [Chs 10 & 11]',NULL,NULL),(365,'ILK0004',15,'Kenilworth / The Gallantry of Sir Walter Raleigh','--- - \"347\" - \"348\" - \"349\" - \"130\" - \"96\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,96,1,'10.5 x 8','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 3]','NLS, F.4.e.25','','Depicts Sir Walter Raleigh laying his cloak on the ground before the Queen, who walks on the arm of Lord Hunsdon, with an escort of soldiers behind. [Ch. 15]',NULL,NULL),(366,'ILK0005',15,'Kenilworth / Mike Lambourne Drunk in the Garden of Cumnor Place, Abusing Tony Foster and the Astrologer','--- - \"350\" - \"25\" - \"31\" - \"67\" - \"326\" - \"34\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,91,1,'10.5 x 8','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 4]','NLS, F.4.e.25','','Depicts the drunken Michael Lambourne, with a frowning Anthony Foster and alarmed Dr Doboobie (\'Alasco\') looking on. [Ch. 18]',NULL,NULL),(367,'ILK0006',15,'Kenilworth / The Entry of Queen Elizabeth into Kenilworth Castle','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"347\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"56\"\n',60,29,1,'10.5 x 8','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 5]','NLS, F.4.e.25','','Depicts the Queen about to enter through the gateway to Kenilworth, accompanied by Leicester and a body of riders, some carrying torches; the portcullis of the gate has been lifted, and the Porter with spiked club waits just inside the entrance [Ch. 30].',NULL,'2009-04-10 14:22:29'),(368,'ILK0007',15,'Kenilworth / The Meeting of Leicester & the Countess at Kenilworth','--- - \"89\" - \"56\" - \"99\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',60,88,1,'10.5 x 8','1821','1821',9,'[Plate 6]','NLS, F.4.e.25','','Depicts Amy Robsart eagerly embracing her husband, Leicester, in an apartment at Kenilworth, with Varney standing at the side menacingly. [Ch. 35]',NULL,NULL),(369,'SKE0001',28,'Doune Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"249\"\n- \"46\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.5','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 8','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Doune Castle, with two horsemen and Highlanders in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 9), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'On the opposite bank . . . rays of the sun. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 78.’ See Ch. 38; EEWN 1, 197. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:51:21'),(370,'SKE0002',28,'Stirling Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"250\"\n',92,1,2,'7.2 x 13.5','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 10','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Jacobite army passing beneath Stirling Castle. Accompanying quoted text (p. 11), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Balmawhapple with his horse-pistol . . . Stirling Castle. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 90.’ See Ch. 39; EEWN 1, 202-03. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:51:44'),(371,'SKE0003',28,'Palace Gate','--- \n- \"48\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 12','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Gate before Holyrood Palace, with Jacobite troops drawn up outside, and horsemen approaching entry. Accompanying quoted text (p. 13), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'He drew up his men . . . guard of Highlanders. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 94.’ See Ch. 39; EEWN 1, 204. Originally issued as part of a separate number 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:52:14'),(372,'SKE0004',28,'White Horse Inn','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"240\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 14','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts White Horse Inn, in Close off the Canongate, with Highlanders in the foreground, and clothing hung out to dry. Accompanying quoted text (p. 13), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'He reached the door . . . called the Canongate. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 106.’ See Ch. 41; EEWN 1, 210. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829. Compare Tilt’s ‘Landscape Illustrations’, #LIW0006, also of White Horse Inn, stated to be from a drawing by Skene, and where the composition is similar, though with minor variations (as in the disposition of figures).',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:52:58'),(373,'SKE0005',28,'Battle of Prestonpans','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 16','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Battle of Prestonpans, with Highlanders engaging cavalry in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 16), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'It was at this moment . . . within his aim. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 168.’ See Ch. 47; EEWN 1, 240-41. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:53:47'),(374,'SKE0006',28,'Carlisle Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"222\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 18','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Gate of Carlisle Castle, with Fergus Mac-Ivor and Evan Dhu Maccombich being led out on a sledge for execution, and troops of soldiers drawn up. Accompanying quoted text (p. 18), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Through the deep . . . on the drawbridge. / Waverley, Vol. II, p. 390.’ See Ch. 69; EEWN 1, 349-50. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:54:11'),(375,'SKE0007',11,'Caerlaverock Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'leaf preceding p. 19','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Caerlaverock Castle, in open countryside, as an equivalent for the old Castle of Ellangowan. Accompanying quoted text (p. 19), with reference to original edition: \'The front of the old Castle . . . the inner court. / Guy Mannering, Vol. I, p. 58.’ See Ch. 4; EEWN 2, 21. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:55:30'),(376,'SKE0008',11,'Court of Caerlaverock','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"108\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 22','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts interior of Caerlaverock Castle, as an equivalent for that of the old castle of Ellangowan, with a figure in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 22), with reference to original edition: \'Upon entering the gateway . . . lofty and large, &c. / Guy Mannering, Vol. I, p. 62.’ See Ch. 4; EEWN 2, 22. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:56:09'),(377,'SKE0009',11,'Rueberry Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 24','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts cliffs along north coast of Solway Firth, with a stormy sea below, and two ships, one wrecked on the rocks, with small figures fleeing. Accompanying quoted text (p. 22), with reference to original edition: \'Some time afterwards . . . with the blue sky. / Guy Mannering, Vol. I, p. 138.’ See Ch. 9; EEWN 2, 48-49. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 08:56:44'),(378,'SKE0010',11,'Tod Willie','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"117\"\n- \"72\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'leaf preceding p. 27','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Tod Willie surrounded by his hounds in wild Border countryside; based, according to Skene\'s commentary, on a sketch taken in 1804, when he was accompanying Scott on an excursion \'among the wild pasture glens of Roxburgh and Dumfries-shire\' (p. 27). Accompanying quoted text (p. 27), with reference to original edition: \'Some dappled mists . . .  but extremely narrow. / Guy Mannering, Vol. II, p. 53.’ See Ch. 25; EEWN 2, 134. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829. ',NULL,'2009-08-19 10:19:50'),(379,'SKE0011',11,'Glencaple','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'leaf preceding p. 29','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Port of Glencaple as an equivalent for Portanferry. Accompanying quoted text (p. 29), with reference to original edition: \'Early on the following morning . . . confinement at Portanferry. / Guy Mannering, Vol. III, p. 78.\' See Ch. 44; EEWN 2, 262. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:33:29'),(380,'SKE0012',11,'The Toors Cave','--- \n- \"314\"\n- \"142\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 30','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Torrs Cave as an equivalent of Dirk Hatteraick\'s cave, with figures representing Hatteraick and Meg Merrilies within the cave, and three other figures concealed near the entrance. Accompanying quoted text (p. 31), with reference to original edition: \'While this dialogue . . .  salmon by night. / Guy Mannering, Vol. III, p. 285.\' See Ch. 54; EEWN 2, 331. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:34:00'),(381,'SKE0013',23,'Glasgow 1764','--- \n- \"321\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.3','1829','1829',1,'leaf preceding p. 35','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the City of Glasgow, as \'taken from a representation . . . that appears to have been executed in the year 1764’ (p. 35). Accompanying quoted text (p. 35), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: ‘The fertile vale of Clyde opened upon us. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 23.’ See Ch. 19; EEWN 5, 154. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:34:43'),(382,'SKE0014',23,'Glasgow Cathedral','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"321\"\n- \"244\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 36','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Glasgow Cathedral, with Gallowgate Burn in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 37), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Situated in a populous . . .  solemnity of the scene. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 27.\' See Ch. 19; EEWN 5, 156-57. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:35:23'),(383,'SKE0015',23,'Barony Kirk','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"321\"\n',92,1,2,'7.7 x 13','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 40','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts interior of the Barony, or Laigh Kirk of Glasgow. Accompanying quoted text (p. 41), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Conceive an extensive range . . . used as a church. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 33.\' See Ch. 20; EEWN 5, 159. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:36:52'),(384,'SKE0016',23,'Aberfoyle','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"72\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'leaf preceding p. 45','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Clachan of Aberfoyle, with mountain behind. Accompanying quoted text (p. 45), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'We crossed the infant Forth . . . and very narrow. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 161.\' See Ch. 28; EEWN 5, 227. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:38:06'),(385,'SKE0017',23,'Lochard','--- \n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',92,1,2,'6.4 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 46','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Loch Ard, with Ben Lomond on the distant horizon. Accompanying quoted text (p. 47), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The road now suddenly . . . which it is encircled. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 161.\' See Ch. 30; EEWN 5, 252. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:38:47'),(386,'SKE0018',23,'Fort of Alianan','--- \n- \"47\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 48','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Ford over the Forth, close to Aberfoyle, with a party of soldiers crossing the river. Accompanying quoted text (p. 49), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Forth . . . horseman to descend at once. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 259.\' See Ch. 33; EEWN 5, 278. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:39:33'),(387,'SKE0019',23,'Lediart','--- \n- \"47\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"351\"\n',92,1,2,'13.4 x 8','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 50','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts waterfall at Ledard, on west coast of Loch Ard. Accompanying quoted text (p. 51), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'They were stationed . . . two distinct leaps. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 311.\' See Ch. 35; EEWN 5, 306. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:44:13'),(388,'SKE0020',23,'Inversnaid','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 52','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Fort of Inversnaid, in rugged Highland landscape. Accompanying quoted text (p. 53), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The extreme strength . . . securing against it. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 318.\' See Ch. 36; EEWN 5, 309. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:44:51'),(389,'SKE0021',23,'Creek on the Shore of Loch Lomond','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1829','1829',1,'facing p. 54','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Loch Lomond, near Inversnaid Mill, with Highlanders and boats on the shore. Accompanying quoted text (p. 55), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'A boat waited . . . four lusty Highland rowers. / Rob Roy, Vol. VIII, p. 318.\' See Ch. 36; EEWN 5, 310. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1829.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:46:10'),(390,'SKE0022',5,'Manor Glen','--- \n- \"242\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 51[2]','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Manor Glen, in Peeblesshire, home of David Ritchie, the supposed original of the Black Dwarf in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 51[2]), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Ye\'ll hae heard o\' Canny Elshie the Black Dwarf, or I\'m muckle mistaen. / Black Dwarf, Vol. IX, p. 3.\' See Ch. 1; EEWN 4a, 12. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830. Compare Tilt\'s \'Landscape Illustrations\', #LIW0021, also of the Manor Glen, stated to be from a drawing by Skene, and where the composition is similar, though with some differences in representation and detail.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:46:49'),(391,'SKE0023',5,'The Dwarf\'s Hut','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"20\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 54[2]','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Black Dwarf\'s hut, with trees behind, and two goats in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 54[2]), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The inside of his hut . . . do their Morai. / Black Dwarf, Vol. IX, p. 44.\' See Ch. 4; EEWN 4a, 32. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-14 13:47:35'),(392,'SKE0024',5,'The Dwarf\'s Grave','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"38\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 58','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the burial of David Ritchie, supposed original of the Black Dwarf, in the churchyard at Manor. Accompanying text (p. 58), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'I am doubting we have lost Cannie Elshie for gude an\' a\'. / Black Dwarf, Vol. IX, p. 212.\' See Ch. 18; EEWN 4a, 122. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:39:20'),(393,'SKE0025',18,'The Martyr\'s Tomb','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"77\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 60','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts \'Old Mortality\' restoring a gravestone, in open woodland, with pony nearby by a tree. Accompanying quoted text (p. 61), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'An old man . . . letters of the inscription. / Old Mortality, Vol. IX, p. 245.\' See Ch. 1; EEWN 4b, 8. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:40:06'),(394,'SKE0026',18,'Craignethan Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"255\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 62','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Craignethan Castle as an equivalent for Tillietudlem. Accompanying quoted text (p. 63), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Tower of Tillietudlem stood . . . with the Clyde. / Old Mortality, Vol. X, p. 48.\' See Ch. 11; EEWN 4b, 97. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:41:01'),(395,'SKE0027',18,'The Conventicle','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"117\"\n- \"88\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.5','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 66','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts stones placed on a moorland hill to commemorate religious services held there as conventicles. Accompanying quoted text (p. 63), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Aweel, they had me up . . . cow in a fremd loaning. / Old Mortality, Vol. X, p. 85.\' See Ch. 14; EEWN 4b, 121. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:41:26'),(396,'SKE0028',18,'Bothwell Bridge','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 68','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Bothwell Bridge, with contestants in battle on the bridge and in surrounding countryside. Accompanying quoted text (p. 69), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The ammunition of the defenders of the Bridge began to fail at this important crisis. / Old Mortality, Vol. X, p. 355.\' See Ch. 32; EEWN 4b, 256. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:41:51'),(397,'SKE0029',18,'The Black Linn','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"244\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 72','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Black Linn, though without attempting to include Balfour of Burley\'s cave as in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 72), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'An awesome place . . . Linn of Linklater . . .  An old oak tree . . . uncertain footing. / Old Mortality, Vol. XI, pp. 95, 99.\' See Chs 42 & 43; EEWN 4b, 333, 335. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:42:17'),(398,'SKE0030',18,'The Creehope Linn','--- \n- \"314\"\n- \"351\"\n',92,1,2,'13.4 x 7.8','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 73','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Creehope Linn, as a site of concealment for Covenanters, and apparently as an equivalent for Balfour of Burley\'s cave as in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 73), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The terrible cavern called Creehope Linn . . . professors of non-conformity. / Old Mortality, Vol. XI, p. 100.\' See Ch. 43; EEWN 4b, 336. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:42:52'),(399,'SKE0031',12,'The Heart of Mid-Lothian','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"85\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 77','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Edinburgh Tolbooth, with side of St Giles Cathedral to the right. Accompanying quoted text (p. 77), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The very reverend Tolbooth itself. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XI, p. 175.\' See Ch. 1, EEWN 6, 13. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:43:25'),(400,'SKE0032',12,'The Guard House','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"96\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 80','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Guard-House, old headquarters of the City Guard, in the Edinburgh High Street. Accompanying quoted text (p. 80), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Guard-House was . . . its beautiful esplanade. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XI, p. 247.\' See Ch. 6; EEWN 6, 51. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:44:08'),(401,'SKE0033',12,'The Grassmarket','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"222\"\n',92,1,2,'13.3 x 7.8','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 84','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Edinburgh Grassmarket, with the Gallows near the foot, and a crowd assembled for an execution. Accompanying quoted text (p. 84), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The area of the Grassmarket . . . dangled the deadly halter. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XI, p. 210\'. See Ch. 4; EEWN 6, 32-33. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:48:31'),(402,'SKE0034',12,'The Netherbow Port','--- \n- \"251\"\n',92,1,2,'13.3 x 7.8','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 86','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the old Netherbow Port, an entrance to Edinburgh, as seen from the Canongate. Accompanying quoted text (p. 87), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Netherbow Port might . . . divides London from Westminster. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XI, p. 246.\' See Ch. 6; EEWN 6, 51. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:49:08'),(403,'SKE0035',12,'Salisbury Crags','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"110\"\n- \"72\"\n',92,1,2,'6.2 x 13.3','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 90','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts edge of Salisbury Crags, with Edinburgh and its Castle in the near distance, and St Leonard\'s Hill as a low ridge to the left. Accompanying quoted text (p. 90), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'If I were to choose . . . called Salisbury Crags. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XI, p. 285.\' See Ch. 8; EEWN 6, 64. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 17:49:32'),(404,'SKE0036',12,'The Parliament Close','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"251\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 92','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Parliament Close, with the equestrian statue of Charles II visible in the square. Accompanying quoted text (p. 93), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'This edifice . . . respect from its antiquity. / Heart of Mid-Lothian, Vol. XII, p. 117.\' See Ch. 22; EEWN 6, 193. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:06:34'),(405,'SKE0037',6,'Wintoun House','--- \n- \"311\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 96','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Wintoun House as an equivalent of Ravenswood Castle. Accompanying quoted text (p. 97), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'They entered the avenue . . . flanked with huge elm-trees. / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIV, p. 135.\' See Ch. 18; EEWN 7a, 143. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:07:02'),(406,'SKE0038',6,'Fast Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"378\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 100','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Fast Castle, viewed upwards from the surrounding cliffs, as the original of Wolf\'s Crag in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 100), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Yonder is Wolf\'s Crag . . . difficult to conceive. / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIII, p. 362.\' See Ch. 7; EEWN 7a, 59. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:07:54'),(407,'SKE0039',6,'Eyemouth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 102','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the seaport of Eyemouth, as an equivalent of Wolf\'s Hope in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 103), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'It was a little hamlet . . . called Wolf\'s-hope. / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIV, p. 50.\' See Ch. 12; EEWN 7a, 100. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:08:19'),(408,'SKE0040',6,'Earnsheugh','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"218\"\n',92,1,2,'13.4 x 8','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 104','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts high cliffs at Earnsheugh, in the vicinity of Fast Castle, itself the original of Wolf\'s Crag in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 105), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'It was a November morning . . . thick and heavy mist. / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIII, p. 287.\' See Ch. 2; EEWN 7a, 19. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:09:03'),(409,'SKE0041',6,'Coldingham Priory','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 106','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Coldingham Priory, with the remnant of the original in ruins. Accompanying quoted text (p. 107), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Under the very arch . . . decayed prosperity. / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIII, p. 289.\' See Ch. 2; EEWN 7a, 20. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830. Compare Tilt\'s \'Landscape Illustrations\', #LIW0032, also of Coldingham, stated to be from a drawing by Skene, and where the composition is similar, with variant details (e.g. presence of cattle within ruined arch).',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:09:59'),(410,'SKE0042',6,'Links of Eyemouth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',92,1,2,'5.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 110','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts harbour at Eyemouth, as in the present time, with reference to the quick-sands in the closing stages of the story. Accompanying quoted texts (p. 110), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'When the last Laird . . . lost for evermore!\' [4 lines verse] / Bride of Lammermoor, Vol. XIV, p. 128\' and \'The links by the sea-shore . . . our only weapons. / Vol. XIV, p. 363.\' See Chs 18 & 32; EEWN 7a, 139, 265. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830. Compare Tilt\'s \'Landscape Illustrations\', #LIW0034, also of Links of Eyemouth, stated to be from a drawing by Skene, and where the composition is similar, though with minor variations (e.g.  figures in foreground).',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:11:07'),(411,'SKE0043',16,'Dunstafnage Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n',92,1,2,'6.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 113','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Dunstafnage Castle, as original of Ardenvohr in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 113), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Ardenvohr rose high . . . preceding evening\' / Legend of Montrose, Vol. XV, p. 134\' and \'Still the captain . . . east of the castle. / vol. XV, p. 143.\' See Ch. 10; EEWN 7b, 77, 82 . Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:11:38'),(412,'SKE0044',16,'Inverara Castle [sic]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 116','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts old Inverary Castle. Accompanying quoted text (p. 116), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The noble old Gothic castle . . . uniform mansion. / Legend of Montrose, Vol. XV, p. 157.\' See Ch. 11; EEWN 7b, 89. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:13:08'),(413,'SKE0045',16,'Inverlochy Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"47\"\n',92,1,2,'6.7 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 118','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Inverlochy Castle, with cattle grazing in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 119), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The ancient Castle of Inverlochy . . . Lochy joins Loch-Eil. / Legend of Montrose, Vol. XV, p. 269.\' See Ch. 18; EEWN 7b, 147. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:14:31'),(414,'SKE0046',14,'Vale of the Don','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"244\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 123','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Vale of the Don, with Coningsburgh Castle in middle distance. Accompanying quoted texts (p. 123), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'In that pleasant district . . . town of Doncaster. / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVI, p. 3\' and \'There are few . . . ancient Saxon fortress, [Coningsburgh].\' See Chs 1 & 41; EEWN 8, 15, 368. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:15:15'),(415,'SKE0047',14,'Rotherwood','--- \n- \"67\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 126','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts interior of the Hall at (the fictitious) Rotherwood, \'taken from a description given in the \"Gunnlanga Saga\" of a state apartment, in the mansion of a Scandanavian chief\' (p. 127). Accompanying quoted text (p. 127), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'And, pointing to a low irregular building . . . Cedric the Saxon. / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVI, p. 35.\' See Ch. 2; EEWN 8, 32. Originally issued as part of a separate number 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:15:47'),(416,'SKE0048',14,'Ashby','--- \n- \"14\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 130','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Castle of Ashby de la Zouch. Accompanying quoted text (p. 130), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Prince John held his high festival . . . his historical fame. / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVI, p. 212.\' See Ch. 14; EEWN 8, 125. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:16:16'),(417,'SKE0049',14,'Jervaulx','--- \n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 133','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts ruins of Jervaulx Abbey. Accompanying quoted text (p. 133), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'O, it is a rich abbey-stede . . . good fathers of Jorvaulx! / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVII, p. 175.\' See Ch. 33; EEWN 8, 285. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:16:47'),(418,'SKE0050',14,'Coningsburgh','--- \n- \"14\"\n',92,1,2,'12.4 x 8','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 134','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts tower of partly-ruined Coningsburgh Castle. Accompanying quoted text (p. 134), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'When Coeur de Lion . . . rude barrier of palisades. / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVII, p. 331.\' See Ch. 41; EEWN 8, 368. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:17:13'),(419,'SKE0051',14,'Fontevraud','--- \n- \"38\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"196\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 138','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts grave of Richard Coeur de Lion and family members in Sepulchral Vault of monastery of Fontevraud in Anjou. Accompanying quoted text (p. 139), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'With the life of a generous . . . his generosity had formed. / Ivanhoe, Vol. XVII, p. 397.\' See Ch. 44; EEWN 8, 401. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:17:41'),(420,'SKE0052',17,'Melrose Abbey','--- \n- \"256\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 142','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts ruins of Melrose Abbey. Accompanying quoted text (p. 143), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'It [the village of Kennaquhair] has . . . founded by David the First of Scotland. / Monastery, Vol. XVIII, p. 2.\' See Ch. 1; EEWN 9, 31. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:18:12'),(421,'SKE0053',17,'Bastel House','--- \n- \"14\"\n',92,1,2,'7.7 x 13','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 146','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Bastel Tower in the village of Darnick. Accompanying quoted text (p. 146), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'These small peel-houses . . . garrison these points of defence. / Monastery, Vol. XVIII, p. 5.\' See Ch. 1; EEWN 9, 33. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:18:44'),(422,'SKE0054',17,'Coomsley','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"382\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 148','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Coomsley Tower as an equivalent of Glendearg in the story. Accompanying quoted text (p. 149), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The site was a beautiful . . . almost hidden situation. / Monastery, Vol. XVIII, p. 10.\' See Ch. 2; EEWN 9, 36. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:20:07'),(423,'SKE0055',17,'Nameless Deen [sic]','--- \n- \"242\"\n- \"72\"\n',92,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 150','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts glen \'well known in he neighbourhood as the Fairy or Nameless Dean\' (p. 151), with Eildon Hills in background. Accompanying quoted text (p. 151), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'As our Glendearg did not . . . Hollow of the Fairies. / Monastery, Vol. XVIII, p. 13.\' See Ch. 2; EEWN 9, 37. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:20:34'),(424,'SKE0056',17,'Old Bridge of Tweed','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"244\"\n',92,1,2,'7 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 152','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts the Old Bridge of Tweed, with Eildon Hills in background. Accompanying quoted text (p. 153), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'A bridge was then standing . . . river was complete. / Monastery, Vol. XVIII, p. 64.\' See Ch. 5; EEWN 9, 62-63. Originally issued as part of a separate number 1830. Compare Tilt\'s \'Landscape Illustrations\', #LIW0044, also of Old Bridge of Tweed, stated to be from a drawing by Skene, and where the composition is similar, though with variations in detail.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:21:09'),(425,'SKE0057',17,'Melrose','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"354\"\n',92,1,2,'7.8 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 154','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Melrose Cross, with the town behind. Accompanying quoted text (p. 155), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'In this order the procession . . . former monarch of Scotland. / Monastery, Vol. XIX, p. 333.\' See Ch. 37; EEWN 9, 343-44. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:21:42'),(426,'SKE0058',1,'The Kirk of Field','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'leaf preceding p. 159','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts ruins of the \'ancient Collegiate Church of St Mary\' (p. 159), eventually included within the walls of Edinburgh. Accompanying quoted text (p. 159), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'But, blessed Lady . . . gambol by burning the church? / The Abbot, Vol. XX, p. 225.\' See Ch. 17; EEWN 10, 134. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:22:13'),(427,'SKE0059',1,'Loch Leven Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"128\"\n',92,1,2,'6 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 162','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Loch Leven Castle (part ruined), on island surrounded by Loch, with boat and boatmen visible in foreground. Accompanying quoted text (p. 162), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The water-girdled fortress . . . air of desolate seclusion. / The Abbot, Vol. XX, p. 347.\' See Ch. 20; EEWN 10, 183. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:22:47'),(428,'SKE0060',1,'Niddrie Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n',92,1,2,'7.5 x 13.4','1830','1830',1,'facing p. 164','EUL, Corson B.SKE.3','','Depicts Niddrie Castle, home of the Seytons, in pastoral setting. Accompanying quoted text (p. 164), with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Long before day-break . . . belonging to Lord Seyton . . . A landscape of wood . . . most favourite nobles. / The Abbot, Vol. XXI, pp. 299, 301.\' See Ch. 36; EEWN 10, 345, 346. Originally issued as part of a separate number in 1830.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:23:33'),(429,'KEP0001',14,'Rebecca','--- \n- \"18\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"87\"\n',60,47,3,'10.1 x 7.9','1827','1827',23,'facing p.216','NLS, NE1027.b','0007510d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54567~100144','Depicts Rebecca seated alone in her cell in Torquilstone Castle, wearing fur-trimmed cloak, decorated belt, and turban-like headdress [Ch. 25]. Accompanying anonymous poem, also titled \'Rebecca\', pp. 216-17, though without direct reference to the novel. Accompanying imprint on plate states \'Printed by McQueen\'.',NULL,'2009-09-04 12:23:17'),(430,'FIS0001',28,'Sir Walter Scott, Bart.','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',45,87,3,'10.5 x 8.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/1','','Depicts Scott seated with cane in hand with his dog Bran in the bottom right corner; a view of the Eildon Hills is to the left. Disliking a recent portrait by David Wilkie, Robert Cadell commissioned this portrait in March 1830. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' (London: Fisher, [1836-38]). A different engraving of the same portrait, by John Horsburgh, accompanies volume 33 of the Magnum Opus edition (see #MAG0065), and also appeared later in the Centenary edition of the Waverley Novels (see #CEN0001). (See Russell, Francis, \'Portraits of Sir Walter Scott\' (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 55, pp.40-41).',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:23:59'),(431,'FIS0002',28,'\'Eh, sirs!\' / \'Oh messieurs!\'','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"97\"\n- \"102\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 1, facing p. 85','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/1','0014644d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57274~100305','Depicts two women washing clothing in large tubs with their feet, as Edward Waverley enters from  the right, and discovers them from across a stream, in the gardens of Tully-Veolan, which is visible through the trees. [Ch. 9; EEWN 1, 40.24] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 4] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:04:52'),(432,'FIS0003',28,'The Pass of Bally-Brough / Passage de Bally-Brough','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"360\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"351\"\n- \"102\"\n',68,62,3,'12.7 x 8.6','1836','1836',32,'vol. 1, facing p. 169','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/1','0014641d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57224~100302','Depicts Evan Dhu in full highland dress holding a rifle, with Edward Waverley and two others, in the pass of Bally-Brough, as an eagle flies above. Mountains, a waterfall, and the setting sun appear in the distance. [Ch. 16] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 2] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:06:32'),(433,'FIS0004',28,'Mac Murrough\'s Chant / Chant de Mac Murrough','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"262\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"102\"\n',63,77,3,'8 x 12.4','1836','1836',32,'vol. 1, facing p. 215','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/1','0014646d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57306~100307','Depicts a Highland banquet attended by Waverley and Fergus MacIvor among many others. In the centre, Mac-Murrough, the family bard, sings Celtic songs, as guests watch and drink, two with raised swords. [Ch. 20] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 3] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:07:40'),(434,'FIS0005',28,'Edinburgh. March of the Highlanders / Edimbourgh. Marche des Montagnards','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"110\"\n- \"46\"\n',103,50,3,'8.4 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/2','0014649d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57357~100310','Depicts large groups of Highlanders marching through Edinburgh, with the North Bridge (unhistorically) and Edinburgh Castle in the distance. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 5] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:09:23'),(435,'FIS0006',28,'Lady Waverley! ten thousand a-year! / Lady Waverley! Dix mille livres de revenu!','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"357\"\n- \"102\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.9','1836','1836',32,'vol. 2, facing p. 355','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/2','0014648d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57341~100309','Depicts Bailie Macwheeble jumping and throwing his cap in the air in excitement at the news that Waverley, who sits in a chair at a desk covered with piles of paper, intends to marry Rose Bradwardine [Ch. 66; EEWN 1, 332.12]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 4] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:13:02'),(436,'FIS0007',28,'Carlisle Castle. Execution of Fergus Mac-Ivor & Evan Dhu / Chateau de Carlisle. Execution de Fergus Mac-Ivor et d\'Evan Dhu','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"222\"\n- \"96\"\n',81,15,3,'8 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 2, facing p. 390','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/2','0014647d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57324~100308','Depicts Carlisle Castle and the crowd assembling for the executions of Fergus MacIvor and Evan Dhu [Ch. 69]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 6] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:14:29'),(437,'FIS0008',11,'Dirk Hatteraick pursued by the Sloop of War / Dirk Hatteraick poursuivi par le Sloop de Guerre','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"140\"\n',6,5,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/3','0007421d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53677~100091','Depicts two large sailing vessels in choppy waters near overhanging cliffs. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 7] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:16:10'),(438,'FIS0009',11,'Pro-di-gi-ous! / Pro-di-gi-eux!','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"92\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 3, facing p. 74','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/3','0007422d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53695~100092','Depicts Dominie Sampson having fallen into the slough of Lochend while playing with the young Harry Bertram, who stands to the side, as two village matrons help him out. [Ch. 8; EEWN 2, 41.23] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 9] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:17:29'),(439,'FIS0010',11,'The Waste of Cumberland / Plaine de Cumberland','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"117\"\n',68,101,3,'8.3 x 13.4','1836','1836',32,'vol. 3, facing p. 237','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/3','0007425d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53745~100095','Depicts Dandy Dinmont and Brown (Harry Bertram) fleeing from their assailant on the pony Dumple, over the Waste of Cumberland, as the dog Wasp follows them. [Ch. 23] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 8] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:20:28'),(440,'FIS0011',11,'Coln. Mannering, Hazlewood & the Smugglers  / Coln. Mannering, Hazlewood et les Contrebandiers','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"178\"\n',103,35,3,'8.2 x 13.4','1836','1836',32,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/4','0007424d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53728~100094','Depicts a band of smugglers attacking Woodbourne, as a shot from inside the house wounds one of them. [Ch. 30] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 10] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:21:58'),(441,'FIS0012',11,'Gape, sinner, and swallow! / Ouvre la bouche, pecheur, et avale!','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"92\"\n- \"98\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 4, facing p. 221','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/4','0007429d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53812~100099','Depicts Meg Merrilies commanding Dominie Sampson to eat the stew sitting in front of him, as a cauldron boils in the background [Ch. 47; EEWN 2, 280.2]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 9] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:23:18'),(442,'FIS0013',11,'Carlaverock Castle. The Common of Ellangowan / Chateau de Carlaverock. Communes d\'Ellangowan','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"358\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"108\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"72\"\n',68,62,3,'8.2 x 13.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 4, facing p. 313','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/4','0007427d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53779~100097','Depicts Meg Merrilies bidding Harry Bertram and Dandy Dinmont to go with her, as Julia Mannering and Lucy Bertram retreat homeward on the left, with a mountain and Ellangowan in the background. [Ch. 53] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 11] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-04 13:24:51'),(443,'FIS0014',3,'Bally-burgh Ness / Pointe de Bally-burgh','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"362\"\n- \"361\"\n',103,34,3,'8.2 x 13.4','1836','1836',32,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/5','0007359d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52682~100033','Depicts Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour with Edie Ochiltree, stranded on the beach, with the sun setting (incorrectly in the east) on the horizon of a turbulent sea [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 12] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:22:34'),(444,'FIS0015',3,'The Antiquary & Lovel / L\'Antiquaire et Lovel','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"267\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"140\"\n',68,11,3,'8.2 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 5, facing p. 160','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/5','0007358d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52665~100032','Depicts Oldbuck accompanied by Lovel bargaining over the price of fish with Maggie Mucklebackit, who is seated alongside buckets of fish, with cottages and fishing boats forming a part of the larger background. [Ch. 11] Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 13] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:24:08'),(445,'FIS0016',3,'Mrs. Heukbane & Mrs. Shortcake / Madame Heukbane & Madame Shortcake','--- \n- \"363\"\n- \"365\"\n- \"364\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.7','1836','1836',32,'vol. 5, facing p. 202','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/5','0007361d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52715~100035','Depicts Mrs Heukbane and Mrs Shortcake examining the love letter of Richard Taffril to Jenny Caxon in Mrs Mailsetter\'s parlour in the Fairport postmaster\'s house [Ch. 15]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 14] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:25:44'),(446,'FIS0017',3,'The Priory of St. Ruth / Prieure de St. Rhuth','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"245\"\n',68,78,3,'12.5 x 8.6','1836','1836',32,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/6','0007362d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52731~100036','Depicts the Priory of St Ruth (in ruins) with a funeral procession (see Ch. 25). Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 15] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:27:16'),(447,'FIS0018',3,'Oldbuck at Elspeth\'s Hut / Oldbuck a la cabane d\'Elspeth','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"367\"\n- \"366\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"79\"\n',21,66,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 6, facing p. 257','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/6','0007363d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52748~100037','Depicts Oldbuck interviewing Elspeth in her cottage, with Edie Ochiltree and Captain McIntyre also present; Elspeth is seated and holds a spindle [Ch. 40]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 16] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:29:15'),(448,'FIS0019',3,'My good friends, favete linguis / Mes bons amis, favete linguis','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"172\"\n- \"367\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"362\"\n- \"361\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.7','1836','1836',32,'vol. 6, facing p. 297','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/6','0007360d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52699~100034','Depicts Oldbuck at the head of the stairs at Knockwinnock Castle, holding a letter triumphantly, with Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour and Captain McIntyre on the landing below [Ch. 43; EEWN 3, 335.33]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 14] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:33:03'),(449,'FIS0020',23,'Osbaldistone Hall / Chateau d\'Osbaldistone','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"81\"\n- \"100\"\n',48,101,3,'8.2 x 13.5','1836','1836',32,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/7','0014597d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56735~100273','Depicts Diana Vernon hunting in pursuit of a fox, as seen by Frank Osbaldistone as he approaches Osbaldistone Hall [Ch. 5]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 18] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:34:29'),(450,'FIS0021',23,'Braes of Balquhidder / Couteaux de Balquhidder','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"72\"\n',68,93,3,'8 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 7, facing p. lxxxiii','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/7','0014605d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56871~100281','Depicts Highland setting with Highlanders drawn up in two troops, with two figures fighting in the space between. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 17] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:35:58'),(451,'FIS0022',23,'F. Osbaldistone at Squire Inglewood\'s / F. Osbaldistone chez l\'Ecuyer Inglewood','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"368\"\n- \"369\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"370\"\n- \"80\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.8','1836','1836',32,'vol. 7, facing p. 114','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/7','0014598d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56752~100274','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone entering the dining room of Inglewood Hall, with Inglewood, his clerk Jobson, and Morris seated around a fire after their meal [Ch. 8]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 19] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:37:57'),(452,'FIS0023',23,'The Pass of Aberfoil, \'Stand!\' / Le Defile d\'Aberfoil, \'Arretz!\'','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"371\"\n- \"96\"\n',8,85,3,'8.2 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/8','0014603d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56836~100279','Depicts Helen MacGregor in Highland dress, standing before the approaching grenadiers of Captain Thornton [Ch. 30; EEWN 5, 253.25]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 20] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:41:02'),(453,'FIS0024',23,'Fray at Jeanie Mac Alpine\'s / Fray chez Jeannie Mac Alpine','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"54\"\n- \"80\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.8','1836','1836',32,'vol. 8, facing p. 170','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/8','0014601d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56803~100277','Depicts Bailie Nicol Jarvie, hot coulter of a plough in hand, and Frank Osbaldistone in fencing position, facing three Highlanders (one of whom lies on the floor) in the Highland inn [Ch. 28]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 19] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 09:42:47'),(454,'FIS0025',23,'Loch Lomond / Lac de Lomond','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"54\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"80\"\n',68,94,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 8, facing p. 319','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/8','0014604d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56854~100280','Depicts Bailie Nicol Jarvie and Frank Osbaldistone being rowed by Highlanders along Loch Lomond, with Rob Roy and his clansmen standing in the distance on a rock [Ch. 36]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 21] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:10:11'),(455,'FIS0026',5,'It\'s Auld Ailie hersell / C\'est la vieille Ailie elle-meme','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"254\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"372\"\n- \"373\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"117\"\n',103,7,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/9','0007384d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53043~100054','Depicts Earnscliff and Hobbie Elliot on Meiklestone Moor, contronting the figure of the Black Dwarf in moonlight [Ch. 3; EEWN 4a, 20.41]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 22] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:11:34'),(456,'FIS0027',5,'Not again - not again! / Non - non, pas deux fois','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"373\"\n- \"49\"\n',26,1,2,'8.1 x 12.8','1836','1836',32,'vol. 9, facing p. 70','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/9','0007386d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53077~100056','Depicts the Black Dwarf exclaiming at the side of his cottage to Hobbie Elliot, whose dog (having killed one of the Dwarf\'s goats) cowers by his side [Ch. 7; EEWN 4a, 46.36]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 24] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:14:01'),(457,'FIS0028',18,'Festival of the Popinjay / Fete du Papegai','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"374\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"49\"\n',28,15,3,'8 x 13','1836','1836',32,'vol. 9, facing p. 260','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/9','0014475d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56212~100242','Depicts Lady Bellenden on a horse beside her carriage and entourage, watching the game of the popinjay being played in the distance [Ch. 2]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 23] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:15:52'),(458,'FIS0029',18,'Castle of Tillietudlem / Chateau de Tillietudlem','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"255\"\n',68,62,3,'8.4 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/10','0014476d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56230~100243','Depicts a mounted trooper approaching the Tower of Tillietudlem from below, in a heavily wooded gorge; possibly reflecting the arrival there of Sergeant Bothwell [Ch. 9]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 25] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:17:12'),(459,'FIS0030',18,'Whiles - at brose-time / Duelquefois a l\'heure des repas','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"375\"\n- \"43\"\n- \"49\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.6','1836','1836',32,'vol. 10, facing p. 311','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/10','0014469d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56110~100236','Depicts Cuddie stealing a kiss from Jenny Dennison after leaving Tillietudlem [Ch. 29; EEWN 4b, 232.25]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 24] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:18:31'),(460,'FIS0031',18,'The Battle of Bothwell Bridge / Bataille du Pont de Bothwell','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"253\"\n- \"96\"\n',48,5,3,'8.2 x 13.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 10, facing p. 354','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/10','0014473d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56177~100240','Depicts the Battle of Bothwell Bridge, with combat taking place across the Bridge, of which one turret is prominent, and with the Royalist troops apparently depicted by the near side [Ch. 32]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 26] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:20:50'),(461,'FIS0032',12,'The Grass Market. Death of Porteous / Le Grass Market. Mort de Porteous','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"110\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"447\"\n- \"222\"\n',81,106,3,'12.5 x 8.5','1836','1836',32,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/11','0007430d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53828~100100','Depicts the Edinburgh Grassmarket, with the Edinburgh Castle in the background, and a large crowd watching the lynching of Porteous [Ch. 6]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 28] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:21:55'),(462,'FIS0033',18,'The Black Linn of Linklater / Le Linn Noir de Linklater','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"351\"\n',21,30,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 11, facing p. 100','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/11','0014676d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57645~100327','Depicts Morton, accompanied by his little girl guide, facing the bridge formed over chasm by an oak-trunk, on his way to confront Balfour of Burley [Ch. 43]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 27] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:23:15'),(463,'FIS0034',12,'Jeanie, I say, Jeanie, woman / Jeannette, e\'coute, Jeanette, ma fille','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n',26,1,2,'8.1 x 12.5','1836','1836',32,'vol. 11, facing p. 322','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/11','0007468d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54219~100123','Depicts Jeanie Deans moving away to avoid the hand of the Laird of Dumbiedikes, who reaches out to grab her shoulder, as he sits in her cottage in St Leonard\'s [Ch. 9; EEWN 6, 83.40]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 29] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:24:36'),(464,'FIS0035',12,'St Anthony\'s Chapel, Salisbury Crags / Chapelle St Antoine, Rochers de Salisbury','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',68,101,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1836','1836',32,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/12','0007460d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54080~100115','Depicts St Anthony\'s Chapel (in ruins) outside Edinburgh, and the Salisbury Crags, with the Firth of Forth in the distance and a hooded female figure in the foreground; apparently representing Jeanie Deans\'s journey at night to Muschat\'s Cairn [Ch. 15]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 30] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:26:36'),(465,'FIS0036',12,'Madge Wildfire & Jeanie Deans / Madge Wildfire et Jeanne Deans','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"104\"\n',61,78,3,'8.3 x 13.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 12, facing p. 285','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/12','0007467d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54201~100122','Depicts Madge Willdfire standing over the distraught Jeanie Deans outside the church at Willingham [Ch. 32]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 31] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 10:28:01'),(466,'FIS0037',12,'A \'summat\' to eat and drink / \'Quelque chose\' a manger et a boire','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"376\"\n- \"377\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.7','1836','1836',32,'vol. 12, facing p. 299','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/12','0007490d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54234~100124','Depicts Mr Stubbs the beadle seated at table with Tummas the footman at Willingham Rectory, with Jeanie Deans apparently seated beside them [Ch. 33; EEWN 6, 289.26]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 29] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:38:35'),(467,'FIS0038',12,'Dunbarton Castle / Chateau de Dunbarton','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',68,103,3,'8.2 x 13.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 13 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/13','0007464d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54149~100119','Depicts a small sailing boat in a choppy sea, apparently navigating a buoy, with large rock and Dunbarton Castle in the background [Ch. 52]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 32] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:40:09'),(468,'FIS0039',12,'The Captain of Knockdunder / Le Capitaine de Knockdunder','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"270\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"269\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.7','1836','1836',32,'vol. 13, facing p. 109','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/13','0007491d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54249~100125','Depicts Duncan Knock smoking his pipe and listening to the preacher in the Kirk of Knocktarlitie, with Jeanie and Reuben seated alongside looking at a book; other figures probably include David Deans and Mr Archibald as well as a congregation of assorted Highlanders [Ch. 46]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 34] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:41:17'),(469,'FIS0040',6,'The Tower of Wolf\'s Crag / Tour de Wolf\'s Crag','--- \n- \"359\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"86\"\n- \"378\"\n',68,34,3,'8.3 x 13.2','1836','1836',32,'vol. 13, facing p. 362','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/13','0007397d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53265~100067','Depicts Ravenswood and Bucklaw approaching Wolf\'s Crag on horseback, with a stormy sea in the background [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 33] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:43:13'),(470,'FIS0041',6,'Wolf\'s Hope / Port de Wolf\'s Hope','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"140\"\n',103,62,3,'8.1 x 13.2','1837','1837',32,'vol. 14 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/14','0007398d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53282~100068','Depicts the settlement of Wolf\'s Hope with the harbour leading to an open sea, and a figure (unidentified) in the left foreground. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 35] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:44:38'),(471,'FIS0042',6,'Caleb catering for Wolf\'s Crag / Caleb a la provision pour Wolf\'s Crag','--- \n- \"379\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"98\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.7','1837','1837',32,'vol. 14, facing p. 65','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/14','0007396d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53249~100066','Depicts Caleb Balderstone making off with a spit with wild fowl on it from Dame Lightbody\'s kitchen, with a bewildered young boy holding the remaining spit by the fire [Ch. 12]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 34] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:45:56'),(472,'FIS0043',6,'The Links of Eyemouth / Dunes d\'Eyemouth','--- \n- \"312\"\n- \"8\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"86\"\n- \"140\"\n',68,101,3,'8.3 x 13.7','1837','1837',32,'vol. 14, facing p. 367','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/14','0007395d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53233~100065','Depicts Col. Henry Ashton waiting for his combatant, Edgar Ravenswood, who rides in on a horse in the background [Ch. 35]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 36] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:47:12'),(473,'FIS0044',16,'The Pass of Leny / Passage de Leny','--- \n- \"272\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',68,36,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 15 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/15','0007584d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55271~100186','Depicts Dugald Dalgetty riding alongside loch and about to meet Lord Menteith and his party coming from the other direction [Ch. 2]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 37] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:50:25'),(474,'FIS0045',16,'Captain Dalgetty at Ardenvohr / Le Capitaine Dalgetty a Ardenvohr','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"13\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"46\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.8','1837','1837',32,'vol. 15, facing p. 136','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/15','0007582d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55239~100184','Depicts Dalgetty\'s landing at Ardenvohr, manhandled by Highlanders with Sir Duncan Campbell (in trews) looking on from the mouth of a cavern. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 38] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:51:49'),(475,'FIS0046',16,'Castle of Inverlochy / Chateau de Inverlochy','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',68,78,3,'12.8 x 8.2','1837','1837',32,'vol. 15, facing p. 269','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/15','0007585d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55288~100187','Depicts the Castle of Inverlochy, with barges in the loch and mountain in the distance [Ch. 18]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 39] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:53:48'),(476,'FIS0047',14,'The Rescue of Ivanhoe / Le Delivrance d\'Ivanhoe','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"196\"\n',3,101,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 16 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/16','0007499d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54387~100133','Depicts the jousting tournament, with the Black Knight in the centre fighting off several attackers, as spectators look on from a tent [Ch. 12]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 41] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:55:25'),(477,'FIS0048',14,'Prince John & Rebecca at the Passage of Arms at Ashby / Le prince Jean & Rebecca au passage des armes a Ashby','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"51\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"380\"\n- \"87\"\n',47,66,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 16, facing p. 111','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/16','0007495d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54315~100129','Depicts Prince John at the tournament addressing Isaac and his daughter Rebecca amongst the spectators there [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 40] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 14:58:57'),(478,'FIS0049',14,'The Black Knight & The Clerk of Copmanhurst / Le chevalier noir et Le Clerc de Copmanhurst','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"285\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 12.8','1837','1837',32,'vol. 16, facing p. 257','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/16','0007516d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54670~100150','Depicts Richard Coeur de Lion in black armour drinking at a table with Friar Tuck (as the Clerk), with horse tethered at the left side of the room, and a fire and brazier at the other [Ch. 16]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 42] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:00:40'),(479,'FIS0050',14,'One foot nearer & I plunge myself from the precipice / Un pied plus et je pres me plonge dans le precipice','--- \n- \"9\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"105\"\n',26,43,3,'12.4 x 8.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 17 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/17','0007547d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54738~100154','Depicts Rebecca, perched on the ramparts of Castle Torquilstone, rebuffing the advances of the Templar Brian de Bois-Guilbert, who stands beneath [Ch. 24; EEWN 8, 200.1-5]. A rare instance of the employment of steel engraving in Cruikshank\'s work in this series. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 43] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:02:32'),(480,'FIS0051',14,'The Castle of Coningsburgh / Chateau de Coningsburgh','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"40\"\n- \"315\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"101\"\n',68,11,3,'8.3 x 13.5','1837','1837',32,'vol. 17, facing p. 330','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/17','0007545d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54703~100152','Depicts the Castle of Coningsburgh, with groups of people approaching from various directions, and four figures apparently representing Richard, Ivanhoe, Gurth, and Wamba, approaching in the foreground [Ch. 41]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 44] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:04:00'),(481,'FIS0052',14,'Athelstane\'s escape from the dungeon / Athelstand s\'evadant du donion','--- \n- \"381\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"85\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13','1837','1837',32,'vol. 17, facing p. 355','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/17','0007548d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54755~100155','Depicts Athelstane breaking free from his dungeon in the Abbey, to find the sacristan and a fellow monk enjoying drinks outside [Ch. 42]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 42] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:05:43'),(482,'FIS0053',17,'Tower of Glendearg / Tour de Glendearg','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"382\"\n- \"288\"\n',68,78,3,'12.8 x 9.1','1837','1837',32,'vol. 18 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/18','0014447d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55740~100214','Depicts the Lord Abbot and his party, one carrying a crosier, riding towards the tower of Glendearg [Ch. 15]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 46] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:06:57'),(483,'FIS0054',17,'Abbot Boniface & Father Philip / L\'Abbe Boniface & Le Pere Philippe','--- \n- \"106\"\n- \"118\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"341\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 18, facing p. 84','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/18','0014444d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55689~100211','Depicts the drenched Father Philip, supported by the convent miller, entering before Abbot Boniface and Father Eustace in the palace of the Monastery of Saint Mary\'s [Chs 6 & 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 49] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:08:46'),(484,'FIS0055',17,'Melrose Abbey / Abbaye de Melrose','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"256\"\n- \"219\"\n',68,101,3,'8.1 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 18, facing p. 128','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/18','0014448d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55757~100215','Depicts Melrose Abbey in the middle distance, with a single rider approaching in the foreground. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 45] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:10:03'),(485,'FIS0056',17,'Melrose Cross / Croix de Melrose / The Earl of Murray & Abbot of St Mary\'s / Le Comte de Murray & L\'abbe de Ste. Marie','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"383\"\n- \"354\"\n',68,101,3,'11.8 x 8.5','1837','1837',32,'vol. 19 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/19','0014445d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55705~100212','Depicts the Earl of Murray with his retinue confronting the monks of Saint Mary\'s, headed by the Abbot, at the Cross of Melrose [Ch. 37]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 48] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:11:20'),(486,'FIS0057',17,'Knowest thou then this token? / Connais-tu donc ce signe?','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"290\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 19, facing p. 24','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/19','0014443d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55673~100210','Depicts Halbert Glendinning offering the silver bodkin received from the White Lady to Sir Piercie, who reacts in an extreme fashion [Ch. 19; EEWN 9, 181.17]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 49] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:13:09'),(487,'FIS0058',17,'Sir Piercie Shafton & Halbert Glendinning / Le Chevalier Piercie Sharton & Halbert Glendinning','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"242\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"290\"\n',4,37,3,'8.3 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 19, facing p. 61','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/19','0014446d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55722~100213','Depicts the duel between Sir Piercie Shafton and Halbert Glendinning [Chs 21 & 22]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 47] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:17:37'),(488,'FIS0059',1,'Holyrood Palace. The Regent Murray & Roland Graeme / Palais de Holyrood. Le Regent Murray & Roland Graeme','--- \n- \"123\"\n- \"48\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"383\"\n- \"146\"\n',21,66,3,'12.4 x 8.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 20 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/20','0007326d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52479~100021','Depicts Roland Graeme having presented himself and his letter to the Earl of Murray at Holyrood Palace [Ch. 18]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 50] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:18:57'),(489,'FIS0060',1,'The Abbot of Unreason / L\'Abbe de la Deraison','--- \n- \"395\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"288\"\n',26,1,2,'8.2 x 13.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 20, facing p. 197','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/20','0007325d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52462~100020','Depicts the entry of the Abbot of Unreason with other revellers into the Church of Saint Mary, with the monks looking on and the figure (apparently) of Roland Graeme to the side [Ch. 14]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 52] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:20:17'),(490,'FIS0061',1,'Loch-Leven Castle / Le Chateau de Loch-Leven','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"128\"\n- \"72\"\n',103,5,3,'8.3 x 13.1','1837','1837',32,'vol. 20, facing p. 351','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/20','0007317d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52322~100012','Depicts Loch Leven, with the Castle in the distance, and a body of horsemen moving towards a rowing boat apparently waiting on the shore. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 51] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:21:51'),(491,'FIS0062',1,'Solway Frith, Embarkation of Queen Mary / Detroit de Solway, Embarquement de la Reine Marie','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"8\"\n- \"122\"\n',68,100,3,'8.2 x 13','1837','1837',32,'vol. 21 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/21','0007320d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52374~100015','Depicts the departure of Mary Queen of Scots from Scotland by sailing vessel over the Solway Firth [Ch. 38].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 54] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:24:22'),(492,'FIS0063',1,'The Pardoners legend / Legende du vendeur de pardons','--- \n- \"395\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"325\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 13.4','1837','1837',32,'vol. 21, facing p. 123','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/21','0007327d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52497~100022','Depicts Catherine Seyton intervening in the (anti-Catholic) folk drama of the Pardoner\'s Legend, and felling the jester [Ch. 27].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 52] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:25:39'),(493,'FIS0064',1,'Niddrie Castle / Chateau de Niddrie','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"122\"\n- \"96\"\n',68,78,3,'8.5 x 13.5','1837','1837',32,'vol. 21, facing p. 299','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/21','0007373d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52922~100047','Depicts horsemen escorting Mary Queen of Scots toward Niddrie Castle, which appears as a large tower on a hillock [Ch. 36].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 53] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:28:51'),(494,'FIS0065',15,'Queen Elizabeth at Greenwich Hospital / La Reine Elisabeth a l\'hopital de Greenwich','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"347\"\n- \"253\"\n- \"74\"\n',68,99,3,'8.1 x 13.5','1837','1837',32,'vol. 22 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/22','0007560d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54921~100165','Depicts the arrival of Queen Elizabeth by barge at the Palace of Greenwich, to a fanfare of trumpets, and with a copious attendance.  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 56] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:30:10'),(495,'FIS0066',15,'Wayland Smith\'s Cave / Cavenne de Wayland Smith','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"95\"\n- \"345\"\n',68,101,3,'8.3 x 13.5','1837','1837',32,'vol. 22, facing p. 187','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/22','0007558d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54888~100163','Depicts Tressilian confronting Wayland Smith outside the entrance to his forge, the latter attired in a bear-skin and holding up a hammer, with Sludge (\'Flibbertigibbet\') kneeling timorously on the ground [Ch. 10]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 55] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:31:27'),(496,'FIS0067',15,'Flibbertigibbet, Tressilian & Wayland Smith / Flibbertigibbet, Tressilian & Wayland Smith','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"95\"\n- \"345\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.9','1837','1837',32,'vol. 22, facing p. 201','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/22','0007563d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54971~100168','Depicts Tressilian and Wayland Smith about to mount their horses, while Sludge (\'Flibbertigibbet\') teasingly offers his farewell [Ch. 11]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 57] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:32:41'),(497,'FIS0068',15,'The Mock-Battle / La Petite Guerre','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n',3,79,3,'8 x 12.6','1837','1837',32,'vol. 23 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/23','0007568d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55053~100173','Depicts the mock battle between the English and the Danes during the entertainments at Kenilworth [Ch. 39]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 59] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:37:26'),(498,'FIS0070',15,'Dickie Sludge and the Giant Porter / Dicke Sludge et le Grand Portier','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"89\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"95\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.8','1837','1837',32,'vol. 23, facing p. 147','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/23','0007561d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54938~100166','Depicts the giant Porter at the entrance to Kenilworth holding Sludge (\'Flibbertigibbet\') aloft in his arms, as Wayland Smith and Amy Robsart (the countess) wait on their horses to pass through [Ch. 26]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 57] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:38:37'),(499,'FIS0071',15,'Kenilworth Castle / Chateau de Kenilworth','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"347\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"56\"\n',3,37,3,'8.6 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 23, facing p. 197','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/23','0007562d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54954~100167','Depicts the entry of Queen Elizabeth, accompanied by Leicester and a party of riders, by torchlight, into Kenilworth Castle [Ch. 30]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 58] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:40:09'),(500,'FIS0072',20,'Sumburgh-Head from Jarlshof. Shipwreck of Cleveland / Rocher de Sumburgh vu de Jarlshof. Naufrage de Cleveland','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"137\"\n',21,78,3,'8.5 x 13.2','1837','1837',32,'vol. 24 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/24','0014510d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56431~100255','Depicts Mordaunt Mertoun attending to the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland with Ulla Troil (Norna) rebuking Bryce the Pedlar for attempting to remove Cleveland\'s rings [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 60] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:41:29'),(501,'FIS0073',20,'Close in lads, he is not half so mad now / Courons dessus camarades, il n\'est pas maintenant si furieux','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"384\"\n',68,11,3,'8.3 x 13.3','1837','1837',32,'vol. 24, facing p. 301','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/24','0014514d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56498~100259','Depicts Magnus Troil exhorting those struggling to harpoon a whale, with Mordaunt Mertoun and Clement Cleveland (not distinguished) among the contestants [Ch. 17; EEWN 12, 60.23]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 61] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:43:15'),(502,'FIS0074',20,'She demanded the wounded man of me. / Elle me demanda le blesse','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"385\"\n- \"137\"\n',68,66,3,'8.1 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 25 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/25','0014511d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56448~100256','Depicts Ulla Troil with stick in one hand pointing to Cleveland, who kneels over the wounded Mertoun in the mouth of a sea-cove, with the figure of Nick Strumpfer darkly visible behind Ulla [Ch. 31; EEWN 12, 297.8]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 62] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:44:26'),(503,'FIS0075',20,'Norna dispatching the provisions / Norna envoyant les provisions','--- \n- \"162\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"385\"\n- \"384\"\n- \"137\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 25, facing p. 145','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/25','0014512d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56465~100257','Depicts Ulla Troil flinging cold meats out of the window in view of an astonished Magnus, while Nick Strumpfer gleefully holds a brandy flask [Ch. 29]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 63] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:45:40'),(504,'FIS0076',20,'Brenda','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"133\"\n',60,71,3,'11.2 x 9.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 25, facing p. 369','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/25','','Portrait of Brenda Troil in a dress adorned with bows and a hat with a feather in it. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0023.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:24:27'),(505,'FIS0077',20,'Minna','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"134\"\n',33,92,3,'9.1 x 10.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 25, facing p. 371','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/25','0014513d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56481~100258','Portrait of Minna Troil seated, looking off into the distance, with the sea in the background. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\' (#POR0022): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-07 15:47:13'),(506,'FIS0078',10,'Dalgarno & Glenvarlock','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"386\"\n- \"293\"\n- \"246\"\n',40,4,3,'8.2 x 13','1838','1838',32,'vol. 26 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/26','0007417d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53608~100087','Depicts Nigel Olifaunt confronting Lord Dalgarno (and companion) in St James\'s Park, and on the point of drawing his sword [Ch. 16]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 64] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:00:34'),(507,'FIS0079',10,'Margaret Ramsay','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"150\"\n',12,87,3,'10.9 x 8.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 26, facing p. 155','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/26','','Portrait of Margaret Ramsay wearing a white bonnet, sitting in a chair, looking off to the side. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0024.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:34:15'),(508,'FIS0080',10,'The Citizen and the Soldier / Le Bourgeois et le Soldat','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"33\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 26, facing p. 251','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/26','0007413d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53540~100083','Depicts the aftermath of the combat in the bowling-alley, with the victorious \'citizen\' heading off to the applause of the onlookers [Ch. 12]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 63] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:02:02'),(509,'FIS0081',10,'King James, Prince Charles & Nigel / Le Roi Jacques, Le Prince Charles & Nigel','--- \n- \"387\"\n- \"388\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"144\"\n- \"293\"\n- \"146\"\n',21,101,3,'8.4 x 13.6','1838','1838',32,'vol. 27 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/26','0007416d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53591~100086','Depicts Nigel Olifaunt pleading with the mounted King James, having interrupted the hunt, with Prince Charles and the Duke of Buckingham standing between them [Ch. 27]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 65] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:03:16'),(510,'FIS0082',10,'Heriots Hospital','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"38\"\n',83,34,3,'8.3 x 11.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 27, facing p. 239','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/27','','Depicts Heriot\'s Hospital, Edinburgh. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0062',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:34:42'),(511,'FIS0083',19,'The Countess of Derby quitting Peveril Castle / Depart de la Comtesse de Derby du Chateau de Peveril','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"389\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"297\"\n',68,101,3,'12.6 x 8.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 28 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/28','0014503d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56314~100248','Depicts Sir Geoffrey Peveril mounted on Black Hastings, and his party leading the Countess of Derby away from Peveril Castle, which is visible on a high ridge [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 66] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:04:35'),(512,'FIS0084',19,'Alice Bridgnorth','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"84\"\n',69,92,3,'11.1 x 9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 28, facing p. 230','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/28','','Portrait of Alice Bridgenorth, wearing a high-necked gown and a bonnet. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0026.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:35:03'),(513,'FIS0085',19,'Holm-Peel Castle. Fenella & Julian Peveril / Chateau de Holm-Peel. Fenella et Julian Peveril','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"157\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"153\"\n',102,107,3,'8.2 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 29 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/29','0014506d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56364~100251','Depicts Julian Peveril departing at night from Holm-Peel Castle, with the figure of Fenella apparently visible on the rocks beneath the castle [Ch. 19]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 67] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:05:43'),(514,'FIS0092',21,'Isabelle de Croye','--- \n- \"167\"\n- \"84\"\n',85,87,3,'11.2 x 9.1','1838','1838',32,'vol. 31, facing p. 51','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/31','','Portrait of Isabelle de Croye, wearing crown, shawl, and ermine cape. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0025.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:35:27'),(515,'FIS0093',21,'Cardinal Balue at the Boar Hunt / Le Cardinal Balue a la chase au sanglier','--- \n- \"397\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 31, facing p. 177','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/31','0014589d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56601~100265','Depicts the Cardinal Balue having lost control of his horse at a boar-hunt [Ch. 9]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 69] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:08:43'),(516,'FIS0094',21,'Murder of the Bishop of Liege / Assassinat de l\'eveque de Liege','--- \n- \"399\"\n- \"400\"\n- \"398\"\n- \"229\"\n',40,77,3,'8.2 x 11.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 32 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/32','0014590d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56617~100266','Depicts the Bishop of Liege lying on the floor, having been executed by Nikkel Blok at the command of William de la Marck [Ch. 22]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 71] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:11:36'),(517,'FIS0095',21,'Peronne','--- \n- \"354\"\n',15,34,3,'11.4 x 8.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 32, facing p. 146','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/32','','Depicts a square in the town of Peronne, France, with horse and cart in foreground. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0070.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:26:36'),(518,'FIS0096',24,'Clara Mowbray & Tyrrel / Clara Mowbray & Tyrrel','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"401\"\n',102,101,3,'8.5 x 13.2','1838','1838',32,'vol. 33 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/33','0014611d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56972~100287','Depicts the meeting of Frank Tyrrel and Clara Mowbray near the Buck-stane, with Clara on point of riding away from him [Ch. 9].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 73] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:13:35'),(519,'FIS0097',24,'Nidpath Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n',107,34,3,'7.6 x 11.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 33, facing p. 6','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/33','','Depicts Neidpath Castle overlooking the river Tweed. (Drawn from a sketch by James Skene.) Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0071.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:36:13'),(520,'FIS0098',24,'Meg Dods and Captain Mac Turk / Meg Dods & le Capitaine Mac Turk','--- - \"402\" - \"403\" - \"240\" - \"296\" - \"20\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 33, facing p. 205','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/33','','Depicts Meg Dods outside her inn assailing Captain MacTurk with a broom [Ch. 12]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 72] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]).',NULL,NULL),(521,'FIS0099',24,'Death of Miss Mowbray / Mort de Mademoiselle Mowbray','--- \n- \"402\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"401\"\n',40,66,3,'7.9 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 34 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/34','0014612d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56989~100288','Depicts Clara Mowbray on her death bed, with Meg Dods in attendance, and the distraught Frank Tyrrel holding Clara\'s hand [Ch. 38].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 74] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:14:41'),(522,'FIS0100',22,'Salmon Hunting / La Chasse au Saumon','--- \n- \"29\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"179\"\n',40,11,3,'8.3 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 35 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/35','0014594d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56684~100270','Depicts Herries of Birrenswork spearing salmon on horseback, with other riders, on the Solway Firth [Letter 4].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 75] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:15:44'),(523,'FIS0101',22,'Dumfries from the South','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"239\"\n- \"354\"\n',21,34,3,'8.1 x 11.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 35, facing p. 36','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/35','','Depicts the town of Dumfries from the south. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0072.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:36:46'),(524,'FIS0102',22,'Craigevar Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"67\"\n',18,34,3,'8.2 x 11.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 35, facing p. 171','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/35','','Depicts a banquet in a room in Craigevar Castle. (Drawn from a sketch by James Skene.) Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0074.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:37:01'),(525,'FIS0103',22,'Dougal Mac Callum and Hutcheon','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"186\"\n- \"404\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 35, facing p. 176','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/35','0014593d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56668~100269','Depicts Dougal MacCallum and his fellow servant Hutcheon entering the room where Sir Robert Redgauntlet\'s coffin is placed, to find the ape \'Major Weir\' sitting on it [Letter 11].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 72] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:16:51'),(526,'FIS0104',22,'Lagg Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"242\"\n- \"245\"\n',83,34,3,'8.1 x 11.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 35, facing p. 184','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/35','','Depicts Lagg Castle (in ruins) with cattle grazing below. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0075.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:37:25'),(527,'FIS0105',22,'Prince Charles Edward taking leave of his adherents / Le Prince Charle Edouard prenant conge de ses adherents','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"301\"\n- \"53\"\n',102,78,3,'8.2 x 13.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 36 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/36','0014591d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56634~100267','Depicts Prince Charles Edward saying farewell to his followers on the point of his last embarkation [Ch. 23].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 76] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:17:59'),(528,'FIS0106',4,'He is gone forever / Il est parti pour toujours','--- \n- \"331\"\n- \"190\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"192\"\n- \"426\"\n',102,78,3,'12.5 x 8.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 37 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/37','0007372d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52905~100046','Depicts Eveline Berenger on the ramparts of the Castle with Rose Flammock, as Father Aldrovand and William Flammock sleep [Ch. 9]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 77] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:19:01'),(529,'FIS0107',4,'Powis Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n',38,34,3,'8.2 x 12','1838','1838',32,'vol. 37, facing p. 10','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/37','0007368d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52835~100042','Depicts Powis Castle in the distance, surrounded by woodland. (Drawn from a sketch by Lady Lucy Clive.) Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0077, see image.',NULL,'2009-05-25 10:42:00'),(530,'FIS0108',4,'Eveline Berenger','--- \n- \"190\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,113,3,'10 x 7.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 37, facing p. 483','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/37','','Portrait of Eveline Berenger, wearing a cross necklace. Adapted from the 1822 Thomas Lawrence portrait of Marguerite Gardiner, Countess of Blessington. Unlike other portraits in the Fisher edition, this does not appear in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\' (1834).',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:29:07'),(531,'FIS0109',26,'Sir Kenneth and the Baron of Gilsland / Le Chevalier Kenneth et le Baron de Gilsland','--- \n- \"197\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"405\"\n- \"199\"\n- \"178\"\n',102,107,3,'8.1 x 13.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 38 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/38','0014635d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57123~100296','Depicts Kenneth in his hut, showing Thomas de Multon his sick squire, who is attended to by the Saladin (as \'El Hakim\') [Ch. 7].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 78] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:26:24'),(532,'FIS0110',26,'Edith Plantagenet','--- \n- \"394\"\n- \"84\"\n',47,72,3,'11.4 x 9.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 38, facing p. 77','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/38','','Portrait of Edith Plantagenet, wearing a white dress and scarf on her head. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0030.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:38:25'),(533,'FIS0111',26,'Sir Kenneth and the Dwarfs of the Chapel / Le Chevalier Kenneth et les Nains de la Chapelle','--- \n- \"162\"\n- \"197\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 38, facing p. 90','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/38','0014636d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57139~100297','Depicts Kenneth contronted by the dwarfs Nectabanus and Guenevra in the chapel of Engaddi.  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 79] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:27:29'),(534,'FIS0112',29,'Cromwell & Lady Fauconberg','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"308\"\n- \"309\"\n',21,66,3,'12.4 x 8.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 39 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/39','0014660d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57540~100321','Depicts a distraught Cromwell standing before a portrait of Charles I and being comforted by his daughter, while Roger Wildrake looks on [Ch. 7].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 80] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:29:18'),(535,'FIS0113',29,'Master Holdenough interrupted in his Vocation','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"307\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"206\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.9','1838','1838',32,'vol. 39, facing p. 7','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/39','0014659d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57524~100320','Depicts the minister Holdenough attempting to climb the pulpit, with Tomkins falling backward in an attempt to stop him, and with armed men in the church as well as parishoners [Ch. 1]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 79] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:30:30'),(536,'FIS0114',29,'Woodstock / From a Drawing in the Collection of George III','--- \n- \"14\"\n',107,34,3,'7.5 x 11.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 39, facing p. 57','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/39','','Depicts Woodstock Lodge in a pastoral setting. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0079.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:39:06'),(537,'FIS0115',29,'Round Tower Windsor, 1660','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"253\"\n',34,34,3,'11.6 x 8.2','1838','1838',32,'vol. 39, facing p. 137','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/39','','Depicts the round tower of Windsor Castle, with English banner. Also appears in \'Landscape Illustrations of the Waverley Novels\': see #LIW0080.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:39:30'),(538,'FIS0116',29,'King Charles & Sir Henry Lee','--- \n- \"203\"\n- \"319\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"201\"\n- \"200\"\n',40,78,3,'8.1 x 13','1838','1838',32,'vol. 40 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/40','0014661d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57557~100322','Depicts Sir Henry Lee touching the head of the restored Charles II, surrounded by his daughter Alice and family, and with the aged hound Bevis looking on [Ch. 38].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 81] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:31:40'),(539,'FIS0117',29,'Phoebe Mayflower','--- \n- \"205\"\n- \"84\"\n',89,19,3,'12.9 x 9.2','1838','1838',32,'vol. 40, facing p. 222','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/40','','Portrait of Phoebe Mayflower in white dress and bonnet. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0032.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:39:55'),(540,'FIS0118',13,'Hamish Bean & his Mother','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"208\"\n- \"207\"\n',102,11,3,'8.3 x 13.2','1838','1838',32,'vol. 41 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/41','0007492d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54264~100126','Depicts Hamish MacTavish and his mother Elspat standing beside their hut, as a party of soldiers arrives in the distance, with Hamish holding a gun and his mother advising him to resist [Ch. 11]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 82] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:33:18'),(541,'FIS0119',13,'Kilchurn Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n',38,59,3,'8.7 x 12.3','1838','1838',32,'vol. 41, facing p. 129','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/41','','Depicts Kilchurn Castle, on a lake [Ch. 7]. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0033.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:40:19'),(542,'FIS0120',9,'The Fair Maid of Perth and Carthusian Monk / Le Belle de Perth & le Moine Carthusien','--- \n- \"214\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"88\"\n',21,66,3,'8.4 x 12.6','1838','1838',32,'vol. 42 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/42','0007412d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53522~100082','Depicts Catharine Glover listening devoutly to instructions from Father Clement under a rock beneath the hill of Kinnoul, with the vale of Tay behind them [Ch. 14].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 83] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:34:29'),(543,'FIS0121',9,'Fair Maid of Perth','--- \n- \"210\"\n- \"84\"\n',57,108,3,'12.4 x 9.1','1838','1838',32,'vol. 42, facing p. 91','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/42','','Portrait of Catharine Glover at prayer, wearing a tartan shawl. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0034.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:36:08'),(544,'FIS0122',9,'The Revellers / Les amis de la joie','--- \n- \"395\"\n',26,1,2,'8 x 12.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 42, facing p. 347','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/42','0007406d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53419~100076','Depicts revellers in Perth on Fastern\'s E\'en outside Simon Glover\'s house, about to share wine [Ch. 16]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 84] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:35:50'),(545,'FIS0123',9,'Ordeal of touching the dead Body / Epreuve de l\'attouchement du corps mort','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"229\"\n',40,93,3,'12.6 x 8.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 43 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/43','0007408d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53452~100078','Depicts the trial of touching the dead body held in the High Church of St John\'s in Perth [Ch. 23].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 85] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:38:28'),(546,'FIS0124',9,'Loch Tay','--- \n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',84,54,3,'8.3 x 12','1838','1838',32,'vol. 43, facing p. 185','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/43','','Depicts Loch Tay, with a mountain in the background. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0035.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:41:12'),(547,'FIS0125',2,'Strasburg Cathedral. Margaret of Anjou & Philipson / Cathedrale de Strasbourg. Marguerite d\'Anjou & Philipson','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"216\"\n- \"217\"\n- \"406\"\n',40,101,3,'12.6 x 8.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/44','0007374d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52939~100048','Depicts Arthur Philipson kneeling before Margaret of Anjou, as the widow of Henry VI, in the chapel of Saint George in Strasburg Cathedral [Ch. 24]. Also appears as frontispiece to vol. 45 in the same edition in many copies.  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 87] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:40:57'),(548,'FIS0126',2,'Philipson & the German Innkeeper / Philipson & l\'Aubergiste Allemand','--- \n- \"240\"\n- \"217\"\n',26,1,2,'8.1 x 12.7','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44, facing p. 9','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/44','0007375d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52957~100049','Depicts the German landlord as found inhospitably positioned behind a large stove [Ch. 19]. Also appears in vol. 45, facing p. 9 of the same edition.  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 84] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 13:42:39'),(549,'FIS0127',2,'The Rhine','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"244\"\n',101,80,3,'8.8 x 13.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44, facing p. 55','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/44','','Depicts castle on a cliff overlooking the Rhine, with an angler in the near foreground and what appears to be a rainbow in the sky. Also appears in vol. 45, facing p. 9 of the same edition in some copies. ',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:33:27'),(550,'FIS0128',8,'\'A Varangian\' at the Golden Gate of Constantinople / \'Un Varangien\' a la Doree de Constantinople','--- \n- \"228\"\n',21,66,3,'12.4 x 8.4','1838','1838',32,'vol. 46 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/46','0007404d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53386~100074','Depicts two women approaching Hereward as he lies apparently sleeping on a bench under the arch of Theodosius in Constantinople [Ch. 2].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 88] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:09:14'),(551,'FIS0129',8,'Bertha at the Camp of the Crusaders / Berthe au Camp des Croises','--- \n- \"407\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n',102,107,3,'8 x 13.2','1838','1838',32,'vol. 47 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/47','0007407d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53435~100077','Depicts Bertha arriving at the camp of the Crusaders, seeking access to their leader [Ch. 23].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 89] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:11:42'),(552,'FIS0130',8,'Brenhilda, Agelastes & Sylvan','--- \n- \"408\"\n- \"410\"\n- \"409\"\n',26,1,2,'7.9 x 12.8','1838','1838',32,'vol. 47, facing p. 98','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/47','0007405d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53403~100075','Depicts Agelastes turning from the reflected mirror image to see the real figure of Sylvanus, the orang-utan, appear from behind a curtain, while Brenhilda still looks toward the reflection [Ch. 26]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 90] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:12:47'),(553,'FIS0131',25,'Zilia de Moncada & her Father / Zelie de Moncade & son Pere','--- \n- \"234\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"233\"\n- \"232\"\n',102,78,3,'8.3 x 13.3','1839','1839',32,'vol. 48 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/48','0014618d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57090~100294','Depicts Zilia de Moncada, in her apartment under the protection of Gideon Gray, fainting after the appearance of her father there, with the King\'s Messenger in the doorway [Ch. 15].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 91] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:14:22'),(554,'FIS0132',7,'Lady Augusta','--- \n- \"231\"\n- \"84\"\n',111,27,3,'12.7 x 8.8','1839','1839',32,'vol. 48, facing p. 11','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/48','','Portrait of Lady Augusta, wearing a hat and with a shawl decorated with seashells. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0039. Exhibited Royal Academy, no. 516, 1833.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:39:07'),(555,'FIS0133',25,'The Middlemas Wives & Gideon Gray / Les femmes de Middlemas & Gideon Gray','--- \n- \"234\"\n- \"354\"\n',26,1,2,'7.8 x 12.8','1839','1839',32,'vol. 48, facing p. 188','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1836-9/48','0014634d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57107~100295','Depicts the arrival of the three old women for Middlemas with news at Gideon Gray\'s home [Ch. 15]. Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 90] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:15:24'),(556,'FIS0134',2,'Anne of Geierstein & Arthur Philipson / Anne de Geierstein & Arthur Philipson','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"217\"\n',102,14,3,'12.6 x 8.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44 (frontispiece)','NLS, ABS.1.84.154/44','0007335d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52629~100030','Depicts Arthur Philipson on the edge of a cliff, holding onto a tree, with Anne of Geierstein visible on the opposite cliff, and a castle in the distance [Ch. 2].  Also appears in \'Landscape-Historical Illustrations of Scotland and the Waverley Novels\' [Plate 86] (London: Fisher, [1836-38]): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:16:45'),(557,'FIS0135',2,'Anne of Geierstein','--- \n- \"84\"\n',17,98,3,'12.5 x 11','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44, facing p. 47','NLS, ABS.1.84.154/44','','Portrait of Anne of Geierstein, wearing a feathered bonnet. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0036.',NULL,'2009-05-18 12:30:58'),(558,'FIS0136',2,'Bale [sic]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"354\"\n',7,5,3,'11.5 x 14.5','1838','1838',32,'vol. 44, facing p. 137','NLS, ABS.1.84.154/44','','Depicts the city of Basle, with a harbour in the foreground and church steeples in the distance. Also appears in \'Portraits of the Principal Female Characters in the Waverley Novels\': see #POR0038.',NULL,'2009-05-18 12:30:44'),(559,'POR0001',28,'Flora Mac-Ivor','--- \n- \"60\"\n- \"84\"\n',20,87,3,'11.5 x 10','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Flora MacIvor, wearing flowered headband and pendant necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The dress of the lady herself . . . set with diamonds.\'--Vol. I, p. 221.\'  See Ch. 1; EEWN 1, 106.31-37. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated September 1832.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:12:37'),(560,'POR0002',28,'Rose Bradwardine','--- \n- \"12\"\n- \"84\"\n',60,71,3,'9.5 x 8','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Rose Bradwardine, wearing pearl necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'She was indeed a very pretty girl . . . face and neck.\'--Vol. I, p. 93.\'  See Ch. 10; EEWN 1, 42-43. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated June 1832.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:39:43'),(561,'POR0003',11,'Lucy Bertram','--- \n- \"358\"\n- \"84\"\n',13,72,3,'9.8 x 7.8','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Lucy Bertram in profile, with chin resting on hand, wearing a dark ruffled scarf over her head. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Miss Bertram no sooner heard . . . Mansion House immediately.\'---Vol. III, p. 152.\'  See Ch. 15; EEWN 2, 82.18-21. Imprint Chapman & Hall dated 1 December 1832.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:13:20'),(562,'POR0004',11,'Julia Mannering','--- \n- \"422\"\n- \"84\"\n',54,108,3,'11.2 x 7.9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Julia Mannering looking over her shoulder, wearing a dark dress with large puffy sleeves. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Is my future friend red-haired?\'--Vol. III, p. 187.\'  See Ch. 18; EEWN 2, 100.38. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 February 1833.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:13:49'),(563,'POR0005',3,'Miss Wardour','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"362\"\n',111,116,3,'11 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Isabella Wardour, wearing a white dress, and holding a letter. Accompanying quoted text \'Miss Wardour in silent terror took up the letter.\'--Vol. VI, p. 272.\'  See Ch. 41; EEWN 3, 322.23. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 November 1832.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:40:54'),(564,'POR0006',23,'Diana Vernon','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"100\"\n',12,92,3,'11 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014599d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56768~100275','Portrait of Diana Vernon touching her chin with her finger, wearing a jacket and a crucifix necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her finger immediately touched her dimpled chin, with an arch look of prohibition.\'--Vol. VII, p. 148.\'  See Ch. 10; EEWN 5, 82.16. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 October 1832.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:18:12'),(565,'POR0007',5,'Isabel Vere','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"268\"\n',74,105,3,'13 x 12','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 7]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007380d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52973~100050','Portrait of Isabel Vere, wearing a beret. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'She twice extended her hand . . . her own bosom.\'--Vol. IX, p. 186.\'  See Ch. 16; EEWN 4a, 107.36-37. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 January 1833.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:19:22'),(566,'POR0008',18,'Edith Bellenden','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"84\"\n',18,19,3,'9.5 x 8','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 8]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014462d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55992~100229','Portrait of Edith Bellenden, wearing a pearl necklace and a hat decorated by feathers. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her grand-daughter, and only earthly care . . . close to winter.\'--Vol. IX, p. 262.\'  See Ch. 2; EEWN 4b, 17.11-13. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 February 1833. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P2908): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:20:25'),(567,'POR0009',12,'The Lily of St. Leonards','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"84\"\n',21,3,3,'8.5 x 9.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 9]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007465d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54166~100120','Portrait of Effie Deans, whose elbow rests on a milk-pail. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Effie Deans now shot up . . . and contentment.\'--Vol. XI, p. 325.\' See Ch. 10; EEWN 6, 84.36-37. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 December 1832.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:21:38'),(568,'POR0010',12,'Jeannie Deans','--- \n- \"23\"\n- \"84\"\n',60,71,3,'9.6 x 7.4','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 10]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007450d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53907~100105','Portrait of Jeanie Deans at prayer, wearing a tartan shawl. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'She wore the tartan plaid . . . deep awe.\'--Vol. XII, p. 354.\' See Ch. 36; EEWN 6, 319.16-21. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 January 1833.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:22:51'),(569,'POR0011',6,'Lucy Ashton','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"84\"\n',33,87,3,'12 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 11]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007399d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53299~100069','Portrait of Lucy Ashton, wearing a veil as a head scarf. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Lucy Ashton\'s exquisitely beautiful . . . hill snow.\'--Vol. XIII, p. 298.\'  See Ch. 3; EEWN 7a, 25.16-20. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1833.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:23:55'),(570,'POR0012',16,'Annot Lyle','--- \n- \"41\"\n- \"59\"\n- \"84\"\n',32,17,3,'11.5 x 9.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 12]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007578d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55170~100180','Portrait of Annot Lyle, holding her harp, wearing a crucifix necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Yet Annot\'s garments . . . pleased the wearer.\'--Vol. XV, p. 126.\'  See Ch. 9; EEWN 7b, 73.23-26. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated July 1833. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P2931): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:25:04'),(571,'POR0013',14,'Rowena','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"90\"\n',98,41,3,'12 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 13]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007511d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54584~100145','Portrait of Rowena, wearing crucifix necklace and braided headband. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Lady Rowena had watched . . . the same intense interest.\'--Vol. XVI, p. 144.\'  See Ch. 8; EEWN 89.26-27. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 November 1832. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P2889): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:28:11'),(572,'POR0014',14,'Rebecca','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"87\"\n',47,17,3,'13.5 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 14]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007506d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~54501~100140','Portrait of Rebecca, wearing a feathered turban. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her turban of yellow silk . . . permitted to be visible.\'--Vol. XVI, p. 111\'  See Ch. 7; EEWN 8.71-72. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 February 1833. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P2974): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:30:13'),(573,'POR0015',17,'Mysie Happer','--- \n- \"318\"\n- \"84\"\n',76,3,3,'9.5 x 8','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 15]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014440d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55622~100207','Portrait of Mysie Happer with dark hair in ringlets looking over her shoulder. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The countenance itself . . . seducing dimple in it.\'--Vol. XVIII, p. 180.\'  See Ch. 13; EEWN 9, 125.17-20. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated July 1832. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P3012): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:31:35'),(574,'POR0016',17,'Mary Avenel','--- \n- \"112\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"84\"\n',37,19,3,'10.5 x 9.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 16]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014441d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55639~100208','Portrait of Mary Avenel, holding a book and wearing a cross necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Mary Avenel had been taught . . . peculiar to her character.\'--Vol. XIX, p. 205.\'  See Ch. 30; EEWN 9, 125.17-20. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1832. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P3016): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:32:40'),(575,'POR0017',17,'The White Lady','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"84\"\n',53,97,3,'11.5 x 10','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 17]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014439d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55606~100206','Portrait of the White Lady of Avenel, wearing a white dress. Accompanying quoted verse and prose text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Noon gleams on the lake . . . steps of Halbert Glendinning.\'--Vol. XVIII, p. 158.\'  See Ch. 11; EEWN 9, 112.30-35.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated May 1833. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P11.515): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:35:05'),(576,'POR0018',1,'Catherine Seyton','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"325\"\n',58,64,3,'16.5 x 12.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 18]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0007371d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52887~100045','Portrait of Catherine Seyton in profile, wearing dark bonnet and cross necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'As they put off . . . loophole to view his departure.\'--Vol. XXI, p. 104.\'  See Ch. 25; EEWN 10, 240.4-7.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 July 1833. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P035): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:37:51'),(577,'POR0019',1,'Mary Queen of Scotland','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"122\"\n- \"84\"\n',111,105,3,'10.6 x 8.4','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 19]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Mary Queen of Scots, holding a small dog, and wearing a hat, lace headdress, and ruffled collar. Drawn from a picture by Federico Zuccaro. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her face, her form . . . that remarkable countenance.\'--Vol. XXI, p. 6.\'  See Ch. 21; EEWN 10, 187.13-16. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1833.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:45:53'),(578,'POR0020',15,'Amy Robsart','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"89\"\n',17,97,3,'10.5 x 8.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 20]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Amy Robsart, wearing a white dress. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The exquisite beauty . . . constantly in her company.\'--Vol. XXII, p. 154.\'  See Ch. 8; EEWN 11, 81.8-10. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 October 1832.',NULL,'2009-04-22 08:29:56'),(579,'POR0021',15,'Janet Foster','--- \n- \"32\"\n- \"131\"\n- \"84\"\n',100,68,3,'12.5 x 10.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 21]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Janet Foster, wearing dark dress and plain white bonnet, holding pearl necklaces. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'\"Surely, lady,\" said Janet . . . fairer neck jewels than these.\"\'--Vol. XXII, p. 75.\'  See Ch. 5; EEWN 11, 38.19-21.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1833.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:34:58'),(580,'POR0022',20,'Minna','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"134\"\n',33,92,3,'9.4 x 11.2','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 22]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014513d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56481~100258','Portrait of Minna Troil seated, looking off into the distance, with the sea in the background. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The ocean, in all its varied forms . . . seasons exhibited them.\'--Vol. XXIV, p. 36.\'  See Ch. 3; EEWN 12, 22.32-35. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 March 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0077.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:39:19'),(581,'POR0023',20,'Brenda','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"133\"\n',60,71,3,'11 x 9.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 23]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Brenda Troil in a dress adorned with bows and a hat with a feather in it. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'A fairy form . . . almost childish lightness of step.\'--Vol. XXIV, p. 35.\'  See Ch. 3; EEWN 12, 21.41-43.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 April 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0076.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:44:51'),(582,'POR0024',10,'Margaret Ramsay','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"150\"\n',12,87,3,'11 x 8.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 24]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Margaret Ramsay wearing a white bonnet, sitting in a chair, looking off to the side. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'At length they attained . . . discontented and disconsolate.\'--Vol. XXVI, p. 155.\'  See Ch. 8; EEWN 13, 97.27-30. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 February 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0079.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:45:07'),(583,'POR0025',21,'Isabelle de Croye','--- \n- \"167\"\n- \"84\"\n',85,87,3,'10 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 25]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Isabelle de Croye, wearing crown, shawl, and ermine cape. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'He speedily made the discovery . . . resembled that of Melpomene.\'--Vol. XXXI [sic].\' See Ch. 4; EEWN 15, 50.1-6. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 15 September 1832. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0092.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:45:21'),(584,'POR0026',19,'Alice Bridgnorth','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"84\"\n',69,92,3,'10.5 x 7','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 26]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Alice Bridgenorth, wearing a high-necked gown and a bonnet. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The sad-coloured gown . . . less graceful than Alice Bridgenorth\'s.\'--Vol. XXVIII, p. 230.\'  See Ch. 13; EEWN 14, 129.15-18. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated July 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0084.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:35:48'),(585,'POR0027',19,'Fenella','--- \n- \"157\"\n- \"84\"\n',53,19,3,'10.5 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 27]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Fenella Christian wearing a turban with a feather. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her face was darker . . . rather a foreign attribute.\'--Vol. XXIX, p. 29.\' See Ch. 16; EEWN 14, 162.18-21. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 June 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0086.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:45:54'),(586,'POR0028',22,'Green Mantle','--- \n- \"181\"\n- \"84\"\n',74,105,3,'15 x 13','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 28]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014592d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56651~100268','Portrait of Greenmantle, wearing a large cape. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'When she entered . . . instant she recognised me.\'--Vol. XXXV, p. 207.\'  See Letter 12; EEWN 17, 106.5-8.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:41:32'),(587,'POR0029',4,'Eveline Berenger','--- \n- \"190\"\n- \"84\"\n',54,108,3,'11.5 x 11','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 29]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Eveline Berenger wearing a cross necklace. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The morning light . . . her beleagured castle.\'--Vol. XXXVII, p. 97.\' See Ch. 8.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:22:00'),(588,'POR0030',26,'Edith Plantagenet','--- \n- \"394\"\n- \"84\"\n',47,72,3,'10.5 x 8.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 30]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Edith Plantagenet, wearing a white dress and veil. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'But a second time they passed the spot . . . a rose-bud.\'--Vol. XXXVIII, p. 77.\'  See Ch. 4. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0110.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:46:27'),(589,'POR0031',29,'Alice Lee','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"201\"\n- \"84\"\n',20,87,3,'14.5 x 11.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 31]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','0014662d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57574~100323','Portrait of Alice Lee, holding a book and wearing a white bonnet and shawl. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The beautiful and high-born young lady . . . in dignity.’—Vol. XXXIX, p. 242.’ See Ch. 13. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 May 1833.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:42:41'),(590,'POR0032',29,'Phoebe Mayflower','--- \n- \"205\"\n- \"84\"\n',89,19,3,'12.5 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 32]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Phoebe Mayflower wearing white dress and bonnet. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'This was no other than . . . had felt a strong vocation’—Vol. XL, p. 222.’ See Ch. 29.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 April 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0117.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:47:07'),(591,'POR0033',13,'Kilchurn Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n',38,59,3,'8.6 x 12.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 33]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Depicts Kilchurn Castle, on a lake. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The now mouldering towers of Kilchurn\'--Vol. XLI, p. 129.’ See Ch. 7; EEWN 20, 71.15-16.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 July 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0119.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:47:40'),(592,'POR0034',9,'Fair Maid of Perth','--- \n- \"210\"\n- \"84\"\n',57,108,3,'14.5 x 9','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 34]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Catharine Glover at prayer, wearing a tartan shawl. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'She had sunk on her knees to pray . . . posture to return thanks’—Vol. XLII, p. 91.’ See Ch. 5; EEWN 21, 44.35-37. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 June 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0121.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:47:49'),(593,'POR0035',9,'Loch Tay','--- \n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',84,54,3,'8.3 x 12.1','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 35]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Depicts Loch Tay, with a mountain in the background. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The magnificent bosom of the lake itself was a scene to gaze on with delight--Vol. XLIII, p. 184.’ See Ch. 27; EEWN 21, 285.40-41. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0124.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:48:20'),(594,'POR0036',2,'Anne of Geierstein','--- \n- \"223\"\n- \"84\"\n',17,98,3,'13 x 11','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 36]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Anne of Geierstein, wearing a feathered cap. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'Her long fair hair . . . at once a character of gentleness.\'--Vol. XLIV, p. 47.’ See Ch. 3; EEWN 22, 27.38-41. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 June 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0135.',NULL,'2009-05-19 08:48:39'),(595,'POR0037',2,'Hermione','--- \n- \"149\"\n- \"84\"\n',74,71,3,'14.5 x 11.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 37]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Hermione in Persian dress. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The silver lamp was extinguished . . .  in the Persian costume’--Vol. XLIV, p. 215.’ See Ch. 11; EEWN 22,116.16-18.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated August 1835.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:36:48'),(596,'POR0038',2,'Bale [sic]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"354\"\n',7,5,3,'11.5 x 14.5','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 38]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Depicts the city of Basle, with a harbour in the foreground and church steeples in the distance. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'The Rhine, a broad and large river . . . into portions the city of Basle—-Vol. XLIV, p. 137.’ See Ch. 8; EEWN 22, 75.11-13. Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0136.',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:37:15'),(597,'POR0039',7,'Lady Augusta','--- \n- \"231\"\n- \"84\"\n',111,27,3,'12.7 x 8.8','1834','1834',16,'[Plate 39]','EUL, Corson A.12.PORP.1834/1','','Portrait of Lady Augusta, wearing a hat and with a shawl decorated with seashells. Accompanying quoted text, with reference to Magnum Opus edition: \'In a moment, I think, of madness, I resolved to set out in this masculine disguise\'--Vol. XLVIII, p. [sic].’ See Ch. 11; EEWN 23b, 109.28-30.  Imprint of Chapman & Hall dated 1 May 1833. Also appears in the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels: see #FIS0132. Exhibited at Royal Academy, no. 516, 1833.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:48:27'),(598,'SSS0001',18,'Edith Bellenden','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"84\"\n',89,87,3,'10.5 x 8','1845','1845',3,'facing p. 124','EUL, Corson 3964.B.STEV','0014477d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56246~100244','Portrait of Edith Bellenden, wearing a scarf over her head.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:44:11'),(599,'SSS0002',28,'Rose Bradwardine','--- \n- \"12\"\n- \"84\"\n',20,92,3,'11.5 x 8.3','1845','1845',3,'facing p. 162','EUL, Corson 3964.B.STEV','0014645d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57290~100306','Portrait of Rose Bradwardine, with hat, necklace, and embroidered dress.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:47:46'),(600,'SSS0003',6,'Scene from The Bride of Lammermoor','--- \n- \"1\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"131\"\n- \"64\"\n- \"86\"\n',88,48,3,'10.8 x 8.4','1845','1845',3,'facing p. 243','EUL, Corson 3964.B.STEV','0007394d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53216~100064','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood confronting Lucy Ashton at the signing of the marriage contract, and holding out the token to Lucy, while Lady Ashton attempts to intervene [Ch. 33]. Published earlier in \'Heath\'s Gallery of British Engravings\', vol. 4 (London: Longman [etc.], 1838), also titled \'Scene from the Bride of Lammermoor\'.',NULL,'2009-12-11 10:41:04'),(601,'SSR0001',18,'Great Hall, Craignethan Castle / Tillietudlem / Old Mortality Vol. II, p. 64','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"393\"\n- \"374\"\n- \"411\"\n- \"10\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"412\"\n',18,43,3,'9.5 x 13.5','1835','1835',11,'frontispiece','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0014467d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56077~100234','Depicts Lady Bellenden at table at Tillietullem, with Edith seated on the the other side of it listening anxiously to the discussion between Major Bellenden and Claverhouse near the window, with Bothwell leaning casually against a trunk on the other side of the room [Ch. 12]. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:50:19'),(602,'SSR0002',18,'A Gallery in Craignethan Castle / Tillietudlem / Old Mortality Vol. II, p. 27','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"375\"\n- \"413\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"96\"\n',18,47,3,'10 x 8','1835','1835',11,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0014466d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56060~100233','Depicts Halliday guarding the door to Morton\'s prison, with Jenny Dennison encouraged by Edith Bellenden behind offering to bribe him for entry [Ch. 10], Appears on alternative engraved title-page, with main title of \'Heath\'s Picturesque Annual for 1835\'. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Later published in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:52:01'),(603,'SSR0003',1,'Melrose Abbey / Abbot Vol. I, p. 183','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"414\"\n- \"123\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"256\"\n',18,50,3,'9.5 x 13.5','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 58','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007318d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52340~100013','Depicts the inside of the Abbey of Saint Mary\'s (based on Melrose) with signs of devastation around an old tomb with reclining stone figure.  Accompanying quoted passage (pp. 59-60), relating to the Magnum Opus edition, reads: \'Magdalene and Roland Graeme remained alone in the great vaulted space . . . had sent headlong from their station.’ See Ch. 13; EEWN 10, 99.14-28. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:53:05'),(604,'SSR0004',17,'Glendearg / Monastery Vol. I, p. 119','--- \n- \"120\"\n- \"118\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"244\"\n',18,115,3,'9.5 x 14','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 62','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0007598d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~55510~100200','Depicts Father Eustace riding alongside the river, with Christie of Clinthill galloping behind in the distance [Ch 9]. Accompanying description (pp. 63) reads: \'The scene is in the dale of the Allan near Melrose, and includes the identical spot where the sub-prior was overtaken by Christie of the Clinthill\'. Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Later published in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845): see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:54:13'),(605,'SSR0011',18,'Craignethan Castle / Tillietudlem / Old Mortality Vol. II, p. 259.','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"43\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"255\"\n',18,109,3,'9.5 x 13.4','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 252','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','0014474d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56195~100241','Depicts Cuddie with three marksmen by a flowing river at  the foot of Tillietudlem, which stands on a high bank [Ch. 25]. Accompanying description (pp. 253-54) reads: \'This, the reader perceives, is the Tillietudlem of Old Mortality, and he will recognise Cuddie Headrigg climbing up the steep with his companions to take the castle by surprise--and to be put to flight by a discharge of scalding brose from his sweetheart.’ Title/caption reference relates to Magnum Opus edition. Later published in H. I. and A. Stevens, ‘Scott and Scotland’ (London, 1845), with title in List of Illustrations as ‘Attack on Craignethan Castle’: see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:55:56'),(606,'SSR0012',18,'Fall of the Clyde / At Stonebyres / Old Mortality','--- - \"14\" - \"244\" - \"143\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',18,115,3,'9.5 x 13.4','1835','1835',11,'facing p. 254','EUL, Corson E.RIT.3874','','Depicts River Clyde with rapids in the foreground and large waterfall behind, surrounded by steep cliff and wooded bank.',NULL,NULL),(607,'WAK0001',23,'Rob Roy / \"What fellow are you\" Vol. 2','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"54\"\n- \"371\"\n',25,43,3,'10.3 x 7.8','1837','1837',30,'frontispiece','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','0014609d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56939~100285','Depicts Nicol Jarvie, surrounded by armed Highlanders, addressing Helen MacGregor, who stands with a long broadsword pointing to the ground. See Ch. 31; EEWN 5, 261.18. Same as #CHT0006, with 1832 imprint, along with different title/caption and without the quotation. Exhibited Royal Academy, no. 610, 1832.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:58:11'),(608,'WAK0002',23,'Rob Roy / \"Do you think I will ask him twice\"','--- \n- \"80\"\n- \"415\"\n- \"416\"\n',110,9,3,'10.3 x 7.7','1837','1837',30,'facing p. 49','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone, seated at a table, being berated by his father for refusing to become a partner, with the head clerk Owen also seated at the table. Ch. 2; EEWN 5, 18.35.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:35:09'),(609,'WAK0003',23,'Rob Roy / \"Stay\" she said, \"stay\" ','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',25,88,3,'10.5 x 7.5','1837','1837',30,'facing p. 97','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone and Diana in the Library at Osbaldistone Hall, with Diana resisting Frank\'s determination to seek out Rashleigh. See Ch. 13; EEWM 5, 109.43. Exhibited at Royal Academy, no. 640, 1832.',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:51:37'),(610,'WAK0004',23,'Rob Roy / \"Taking out another Glove, threw it towards me.\" Vol. I','--- - \"80\" - \"100\" - \"57\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',82,47,3,'10.7 x 7.5','1837','1837',30,'facing p. 153','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','','Depicts Diana Vernon throwing a second glove on to a table, in an effort to assuage Frank\'s suspicions, in the library at Osbaldistone Hall. See Ch. 17; EEWN 5, 139.22.',NULL,NULL),(611,'WAK0005',23,'Rob Roy / \"What--Shedding each other’s bluid as it were Strangers” Vol. 2','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"417\"\n',110,47,3,'10.6 x 7.5','1837','1837',30,'facing p. 199','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','0014600d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56786~100276','Depicts Rob Roy with broadsword raised intervening in the encounter between Frank and Rashleigh, in the College-yards at Glasgow. See Ch. 25; EEWN 5, 210.11-12.',NULL,'2009-09-08 14:59:27'),(612,'WAK0006',23,'Rob Roy / \"She pressed my hand &c.\" Vol. 2','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',110,47,3,'10.2 x 7.6','1837','1837',30,'facing p. 262','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVK.1837','0014608d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56922~100284','Depicts Diana Vernon on horseback, saluting Frank Osbaldistone who stands beneath her, as her fellow-rider waits behind. See Ch. 33; EEWN 5, 285.16.',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:11:19'),(613,'KEP0002',28,'Flora','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"36\"\n- \"41\"\n- \"60\"\n',89,29,3,'13 x 9.2','1832','1832',25,'facing p. 136','EUL, Corson E.KEE.6','0014642d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57241~100303','Depicts Flora MacIvor at full length in flowing dress in woodland setting, besides pool, with another female figure behind her, holding a harp [Ch. 22]. Employed in \'The Keepsake\' to illustrate \'The Brother and Sister. An Italian Story. By the Author of Frankenstein [Mary Shelley]\'. Published later in \'Heath\'s Gallery of British Engravings\', vol. 2 (London: Longman [etc.], 1836), titled \'Flora Mac Ivor\'; and in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845): see image.',NULL,'2009-12-11 10:36:06'),(614,'KEP0003',28,'Alice','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"418\"\n- \"102\"\n',25,88,3,'10.5 x 8','1832','1832',25,'facing p. 152','EUL, Corson E.KEE.6','0014658d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57507~100319','Depicts Alice in the hut where Waverley is held, signalling to him her intention to conceal a packet of papers in the portmanteau which lies opened to the left corner of the picture, while three Highlanders are otherwise involved behind them [Ch. 37]. Employed here to illustrate a short tale, \'The Highland Cottage\', by John Carne. Published later in H. I and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845), facing p. 168, titled there in List of Illustrations as \'Interior of the Cottage\'. Exhibited at Royal Academy in 1832 as \'Alice Showing the papers to Waverley\' (no. 642).',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:14:33'),(615,'KEP0004',12,'Jeanie in the Outlaw\'s Hut','--- \n- \"23\"\n- \"419\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"104\"\n',82,88,3,'10.5 x 8','1832','1832',25,'facing p. 283','EUL, Corson E.KEE.6','0007452d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53941~100107','Depicts Jeanie Deans in sleeping position on straw in hovel, with crone-like woman holding a candle and looking at her, while another female sleeps in sprawling position behind [Ch. 31]. Employed here to illustrate a short tale, \'The Outlaw\'s Bride\', by Leitch Ritchie.',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:16:18'),(616,'KEP0005',3,'One Peep was Enough','--- \n- \"363\"\n- \"365\"\n- \"364\"\n',82,9,3,'10.8 x 8','1832','1832',25,'facing p. 303','EUL, Corson E.KEE.6','0007336d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52647~100031','Depicts Mrs Heukbane and Mrs Shortcake examining the love letter of Richard Taffril to Jenny Caxon in Mrs Mailsetter\'s parlour in the Fairport postmaster\'s house [Ch. 15]. Employed here to illustrate a short tale, \'One Peep was Enough; or, The Post-Office\', by Miss L. E. Landon. Published later in H. I. and A. Stevens, \'Scott and Scotland\' (London, 1845), facing p. 217, with caption \'Scene in the Post Office at Fairport / Vide Antiquary, Ch. 15\': see image.',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:17:49'),(617,'WAG0001',10,'Margaret Ramsay','--- - \"150\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,27,3,'14.5 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Margaret Ramsay in men\'s clothing and cape, having removed a feathered cap to expose her ringlets. Accompanying quoted text: \'The boy was clad . . . fair god-daughter Margaret Ramsay.\' [Ch. 28].',NULL,NULL),(618,'WAG0002',23,'Diana Vernon','--- - \"100\" - \"49\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',66,104,3,'13.8 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Diana Vernon on horseback, carrying a whip. Accompanying quoted text: \'A vision that passed . . . chase was at a close.\' [Ch. 5].',NULL,NULL),(619,'WAG0003',29,'Alice Lee','--- - \"201\" - \"163\" - \"88\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',111,87,3,'13.6 x 10.4','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Alice Lee at prayer, with a prayer book in front of her. Accompanying quoted text: \'Alice Lee kneeled . . . to administer comfort to others.\' [Ch. 13].',NULL,NULL),(620,'WAG0004',24,'Clara Mowbray','--- \n- \"177\"\n- \"84\"\n',95,51,3,'13.1 x 10.3','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Clara Mowbray taking down her hair. Accompanying quoted text: \'A woman must be borne down . . . statues of the Grecian nymphs.\' [Ch. 23]. Legend states published 1 March 1841.',NULL,'2009-04-10 11:42:43'),(621,'WAG0005',7,'Lady Augusta Berkeley','--- - \"231\" - \"84\" - \"30\" - \"352\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,53,3,'12.8 x 10.4','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Lady August Berkley in a wooded area by a spring, wearing a hood decorated with sea shells. Accompanying quoted text: \'He placed her as he said . . . was obvious to her eye.\' [Ch. 17]. Legend states published 1 March 1841.',NULL,NULL),(622,'WAG0006',11,'Julia Mannering','--- - \"422\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',49,8,3,'14.2 x 11','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Julia Mannering wearing a tartan shawl. Accompanying quoted text: \'At length the trampling of horses . . . thought excited by the second.\' [Ch. 20].',NULL,NULL),(623,'WAG0007',17,'The White Lady','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"84\"\n',75,73,3,'13.9 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 7]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts the White Lady of Avenel standing in shallow water. Accompanying quoted text: \'Halbert Glendinning cast the leathern brogue . . . not a third time daunt him.\' [Chs 11 & 12].',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:39:55'),(624,'WAG0008',9,'The Glee Maiden','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"524\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"84\"\n',65,73,3,'14.4 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 8]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts the Glee Maiden Louise with a small black dog, and a stringed musical instrument on her back. Accompanying quoted text: \'The Glee-Maiden had planted herself . . . the freshness of early youth.\' [Ch. 11].',NULL,'2009-06-14 18:23:53'),(625,'WAG0009',12,'Effie Deans','--- - \"279\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,56,3,'14.4 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 9]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Effie Deans wearing a tartan shawl and carrying a milk-pail on her head. Accompanying quoted text: \'Effie Deans, under the tender and affectionate . . . loveliness of face and person.\' [Ch. 10].',NULL,NULL),(626,'WAG0010',28,'Flora Mac Ivor','--- - \"60\" - \"84\" - \"204\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',51,55,3,'14.8 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 10]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Flora MacIvor holding a white cloth and thread, sitting by an open window. Accompanying quoted text: \'When Waverley reached . . . fainted in her chair.\' [Ch. 68].',NULL,NULL),(627,'WAG0011',22,'Rachael Geddes','--- - \"420\" - \"84\" - \"34\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,55,3,'13.4 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 11]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Rachael Geddes wearing a large bonnet, picking flowers in a garden. Accompanying quoted text: \'In a few minutes . . . sallied with my fair Quaker.\' [Letter 7].',NULL,NULL),(628,'WAG0012',2,'Anne of Gierstein [sic]','--- - \"223\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,26,3,'13.1 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 12]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Anne of Geierstein on a mountain-top, wearing a feathered cap and holding a staff. \'Upon the very summit . . . mixture of pity and wonder.\' [Ch. 2].',NULL,NULL),(629,'WAG0013',26,'Queen Berengaria','--- \n- \"421\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"178\"\n',51,8,3,'14.4 x 11','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 13]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts the Queen Berengaria kneeling beside a bed, holding the hand of Richard, who lies in it. Accompanying quoted text: \'Berengaria knew well . . . to his wife Dejanira.\' [Ch. 17].',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:54:21'),(630,'WAG0014',9,'Catharine','--- - \"210\" - \"84\" - \"85\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,27,3,'13.2 x 10.7','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 14]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Catharine Glover wearing a tartan shawl poking a stick through the metal bars to deliver food to a prisoner. Accompanying quoted text: \'Here is dead silence . . . the preserver of my life!\' [Ch. 32].',NULL,NULL),(631,'WAG0015',22,'The Unknown','--- - \"84\" - \"53\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,13,3,'13.2 x 10.4','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 15]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts the mistress of Prince Charles Edward (based in the story on Clementina Walkinshaw). Accompanying quoted text: \'A small side door . . . not the retiring beauty.\' [Ch. 16].',NULL,NULL),(632,'WAG0016',3,'Miss Wardour','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"362\"\n',51,73,3,'14.2 x 10.7','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 16]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Isabella Wardour sitting on a dangling chair suspended by a rope. Accompanying quoted text: \'The fishers had brought . . . the brink of the precipice.\' [Ch. 8].',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:55:01'),(633,'WAG0017',21,'Jacqueline','--- - \"167\" - \"84\" - \"67\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,52,3,'14.2 x 10.8','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 17]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Jacqueline carrying a platter on which are a plate of dried plums and a golden chalice. Accompanying quoted text: \'As he spoke . . . wrapped in silence and mystery.\' [Ch. 4].',NULL,NULL),(634,'WAG0018',15,'Janet Foster','--- - \"32\" - \"204\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',111,104,3,'14.2 x 10.8','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 18]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Janet Foster working at some embroidery. Accompanying quoted text: \'\"Oh Janet!\" the countess exclaimed . . . at the bottom of the apartment.\' [Ch. 6].',NULL,NULL),(635,'WAG0019',18,'Jenny Dennison','--- - \"375\" - \"84\" - \"69\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,53,3,'13.7 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 19]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Jenny Dennison, wearing a tartan shawl, holding a silver dollar.  Accompanying quoted text: \'\"So ye\'re no thinking . . . wadna ha\' stude twice about it.\"\' [Ch. 10].',NULL,NULL),(636,'WAG0020',18,'Edith Bellenden','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"85\"\n',30,63,3,'13.8 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 20]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Edith Bellenden wearing a tartan shawl on the point of entering a room where Morton is imprisoned. Accompanying quoted text: \'The door, which slowly opened . . . poor lad Halliday punished for his civility.\"\' [Ch. 10].',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:42:56'),(637,'WAG0021',14,'Lady Rowena','--- - \"90\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',51,56,3,'14.4 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 21]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Rowena seated, in regal dress. Accompanying quoted text: \'As soon as Prince John . . . greeted her new dignity.\' [Ch. 12].',NULL,NULL),(638,'WAG0022',23,'Helen Macgregor','--- - \"371\" - \"84\" - \"103\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,8,3,'13.9 x 10.9','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 22]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Helen MacGregor wearing a tartan shawl and feathered cap, holding a sword. Accompanying quoted text: \'The attack which Captain Thornton meditated . . . pair of pistols at her girdle.\' [Ch. 30].',NULL,NULL),(639,'WAG0023',4,'Rose Flammock','--- - \"192\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,19,3,'13.2 x 10','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 23]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Rose Flammock wearing a cross necklace, gazing out a window. Accompanying quoted text: \'She went to the window . . . should assure her of Eveline\'s safety.\' [Ch. 14]. Legend states published 1 October 1840.',NULL,NULL),(640,'WAG0024',22,'Greenmantle','--- \n- \"181\"\n- \"84\"\n',95,73,3,'13.9 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 24]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Greenmantle wearing a large dark cape and taking away the mask from her face. Accompanying quoted text: \'Alan Fairford was in the act . . . my dearest affections were concerned.\' [Ch. 23].',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:41:42'),(641,'WAG0025',14,'Rebecca','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"87\"\n',30,13,3,'14.2 x 10.3','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 25]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','0030090d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~59126~100418','Depicts Rebecca at a balcony in elaborate feathered hat and cape. Accompanying quoted text: \'The figure of Rebecca might . . . those who affected to deride them.\' [Ch. 7]. Legend states published 1 November 1840. Also appears in a later German translation, though with artist listed as Alfred Henry Payne: see image. ',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:20:39'),(642,'WAG0026',15,'Amy Robsart','--- \n- \"131\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"89\"\n',49,71,3,'14.4 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 26]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Amy Robsart leaning over a dresser, holding a pearl necklace. Accompanying quoted text: \'The Earl had re-entered the bedchamber . . . \"that which might ruin us both.\"\' [Ch. 7].',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:56:17'),(643,'WAG0027',25,'Zilia de Moncada','--- \n- \"17\"\n- \"232\"\n- \"84\"\n',30,27,3,'14.7 x 10.8','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 27]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Zilia de Moncada weeping over her sleeping infant in bed. Accompanying quoted text: \'While Lawford drew up a proper minute . . . \"watch the door of the room on the outside.\"\' [Ch. 15].',NULL,'2009-06-25 16:57:05'),(644,'WAG0028',5,'Miss Vere','--- - \"268\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',95,104,3,'13.7 x 10.8','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 28]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Isabel Vere sitting in a chair, resting her head on her hand. Accompanying quoted text: \'He found Miss Vere seated . . . to take leave of her for ever.\'  [Ch. 14]. Legend states published 1 December 1840.',NULL,NULL),(645,'WAG0029',12,'Madge Wildfire','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"104\"\n',30,8,3,'13.9 x 11.2','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 29]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','0030056d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58576~100384','Depicts Madge Wildfire wearing a large feathered hat and carrying a stick, with a church in the distance. Accompanying quoted text: \'Across the man\'s cap . . . to conduct her to the Interpreter\'s house.\' [Ch. 32]. Legend states published 1 December 1840. Also appears in a New York edition (1860) of this publication (see image).',NULL,'2009-09-08 15:22:24'),(646,'WAG0030',19,'Alice Bridgenorth','--- - \"152\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',111,53,3,'13.7 x 10.4','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 30]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Alice Bridgenorth wearing a large dark shawl that moves in the wind. Accompanying quoted text: \'As he approached the monument . . . by the side of Alice Bridgenorth.\' [Ch. 17]. Legend states published 1 December 1840.',NULL,NULL),(647,'WAG0031',28,'Miss Bradwardine','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"12\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"84\"\n',30,18,3,'13.4 x 10.3','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 31]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Rose Bradwardine reading a book, sitting by a latticed window, with a tea service next to her. Accompanying quoted text: \'Miss Bradwardine was but seventeen. . . . with one of a kindred taste.\' [Chs 10 & 14]. Legend states published 1 January 1841.',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:40:22'),(648,'WAG0032',1,'Catharine Seyton','--- - \"325\" - \"84\" - \"204\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',39,87,3,'13.5 x 10.4','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 32]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Catherine Seyton wearing a cross necklace and holding a large needle and thread. Accompanying quoted text: \'Roland\'s eyes found better employment . . . utmost skill of the most expert seamstress.\' [Ch. 10]. Legend states published 1 January 1841.',NULL,NULL),(649,'WAG0033',4,'Eveline Berenger','--- - \"190\" - \"84\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',30,27,3,'13.9 x 10.5','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 33]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Eveline Berenger wearing a long veil and an ermine-trimmed cloak. Accompanying quoted text: \'Eveline entered leaning on . . . to receive happiness or misery, life or death.\' [Ch. 19].',NULL,NULL),(650,'WAG0034',12,'Jeannie Deans','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"84\"\n',30,27,3,'14.2 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 34]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Jeanie Deans wearing a tartan shawl, carrying a cloth bundle, and holding a book. Accompanying quoted text: \'\"Ye see, and ye ken . . . God bless ye, Reuben!\"\' [Ch. 28].',NULL,'2009-08-16 19:42:35'),(651,'WAG0035',6,'Lucy Ashton','--- - \"2\" - \"84\" - \"352\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\" - \"\"',74,71,3,'14.3 x 11.8','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 35]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depicts Lucy Ashton seated by a fountain wearing a white dress and tartan shawl. Accompanying quoted text: \'She sate upon one of the disjointed stones . . . did not rise from the stone on which she was seated.\' [Ch. 20].',NULL,NULL),(652,'WAG0036',20,'Minna Troil','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"134\"\n',49,71,3,'14.2 x 10.6','1841','1841',29,'[Plate 36]','EUL, Corson A.12.WAVG.1841','','Depict Minna Troil seated and gazing upward. Legend states published 1 April 1841. Accompanying quoted text: \'From her mother, Minna inherited . . . which belonged to her disease and character.\' [Chs 3 & 28].',NULL,'2009-04-10 15:40:53'),(653,'CAB0001',28,'Lucky Macleary\'s','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"259\"\n- \"11\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"261\"\n- \"260\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"102\"\n',2,119,6,'9.5 x 7.9','1841','1841',33,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/1','','Depicts the Baron of Bradwardine at cross swords over a table with the Laird of Balmawhapple, in Luckie Macleary’s inn, with Waverley falling over, and the prostrate figure of the Laird of Killancureit under the table, the hostess entering plaid in hand to throw over the weapons [Ch. 11]. A reengraving of #ILN0002.','2009-04-11 14:19:53','2009-08-16 19:42:58'),(654,'CAB0002',11,'Meg Merrilies and Godfrey Bertram','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"263\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"264\"\n',2,119,6,'10.3 x 7.4','1841','1841',33,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/2','','Depicts Godfrey Bertram and groom on horseback, with turbaned Meg Merrilies above them on bank, issuing her curse, with sapling bough in raised right hand [Ch. 8]. A reengraving of #ILN0004.','2009-04-11 14:30:24','2009-08-16 19:43:14'),(655,'CAB0003',3,'The Antiquary Buying Fish','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"267\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"140\"\n',2,119,6,'9.8 x 7.7','1841','1841',33,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/3','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck bargaining over the price of fish with Maggie Mucklebackit, who is seated with a pail of fish at her feet [Ch. 11]. A reengraving of #ILN0006.','2009-04-11 14:33:35','2009-08-16 19:43:32'),(656,'CAB0004',23,'Clachan of Aberfoil','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"54\"\n',2,119,6,'9.8 x 7.3','1841','1841',33,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/4','','Depicts Bailie Nicol Jarvie engaged in combat with party of Highlanders, with others also engaged in the fray [Ch. 28]. A reengraving of #ILN0008.','2009-04-11 14:36:44','2009-08-16 19:43:45'),(657,'CAB0005',18,'Old Mortality and P. Pattieson','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"77\"\n- \"258\"\n',2,119,6,'9.8 x 7.9','1841','1841',33,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/5','','Depicts ‘Old Mortality’ restoring Covenanting gravestones with hammer and chisel, greeted by a stranger (Pattieson) holding a cane and uplifting his hat [Ch. 1]. A reengraving of #ILN0001.','2009-04-11 14:41:18','2009-08-16 19:44:02'),(658,'CAB0006',5,'Black Dwarf and Isabella Vere','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"268\"\n',2,119,6,'9.5 x 7.3','1841','1841',33,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/6','','Depicts Isabel Vere in the Black Dwarf’s hut, with the latter interposing and clutching her hand, as she attempts to leave [Ch. 16]. No artist given, but apparently a reengraving of #ILN0009, by William Allan.','2009-04-11 14:46:30','2009-08-16 19:44:18'),(659,'CAB0007',16,'Dalgetty and Children of the Mist','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"96\"\n',2,119,6,'9.8 x 7.5','1841','1841',33,'vol. 6 (internal title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/6','','Depicts Dugald Dalgetty in armour on his horse, Gustavus, with Ranald MacEagh instructing a gillie to take charge of his horse [Ch. 14]. No artist given, but apparently a reengraving of #ILN0013, by William Allan.','2009-04-11 14:51:15','2009-08-16 19:44:33'),(660,'CAB0008',12,'Madge Wildfire and Jeanie Deans at Church','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"104\"\n',113,119,6,'9.9 x 7.9','1841','1841',33,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/7','','Depicts Madge Wildfire leading Jeanie Deans by the hand through the church at Willingham, as congregants look on [Ch. 32].','2009-04-11 14:55:47','2009-04-11 14:57:55'),(661,'CAB0009',6,'Ravenswood and Lucy Ashton','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"86\"\n',2,119,6,'9.5 x 7.5','1841','1841',33,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/8','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood, with his hunting gun, and Lucy Ashton in a semi-embrace by the fountain in a woodland setting. She rests her head on his shoulder as he places his hand over his heart [Ch. 20]. No artist given, but apparently a reengraving of #ILN0012, by William Allan.','2009-04-11 15:01:11','2009-08-16 19:44:55'),(662,'CAB0010',14,'The Black Knight and Friar Tuck','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"285\"\n',41,119,6,'9.7 x 6.9','1841','1841',33,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/9','','Depicts Richard Coeur de Lion (as the Black Knight) at the door of the cottage of Friar Tuck, who stands on the threshold with a torch [Ch. 16].','2009-04-11 15:03:47','2009-06-25 16:58:45'),(663,'CAB0011',17,'Father Philip and the White Lady of Avenel','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"341\"\n',41,120,6,'9.6 x 7.4','1841','1841',33,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/10','','Depicts Father Philip on a horse attempting to ride through shallow water, with the floating figure of the White Lady above them [Ch. 5]. Imprint date of 1841; letterpress title-page dated 1842.','2009-04-11 15:06:56','2009-04-11 15:09:23'),(664,'CAB0012',1,'Queen Mary signing her Abdication','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"127\"\n- \"122\"\n- \"126\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"125\"\n',1,1,6,'7.5 x 9.3','1841','1841',33,'vol. 11 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/11','','Depicts Lord Ruthven, Sir Robert Melville, and Lord Lindesay encouraging Mary Queen of Scots (who is accompanied by two women) to sign the abdication contract [Ch. 22]. Imprint date of 1841; letterpress title-page dated 1842.','2009-04-11 15:13:11','2009-04-11 15:13:11'),(665,'CAB0013',15,'Tressilian and Wayland Smith','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"95\"\n- \"345\"\n',41,119,6,'9.6 x 7.2','1842','1842',33,'vol. 12 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/1','','Depicts Wayland Smith, with his smith’s tools and holding a horse, as Tressilian emerges to confront him with sword drawn, as Flibbertigibbet crouches in the shadows tugging at his coat [Ch. 10].','2009-04-11 15:23:48','2009-04-11 15:23:48'),(666,'CAB0014',20,'Cleveland Cast Ashore','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"137\"\n',1,121,6,'9.8 x 6.8 ','1842','1842',33,'vol. 13 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/2','','Depicts Mordaunt Mertoun attending the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland, with Ulla Troil (Norna) standing with arm raised behind them, and Bryce the pedlar just visible in the shadows [Ch. 7].','2009-04-11 15:26:45','2009-04-11 15:28:52'),(667,'CAB0015',10,'King James and Nigel','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"144\"\n- \"293\"\n',1,122,6,'9.7 x 7.3','1842','1842',33,'vol. 14 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson. A.7.a.1841-3/3','','Depicts King James VI and I and Nigel, accompanied by hunting dogs, a horse, and a dead buck [Ch. 27].','2009-04-11 15:31:38','2009-08-16 19:45:20'),(668,'CAB0016',19,'Julian Peveril and Alice Bridgenorth','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"153\"\n',1,123,6,'9.5 x 7.5','1842','1842',33,'vol. 15 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/15','','Depicts Julian Peveril and Alice Bridgenorth standing together in front of a large rock, as a third figure dressed as a Puritan (possibly representing Alice’s father) looms behind them to the left.','2009-04-11 15:35:26','2009-04-11 15:38:15'),(669,'CAB0017',21,'Murder of the Bishop of Liege','--- \n- \"400\"\n- \"398\"\n- \"229\"\n',114,1,6,'9.7 x 7.1','1842','1842',33,'vol. 16 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/16','','Depicts the Bishop of Liege lying murdered on the floor, as William de la Marck in armour stands above him with one arm raised, as others look on [Ch. 22].','2009-04-11 15:39:49','2009-04-11 15:42:22'),(670,'CAB0018',24,'Reappearance of Tyrell [sic] at the Cleikum Inn','--- \n- \"402\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"401\"\n- \"98\"\n',115,124,6,'9.8 x 7.5','1842','1842',33,'vol. 17 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/4','','Depicts Frank Tyrrel entering the Cleikum Inn through a doorway, with Meg Dods sprawled on the floor in alarm, and her male servants holding makeshift weapons [Ch. 27].','2009-04-11 15:43:58','2009-04-11 15:46:49'),(672,'CAB0019',22,'Dairsie [sic] Latimer and Joshua Geddes','--- \n- \"428\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"180\"\n',114,123,6,'9.7 x 7.4','1842','1842',33,'vol. 18 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/5','','Depicts Joshua Geddes on horseback, with Darsie Latimer walking alongside, and another rider in the distance turning away [Letter 6].','2009-04-11 15:58:19','2009-08-16 19:45:44'),(673,'CAB0020',4,'Lady Eveline and the Priest on the Battlements','--- \n- \"331\"\n- \"190\"\n- \"192\"\n- \"193\"\n',114,123,6,'9.8 x 7.5','1842','1842',33,'vol. 19 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/6','','Depicts Eveline Berenger and Rose Flammock on the ramparts of the Castle, with crouching figure of Wilkin Flammock below them, resting on pickaxe, and Father Aldrovand pointing to the distance [Ch. 8].','2009-04-11 16:01:37','2009-06-25 17:00:23'),(674,'CAB0021',26,'Berengaria Supplicating for the Life of Sir Kenneth','--- \n- \"421\"\n- \"146\"\n- \"394\"\n- \"196\"\n',1,125,6,'9.6 x 7.4','1842','1842',33,'vol. 20 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/7','','Depicts Queen Berengaria supplicating at the feet of the seated Richard, while Lady Edith stands behind her [Ch. 17].','2009-04-11 16:04:44','2009-04-11 16:08:08'),(675,'CAB0022',29,'Sir Henry Lee and His Daughter Surprised by a Roundhead','--- \n- \"201\"\n- \"200\"\n- \"206\"\n',1,122,6,'9.7 x 7.3','1842','1842',33,'vol. 21 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/8','','Depicts Sir Henry and Alice Lee, in the park at Woodstock, confronted by Joseph Tomkins [Ch. 2].','2009-04-11 16:09:57','2009-04-11 16:09:57'),(676,'CAB0023',9,'Combat of Henry Wynd with Bonthron','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"429\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"209\"\n',1,126,6,'9.5 x 7','1843','1843',33,'vol. 22 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1171.i/22','','Depicts a combat with axes between Henry Gow (‘Henry of the Wynd’) and Bonthron, as others look on [Ch. 23].','2009-04-11 16:14:01','2009-04-11 16:15:44'),(677,'CAB0024',2,'Death of Queen Margaret','--- \n- \"216\"\n- \"178\"\n',114,123,6,'10.1 x 7.7','1843','1843',33,'vol. 23 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/9','','Depicts the dying Queen Margaret seated, with mourners gathered around her [Ch. 32].','2009-04-12 13:29:05','2009-04-12 13:29:05'),(678,'CAB0025',8,'Agelastes, the Countess Brenhilda, and Count Robert of Paris','--- \n- \"408\"\n- \"103\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"410\"\n- \"226\"\n',1,122,6,'9.8 x 7.6','1843','1843',33,'vol. 24 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/10','','Depicts Agelastes, who is seated with a parchment on his knee, Brenhilda, and the fully-armoured Count Robert of Paris, in a wooded area [Ch. 10].','2009-04-12 13:32:09','2009-06-25 17:00:47'),(679,'CAB0026',7,'Sic Sedebat / Sir Walter Scott Bart.','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',1,127,1,'11.5 x 7.6','1843','1843',33,'vol. 25 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/11','','Engraving of the statue of Scott sculpted by John Greenshields between 1832 and 1835. Purchased from the artist by Robert Cadell, the statue was originally engraved for the second volume of Lockhart’s ‘Life of Scott’ (1837-38). See Russell, Francis, ‘Portraits of Sir Walter Scott’ (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 74, p. 46. Also appears as a frontispiece to vol. 10 of the Abbotsford edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0096.','2009-04-12 13:38:49','2009-05-19 08:57:22'),(680,'CAB0027',7,'The Bishop of Glasgow Blessing the People on Ash-Wednesday','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"430\"\n- \"88\"\n',1,1,6,'9.6 x 7.3','1843','1843',33,'vol. 25 (title-page vignette)','EUL, Corson A.7.a.1841-3/11','','Depicts the Bishop of Glasgow with mitre blessing several figures, some wearing armour [Ch. 19].','2009-04-12 13:45:53','2009-04-12 13:47:41'),(682,'PEO0001',30,'Sir Walter Scott, Bart.','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',116,57,3,'14.9 x 11.7','1847','1847',34,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/1','','Portrait of a young Scott by Henry Raeburn in 1808. Depicts Scott seated on a stone under a ruined wall, with a dog at his feet, with Hermitage Castle and the Hills of Liddesdale in the background. See Russell, Francis, ‘Portraits of Sir Walter Scott’ (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 161, pp. 73-74. No imprint date for this frontispiece, though engraved title-page opposite is dated 1847, while main title-page imprint date is 1843, and that to ‘Waverley’, as the first novel contained, is 1842.','2009-04-12 13:58:08','2009-04-12 13:58:08'),(683,'PEO0002',30,'Waverley Novels Vol I','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"172\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"262\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"94\"\n',48,119,6,'19.8 x 11.8','1847','1847',34,'vol. 1 (engraved title-page)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/1','','Collage of images associated with the works in this volume (i.e. Waverley, Guy Mannering, The Antiquary, Rob Roy, and Old Mortality), which includes a bust of Scott, several large figures, including what are probably meant to be Fergus MacIvor and Meg Merrilies, and a central battle scene (possibly reflecting Rob Roy). Imprint date of 1847, letterpress title-page dated 1843.','2009-04-12 14:00:44','2009-04-12 14:00:44'),(684,'PEO0003',30,'Waverley Novels Vol II','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"49\"\n',48,119,6,'19.8 x 11.8','1847','1847',34,'vol. 2 (engraved title-page)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/2','','Collage of images associated with the works in this volume (i.e. The Black Dwarf, A Legend of Montrose, The Heart of Mid-Lothian, The Bride of Lammermoor, Ivanhoe, and The Monastery), which includes the Deans sisters embracing at the top, and several other figures. Imprint date of 1847, letterpress title-page dated 1845.','2009-04-12 14:03:17','2009-04-12 14:03:17'),(685,'PEO0004',30,'Waverley Novels Vol III','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"347\"\n- \"56\"\n- \"122\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"139\"\n',1,119,6,'19.8 x 11.8','1847','1847',34,'vol. 3 (engraved title-page)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/3','','Collage of images associated with the works in this volume (i.e. The Abbot, Kenilworth, The Pirate, The Fortunes of Nigel, and Peveril of the Peak), which includes a shipwreck scene at the top (probably from The Pirate), and the figures of (probably) Mary Queen of Scots to the left and Queen Elizabeth and the Earl of Leicester on the right, with a castle scene (possibly representing Elizabeth’s entry into Kenilworth) in the centre. Imprint date of 1847, letterpress title-page dated 1845.','2009-04-12 14:05:56','2009-04-12 14:05:56'),(686,'PEO0005',30,'Waverley Novels Vol IV.','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"165\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"199\"\n',1,119,6,'19.8 x 11.8','1847','1847',34,'vol. 4 (engraved title-page)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/4','','Collage of images associated with the works in this volume (i.e. Quentin Durward, St Ronan’s Well, Redgauntlet, The Betrothed, The Highland Widow, The Talisman, The Two Drovers, and three short stories), with several figures. These include Saladin and Kenneth of Scotland, seated together at the oasis, at the foot; and Louis XI of France among those at the top. Imprint date of 1847, letterpress title-page dated 1846.','2009-04-12 14:09:13','2009-04-12 14:09:13'),(687,'PEO0006',30,'Waverley Novels Vol V','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"203\"\n- \"319\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n',1,119,6,'19.8 x 11.8','1847','1847',34,'vol. 5 (engraved title-page)','EUL, Corson Add. A.7.a.1843-b/5','','Collage of images associated with the works in this volume (i.e. Woodstock, The Fair Maid of Perth, Anne of Geierstein, Count Robert of Paris, The Surgeon’s Daughter, and Castle Dangerous), which includes several figures, including apparently Charles II near the foot and the hound Bevis (both from Woodstock). Imprint date of 1847, matching that on letterpress title-page.','2009-04-12 14:11:21','2009-04-12 14:11:21'),(688,'ABB0001',28,'Highland Hills from the Teith below Callander','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,69,3,'14.2 x 8.7','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts cattle grazing in a river below a mountain range. ','2009-04-13 11:01:22','2009-05-21 13:23:54'),(690,'ABB0002',30,'Waverley Novels','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"103\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n',48,119,6,'20 x 12','1842','1842',35,'engraved title-page to all vols.','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','0030099d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~59275~100427','A collage of images associated with the Waverley Novels, mainly including arms and armaments, and resembling the entrance hallway to Abbotsford. Also appears as outer wrapper to part issues of the edition: see image.','2009-04-13 11:08:10','2009-09-08 15:24:19'),(692,'ABB0003',28,'Colonel Gardiner','--- \n- \"431\"\n- \"84\"\n',117,127,3,'13.5 x 10','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 70','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Portrait of Colonel Gardiner, in white curly wig.','2009-04-13 11:17:51','2009-05-21 13:24:46'),(693,'ABB0004',28,'Holyrood from the Calton-hill','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"48\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 14.1','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 221','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts Holyrood Palace and chapel from Calton Hill, with the Salisbury Crags and the Firth of Forth in the background. ','2009-04-13 11:23:55','2009-05-21 13:25:03'),(694,'ABB0005',28,'Battle-field of Preston Pans','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"239\"\n',94,128,3,'8.8 x 14.2','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 257','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts a man and woman in an agrarian setting, with grazing cattle and sheep on the site of the Battle of Prestonpans, with the Firth of Forth in the background.','2009-04-13 11:29:33','2009-05-21 13:25:23'),(695,'ABB0006',28,'Prince Charles Edward Stuart','--- \n- \"301\"\n- \"84\"\n',118,127,3,'13 x 9','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 288','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Portrait of Prince Charles Edward Stuart, wearing armour. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 18 frontispiece: see #LIB0035.','2009-04-13 11:43:02','2009-05-21 13:25:43'),(696,'ABB0007',11,'Dirk Hatteraicks Cave--Coast of Galloway','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"140\"\n',94,57,3,'9.5 x 14.5','1842','1842',35,'facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts two figures on the seashore before high cliffs, with a strong sea in front of them, and a sailing vessel just visible on the horizon. ','2009-04-13 11:52:12','2009-05-21 13:26:45'),(697,'ABB0008',11,'View on the Solway, Mouth of the Nith, Criffel in the distance','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,115,3,'9.1 x 14.7','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 419','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts a boat being unloaded on the Solway Firth, with the hill Criffel in the distance.','2009-04-13 11:58:54','2009-05-21 13:27:54'),(698,'ABB0009',11,'Windermere--The Ferry','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,42,3,'8.7 x 14.5','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 454','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts three boats, two with small sails, on Lake Windermere, the nearest of these apparently occupied by a pleasure party.','2009-04-13 12:03:39','2009-05-21 13:28:13'),(699,'ABB0010',11,'Skiddaw and Saddleback from Bolton Moor','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,12,3,'8.4 x 14.3','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 476','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts the mountains Skiddaw and Saddleback from Bolton Moor, with a group of travellers, possibly representing gypsies, in the foreground.','2009-04-13 12:10:20','2009-05-21 13:28:48'),(700,'ABB0011',11,'View from James\' Court, Edinburgh','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 14','1842','1842',35,'vol. 1, facing p. 559','ABD, WS E13, vol. 1','','Depicts the northern view of Edinburgh from James\' Court, with the North Loch, North Bridge, and the Firth of Forth visible in the background.','2009-04-13 12:16:53','2009-05-21 13:29:07'),(701,'ABB0012',3,'The Queensferry from the South East','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"140\"\n',94,69,3,'9.1 x 14.1','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts the Firth of Forth, with several boats, and a horse and carriage proceeding along the road in the foreground.','2009-04-13 13:22:14','2009-05-21 13:52:02'),(702,'ABB0013',3,'Dundee in the time of the Antiquary','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,42,3,'8.5 x 14.5','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 17','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts several boats on a stormy sea, with Dundee and a mountain in the background.','2009-04-13 13:27:15','2009-05-21 13:52:51'),(703,'ABB0014',3,'Cliffs near Arbroath. Coast of Forfarshire','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"29\"\n',94,115,3,'8.8 x 14.5','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 49','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts a thatched cottage under cliffs along the coast, with a woman apparently mending fishing nets outside.','2009-04-13 13:33:22','2009-05-21 13:53:11'),(704,'ABB0015',3,'Arbroath Abbey from the West','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',94,129,3,'9.3 x 14.5','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 161','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts Arbroath Abbey (in ruins), with visitors and grazing cattle, as farm workers gather grain in the foreground.','2009-04-13 13:38:12','2009-08-16 19:48:20'),(705,'ABB0016',5,'The Vale of Manor Water, Peebles-shire','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"245\"\n',94,12,3,'8.3 x 14.2','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts a bridge over a stream, with a ruined tower behind it and enclosing hills in the background.','2009-04-13 13:46:13','2009-05-21 13:53:49'),(706,'ABB0017',5,'Gilnockie Tower on the Esk, near Langholm','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"245\"\n',71,130,3,'8.6 x 14.5','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 337','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts Gilnockie Tower (in ruins) on the River Esk, with a small figure fishing in the foreground.','2009-04-13 13:50:52','2009-05-21 13:54:07'),(707,'ABB0018',18,'Corra-Linn, near Lanark','--- \n- \"29\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"351\"\n',94,42,3,'13 x 9.2','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts the waterfall Corra Linn, in the Fall of Clyde near Lanark, with figures on the east side fishing.','2009-04-13 14:05:18','2009-05-21 13:54:23'),(708,'ABB0019',18,'Craignethan Castle, Lanarkshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"245\"\n',94,12,3,'8.4 x 14.4','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 473','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts Craignethan Castle (in ruins), with grazing sheep and three figures (a man, a woman with a baby in her arms) in the foreground.','2009-04-13 14:10:14','2009-06-25 17:01:48'),(709,'ABB0020',18,'John Graham of Claverhouse, Viscount Dundee','--- \n- \"412\"\n- \"84\"\n',119,127,3,'13.5 x 10.5','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 525','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Portrait of John Grahame of Claverhouse, Viscount Dundee. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 10 in vol. 9, with a more detailed background, engraved by H. Robinson.','2009-04-13 14:17:37','2009-05-22 10:17:02'),(710,'ABB0021',18,'Bothwell Bridge, Lanarkshire','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"239\"\n',94,128,3,'8.7 x 14.2','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 589','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts Bothwell Bridge over the River Clyde, being crossed by a dog, sheep, cattle, and a mounted figure.','2009-04-13 14:22:18','2009-05-21 13:55:27'),(711,'ABB0022',18,'Bass Rock, East Lothian','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"143\"\n',120,69,3,'9 x 14','1843','1843',35,'vol. 2, facing p. 623','ABD, WS E13, vol. 2','','Depicts the Bass Rock, off the 