n of the Waverley Novels as vol. 6 frontispiece: see #LIB0011. ','2009-04-22 09:37:44','2009-06-12 13:30:06'),(729,'ABB0039',16,'Inverary','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"354\"\n',123,130,3,'8.8 x 13.9','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing p. 297','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts the town of Inverary on Loch Fyne, with several boats on the water and in the harbour, and a mountain visible in the distance.','2009-04-22 09:39:58','2009-05-21 14:02:27'),(730,'ABB0040',16,'Inverlochy','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',123,133,3,'8.9 x 14.3','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing p. 341','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts Inverlochy Castle on the River Lochy, with mountains visible in the distance and two small figures in the foreground. Illustrations list at front of vol. gives title as ‘Inverlochy Battlefield’.','2009-04-22 09:43:52','2009-05-21 14:02:44'),(731,'ABB0041',14,'Durrenstein, on the Danube','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"244\"\n',108,69,3,'11.4 x 9.2','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts the Castle and mountain of Durrenstein in the Dolomites on the Danube River. Illustrations list at front of volume adds \'Where Richard I was imprisoned\', and to this adding a quotation from the ‘Life of Sir David Wilkie’: ‘Passed by the Castle of Durrenstein . . . peaceful security of modern times.’ Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels, as vol. 9 frontispiece: see #LIB0017. ','2009-04-22 09:50:32','2009-05-21 14:03:01'),(732,'ABB0042',14,'The Temple Church, London','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n',94,134,3,'13.5 x 9.2','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing p. 612','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts the interior of the Temple Church, London, with several clergy and other figures.','2009-04-22 09:52:58','2009-05-21 14:03:17'),(733,'ABB0043',14,'Coningsburgh Castle and Village, Yorkshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,128,3,'8.5 x 13.8','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing p. 659','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts Conisbrough Castle and the village of Congisbrough in South Yorkshire, with several figures in the foreground, including a horseman crossing a bridge and a woman with donkey proceeding down the road. ','2009-04-22 09:57:11','2009-05-21 14:03:41'),(734,'ABB0044',14,'York Minster, Moon Light view','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,69,3,'8.9 x 14.2','1844','1844',35,'vol. 4, facing p. 683','ABD, WS E13, vol. 4','','Depicts York Minster by moonlight, with cattle visible in the foreground.','2009-04-22 09:59:24','2009-05-21 14:04:00'),(735,'ABB0045',17,'Melrose Abbey, from the Quarry','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,12,3,'8.4 x 14.2','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Depicts Melrose Abbey in the distance, with several figures working in a quarry in the foreground.','2009-04-22 10:14:03','2009-05-21 14:15:03'),(736,'ABB0046',17,'The Vale of Gala Water / From the Woods of Abbotsford','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,130,3,'8.6 x 14.5','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 41','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Depicts Gala Water flowing through hilly country, as seen from the Abbotsford estate.','2009-04-22 10:19:19','2009-05-21 14:15:18'),(737,'ABB0047',17,'Melrose from Turn-Again','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"256\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,135,3,'9.1 x 14.2','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 217','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Depicts Melrose Abbey in the distance, with a small seated figure (possibly depicting Scott) and a dog in the foreground.','2009-04-22 10:23:44','2009-06-25 17:04:12'),(738,'ABB0048',17,'James Stuart, Earl of Murray / Regent of Scotland','--- \n- \"383\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12 x 8.5','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 257','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Portrait of James Stuart, Earl of Murray, Regent of Scotland. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 15 in vol. 2, somewhat larger than in the Abbotsford edition, engraved by H. Robinson. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels, as vol. 10 frontispiece: see #LIB0019','2009-04-22 10:38:49','2009-08-16 19:49:03'),(739,'ABB0049',1,'Loch Leven and Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,69,3,'8.4 x 14.1','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','0007311d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52215~100006','Depicts two working boats on Loch Leven in choppy water, with the castle in the distance. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson collection (P. 3071), with attributed date 1877 (see image).','2009-04-22 10:44:49','2009-09-08 15:25:42'),(740,'ABB0050',1,'James Douglas, Earl of Morton, Regent of Scotland','--- \n- \"434\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.5 x 9','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 413','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Portrait of James Douglas, fourth earl of Morton. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 6 in vol. 3, with more background, engraved by S. Freeman.','2009-04-22 10:51:08','2009-05-22 10:30:03'),(741,'ABB0051',1,'Barnbugle Castle and the Firth of Forth','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"15\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 13.8','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 433','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','0007330d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52548~100025','Depicts Barnbougle Castle along the Firth of Forth, with cattle drinking in the foreground. Also appears as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P. 3070), with attributed date 1877 (see image).','2009-04-22 10:56:36','2009-09-08 15:26:54'),(742,'ABB0052',1,'Mary, Queen of Scots','--- \n- \"122\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,1,'12.5 x 9','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 545','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Portrait of Mary, Queen of Scots. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 10 in vol. 3, engraved by W. T. Fry. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels, as vol. 11 frontispiece: see #LIB0021. ','2009-04-22 11:01:10','2009-05-22 10:31:46'),(743,'ABB0053',1,'Dundrennan Abbey / Kircudbrightshire, Port Mary in the distance','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"245\"\n',94,21,3,'9.1 x 14.4','1844','1844',35,'vol. 5, facing p. 575','ABD, WS E13, vol. 5','','Depicts Dundrennan Abbey (ruins), in Kirkcudbrightshire, with grazing cattle and two small figures along a road in the foreground.','2009-04-22 11:04:52','2009-05-21 14:18:16'),(744,'ABB0054',15,'Kenilworth as in 1816','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"245\"\n',94,21,3,'9.4 x 14.1','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts Kenilworth Castle as in 1816 (partially in ruins), with several cattle and two small figures visible in the foreground.','2009-04-23 08:37:38','2009-06-25 17:04:50'),(745,'ABB0055',15,'Warwick Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"129\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 12.7','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 202','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts Warwick Castle, surrounded by a moat and trees. List of Illustrations indicates that this is a \'Moonlight View\'.','2009-04-23 08:41:43','2009-05-21 14:18:58'),(746,'ABB0056',15,'Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester','--- \n- \"56\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.5 x 10','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 229','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Portrait of Robert Dudley, Earl of Leicester. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 11 in vol. 3, engraved by H. T. Ryall. ','2009-04-23 08:49:19','2009-05-22 10:35:33'),(747,'ABB0057',15,'Queen Elizabeth','--- \n- \"347\"\n- \"84\"\n',124,127,3,'12 x 9','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 254','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Portrait of Queen Elizabeth, wearing a dress made out of a fabric featuring images of human eyes and ears. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 1 in vol. 4,  with slightly different background and shading, engraved by W. T. Fry. Also appears in Library Edition of the Waverley Novels, as vol. 12 frontispiece: see #LIB0023.','2009-04-23 08:57:34','2009-05-22 10:47:25'),(748,'ABB0058',20,'View of the Orkney Islands / From Thurso Bay Headland, \'The Man of Hoy\'','--- \n- \"241\"\n- \"140\"\n',22,12,3,'8.4 x 14.1','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts a view of the Orkney Islands from a distance over water from the mainland, with two figures visible in the foreground.','2009-04-23 09:02:43','2009-05-21 14:19:47'),(749,'ABB0059',20,'Scalloway Bay and Castle / Zetland','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"241\"\n',22,57,3,'8.2 x 13','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 332','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts Scalloway Bay and Castle, in Shetland, with two female figures visible outside a cottage on the left.','2009-04-23 09:10:03','2009-05-21 14:20:10'),(750,'ABB0060',20,'View in Zetland / in the Valley of Tingwall on the road from Scalloway to Lerwick','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"72\"\n',22,128,3,'8.5 x 14','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 398','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts a cottage and three figures riding Shetland ponies in the foreground, with open countryside behind. ','2009-04-23 09:13:30','2009-05-21 14:20:29'),(751,'ABB0061',20,'View on the Coast of Zetland / The Morning after a Storm','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n',22,69,3,'8.7 x 12.6','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 444','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts broken pieces of a boat along a craggy shore, with two small figures in the centre, one apparently signalling for help and the other clinging to a makeshift raft. ','2009-04-23 09:18:10','2009-06-25 17:05:44'),(752,'ABB0062',20,'View on the Coast of Zetland, near the Noss / Moonlight','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"140\"\n',22,69,3,'8.8 x 13.4','1844','1844',35,'vol. 6, facing p. 509','ABD, WS E13, vol. 6','','Depicts a beach surrounded by craggy rocks with a calm sea by moonlight in Shetland, and the figure of a man apparently pursuing a woman in the foreground.','2009-04-23 09:22:02','2009-08-16 19:49:27'),(753,'ABB0063',10,'James 1st / From the Original Picture at Holyrood','--- \n- \"144\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.5 x 10','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Portrait of King James the VI and I, wearing a cloak with ermine trim. ','2009-04-23 09:27:30','2009-05-21 14:29:08'),(754,'ABB0064',10,'George Heriot / From the Original Picture in Heriot\'s Hospital, Edinburgh','--- \n- \"151\"\n- \"69\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'13 x 9.5','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 28','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Portrait of George Heriot, with a ruff collar and what appears to be coin or gold pieces in his hand. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 14 frontispiece: see #LIB0027. ','2009-04-23 09:31:14','2009-08-16 19:50:13'),(755,'ABB0065',10,'Palace of St. James, Westminster Abbey and Hall / From village of Charing, temp. of tale','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"322\"\n- \"311\"\n',125,21,3,'9.3 x 14','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 77','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts the Palace of St James and Westminster Abbey in the distance, with several small figures in the foreground. Drawn after a sketch by Wenceslaus Hollar. ','2009-04-23 09:48:59','2009-05-21 14:29:32'),(756,'ABB0066',10,'Greenwich Park and Palace, Old London in the Distance / Tower in Foreground built by Humphrey Duke of Gloster','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"322\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"246\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"245\"\n- \"354\"\n',1,107,3,'9.9 x 14','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 245','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Greenwich Park and Palace with London and the River Thames in the distance and a ruined tower in the left foreground. List of Illustrations at front of volume adds \'From a picture, temp. of tale\'. ','2009-04-23 09:55:34','2009-05-21 14:29:45'),(757,'ABB0067',10,'Royal Palace, Whitehall / As designed by Inigo Jones','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"244\"\n',126,57,3,'8.4 x 14.1','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 293','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts the Palace of Whitehall as it looked at the time of the tale, with the River Thames in the foreground, down which several barges are passing. List of Illustrations at front of volume adds \'From plans by the architect in the British Museum\'.','2009-04-23 10:04:43','2009-05-21 14:30:00'),(758,'ABB0068',19,'Peak Castle, Derbyshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',123,128,3,'8.6 x 12.1','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Peveril Castle in Derbyshire, up on a hill, with a stream and several cottages below. ','2009-04-23 10:11:42','2009-06-25 17:06:33'),(759,'ABB0069',19,'Haddon Hall, Derbyshire','--- \n- \"311\"\n',123,57,3,'8.4 x 12.4','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 372','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Haddon Hall, in Derbyshire, with two small figures resting on the field in the foreground. ','2009-04-23 10:16:12','2009-08-16 19:52:52'),(760,'ABB0070',19,'Castle Rushin, Castleton / Isle of Man','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"241\"\n- \"354\"\n',123,69,3,'8.9 x 13.3','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 413','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Castle Rushen, in Castletown on the Isle of Man, with some boats docked along the waterfront in the foreground, and several figures in front of the houses and cottages along the harbour.','2009-04-23 10:20:04','2009-05-21 14:30:51'),(761,'ABB0071',19,'Peel Castle, Isle of Man','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"241\"\n',123,69,3,'9 x 13.4','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 461','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Peel Castle, on the Isle of Man, from across a lagoon. Several small figures are visible resting on the rocks in the foreground.','2009-04-23 10:23:41','2009-05-21 14:31:06'),(762,'ABB0072',19,'Glen May, Isle of Man','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"244\"\n',123,130,3,'8.8 x 13.7','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 476','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Depicts Glen May on the Isle of Man.','2009-04-23 10:28:02','2009-05-21 14:31:26'),(763,'ABB0073',19,'Charles II','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"84\"\n',127,127,3,'11.7 x 10.4','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 584','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Portrait of King Charles II wearing large dark wig. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 15 frontispiece: see #LIB0029.','2009-04-23 10:32:28','2009-05-21 14:31:44'),(764,'ABB0074',19,'George Villiers, Duke of Buckingham','--- \n- \"156\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'13 x 10','1845','1845',35,'vol. 7, facing p. 596','ABD, WS E13, vol. 7','','Portrait of George Villiers, the Second Duke of Buckingham, wearing a large wig, with chain of office and pendent of George and the dragon, \'From an Original Picture\'. ','2009-04-23 10:38:35','2009-05-21 14:31:57'),(765,'ABB0075',21,'Louis XI','--- \n- \"165\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.6 x 9.8','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Portrait of Louis XI in profile, wearing a fur cap and cloak. A facsimile of his signature appears at the bottom. Accompanying quoted text reads: \'His countenance was partly attractive . . . commanding and sinister.\' [6 lines; Ch. 2; EEWN 15, 33.16-21]. Engraved from a \'rare print\' at Versailles. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 16 frontispiece: see #LIB0031.','2009-04-23 10:47:17','2009-05-21 14:32:17'),(766,'ABB0076',21,'Cologne','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 13.8','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 125','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts the city of Cologne, with several boats docked along the River Rhine in the foreground.','2009-04-23 11:00:51','2009-05-21 14:32:30'),(767,'ABB0077',21,'Tours','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,69,3,'8.7 x 13.8','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 133','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts the city of Tours, with boats on the Loire River in the foreground.','2009-04-23 11:04:33','2009-05-21 14:32:50'),(768,'ABB0078',21,'Liege','--- \n- \"354\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 13.8','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 170','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts the city of Liege, with industrial smokestacks visible on the right.','2009-04-23 11:07:11','2009-05-21 14:33:07'),(769,'ABB0079',21,'Peronne','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,12,3,'8.5 x 13.9','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 216','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts the fortified city of Peronne, with mounted soldiers in Napoleonic style uniform in the foreground.','2009-04-23 11:11:10','2009-05-21 14:33:19'),(770,'ABB0080',24,'Abbotsford from North Bank of Tweed','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"244\"\n',128,69,3,'8.8 x 13.7','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 340','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts Abbotsford from the north bank of the River Tweed, with several small figures visible in the foreground, one fishing and another on horseback. (Elizabeth Wemyss Nasmyth took the surname Terry when she married the actor Daniel Terry).','2009-04-23 11:17:11','2009-05-21 14:33:34'),(771,'ABB0081',24,'Peebles and the Tweed, from Neidpath Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"245\"\n- \"354\"\n',129,69,3,'8.8 x 12.9','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 416','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts Peebles and the River Tweed from Neidpath Castle, some of which is visible in the foreground. ','2009-04-23 11:22:07','2009-05-21 14:33:57'),(772,'ABB0082',24,'Abbotsford, from the Haugh / (Sir W. Scott and his dog Bran in the foreground)','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"94\"\n',128,128,3,'8.6 x 13.1','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 451','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts Abbotsford from the Tweed Valley, with a dog and small reclining figure in the foreground, apparently representing Walter Scott and his dog Bran. ','2009-04-23 11:31:00','2009-05-21 14:34:17'),(773,'ABB0083',24,'St Mary\'s Loch','--- \n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',129,69,3,'8.8 x 12.7','1845','1845',35,'vol. 8, facing p. 501','ABD, WS E13, vol. 8','','Depicts St Mary\'s Loch in the Scottish borders, with a few small figures visible in the foreground, the two largest of whom are seated and appear to have a jug in front of them. ','2009-04-23 11:34:48','2009-05-21 14:34:46'),(774,'ABB0084',22,'Dumfries','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,69,3,'8.9 x 14','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts the city of Dumfries in the Scottish borders, with several figures bathing in the River Nith in the foreground, and a rowing boat on the river. ','2009-04-23 11:40:27','2009-05-21 14:44:33'),(775,'ABB0085',22,'Solway Frith','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',94,57,3,'8.4 x 13.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 71','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts a boat on the Solway Firth apparently being unloaded by men with a horse and cart, with the setting sun in the distance. ','2009-04-23 11:43:24','2009-05-21 14:44:55'),(776,'ABB0086',22,'The Mouth of the Annan, and Solway Frith, / Skiddaw in the Distance','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,69,3,'8.8 x 13.7','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 142','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts boats docked along the shore in front of the mouth of the Annan River on the Solway Firth, with the mountain Skiddaw visible across the Firth in the distance. ','2009-04-23 11:50:04','2009-05-21 14:50:17'),(777,'ABB0087',22,'Annan, from the Bridge','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"351\"\n',94,21,3,'9.1 x 14.4','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 188','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts a small waterfall along the River Annan, with a figure fishing in the foreground and cottages with a loading point in the background. ','2009-04-23 11:55:44','2009-05-21 14:50:39'),(778,'ABB0088',4,'Harlech Castle, Merionethshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"244\"\n',130,128,3,'8.5 x 13.1','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','0007369d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52852~100043','Depicts Harlech Castle in Merionethshire, north Wales, as the prototype for the fictional castle \'Garde Doloureuse\', with a stream in the foreground. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 19 frontispiece: see #LIB0037. Also appears as a print in the Corson Collection (P. 2383), with attributed date 1891 (see image).','2009-04-24 08:50:10','2009-09-08 15:28:31'),(779,'ABB0089',4,'Pass of Llanberis, Carnarvonshire','--- \n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n',130,69,3,'13.5 x 8.8','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 359','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts a man carrying a rucksack travelling through the Llanberis Pass in Wales, with mountains in the background.','2009-04-24 08:54:23','2009-05-21 14:52:17'),(780,'ABB0090',4,'Chirk Castle, Denbighshire','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"49\"\n',130,57,3,'8.4 x 13.2','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 383','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts a team of horses pulling tree trunks on a large cart, guarded by a working man outside Chirk Castle in Denbighshire, Wales.','2009-04-24 09:00:10','2009-05-21 14:52:50'),(781,'ABB0091',4,'Pass of Nantfrangon, Carnarvonshire','--- \n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n',130,69,3,'8.8 x 13.5','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 453','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts a traveller resting on a stone in the Pass of Nantfrangon, in Wales.','2009-04-24 09:06:09','2009-05-21 14:53:29'),(782,'ABB0092',26,'The Talisman / The Dead Sea','--- \n- \"352\"\n- \"140\"\n',83,69,3,'8.9 x 14','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts the Dead Sea with two small figures seated at an oasis with palm trees in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text: \'Beneath these sluggish waves . . . a tribute to the ocean.\' [4 lines; Ch. 1]. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 20 frontispiece: see #LIB0039.','2009-04-24 09:11:01','2009-08-16 19:53:59'),(783,'ABB0093',26,'Convent of St. Saba, Wilderness of Engadi, / Holy Land','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"219\"\n',83,57,3,'12.1 x 8.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 554','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts the Convent of St Saba, east of Bethlehem, situated among large cliffs. ','2009-04-24 09:16:39','2009-05-21 14:54:49'),(784,'ABB0094',26,'Convent of the Holy Nativity, Bethlehem','--- \n- \"219\"\n- \"354\"\n',108,42,3,'8.4 x 14.4','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 594','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts the Convent of the Holy Nativity in Bethlehem, and the surrounding countryside, with a party of camels moving down the road toward the town.','2009-04-24 09:21:41','2009-05-21 14:55:39'),(785,'ABB0095',26,'Jerusalem','--- \n- \"72\"\n- \"354\"\n',83,21,3,'9.1 x 14.5','1846','1846',35,'vol. 9, facing p. 657','ABD, WS E13, vol. 9','','Depicts the walled city of Jerusalem and the surrounding countryside.','2009-04-24 09:26:03','2009-05-21 14:56:24'),(786,'ABB0096',30,'Sic Sedebat / Sir Walter Scott Bart. / From the Statue by Greenshields, 1830-31/ In the Possession of Mr Cadell, Edinburgh','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',131,127,3,'13 x 8.5','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Engraving of the statue of Scott sculpted by John Greenshields between 1832 and 1835. Purchased from the artist by Robert Cadell, the statue was originally engraved for the second volume of Lockhart’s ‘Life of Scott’ (1837-38). See Russell, Francis, ‘Portraits of Sir Walter Scott’ (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 74, p. 46. Also appears as a frontispiece to vol. 25 of the Cabinet edition of the Waverley Novels: see #CAB0026.','2009-04-24 09:37:05','2009-05-21 15:01:03'),(787,'ABB0097',29,'Blenheim','--- \n- \"50\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"311\"\n',132,21,3,'9.9 x 14.5','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 27','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts Blenheim Palace from across artificial lake, with a sportsman with gun and two seated women in the foreground.','2009-04-24 09:46:27','2009-05-21 15:01:30'),(788,'ABB0098',29,'Windsor','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"244\"\n',132,57,3,'8.1 x 11.9','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 82','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts Windsor Castle from across the River Thames in the moonlight. ','2009-04-24 09:52:58','2009-05-21 15:01:45'),(789,'ABB0099',29,'Woodstock','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"354\"\n',132,130,3,'8.9 x 13','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 156','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts the village of Woodstock, with a grove and a single female figure in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 09:59:01','2009-05-21 15:02:03'),(790,'ABB0100',29,'Worcester','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',132,69,3,'8.7 x 11.9','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 244','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts the city of Worcester from across a river, with three small figures visible on the far left.','2009-04-24 10:02:39','2009-05-21 15:02:35'),(791,'ABB0101',29,'Oxford','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',132,128,3,'8.4 x 11.7','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 258','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts the town of Oxford from across open countryside, with several small figures and a horse drinking in a stream visible in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 10:06:21','2009-05-21 15:02:53'),(792,'ABB0102',29,'Oliver Cromwell','--- \n- \"308\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.6 x 10','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 311','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Portrait of Oliver Cromwell in military uniform. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 9 in vol. 7,  with more background and an additional figure, a child, adjusting the sash around Cromwell\'s waist. Title/caption also supplies facsimile signature of \'Cromwell\'. Accompanying quoted text: \'He was a man of middle stature . . . depth of thought.\' [Ch. 8]. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 21 frontispiece: see #LIB0041.','2009-04-24 10:10:26','2009-06-25 17:07:22'),(793,'ABB0103',30,'Sir Walter Scott, Bart.','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',25,35,3,'19 x 12','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','A collage of different portraits of Scott, mounted on a bookshelf, with dogs seated and armaments strewn below. At the top centre is the Bath miniature of Scott, painted in 1767 or 1777, attributed to Abraham Daniels (See Russell, Francis, \'Portraits of Sir Walter Scott\' (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 43, pp. 36-37). On the left is the Francis Legatt Chantrey bust of Scott, sculpted 1820 (See Russell, Item 33, pp. 33-34). On the right is the portrait of Scott painted by John Watson Gordon in 1830 (See Russell, Item 55, pp. 40-41), and engraved to adorn the Magnum Opus, Fisher, and Centenary editions of the Waverley Novels (See #MAG0065, #FIS0001, and #CEN0001). In the centre is the full-length portrait of Scott in his study, painted by William Allan in 1831 (See Russell, Item 10, p. 27). ','2009-04-24 10:31:55','2009-06-25 17:07:56'),(794,'ABB0104',13,'Kilchurn Castle, Loch Awe','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"15\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',133,69,3,'8.8 x 13.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing internal title-page','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts Kilchurn Castle from across Loch Awe, with several cattle in the foreground.','2009-04-24 10:47:22','2009-05-21 15:03:57'),(795,'ABB0105',25,'Selkirk','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"354\"\n',129,69,3,'8.8 x 12.8','1846','1846',35,'vol. 10, facing p. 475','ABD, WS E13, vol. 10','','Depicts the town of Selkirk and surrounding countryside. List of Illustrations at front explains \'Sir Walter Scott was Sheriff of the county of this name; and in the Town resided the prototype of Mr Middlemas, of \"The Surgeon\'s Daughter\"\'. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 25 frontispiece: see #LIB0049.','2009-04-24 10:55:02','2009-05-21 15:04:20'),(796,'ABB0106',9,'Perth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"213\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"212\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,21,3,'9.2 x 14.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 14','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts the town of Perth in the distance with the valley of the Tay in front of it. ','2009-04-24 11:02:22','2009-05-21 15:13:20'),(797,'ABB0107',9,'Kinfauns','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,130,3,'8.3 x 13.1','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 61','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts the castle of Kinfauns, situated on a hill, from across a loch, with a few small figures and a herd of sheep visible on the far right. ','2009-04-24 11:07:55','2009-05-21 15:13:52'),(798,'ABB0108',9,'Ben Lawers, from the South Shore of Loch Tay','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,12,3,'8.7 x 13.7','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 117','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts the mountain Ben Lawers, with several small figures crossing a bridge in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 11:13:12','2009-05-21 15:14:10'),(799,'ABB0109',9,'A Street in Perth','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"213\"\n- \"96\"\n',94,128,3,'12.9 x 8.9','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 147','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts a group of Highland soldiers parading in a street in Perth, with a tall house immediately behind them. List of Illustrations at front adds \'the Meal Venell\'.','2009-04-24 11:17:36','2009-06-25 17:08:19'),(800,'ABB0110',9,'Loch Tay / From above the Village of Killin','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,42,3,'8.4 x 14.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 218','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts Loch Tay on the right, with a mountain in the background and several small figures of Highlanders, with a horse and dog to the left.','2009-04-24 11:21:46','2009-05-21 15:15:14'),(801,'ABB0111',2,'Scene from Mount Pilate, Switzerland','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"244\"\n',94,21,3,'13.4 x 9.5','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 308','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts several figures on Mount Pilate in Switzerland, one kneeling before an effigy of the crucified Jesus, with a river and rapids on the left.','2009-04-24 11:33:25','2009-05-21 15:16:11'),(802,'ABB0112',2,'Eppstein Castle, Taunus Mountains','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,130,3,'8.5 x 13.2','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 335','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts Eppstein Castle in the Taunus Mountains in Hessen, Germany, with the figures of a man and woman in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 11:39:23','2009-05-21 15:16:32'),(803,'ABB0113',2,'St Omer and the Cathedral, Strasbourg','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"354\"\n',94,128,3,'12.3 x 8.9','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 452','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts the city of Strasbourg, with the cathedral in the centre and the river in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 11:47:37','2009-05-21 15:17:15'),(804,'ABB0114',2,'Lake of Lucerne','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,21,3,'9.2 x 14.3','1846','1846',35,'vol. 11, facing p. 563','ABD, WS E13, vol. 11','','Depicts Lake Lucerne, with mountains in the background, with passengers crossing a bridge and mechanical works (possibly part of a mine) to the left. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 23 frontispiece: see #LIB0045.','2009-04-24 11:53:34','2009-05-21 15:17:37'),(805,'ABB0115',30,'The Abbotsford Family','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n- \"98\"\n',108,166,3,'9.8 x 12.9','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts Scott and his family, including servants and friends, dressed as country folk. The illustrations list identifies the figures as Sir Walter Scott, Lady Scott, Miss Sophia Scott (afterwards Mrs Lockhart), Miss Anne Scott, Mr Walter Scott (the present Sir Walter Scott, Lieut.-Col. 15th Hussars), Mr Charles Scott, Sir Adam Ferguson, and Tom Purdie. Painted by David Wilkie in 1817. (See the \'Letters of Sir Walter Scott\' ed. H.J.C. Grierson, 12 vols (London: Constable, 1932-37), Vol. 10, pp. 168-69).','2009-04-24 12:03:33','2009-06-25 17:10:03'),(806,'ABB0116',8,'Constantinople','--- \n- \"247\"\n- \"354\"\n',3,69,3,'8.6 x 13.6','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 23','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts the city of Constantinople from across the water, with several figures sitting in Arabian dress in an open space in the foreground. Also appears in the Library Edition of the Waverley Novels as vol. 24 frontispiece: see #LIB0047.','2009-04-24 12:10:04','2009-05-21 15:18:37'),(807,'ABB0117',8,'Ruins of Laodicea','--- \n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',3,69,3,'9 x 13.7','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 57','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts the ruins in Laodicea, Turkey, with a train of camels and a group of travellers in the foreground. ','2009-04-24 12:16:06','2009-05-21 15:19:04'),(808,'ABB0118',8,'The Walls of Constantinople / and distant View of the sea of Marmora','--- \n- \"245\"\n- \"354\"\n',3,130,3,'8.5 x 13.3','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 169','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts the city walls of Constantinople (ruins), with the sea of Marmora and a mountain visible in the distance, and several small figures in Arabian dress visible on the lower right.','2009-04-24 12:20:48','2009-05-21 15:19:38'),(809,'ABB0119',8,'The Bosphorus with the Castles of Europe and Asia','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n',3,128,3,'8.8 x 14','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 227','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts several boats on the Borphorus, with castles or palaces on the left and a mountain in the distance. ','2009-04-24 12:25:00','2009-05-21 15:19:58'),(810,'ABB0120',7,'Remains of the Old Castle of Dougals / Douglas-dale, Lanarkshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"245\"\n',134,130,3,'8.9 x 13.5','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 343','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts the remains of Castle Douglas in the background with a mounted huntsman with dogs in the middle distance, and a horse and cart with woodcutters in the foreground.','2009-04-24 12:32:53','2009-05-21 15:20:39'),(811,'ABB0121',7,'Cadzow Forest, Lanarkshire','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"30\"\n',133,130,3,'8.8 x 13.2','1847','1847',35,'vol. 12, facing p. 375','ABD, WS E13, vol. 12','','Depicts the Cadzow Forest in Lanarkshire, with a herd of grazing cattle among the trees.','2009-04-24 12:36:17','2009-05-21 15:21:21'),(812,'EWA0001',28,'Tully Veolan','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"35\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"97\"\n',123,69,3,'16 x 26.5','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','','Depicts river, woodland, and terracing in the environs of Tully-Veolan, with the house itself as a tower-like structure on a bank in the distance; bonneted figure with two dogs in foreground probably representing Davie Gellatley. Matches #BOR0004, though with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The Garden was laid out . . . covered by ancient trees’ [Ch.9; see EEWN 1, 40.1-14]. The painting is dated 1841 (Corson).','2009-05-04 11:00:59','2009-06-25 17:10:32'),(813,'EWA0002',28,'Waverley and Rose Bradwardine at their Studies','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"12\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"102\"\n',135,52,3,'22.5 x 16','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','','Depicts the seated Rose holding a book in her hand, which Waverley also looks at attentively from behind her chair. Matches #BOR0005, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Miss Bradwardine . . . attached herself to the opportunities . . . in her version of it’ [Ch.14; see EEWN 1, 69-71].','2009-05-04 11:05:45','2009-05-19 13:26:54'),(814,'EWA0003',28,'The Hold of a Highland Robber','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"418\"\n- \"435\"\n- \"360\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"102\"\n',136,136,3,'15.5 x 23.8','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','','Depicts Waverley, Evan Dhu, and Donald Bean Lean seated in the latter’s cave, being served by Alice, and with Highland figures on either side of them, one apparently working at a fire. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Being placed at a convenient distance . . . that you must follow’ [Ch.17; EEWN 1, 86-87].','2009-05-04 11:12:57','2009-05-04 11:18:03'),(815,'EWA0004',28,'Scene at Cairnvreckan','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"353\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"436\"\n- \"437\"\n- \"102\"\n',137,103,3,'16 x 24','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','','Depicts John Mucklewrath the blacksmith being berated by his wife, who has a small child in one arm, while local bystanders look on; another figure, to the right, works on a pistol with pincers in one hand. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Ere Waverley could ask particulars . . . termagant out of his presence’ [Ch. 30; EEWN 1, 160-62].','2009-05-04 11:28:39','2009-08-16 19:55:00'),(816,'EWA0005',28,'Fergus Mac-Ivor introduces Waverley to the Prince','--- \n- \"301\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"262\"\n- \"102\"\n',136,137,3,'15.8 x 23','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','0030059d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58626~100387','Depicts Waverley in Holyrood Palace kneeling before and kissing the hand of Charles Edward, while a bearded Fergus looks on; various other male figures converse behind a table near the back. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The politic Chieftain . . . vindication of his rights’ [Ch. 40; EEWN 1, 206]. ','2009-05-04 11:36:02','2009-09-08 15:30:36'),(817,'EWA0006',28,'Bailie Macwheeble at Breakfast','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"357\"\n',138,136,3,'23 x 17','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','','Depicts Macwheeble at his desk eating porridge and surrounded by legal books and papers. Matches #BOR0012, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Waverley found him in his office . . . with such digestive’ [Ch. 66; EEWN 1, 330.8-15]. Exhibited British Institution 1853, no. 483; and Royal Scottish Academy 1863, no. 132.','2009-05-04 11:38:33','2009-06-25 17:11:52'),(818,'EWA0007',28,'Baron Bradwardine in Hiding','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"11\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"30\"\n',137,130,3,'18 x 25','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 7]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','0030049d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58460~100377','Depicts Bradwardine with head and shoulders projecting out of his narrow cavern hideaway, holding a small book in one hand, and in rocky landscape with fine detail of vegetation there. Accompanying quoted text: ‘And now I hae gotten a house . . . entering his retreat. . . . At a low whistle . . . old badger with his head out of his hole’ [Chs 65 & 67; EEWN 1, 313, 336].','2009-05-04 11:43:03','2009-09-08 15:34:24'),(819,'EWA0008',28,'Waverley’s Last Visit to Flora Mac-Ivor','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"60\"\n- \"102\"\n',135,137,3,'22 x 16','1865','1865',36,'[Plate 8]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.2','0030048d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58443~100376','Depicts Flora seated at a latticed window, white cloth in hand and embroidery materials nearby, and a religious book on a stand, with Waverley emerging from a doorway behind to meet her. Matches #BOR0013, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘When Edward reached . . . attention to neatness’ [Ch. 68; EEWN 1, 343-44]. The painting is dated 1864 (Corson).','2009-05-04 11:45:18','2009-09-08 15:36:00'),(820,'EGM0001',11,'Ellangowan Castle by Moonlight','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"108\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"140\"\n',139,128,3,'16.5 x 26.5','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts old Ellangowan Castle above cliffs, with a bay and expanse of sea, and a boat landed on the shore below. Accompanying quoted text: ‘We have observed . . . intricacies of the woodland scenery’ [Ch. 3; EEWN 2, 17-18].','2009-05-04 11:51:17','2009-05-04 12:49:34'),(821,'EGM0002',11,'The Departure of the Gipsies from Ellangowan','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"116\"\n',140,137,3,'16.5 x 26.5','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts the ejected gypsies with their loaded carts leaving the Ellangowan estate. Matches #BOR0016, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘At length the term-day . . . quorum nor custos rotulorum’ [Ch. 8; EEWN 2, 41-42].','2009-05-04 12:46:54','2009-05-19 13:31:04'),(822,'EGM0003',11,'Dandie Dinmont and his Terriers','--- \n- \"24\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"49\"\n',141,103,3,'21 x 25','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts the store-farmer Dandy Dinmont with his pony Dumple standing in his stable and surrounded by terrier dogs at his feet [Ch. 23]. Matches #BOR0018, but with different engraver. No accompanying text found in copy seen.','2009-05-04 12:52:30','2009-05-19 13:31:22'),(823,'EGM0004',11,'Henry Bertram in the Kaim of Derncleugh','--- \n- \"266\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n',142,138,3,'17.5 x 25','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts Brown hidden in straw, his face and hands only visible, while Meg supposedly sleeps, and a boy and robber-pirates examine his portmanteau of clothes. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The boy stepped to the door . . . they had acquired it’ [Ch. 28; see EEWN 2, 150.6-15].','2009-05-04 13:00:17','2009-06-26 12:24:44'),(824,'EGM0005',11,'The Party at Mannering’s on the Eve of Bertram’s Return','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"83\"\n- \"92\"\n',135,52,3,'21.5 x 16','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts Julia Bertram playing harpsichord at Woodbourne, with Pleydell standing close behind her, and (apparently) Dominie Sampson absorbed in reading at the far side of the room. Matches #BOR0021, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘In marched the lawyer . . . mode of spending the evening’ [Ch. 49; EEWN 2, 296-300]. ','2009-05-04 13:03:05','2009-05-19 13:31:45'),(825,'EGM0006',11,'The Capture of Dirk Hatteraick','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"266\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"42\"\n- \"427\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n',136,136,3,'16 x 25','1866','1866',37,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.7','','Depicts Meg Merrilies, staff in one hand, pointing accusingly at the seated figure of Dirk Hatteraick in his cave, while Bertram, Dinmont, and Hazlewood look on concealed in the shadows. Matches #BOR0023, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Here—beldam—Deyvil’s kind . . . way that it should be’ [Ch. 54; EEWN 2, 331-34].','2009-05-04 13:06:34','2009-06-26 12:25:19'),(826,'EAN0001',3,'The Antiquary and Lovel entering the Sanctum','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"276\"\n',135,136,3,'24.5 x 19.8','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck entering his study (with the face of Lovel faintly visible behind), and confronting (apparently) the chambermaid Jenny Rintherout, who stands holding a broom behind her back. Matches #BOR0024, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Mr. Oldbuck had by this time . . . value your ears’ [Ch. 3; EEWN 3, 20-21].','2009-05-04 13:11:15','2009-05-19 13:32:35'),(827,'EAN0002',3,'Sir Arthur and Miss Wardour setting out along the Shore—Sunset','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',139,128,3,'16.7 x 27','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts seashore, with cliffs behind, and sun setting out to sea, in accordance with the novel though not its source topography; two small figures walking on the sand represent Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour. Matches #BOR0026, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Sir Arthur and Miss Wardour . . . gained upon the sand’ [Ch. 7; see EEWN 3, 54].','2009-05-04 13:14:20','2009-05-19 13:32:54'),(828,'EAN0003',3,'The Storm','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"362\"\n- \"361\"\n',143,130,3,'17 x 25.5','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour with Edie Ochiltree perched on a ledge in the cliffs, with a billowing sea beneath them. Matches #BOR0027, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘It was indeed a dreadful evening . . . progress of the raging element’ [Ch. 7; EEWN 3, 57-58].','2009-05-05 12:21:27','2009-05-19 13:33:14'),(829,'EAN0004',3,'The Interview between Miss Wardour and Edie Ochiltree','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"362\"\n',142,138,3,'25.3 x 18.5','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts Edie Ochiltree sitting on a bench in the courtyard at Monkbarns, with Isabella Wardour leaning out of the parlour window to talk with him. Matches #BOR0028, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The young lady . . . it would be a public loss’ [Ch. 12; EEWN 3, 91-93].','2009-05-05 12:29:01','2009-05-19 13:33:33'),(830,'EAN0005',3,'The Kaim of Kinprunes','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"79\"\n',136,139,3,'17 x 26','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck, cane in hand, pointing out the Kaim as a Roman archaeological site, with the figure of Ochiltree emerging around a mound of earth behind. Matches #BOR0025, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Here, then, let us take our stand . . . I mind the bigging o’t’ [Ch. 4; EEWN 3, 30].','2009-05-05 12:31:50','2009-08-16 19:55:55'),(831,'EAN0006',3,'Sir Arthur and Douterswivel in the Ruins of St. Ruth','--- \n- \"277\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"245\"\n- \"361\"\n',144,128,3,'17 x 24.5','1867','1867',38,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.13','','Depicts Douterswivel kneeling with a pickaxe by his side, and Sir Arthur looking on, at nightfall among sepulchres in the ruined monastery grounds. Accompanying quoted text: ‘With all my heart . . . strong and pungent odour’ [Ch. 21; EEWN 3, 171-72].','2009-05-05 12:39:23','2009-05-05 12:39:23'),(832,'ERR0001',23,'Cattle Lifting','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"142\"\n',141,103,3,'17 x 26','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts herd of long-horned cattle in wild terrain, with two figures to the right of the picture, one with a shepherd’s crook and dog. Accompanying quoted text: ‘About noon, Rob commanded . . . to drive them over the hill’ [Magnum Opus edition, Introduction, vol. 7, lxxiii-lxxiv].','2009-05-05 12:42:09','2009-08-16 19:56:25'),(833,'ERR0002',23,'Diana Vernon and Frank Osbaldistone in the Library—The Glove Scene','--- \n- \"57\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',135,136,3,'18.5 x 24.2','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts Diana Vernon standing in the library at Osbaldistone Hall, and reacting to Frank, who leans toward her over a table holding one of two gloves there. Accompanying quoted text: ‘In removing one or two books . . . my affection called to account?’ [Ch. 17; EEWN 5, 139-40].','2009-05-05 12:45:15','2009-06-26 12:27:33'),(834,'ERR0003',23,'Rob Roy parting the Duellists, Rashleigh and Frank Osbaldistone','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"417\"\n',136,138,3,'17.5 x 25.5','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts Rob Roy restraining Frank, whose sword is drawn and pointing to Rashleigh, who himself is wiping the blade of his sword clean. Accompanying quoted text: ‘He seconded his words . . . lost among the bushes’ [Ch. 25; EEWN 5, 203.7-17].','2009-05-05 12:49:33','2009-05-05 12:49:33'),(835,'ERR0004',23,'The Clachan of Aberfoil and Loch Ard—Morning','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',139,69,3,'17.2 x 25.5','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts Loch Ard, with surrounding hills and a settlement of thatched dwellings in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text: ‘I shall never forget . . . lost had \"gane through the riggin’\"’ [Ch. 30; EEWN 5, 248-49].','2009-05-05 12:52:57','2009-05-05 12:52:57'),(836,'ERR0005',23,'The Escape of Rob Roy at the Ford','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"96\"\n',143,130,3,'17.4 x 25.8','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts troop of horse crossing the river Forth, with (apparently) Rob Roy visible as a small figure tied behind one of the riders on the same horse.  Matches #BOR0042, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘It was while we were . . . that secures Rob Roy!’ [Ch. 33; EEWN 5, 279].','2009-05-05 12:55:42','2009-08-16 19:56:55'),(837,'ERR0006',23,'Loch Lomond','--- \n- \"47\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"54\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"80\"\n',143,139,3,'16 x 27.5','1868','1868',39,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.11','','Depicts small figures of Frank Osbaldistone and Bailie Jarvie riding on a track alongside Loch Lomond. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The Bailie and I mounted . . . Saint Mungo’s steeple again!’ [Ch. 36; EEWN 5, 312.6-18].','2009-05-05 12:58:22','2009-05-05 12:58:22'),(838,'EOM0001',18,'The Graveyard—Old Mortality at the Tombs','--- \n- \"77\"\n- \"21\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n',139,128,3,'17 x 25.6','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts ‘Old Mortality’ restoring a Covenanting gravestone with hammer and chisel, in a deserted graveyard, and his white pony standing nearby. Matches #BOR0045, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘One summer evening . . . by the title of Old Mortality’ [Ch. 1; EEWN 4b, 8-9].','2009-05-05 13:04:27','2009-05-19 13:35:23'),(839,'EOM0002',18,'Lady Margaret Bellenden in Mause Headrigg’s Cottage','--- \n- \"374\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"45\"\n',145,138,1,'18.6 x 24.5','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts Lady Margaret with staff in hand imperiously marching out of Mause’s cottage, as Mause herself stands with back to the fireplace with hands raised. Accompanying quoted text: ‘There was an air of consciousness . . . lifted up her voice and wept aloud’ [Ch. 7; EEWN 4b, 52-55].','2009-05-05 13:10:35','2009-05-05 13:10:35'),(840,'EOM0003',18,'Lady Margaret and Major Bellenden finding Edith on the Battlements','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"393\"\n- \"374\"\n- \"411\"\n- \"14\"\n',135,139,3,'22.6 x 19.2','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts Edith Bellenden observing from the battlements of Tillietudlem, with Lady Margaret and Major Bellenden entering at a doorway. Matches #BOR0044, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Thus speaking . . . had omitted to notice’ [Ch. 11; EEWN 4b, 96.11-23].','2009-05-05 13:15:07','2009-05-19 13:35:50'),(841,'EOM0004',18,'Major Bellenden interceding with Claverhouse for the Life of Henry Morton','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"374\"\n- \"411\"\n- \"10\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"412\"\n- \"146\"\n',146,52,3,'18.4 x 23.6','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts Edith leaning away from the table and listening anxiously to a discussion between Major Bellenden and Claverhouse in an alcove, with Bothwell casually pouring himself a drink at the other side of the table. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Major Bellenden . . . intercession in behalf of Henry Morton’ [Ch. 12; EEWN 4b, 104.14-28].','2009-05-05 13:18:29','2009-06-26 12:28:46'),(842,'EOM0005',18,'The Battle of Bothwell Bridge','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"96\"\n',143,130,3,'17 x 25.5','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts the Battle of Bothwell Bridge, with combat taking place across the Bridge, of which one turret is prominent, and with troops assembling on the near side. Matches #BOR0051, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Morton, who beheld . . . involved in wreaths of smoke’ [Ch. 31; EEWN 4b, 253-54].','2009-05-05 13:23:26','2009-05-19 13:36:15'),(843,'EOM0006',18,'Morton awaiting his Death at the hands of the Cameronians in the Farm-house of Drumshinnel','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"85\"\n',145,136,3,'18 x 25','1869','1869',40,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.8','','Depicts Henry Morton held from behind and being disarmed, facing panel of Covenanters, who sit facing him at a table, two with hands raised. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Peace! said Macbriar . . . the moment of execution’ [Ch. 33; EEWN 4b, 263-64].','2009-05-05 13:25:41','2009-05-05 13:25:41'),(844,'EBL0001',6,'Ravenswood Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n',147,130,3,'16.4 x 27','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Ravenswood Castle as large house with gables and small turrets, with a terrace in front, and surrounded by trees. Matches #BOR0070, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The Castle of Ravenswood, occupying . . . conspicuous part in story’ [Ch. 2; EEWN 7a, 15.13-18].','2009-05-05 13:30:58','2009-06-26 12:29:09'),(845,'EBL0002',6,'Wolf\'s Crag','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"378\"\n',143,130,3,'16.4 x 27.3','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Wolf’s Crag in a storm, perched on cliffs with the sea beneath, and with three riders on a path leading to it. Matches #BOR0071, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘At this moment the cloud . . . red and dazzling light’ [Ch. 9; EEWN 7a, 84.16-25].','2009-05-05 13:33:29','2009-05-19 13:37:04'),(846,'EBL0003',6,'Caleb Balderstone’s Ruse','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"379\"\n- \"438\"\n- \"98\"\n',148,140,3,'17.5 x 24.3','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Caleb deliberately knocking down domestic utensils from a shelf in the kitchen at Wolf’s Crag, with Mysie looking on in consternation. Matches #BOR0072, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The thunderbolt, which had stunned . . . wud wi’ the thunner!’ [Ch. 11; EEWN 7a, 94-95]. Exhibited at Royal Scottish Academy, no. 71, 1876.','2009-05-05 13:42:23','2009-06-26 12:29:53'),(847,'EBL0004',6,'Lucy Ashton at the Fountain','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"352\"\n',135,140,3,'25.3 x 17.7','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Lucy seated by herself at the fountain, wearing a pale dress and tartan shawl. Accompanying quoted text: ‘She sate upon one of the disjointed stones . . . Nymph of the Fountain’ [Ch. 20; EEWN 7a, 155.25-38].','2009-05-05 13:44:57','2009-05-05 13:44:57'),(848,'EBL0005',6,'Lady Ashton’s Interview with her Husband relative to Ravenswood’s quitting the Mansion','--- \n- \"1\"\n- \"3\"\n',149,138,3,'19.2 x 23.7','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Sir William seated at a table attempting to reason with Lady Ashton, who stands in a haughty manner with a letter in her hand. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Think what you are doing . . . answered the lady contemptuously’ [Ch. 22; EEWN 7a, 183.36-39].','2009-05-05 13:47:53','2009-05-05 13:47:53'),(849,'EBL0006',6,'Scene in the Bridal Chamber—Colonel Ashton finding the body of Bucklaw','--- \n- \"439\"\n- \"359\"\n- \"178\"\n',150,139,3,'17 x 25.9','1875','1875',41,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.15','','Depicts Bucklaw on the floor being attended to by a doctor and clergyman, while Colonel Ashton stands in the doorway with sword drawn. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Arrived at the door of the apartment . . . opinion of the surgeon’ [Ch. 34; EEWN 7a, 260.3-18].','2009-05-05 13:54:47','2009-05-05 13:54:47'),(850,'ELM0001',16,'The Pass to the Castle of Darnlinvarach','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"116\"\n',143,130,3,'17.2 x 26.3','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts Lord Menteith’s party (with Menteith and Dalgetty perceptible to the fore) on horseback travelling along a road towards Darnlinvarach Castle, itself on a hill partly obscured by trees. Accompanying quoted text: ‘A hill was now before . . . which we the usual offices’ [Ch. 4; EEWN 7b, 25.23-33].','2009-05-05 14:04:26','2009-08-16 19:57:28'),(851,'ELM0002',16,'The Children of the Mist','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"72\"\n',147,130,3,'17.2 x 26.3','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts wild Highland terrain, with three small figures in a cleft of rock, one with a bow and arrow.  Accompanying quoted text: \'\"The clan,\" said Lord Menteith, . . . the restraint of civilised society\' [Ch. 5; EEWN 7b, 39.14-20].','2009-05-05 14:08:56','2009-05-05 14:08:56'),(852,'ELM0003',16,'Annot Lyle, Lord Menteith, and Allan M‘Aulay','--- \n- \"41\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"59\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"68\"\n- \"74\"\n',150,139,3,'25.5 x 18.2','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts Allan McAulay (in kilt) seated with hand on brow, and listening to Annot Lyle singing to her harp, with Lord Menteith behind, and a large tapestry as background. Matches #BOR0066, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: \'All accompanied their hospitable landlord . . . passed from my spirit\' [Ch. 5; EEWN 7b, 48-50]. Exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 194, 1877.','2009-05-05 14:13:02','2009-08-16 19:58:05'),(853,'ELM0004',16,'Dalgetty and Ranald’s Encounter with the Clergyman in the Chapel at Inverary','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"96\"\n',148,139,3,'18.5 x 25.5','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts Dalgetty (helmet in hand) in the Marquis of Argyle’s chapel at Inverary, bowing before the clergyman, with Ranald MacEagh looking on from a doorway. Matches #BOR0067, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: \'But although the congregation . . . dined in the Castle of Ardenvohr!\' [Ch. 14; EEWN 7b, 111.9-27]. Exhibited at Royal Scottish Academy, no. 338, 1878.','2009-05-05 14:15:53','2009-06-26 12:31:22'),(854,'ELM0005',16,'The Passage of the Army of Montrose during Winter through the Passes of Strathfillan','--- \n- \"47\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"96\"\n',151,138,3,'17.5 x 25.5','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts Montrose’s army advancing through a Highland pass in snow [Ch. 17]. Matches #BOR0064, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Montrose advanced by rapid marches . . . already covered with snow’ [Ch. 17; EEWN 7b, 140-41].','2009-05-05 14:22:33','2009-05-19 13:38:40'),(855,'ELM0006',16,'Inverlochy Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n',133,139,3,'16.5 x 28.5','1877','1877',42,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.5','','Depicts the Castle of Inverlochy, with the lake in front and empty rowing boat in the foreground, and mountains behind and in the distance. Matches #BOR0068, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘While Montrose executed . . . where the Lochy joins Loch-Eil’ [Ch. 18; EEWN 7b, 147.19-26].','2009-05-05 14:24:38','2009-05-19 13:39:02'),(856,'EHM0001',12,'The Laird of Dumbiedykes in Jeanie’s Cottage','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"204\"\n',152,140,3,'17.8 x 24.8','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts Dumbiedikes standing pipe in hand awkwardly watching Jeanie as she attends to domestic work (apparently tightening the cords on a case) in preparation for the removal from their original family cottage. Matches #BOR0056, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘When Davie Deans intimated . . . heard to ejaculate, \"Hegh, sirs!\' [Ch. 9; EEWN 6, 82.11-17].','2009-05-05 14:28:39','2009-05-19 13:40:06'),(857,'EHM0002',12,'Muschat’s Cairn','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',143,130,3,'16.5 x 25.8','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts nightfall scene, including St Anthony\'s Chapel (in ruins), among Salisbury Crags, with rough boulders below and Arthur’s Seat in the background; the cairn and two small figures are barely perceptible in the left foreground. Matches #BOR0057, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Jeanie Deans . . . at length approached the place . . . your burial-place!’ [Ch. 15; EEWN 6, 136-37].','2009-05-05 14:31:26','2009-05-19 13:41:10'),(858,'EHM0003',12,'Madge Wildfire before Bailie Middleburgh','--- \n- \"188\"\n- \"440\"\n- \"419\"\n- \"104\"\n',150,138,3,'16 x 27.5','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts Madge Wildfire bowing before the magistrate Mr Middleburgh, who is seated at a table with colleagues, and has a guard behind him, as her mother Meg Murdockson stands in the half-open doorway. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Mr Middleburgh had just taken his seat . . . at length approached the place . . . that’s Satan, ye ken, sirs’ [Ch. 18; EEWN 6, 165-70].','2009-05-05 14:35:21','2009-05-05 14:35:21'),(859,'EHM0004',12,'The Interview between Effie Deans and her Sister in Prison','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"280\"\n',135,141,3,'21.3 x 28','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts Jeanie Deans seated on a bed holding hands with and comforting her sister Effie in confinement in the Edinburgh Tolbooth, with Ratcliffe the jailer looking on in the background and dangling the keys. Matches #BOR0058, but with different engraver. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Jeanie Deans was admitted . . . at length approached the place . . . touch of human sympathy’ [Ch. 21; EEWN 6, 184-85]. Painting exhibited at Royal Scottish Academy in 1873, inscribed \"RH 1872\".','2009-05-05 14:39:52','2009-06-14 09:01:31'),(860,'EHM0005',12,'The View from Richmond Hill','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"244\"\n',143,128,3,'17 x 25.5','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts the Thames and open woodlands as viewed from Richmond Hill, where a carriage stands, outside of which tiny figures of Jeanie Deans and the Duke of Argyle viewing the prospect. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The carriage rolled rapidly . . . at length approached the place . . . unrivalled landscape that it presented’  [Ch. 37; EEWN 6, 329.15-22].','2009-05-05 14:44:41','2009-06-26 12:31:48'),(861,'EHM0006',12,'The Death of Sir George Staunton','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"441\"\n- \"269\"\n- \"229\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"443\"\n- \"442\"\n',136,139,3,'17.2 x 25.7','1873','1873',43,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.19','','Depicts Duncan Knock running through Donacha Dhu with his sword, with the prostrate body of Sir George Staunton below them, while the figure presumably representing the Whistler is taken captive in the background. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Duncan and his party . . . at length approached the place . . . but life had wholly left him’ [Ch. 53; EEWN 6, 461-62].','2009-05-05 14:54:33','2009-05-05 14:55:52'),(862,'BOR0001',28,'[none]','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"29\"\n',1,1,2,'5 x 6','1892','1892',44,'verso of half-title (in all vols)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Abbotsford, with River Tweed and small figure of fisherman in foreground.','2009-05-06 12:39:27','2009-08-16 19:59:50'),(863,'BOR0002',28,'Portrait of Sir Walter Scott','--- \n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',116,142,2,'13.5 x 9.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Based on the Raeburn painting of 1823. See Russell, Francis, \'Portraits of Sir Walter Scott\' (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 163, pp. 74-75. Half-length portrait of Scott in full face, wearing jacket and waistcoat with cravat at neck.','2009-05-06 12:46:37','2009-06-29 12:01:26'),(864,'BOR0003',28,'Abbotsford from the Tweed','--- \n- \"248\"\n- \"191\"\n- \"244\"\n',153,143,2,'13.7 x 9.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1, facing p. lxxii','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Abbotsford, with River Tweed and large tree trunk prominent in foreground. List of Illustrations states \'Drawn by D. Herdman\', but cannot be identified. Possibly a mistake for Robert Herdman. ','2009-05-06 12:56:46','2009-06-26 12:36:28'),(865,'BOR0004',28,'Tully-Veolan','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"35\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"97\"\n',123,142,2,'8.5 x 13','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1, facing p. 64','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts river, woodland, and terracing in the environs of Tully-Veolan, with the house itself as a tower-like structure on a bank in the distance; bonneted figure with two dogs in foreground probably representing Davie Gellatley [Ch. 9]. Matches #EWA0001, though with different engraver. The painting is dated 1841 (Corson).','2009-05-06 12:59:40','2009-05-19 13:43:53'),(866,'BOR0005',28,'Waverley and Rose Bradwardine','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"12\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"102\"\n',135,144,2,'13.5 x 8','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1, facing p. 120','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the seated Rose holding a book in her hand, which Waverley also looks at attentively from behind her chair [Ch. 14]. Matches #EWA0002, but with different engraver.','2009-05-06 13:04:48','2009-05-19 13:45:00'),(867,'BOR0006',28,'Hold of a Highland Robber','--- \n- \"314\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"418\"\n- \"435\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"102\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1, facing p. 152','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Donald Bean Lean in his cave bowing in welcome to Waverley, with his daughter Alice prominent amongst other inhabitants, and the carcases of cattle hanging to the left [Ch. 17].','2009-05-06 13:31:56','2009-05-06 13:31:56'),(868,'BOR0007',28,'Flora Mac-Ivor at the Waterfall','--- \n- \"36\"\n- \"242\"\n- \"41\"\n- \"60\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"351\"\n- \"102\"\n',155,145,3,'13,7 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 1, facing p. 208','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Flora MacIvor reclining on a rock with her harp, as Waverley approaches from behind in the middle distance, with large waterfall in the background [Ch. 22].','2009-05-06 13:34:46','2009-05-06 13:34:46'),(869,'BOR0008',28,'Prince Charles Edward in Shelter','--- \n- \"301\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"46\"\n',135,146,2,'9.5 x 14.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Charles Edward in a humble Highland dwelling, with man kneeling at his feet, and other family members looking on. Bears no specific relation to incidents in the story. Probably based on the artist\'s \'Charles Edward seeking shelter in the home of an adherent\', exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 426, 1879, and Royal Academy, no. 442, 1880.','2009-05-06 13:38:16','2009-06-26 12:38:09'),(870,'BOR0009',28,'Bonnie Prince Charlie','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"53\"\n- \"301\"\n',156,146,2,'13 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Charles Edward at Holyrood Palace, in combination of courtly and Highland dress, backed by two Highland gentlemen [Ch. 40]. Exhibited Royal Academy, no. 89, 1892.','2009-05-06 13:45:06','2009-05-06 13:45:06'),(871,'BOR0010',28,'Colonel Gardiner','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"431\"\n- \"96\"\n',157,146,2,'13.5 x 9.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Colonel Gardiner, with hand on sword in scabbard, and signs of a military engagement involving artillery in the background, matching his actions at the battle of Prestonpans [Ch. 47].','2009-05-06 13:48:22','2009-05-06 13:49:01'),(872,'BOR0011',28,'Disbanded','--- \n- \"46\"\n',156,147,2,'13.5 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts solitary Highlander, carrying weapons and large knapsack, apparently after disbandment. Corson dates painting 1877; exhibited at Royal Scottish Academy  no. 312, 1878.','2009-05-06 13:52:11','2009-05-06 13:52:11'),(873,'BOR0012',28,'Baillie McWheeble','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"357\"\n',138,148,2,'13.5 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2, facing p. 306','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Macwheeble at his desk eating porridge surrounded by legal books and papers [Ch. 66]. Matches #EWA0006, but with different engraver. Exhibited at British Institution, no. 483, 1853; and Royal Scottish Academy, no. 132, 1863.','2009-05-06 13:57:23','2009-06-26 12:39:41'),(874,'BOR0013',28,'Waverley’s Last Visit to Flora Mac-Ivor','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"60\"\n- \"102\"\n',135,149,2,'13.5 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 2, facing p. 328','NLS, Lit.S.22','0030048d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58443~100376','Depicts Flora seated at a latticed window, white cloth in hand and embroidery materials nearby, and a religious book on a stand, with Waverley emerging from a doorway behind to meet her [Ch. 68]. Matches #EWA0008, but with different engraver: see image. The painting is dated 1864 (Corson).','2009-05-06 14:03:09','2009-09-08 15:38:28'),(875,'BOR0014',11,'Meg Merrilies','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"263\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"264\"\n',158,149,2,'14 x 9.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Godfrey Bertram and groom on horseback, with Meg Merrilies above them on bank, issuing her curse, and holding sapling bough in raised right hand [Ch. 8].','2009-05-06 14:09:56','2009-05-06 14:09:56'),(876,'BOR0015',11,'Ellangowan Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"108\"\n- \"245\"\n- \"140\"\n',139,150,2,'8.5 x 13.2','1892','1892',44,'vol. 3, facing p. 2','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the old Ellangowan Castle on promontory, with a small boat landed in the bay beneath, and open estuary in the middle distance [Ch. 4].','2009-05-06 14:13:35','2009-05-06 14:13:35'),(877,'BOR0016',11,'Departure of the Gipsies','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"116\"\n',140,151,2,'9 x 14.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 3, facing p. 70','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the ejected gypsies with their loaded carts leaving the Ellangowan estate [Ch. 8]. Matches #EGM0002, but with different engraver.','2009-05-06 14:17:13','2009-05-19 13:47:35'),(878,'BOR0017',11,'Dominie Sampson in the Library','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"92\"\n',158,149,2,'13.5 x 9','1892','1892',44,'vol. 3, facing p. 180','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Dominie Sampson in library at Woodbourne, arms upraised in wonderment, with the figure (apparently) of Julia Mannering observing in the rear from behind a half-opened door [Ch. 20].','2009-05-06 14:19:55','2009-05-06 14:19:55'),(879,'BOR0018',11,'Dandie Dinmont','--- \n- \"24\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"82\"\n- \"49\"\n',141,146,2,'9 x 14.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 3, facing p. 212','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the store-farmer Dandy Dinmont with his pony Dumple standing in his stable and surrounded by terrier dogs at his feet [Ch. 23]. Matches #EGM0003, but with different engraver.','2009-05-06 14:22:26','2009-05-19 13:48:00'),(880,'BOR0019',11,'Pleydell as King','--- \n- \"24\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"63\"\n- \"83\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.2','1892','1892',44,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Pleydell at the game of High-Jinks in an Edinburgh tavern, as observed by Dinmont and Colonel Mannering on entering the room [Ch. 36].','2009-05-06 14:25:52','2009-05-06 14:25:52'),(881,'BOR0020',11,'The Solway Firth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',159,152,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1892','1892',44,'vol. 4, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts small boat with single sail and three occupants struggling through a heavy sea, matching Henry Bertram’s passage on the Solway Firth [Ch. 40].','2009-05-07 13:33:20','2009-05-07 13:33:20'),(882,'BOR0021',11,'The Party at Colonel Mannering’s','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"83\"\n- \"92\"\n',135,164,2,'13.5 x 9.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 4, facing p. 234','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julia Bertram playing harpsichord at Woodbourne, with Pleydell standing close behind her, and (apparently) Dominie Sampson absorbed in reading at the far side of the room [Ch. 49]. Matches #EGM0005, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 13:35:46','2009-06-26 12:40:16'),(883,'BOR0022',11,'Meg at Gibbie’s Knowe','--- \n- \"358\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"252\"\n- \"422\"\n- \"265\"\n',158,149,2,'9 x 14.2','1892','1892',44,'vol. 4, facing p. 272','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Meg Merrilies, staff in hand, signalling Bertram and Dinmont to follow her, with the decorously-dressed Lucy Bertram and Julia Mannering behind the men [Ch. 53].','2009-05-07 13:39:54','2009-05-07 13:39:54'),(884,'BOR0023',11,'Capture of Dirk Hatteraick','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"266\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"24\"\n- \"42\"\n- \"427\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n',136,154,2,'9 x 14.5','1892','1892',44,'vol. 4, facing p. 288','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Meg Merrilies, staff in one hand, pointing accusingly at the seated figure of Dirk Hatteraick in his cave, while Bertram, Dinmont, and Hazlewood look on concealed in the shadows [Ch. 54]. Matches #EGM0006, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 13:46:28','2009-05-19 13:49:13'),(885,'BOR0024',3,'The Antiquary’s Sanctum','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"276\"\n',135,144,2,'13.7 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck entering his study, and confronting (apparently) the chambermaid Jenny Rintherout, who stands holding a broom behind her back [Ch. 3]. Matches #EAN0001, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 13:50:57','2009-05-19 13:49:29'),(886,'BOR0025',3,'The Kaim of Kinprunes','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"79\"\n',136,155,2,'8.5 x 13','1893','1893',44,'vol. 5, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck, cane in hand, pointing out the Kaim as a Roman archaeological site, with the figure of Ochiltree emerging around a mound of earth behind [Ch. 4]. Matches #EAN0005, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 13:55:22','2009-06-26 12:40:42'),(887,'BOR0026',3,'On the Shore---Sunset','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n',139,150,2,'8.5 x 13.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 5, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts seashore, with cliffs behind, and sun setting out to sea, in accordance with the novel though not its source topography; two small figures walking on the sand represent Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour [Ch. 7]. Matches #EAN0002, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 14:05:23','2009-05-19 13:50:02'),(888,'BOR0027',3,'The Storm','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"362\"\n- \"361\"\n',143,151,2,'9.2 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 5, facing p. 96','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Arthur and Isabella Wardour with Edie Ochiltree perched on a ledge in the cliffs, with a billowing sea beneath them [Ch. 7]. Matches #EAN0003, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 14:14:26','2009-05-19 13:50:18'),(889,'BOR0028',3,'Edie Ochiltree','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"362\"\n',142,149,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 5, facing p. 148','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edie Ochiltree sitting on a bench in the courtyard at Monkbarns, with Isabella Wardour leaning out of the parlour window to talk with him [Ch. 12]. Matches #EAN0004, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 14:16:36','2009-05-19 13:50:37'),(890,'BOR0029',3,'Edie in Prison','--- \n- \"7\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"79\"\n- \"85\"\n',160,156,2,'13.3 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edie Ochiltree on a bench in prison, with Oldbuck appearing round the corner to visit him [Ch. 38].','2009-05-07 14:23:08','2009-05-07 14:23:08'),(891,'BOR0030',3,'Funeral of the Countess','--- \n- \"444\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"88\"\n',160,157,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 6, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts procession at the night time burial of Countess of Glenallan in the ruins of St Ruth’s priory [Ch. 25].','2009-05-07 14:28:46','2009-05-07 14:28:46'),(892,'BOR0031',3,'Lord Glenallan and Elspeth','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"445\"\n- \"366\"\n',160,154,2,'13.5 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 6, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Elspeth kneeling with hands clasped at the feet of the Earl of Glenallan, who is about to leave through the door of her cottage, having heard her revelations [Ch. 33].','2009-05-07 14:32:47','2009-05-07 14:32:47'),(893,'BOR0032',3,'Breakfast at Monkbarns','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"445\"\n- \"367\"\n- \"66\"\n- \"78\"\n- \"79\"\n',160,158,2,'13.3 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 6, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts family breakfast at Monkbarns, with Jonathan Oldbuck engaging the Earl of Glenallan in conversation [Ch. 35].','2009-05-07 14:40:02','2009-05-07 14:40:02'),(894,'BOR0033',3,'The Antiquary Arming','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"225\"\n- \"78\"\n- \"79\"\n',160,164,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 6, facing p. 294','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck at his house, sword in hand and staring through the window at a beacon, preparing himself at the news of French invasion, with his sister other females of the house assisting [Ch. 45].','2009-05-07 14:43:41','2009-05-12 08:41:19'),(895,'BOR0034',23,'Di Vernon and Frank','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"81\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Diana Vernon exuberantly hunting, with whip raised in hand, and passing Frank, also on horseback, with Osbaldistone Hall in the background. [Ch. 5]','2009-05-07 14:48:10','2009-06-26 12:41:34'),(896,'BOR0035',23,'Stealing Cattle','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"142\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14','1893','1893',44,'vol. 7, facing p. lxviii','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts long-horned cattle in rugged terrain, assailed by Highland rustlers, the first mounted and with whip. Positioned alongside Magnum Introduction, describing depredations of Rob Roy MacGregor.','2009-05-07 14:51:07','2009-06-26 12:42:01'),(897,'BOR0036',23,'Di Vernon at Judge Inglewood\'s','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"368\"\n- \"369\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"370\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14','1893','1893',44,'vol. 7, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Diana Vernon, in riding outfit, entering Squire Inglewood’s apartment, where a meal has been laid, and where Frank Osbaldistone is appealing for justice [Ch. 8].','2009-05-07 14:54:17','2009-05-07 14:54:17'),(898,'BOR0037',23,'Frank and Fairservice','--- \n- \"423\"\n- \"34\"\n- \"81\"\n- \"80\"\n',155,145,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 7, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone in conversation with Andrew Fairservice, while the latter tends the garden at Osbaldistone Hall, the house visible to the right [Ch. 14].','2009-05-07 14:57:16','2009-05-07 14:57:16'),(899,'BOR0038',23,'Di Vernon and Frank---The Glove Scene','--- \n- \"57\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',155,145,2,'14 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 7, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Diana Vernon holding out a man’s glove to the suspicious-looking Frank, in the library at Osbaldistone Hall [Ch.17].','2009-05-07 15:04:09','2009-05-07 15:04:09'),(900,'BOR0039',23,'Parting of Di Vernon and Frank','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n',155,145,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Diana Vernon on horseback, placing her hand on the shoulder of the standing Frank, while her fellow-rider waits in the near distance [Ch. 33].','2009-05-07 15:06:19','2009-05-07 15:06:19'),(901,'BOR0040',23,'Rob Roy in the Tolbooth','--- \n- \"54\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"85\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 8, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Nicol Jarvie confronting Rob Roy in the Glasgow Tolbooth [Ch. 23].','2009-05-07 15:08:29','2009-05-07 15:08:29'),(902,'BOR0041',23,'Helen MacGregor','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"371\"\n',155,145,2,'13.5 x 9 ','1893','1893',44,'vol. 8, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts commanding figure of Helen MacGregor on the summit of rock, claymore pointing downwards in one hand, and left arm raised [Ch. 30].','2009-05-07 15:09:58','2009-05-07 15:09:58'),(903,'BOR0042',23,'The Escape of Rob Roy','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"96\"\n',143,151,2,'8.5 x 13 ','1893','1893',44,'vol. 8, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts troop of horse crossing the river Forth, with (apparently) Rob Roy visible as a small figure tied behind one of the riders on the same horse [Ch. 33]. Matches #ERR0005, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 15:12:11','2009-08-16 20:00:19'),(904,'BOR0043',23,'Death of Rashleigh','--- \n- \"80\"\n- \"417\"\n- \"178\"\n',155,145,2,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 8, facing p. 336','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the mortally wounded Rashleigh in a chair in the Stone Hall at Osbaldistone Hall, with Frank Osbaldistone standing over him. [Ch. 39].','2009-05-07 15:16:43','2009-05-07 15:16:43'),(905,'BOR0044',18,'Edith on the Battlements','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"393\"\n- \"374\"\n- \"411\"\n- \"14\"\n',135,142,2,'13.2 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edith Bellenden observing from the battlements of Tillietudlem, with Lady Margaret and Major Bellenden entering at a doorway [Ch. 11]. Matches #EOM0003, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 15:18:44','2009-05-19 13:53:16'),(906,'BOR0045',18,'The Graveyard','--- \n- \"77\"\n- \"21\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n',139,142,2,'8.6 x 12.9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 9, facing p. 6','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts ‘Old Mortality’ restoring a Covenanting gravestone with hammer and chisel, in a deserted graveyard, and his white pony standing nearby [Ch. 1]. Matches #EOM0001, but with different engraver.','2009-05-07 15:20:48','2009-05-19 13:53:38'),(907,'BOR0046',18,'Tillietudlem Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"255\"\n',161,143,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 9, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts view of turreted Tillietudlem as approached by a road, along which a single rider approaching it is visible. Apparently not incident-related.','2009-05-07 15:23:15','2009-05-07 15:23:15'),(908,'BOR0047',18,'Claverhouse','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"412\"\n- \"84\"\n',162,147,2,'13.4 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 9, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Claverhouse, with one hand on sword and the other with forefinger pointing downwards, in wainscoted room with a portrait on the wall. Apparently relating to the description of him at Tillietudlem [Ch. 12].','2009-05-07 15:26:24','2009-05-07 15:26:24'),(909,'BOR0048',18,'The Duel','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"5\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"10\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n',163,160,2,'13.2 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 9, facing p. 228','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Bothwell and Balfour in combat at Battle of Drumclog [Ch. 16].','2009-05-07 15:28:25','2009-05-07 15:29:58'),(910,'BOR0049',18,'Morton Waiting Death','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"70\"\n',164,157,2,'13.7 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Morton (standing) waiting for accomplishment of his death sentence, with his accusing Covenanters seated around a table [Ch. 33].','2009-05-07 15:32:32','2009-05-07 15:32:32'),(911,'BOR0050',18,'Jenny Dennison','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"375\"\n- \"98\"\n',164,156,2,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 10, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jenny Dennison about to throw scalding brose from a pot during the attempted insurgency at Tillietudlem [Ch. 25].','2009-05-08 11:37:33','2009-05-08 11:37:33'),(912,'BOR0051',18,'Bothwell Bridge','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"96\"\n',143,151,2,'9 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 10, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Battle of Bothwell Bridge, with combat taking place across the Bridge, of which one turret is prominent, and with troops assembling on the near side [Ch. 31]. Matches #EOM0005, but with different engraver.','2009-05-08 11:42:34','2009-05-19 13:54:20'),(913,'BOR0052',18,'Edith and the Apparition','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"393\"\n- \"446\"\n',164,161,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 10, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edith Bellenden having fainted at the sight of Henry Morton at a latticed window, with Lady Emily attending to her and looking backwards at the departing figure of Morton (just visible) [Ch. 38].','2009-05-08 11:46:39','2009-05-08 11:46:39'),(914,'BOR0053',18,'At the Black Linn','--- \n- \"5\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"70\"\n',164,142,2,'13.1 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 10, facing p. 272','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Balfour of Burley in the cavern at the Black Linn about to burn parchment in the fire, with Morton attempting to prevent him [Ch. 43].','2009-05-08 11:48:28','2009-05-08 11:48:28'),(915,'BOR0054',12,'Effie and Geordie','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"279\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"443\"\n',165,162,2,'13.2 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Effie leaning against a wall, alongside a collie dog, in conversation with Robertson (with smart bonnet, cravat, and tunic) on the other side of the wall. Apparently not incident-specific, but probably stemming from Jeanie Deans\'s question to her sister: \'And wha was that parted wi\' ye at the stile\' [Ch. 10]. Painted in 1877.','2009-05-08 11:56:16','2009-06-26 12:43:43'),(916,'BOR0055',12,'The Porteous Mob','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"447\"\n- \"222\"\n',166,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 11, facing p. 96','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Porteous being carried along through the street towards his execution by an armed crowd, some carrying torches [Ch. 7].','2009-05-08 12:02:57','2009-05-19 13:55:19'),(917,'BOR0056',12,'The Laird in Jeanie’s Cottage','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"204\"\n',152,146,2,'9.4 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 11, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Dumbiedikes standing pipe in hand awkwardly watching Jeanie as she attends to domestic work (apparently tightening the cords on a case) in preparation for the removal from their original family cottage [Ch. 9]. Matches #EHM0001, but with different engraver.','2009-05-08 12:05:26','2009-05-19 13:55:37'),(918,'BOR0057',12,'Muschat’s Cairn','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n',143,144,2,'9.3 x 14.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 11, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts nightfall scene, including St Anthony\'s Chapel (in ruins), among Salisbury Crags, with rough boulders below and Arthur’s Seat in the background; the cairn and two small figures are barely perceptible in the left foreground [Ch. 15]. Matches #EHM0002, but with different engraver.','2009-05-08 12:10:39','2009-05-19 13:55:53'),(919,'BOR0058',12,'Jeanie and Effie','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"280\"\n',135,146,2,'9.3 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 11, facing p. 304','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jeanie Deans seated on a bed holding hands with and comforting her sister Effie in confinement in the Edinburgh Tolbooth, with Ratcliffe the jailer looking on in the background and dangling the keys [Ch. 21]. Matches #EHM0004, but with different engraver. Painting exhibited at Royal Scottish Academy in 1873, inscribed \"RH 1872\".','2009-05-08 12:12:46','2009-06-14 09:02:49'),(920,'BOR0059',12,'Jeanie on the Road','--- \n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n',166,157,2,'13.7 x 9.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jeanie Deans walking along the road wearing a full plaid, and small pack in hand, with Dumbiedikes, roughly dressed, on his horse having just caught up with her [Ch. 27].','2009-05-08 12:14:49','2009-05-08 12:14:49'),(921,'BOR0060',12,'The Outlaw\'s Hut','--- \n- \"23\"\n- \"448\"\n- \"419\"\n- \"142\"\n',166,161,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 12, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jeanie Deans in sleeping position on straw in hovel, with woman holding a candle and looking at her, and man in tunic-like coat looking on [Ch. 31].','2009-05-08 12:17:31','2009-05-08 12:17:31'),(922,'BOR0061',12,'Madge and Jeanie','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"104\"\n',166,142,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 12, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Madge Wildfire wearing bonnet with feather, and stick in hand, leading the abashed Jeanie Deans along the centre aisle of the church at Willingham [Ch. 32].','2009-05-08 12:19:05','2009-05-08 12:19:05'),(923,'BOR0062',12,'Jeanie and Queen Caroline','--- \n- \"283\"\n- \"281\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"246\"\n- \"146\"\n',166,147,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 12, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jeanie Deans, with plaid over shoulders, petitioning Queen Caroline, in wooded parkland, with Duke of Argyle looking on, and largely-obscured figure behind the Queen, probably representing Lady Suffolk [Ch. 38].','2009-05-08 12:21:33','2009-05-08 12:21:33'),(924,'BOR0063',12,'Death of Sir George Staunton','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"441\"\n- \"269\"\n- \"229\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"443\"\n- \"442\"\n',166,163,2,'13.6 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 12, facing p. 402','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Duncan Knock shooting at and killing Donacha Dhu, while the figure of the Whistler in tartan plaid crouches knife in hand over the body of Sir George Staunton [Ch. 53].','2009-05-08 12:25:36','2009-05-08 12:25:36'),(925,'BOR0064',16,'Passage of the Army','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"96\"\n',151,142,2,'9.2 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 13 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Montrose’s army advancing through a Highland pass in snow [Ch. 17]. Matches #ELM0005, but with different engraver.','2009-05-08 12:27:06','2009-05-19 13:56:59'),(926,'BOR0065',16,'Darnlinvarach Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"116\"\n',167,164,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 13, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Lord Menteith’s party on horseback travelling along a road towards Darnlinvarach Castle, itself on a hill partly obscured by trees [Ch. 4]. ','2009-05-08 12:33:32','2009-06-26 12:44:14'),(927,'BOR0066',16,'Annot Lyle','--- \n- \"41\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"59\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"68\"\n- \"74\"\n',150,164,2,'13.6 x 9.1','1893','1893',44,'vol. 13, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Allan McAulay (in kilt) seated with hand on brow listening to Annot Lyle singing to her harp, with Lord Menteith behind, and a large tapestry as background [Ch.6]. Matches #ELM0003, but with different engraver. Exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 194, 1877.','2009-05-09 13:32:43','2009-05-19 13:57:22'),(928,'BOR0067',16,'Dalgetty in the Chapel','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"96\"\n',148,157,2,'9.2 x 13.6','1893','1893',44,'vol. 13, facing p. 178','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Dalgetty (helmet in hand) in the Marquis of Argyle’s chapel at Inverary, bowing before the clergyman, with Ranald MacEagh looking on from a doorway [Ch. 14] Matches #ELM0004, but with different engraver. Exhibited at painting at Royal Scottish Academy, no. 338, 1878.','2009-05-09 13:35:38','2009-08-16 20:02:45'),(929,'BOR0068',16,'Inverlochy Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"47\"\n- \"124\"\n',133,158,2,'9.2 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 13, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Castle of Inverlochy, with the lake in front and empty rowing boat in the foreground, and mountains behind and in the distance [Ch. 18]. Matches #ELM0006, but with different engraver.','2009-05-09 13:38:24','2009-05-19 13:57:59'),(930,'BOR0069',6,'Lucy and the Master','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"86\"\n- \"244\"\n',165,146,2,'13.4 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 14 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood, with military-style boots, cape, and hat with large brim, arms entwined with those of Lucy Ashton by the fountain, as they prepare to part [Ch. 20]. Painted 1878.','2009-05-09 13:40:45','2009-05-09 13:41:02'),(931,'BOR0070',6,'Ravenswood Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n',147,146,2,'9 x 14.8','1893','1893',44,'vol. 14, facing p. 22','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ravenswood as large house with gables and small turrets, with a terrace in front, and surrounded by trees. Matches #EBL0001, but with different engraver.','2009-05-09 13:42:58','2009-05-19 13:58:19'),(932,'BOR0071',6,'Wolf\'s Crag','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"378\"\n',143,146,2,'9.1 x 14.8','1893','1893',44,'vol. 14, facing p. 96','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Wolf’s Crag in a storm, perched on cliffs with the sea beneath, and with three riders on a path leading to it. Matches #EBL0002, but with different engraver.','2009-05-09 13:44:50','2009-05-19 13:58:33'),(933,'BOR0072',6,'Caleb Balderstone’s Ruse','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"379\"\n- \"438\"\n- \"98\"\n',148,146,2,'9.5 x 14.7','1893','1893',44,'vol. 14, facing p. 160','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Caleb deliberately knocking down domestic utensils from a shelf in the kitchen at Wolf’s Crag, with Mysie looking on in consternation [Ch. 11]. Matches #EBL0003, but with different engraver. Exhibited as painting at Royal Scottish Academy, no. 71, 1876.','2009-05-09 13:47:24','2009-06-26 12:47:28'),(934,'BOR0073',6,'Henry Ashton and the Master','--- \n- \"312\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"86\"\n',157,146,2,'13.9 x 9.6','1893','1893',44,'vol. 14, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Henry Ashton disturbed by the appearance of Ravenswood at Ravenswood Castle, as the latter stands before a large case of books, and seeking the protection of his father who places a hand round his shoulder [Ch. 18].','2009-05-09 13:50:46','2009-06-26 12:48:01'),(935,'BOR0074',6,'Lucy\'s Madness','--- \n- \"2\"\n',157,146,2,'14 x 9.6','1893','1893',44,'vol. 15 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Lucy cowering in the corner of a chimney piece in the bridal chamber on her wedding night [Ch. 34].','2009-05-09 13:51:54','2009-05-09 13:51:54'),(936,'BOR0075',6,'The Apparition','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"39\"\n- \"86\"\n',157,146,2,'13.9 x 9.1','1893','1893',44,'vol. 15, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood at the fountain viewing the ghost-like figure of Blind Alice [Ch. 23].','2009-05-09 13:54:35','2009-05-09 13:54:35'),(937,'BOR0076',6,'The Broken Contract','--- \n- \"1\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"64\"\n- \"86\"\n',157,146,2,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 15, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood standing before Lucy Ashton and her mother at the signing of the marriage contract, and holding out a letter in his hand [Ch. 33].','2009-05-09 13:57:07','2009-05-09 13:57:07'),(938,'BOR0077',5,'The Interview','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"268\"\n',157,146,2,'9.3 x 14.7','1893','1893',44,'vol. 15, facing p. 352','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Isabel Vere in the Black Dwarf’s hut, with the latter setting the lamp on the table [Ch. 16].','2009-05-09 13:59:30','2009-05-09 13:59:30'),(940,'BOR0078',5,'The Marriage','--- \n- \"38\"\n- \"450\"\n- \"64\"\n- \"268\"\n- \"449\"\n',157,146,2,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',4