eral armed Highlanders toward the edge of a cliff, with the commanding figure of Helen MacGregor above them sword pointed downwards in the direction of the loch [Ch. 31].','2009-05-11 12:59:10','2009-05-11 12:59:10'),(979,'DRY0039',23,'Rob Roy Welcomed by his Clan','--- \n- \"46\"\n- \"61\"\n',162,165,6,'9.5 x 14.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 4, facing p. 333','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts a group of Highlanders, men and women, responding enthusiastically to the return of Rob Roy, one in the foreground dancing and firing a pistol into the air  [Ch. 34].','2009-05-11 13:00:55','2009-05-11 13:00:55'),(980,'DRY0040',23,'Death of Rashleigh','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"417\"\n',162,165,6,'14.4 x 9.4','1893','1893',45,'vol. 4, facing p. 392','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Rob Roy, wiping his sword and standing above the sprawling figure of Rashleigh, who lies of the ground [Ch. 39].','2009-05-11 13:13:33','2009-05-11 13:13:33'),(981,'DRY0041',5,'Earnscliff and Hobbie Encounter The Black Dwarf','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"372\"\n- \"373\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"117\"\n',166,165,6,'14.5 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Earnscliff and Hobbie Elliot, hunting with guns and dogs, and encountering the Black Dwarf, who stands against one of the boulders with a dagger in his hand [Ch. 3].','2009-05-11 13:16:14','2009-05-11 13:16:14'),(982,'DRY0042',16,'Dalgetty and the Children of the Mist Defending the Pass','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"46\"\n',162,165,6,'7.5 x 6.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Dalgetty in armour looking out down the pass, with a group of Highlanders using bows and arrows immediately beneath him [Ch. 14]. Title/caption from List of Illustrations.','2009-05-11 13:18:43','2009-06-26 12:56:58'),(983,'DRY0043',5,'Attack on the Tower of Westburnflat','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"372\"\n- \"373\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"455\"\n',166,165,6,'9.3 x 14.4','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 62','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Earnscliff on horseback firing a pistol at the Tower of Westburnflat, out of which Willie of Westburnflat peers from a window, and with Hobbie Elliot advancing towards some brushwood in front of the tower with a torch [Ch. 9].','2009-05-11 13:23:14','2009-05-11 13:23:14'),(984,'DRY0044',5,'Miss Vere’s Visit to the Black Dwarf','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"268\"\n',166,165,6,'14.3 x 9.4','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 118','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Isabel Vere in riding cloak, having just entered through the door of the Black Dwarf’s hut, and with the Black Dwarf, lamp in hand, facing her [Ch. 16].','2009-05-11 13:26:04','2009-08-16 20:03:49'),(985,'DRY0045',5,'The Marriage Scene in the Chapel','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"450\"\n- \"64\"\n- \"268\"\n- \"449\"\n',166,165,6,'14.5 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 126','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Miss Vere in the chapel, with hands clasped, after the disruption of the wedding by the Black Dwarf, the figures of her father and Sir Frederick Langley on either side of her, with the Dwarf apparently addressing the former; a clergyman with open book stands behind, and various onlookers hold torches in the background [Ch. 17].','2009-05-11 13:29:16','2009-08-16 20:04:16'),(986,'DRY0046',16,'The Wager of the Candlesticks','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"68\"\n- \"456\"\n',162,165,6,'14.2 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 180','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Highlanders holding torches aloft around the dining table at Darnlinvarach, with Lord Menteith and Sir Miles Musgrave looking on; the large figure in the foreground, with broadsword and torch and facing inwards, is presumably meant to represent Allan McAulay [Ch. 4].','2009-05-11 13:34:19','2009-06-26 12:58:20'),(987,'DRY0047',16,'Annot Lyle Playing the Harp to Allan','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"41\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"59\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"74\"\n',162,165,6,'14.2 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 200','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Allan McAulay (in kilt) crouched gloomily with hand on chin, in a room with arms displayed on the wall, listening to the seated Annot Lyle plucking her harp [Ch.6].','2009-05-11 13:36:23','2009-05-11 13:36:23'),(988,'DRY0048',16,'Scene in the Dungeon at Inverary','--- \n- \"457\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"273\"\n- \"85\"\n',162,165,6,'9.5 x 14.7','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 262','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts the dark figure of the Marquis of Argyle (as Murdoch Campbell) holding a lantern, and facing the seated figure of Dugald Dalgetty, whose fellow-prisoner Ranald MacEagh lies sprawled behind them on the floor of the dungeon [Ch. 13].','2009-05-11 13:39:48','2009-05-11 13:39:48'),(989,'DRY0049',16,'The Knight of Ardenvohr Cut Down by Ranald','--- \n- \"13\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"273\"\n',162,165,6,'14.2 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 324','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts the mounted figure of Sir Duncan Campbell, whose pistol falls from his hand, on the point of being cut down by the broadsword of Ranald MacEagh, as a fierce battle rages around them [Ch. 19].','2009-05-11 13:42:08','2009-06-26 12:58:43'),(990,'DRY0050',16,'Allan Attempts to Stab Menteith','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"68\"\n- \"229\"\n',162,165,6,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 5, facing p. 354','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Allan McAulay, dagger in hand and in motion, having just stabbed Menteith, who lies on the floor with hand clutching his neck; another figure, presumably a guard, raises an axe, while other figures look on in consternation, two descending a flight of stairs [Ch. 23].','2009-05-11 13:44:32','2009-06-26 12:59:03'),(991,'DRY0051',18,'Fight Between Bothwell and Burley at Drumclog','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"5\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"10\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n',171,165,6,'14.2 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Bothwell and Balfour in mounted single combat at the Battle of Drumclog, swords raised, and with a pistol lying on the floor [Ch. 16]','2009-05-11 13:48:14','2009-05-11 13:48:14'),(992,'DRY0052',18,'Old Mortality (Robert Paterson)','--- \n- \"77\"\n- \"21\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"49\"\n',171,165,6,'8 x 6','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Old Mortality with hammer and chisel attending to a Covenanting gravestone, with his pony immediately behind him [Ch. 1]. Title/caption from List of Illustrations.','2009-05-11 13:51:00','2009-05-11 13:51:00'),(993,'DRY0053',18,'Shooting at the Popinjay','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"458\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"70\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 20','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts (presumably) figure of Henry Morton, standing with rifle raised to firing position at the popinjay contest, as Evandale watches by his side, and a body of onlookers behind them watch on [Ch. 3].','2009-05-11 13:56:48','2009-06-26 13:00:56'),(994,'DRY0054',18,'Bothwell Thrown by Burley at Niel Blane’s','--- \n- \"5\"\n- \"10\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"413\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"70\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 32','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Burley standing over the prostrate figure of Bothwell on the floor in Niel Blane’s Inn, with Halliday about to draw his sword and Morton intervening to prevent bloodshed [Ch. 4].','2009-05-11 14:01:50','2009-05-11 14:01:50'),(995,'DRY0055',18,'Mause Headrigg Denouncing the Troopers','--- \n- \"10\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"43\"\n- \"45\"\n- \"71\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"96\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 76','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Bothwell seated casually at the table in Milnwood’s house, with Mause standing opposite with pointed finger berating him, as other participants watch with varying responses [Ch. 8].','2009-05-11 14:05:02','2009-05-11 14:05:02'),(996,'DRY0056',18,'Lady Margaret Bellenden Interceding for Morton','--- \n- \"374\"\n- \"411\"\n- \"458\"\n- \"412\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"146\"\n- \"85\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 130','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Lady Bellenden appealing to Claverhouse, who is seated with drawn sword resting on his thigh; Morton with hands tied stands beside a table, on which the pleading figure of Major Bellenden leans, while Evandale remains in the background with hands on sword [Ch. 13].','2009-05-11 14:08:58','2009-05-11 14:08:58'),(997,'DRY0057',18,'Jenny Dennison Scalding Cuddie with the Brose','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"375\"\n- \"43\"\n- \"98\"\n',171,165,6,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 236','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Jenny Dennison at a window pouring scalding brose from a pot onto the helmeted Cuddie, during the attempted insurgency at Tillietudlem [Ch. 25].','2009-05-11 14:11:13','2009-05-11 14:11:13'),(998,'DRY0058',18,'Morton Condemned to Death by the Cameronians','--- \n- \"21\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"222\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 304','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Morton (seated and bound) awaiting his death sentence as a candle burns on the table, around which are seated his accusing Covenanters, one of whom stands and points a finger at Morton [Ch. 33].','2009-05-11 14:13:41','2009-06-29 12:04:14'),(999,'DRY0059',18,'Morton Recognised by his Old Dog and Ailie','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"459\"\n- \"70\"\n',171,165,6,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 366','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts the returned Morton, greeted by the family dog at the house of Milnwood, with the housekeeper Ailie, holding a stick, on the point of recognition herself [Ch. 39].','2009-05-11 14:16:32','2009-05-11 14:16:32'),(1000,'DRY0060',18,'Burley and Morton in the Cave','--- \n- \"5\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"351\"\n',171,165,6,'9.5 x 14','1893','1893',45,'vol. 6, facing p. 394','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Balfour of Burley in the cavern at the Black Linn, with sword drawn in confronting Morton, and a waterfall visible behind his back [Ch. 43].','2009-05-11 14:32:39','2009-05-11 14:32:39'),(1001,'DRY0061',12,'Dumbiedikes\' Courtship','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"22\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"98\"\n',149,165,6,'13.8 x 9.8','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Dumbiedikes leaning pipe in hand in a window ledge, next to the seated Davie Deans, but looking at Jeanie as she attends to a cauldron over grated fire in the cottage [Ch. 9].','2009-05-11 14:36:42','2009-05-11 14:36:42'),(1002,'DRY0062',12,'Saddletree Laying Down the Law','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"270\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"460\"\n',149,165,6,'7 x 6.8','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Saddletree with a document in his hand expounding law to a weary-looking Butler in the latter’s home; shelves of books provide the background [Ch. 28]. Title/caption from List of Illustrations.','2009-05-11 14:39:36','2009-05-11 14:39:36'),(1003,'DRY0063',12,'Porteous in the Hands of the Mob','--- \n- \"251\"\n- \"447\"\n- \"222\"\n',149,165,6,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 66','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Porteous being led through the street towards his execution, supported by two of the rioters, and with a man with sword dressed as a woman pointing the way; some of the crowd behind are carrying torches [Ch. 7].','2009-05-11 14:43:40','2009-05-11 14:43:40'),(1004,'DRY0064',12,'Jeanie Deans and Robertson at Muschat’s Cairn','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"245\"\n- \"443\"\n',149,165,6,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 156','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Robertson pointing pistol at the imploring figure of Jeanie by Muschat’s Cairn, with the ruined St Anthony’s Chapel visible in moonlight on a hill behind them [Ch. 15]. ','2009-05-11 14:48:24','2009-05-11 14:48:24'),(1005,'DRY0065',12,'Madge and her Mother before Bailie Middleburgh','--- \n- \"188\"\n- \"440\"\n- \"419\"\n- \"104\"\n',149,165,6,'9.3 x 13.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 192','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Madge Wildfire curtsying before the magistrate Mr Middleburgh, who is seated at one end of a table, with his clerk to the right and others at the other end, as Madge’s mother Meg Murdockson struggles in the doorway with guards [Ch. 18].','2009-05-11 14:51:19','2009-05-11 14:51:19'),(1006,'DRY0066',12,'The Trial of Effie Deans','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"188\"\n',149,165,6,'13.5 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 246','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Effie Deans at the bar in the court, with two armed guards seated behind her, and a wigged lawyer gesturing toward her in apparently addressing the judges (not themselves included) [Ch. 24].','2009-05-11 14:52:52','2009-08-16 20:04:52'),(1007,'DRY0067',12,'Madge Wildfire Leading Jeanie into Church','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"104\"\n',149,165,6,'13.7 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 324','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Madge Wildfire, wearing bonnet, leading a contrite-looking Jeanie Deans along the centre aisle of the church at Willingham, with congregation one side and a clerical figure to the left [Ch. 32].','2009-05-11 14:54:46','2009-05-11 14:54:46'),(1008,'DRY0068',12,'Jeanie\'s Interview with the Queen','--- \n- \"283\"\n- \"281\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"246\"\n- \"146\"\n- \"282\"\n',149,165,6,'13.5 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 390','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Jeanie Deans, with plaid over shoulders, petitioning Queen Caroline, in wooded parkland, with Duke of Argyle looking on, and Lady Suffolk in the background under a large tree [Ch. 38].','2009-05-11 14:57:52','2009-06-26 13:03:35'),(1009,'DRY0069',12,'Mrs. Dolly Dutton Objects to Cross the Water','--- \n- \"461\"\n- \"271\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"116\"\n',149,165,6,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 426','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Dolly Dutton being carried against her will through the surf by a Highlander, as Mr Archibald looks on, and with travellers’ coach behind them along the road by a cottage [Ch. 42].','2009-05-11 15:03:02','2009-05-11 15:03:43'),(1010,'DRY0070',12,'Lady Staunton\'s Rescue by the Whistler','--- \n- \"462\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"279\"\n- \"351\"\n- \"442\"\n',149,165,1,'14.1 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 7, facing p. 508','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Effie Deans (now Lady Staunton), accompanied by young David Butler,  being rescued from a precipitous rock by a waterfall through the intercession of The Whistler, who lowers a ladder towards them [Ch. 51].','2009-05-11 15:06:12','2009-06-26 13:04:10'),(1011,'DRY0071',6,'Sir William Ashton and Lucy at Wolf’s Crag','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'14.3 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Sir William Ashton and Ravenswood seated at an arched window in Wolf’s Crag, with Lucy Aston standing beside them [Ch. 16].','2009-05-11 15:08:52','2009-05-11 15:12:03'),(1012,'DRY0072',6,'Caleb Recovering the Feather','--- \n- \"379\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"378\"\n',172,165,6,'8.2 x 6','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Caleb Balderstone about to pick up Ravenswood’s hat feather as it floats in the sea; the cliffs and fortress in the background are presumably meant to represent Wolf’s Crag [Ch. 35].','2009-05-11 15:17:33','2009-05-11 15:17:33'),(1013,'DRY0073',6,'Lucy Ashton Playing the Lute','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"74\"\n',172,165,6,'14.3 x 9.3','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 26','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Sir William Ashton entering through curtains, to view his daughter, Lucy, whose fingers are on the strings of a lute, and who looks in the other direction [Ch. 3].','2009-05-11 15:22:04','2009-05-11 15:22:04'),(1014,'DRY0074',6,'‘I am the Master of Ravenswood’','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'14.3 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 46','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Ravenswood with arms folded, besides a large forest tree, facing Sir William and Lucy Ashton, whose arms are linked, and with a forester behind them [Ch. 5].','2009-05-11 15:24:14','2009-06-26 13:04:37'),(1015,'DRY0075',6,'Caleb\'s Reception of Ravenswood and Bucklaw','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"379\"\n- \"359\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'9.5 x 14.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 68','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Caleb Balderstone at the gate of Wolf’s Crag, lamp in hand, standing before Ravenswood, who has dismounted, and Bucklaw (still on his horse) [Ch. 7]','2009-05-11 15:26:49','2009-05-11 15:26:49'),(1016,'DRY0076',6,'Caleb\'s Visit to Gibbie Girder\'s','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"379\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"463\"\n- \"464\"\n- \"98\"\n',172,165,6,'9.5 x 14.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 120','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Caleb Balderstone entering the cottage of the cooper, Gibbie Girder, whose wife, Jean Lightbody, preens herself before a mirror, while her mother sits, and two boys attend to cooking in the fireplace [Ch. 12].','2009-05-11 15:29:40','2009-05-11 15:30:39'),(1017,'DRY0077',6,'Henry Ashton\'s Terror at Ravenswood','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"312\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'9.5 x 14.8','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 170','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Henry Ashton having run in fear towards his father, Sir William, having noticed Ravenswood’s likeness to the picture of Sir Malise Ravenswood; Ravenswood himself meanwhile looks towards a painting on the wall, while Lucy Ashton stands in a curtain doorway in the background [Ch. 18].','2009-05-11 15:34:00','2009-06-26 13:05:22'),(1018,'DRY0078',6,'Ravenswood and Lucy at the Fountain','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'14.3 x 9.2','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 190','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Ravenswood and Lucy Ashton, who wears a pale dress and tartan shawl, seated by the fountain, with Ravenswood holding Lucy’s hand as she looks partly away [Ch. 20].','2009-05-11 15:36:14','2009-05-11 15:36:14'),(1019,'DRY0079',6,'Ravenswood Interrupts the Betrothal','--- \n- \"1\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"64\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'9.5 x 14.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 296','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood confronting Lucy Ashton, with a letter in his hand,  at the signing of her marriage contract, while Lady Ashton attempts to intervene, and others respond in agitated fashion [Ch. 33].','2009-05-11 15:38:14','2009-05-11 15:38:14'),(1020,'DRY0080',6,'Caleb\'s Last Entreaty','--- \n- \"379\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"86\"\n',172,165,6,'14.5 x 9.5','1893','1893',45,'vol. 8, facing p. 312','NLS, NG.1179.d','','Depicts Caleb Balderstone on his knees, imploring Ravenswood not to ride out to fulfil his engagement to duel with Colonel Ashton; Ravenswood’s horse waits nearby close to the entrance of Wolf’s Crag [Ch. 35].','2009-05-11 15:40:39','2009-06-26 13:05:37'),(1021,'CHT0001',5,'The Lawyer\'s Daughter','--- \n- \"254\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"162\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"268\"\n',82,30,3,'10 x 7.5','1833','1833',46,'frontispiece','O, 1832.851','0007385d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53060~100055','Depicts Isabel Vere in the Black Dwarf’s hut, right arm raised, facing the Dwarf who appears below her on the floor, with a stonemason’s hammer at his feet [Ch. 16]. Imprint reads: London. Published Nov 1 1832 by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the last of four stories in the collection. Also occurs as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P.2923) (see image).','2009-05-12 09:00:18','2009-09-08 15:41:10'),(1022,'CHT0002',28,'The Lost Deed','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"12\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"102\"\n',82,9,3,'10 x 7.5','1833','1833',46,'facing p. 23','O, 1832.851','','Depicts Rose Bradwardine seated by an opened lattice window and with hand over breast, looking attentively at Waverley who is dressed in uniform and reads a book, with more books on the table; a portrait of Rose’s father as a boy hangs on the wall, and flowers are visible outside the window [Chs 13 & 14]. Imprint reads: London. Published June 1 1832, by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the first of the four stories in the collection. ','2009-05-12 09:06:10','2009-08-19 10:24:24'),(1023,'CHT0003',28,'The Novice','--- \n- \"323\"\n- \"431\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"96\"\n- \"102\"\n',110,9,3,'10.2 x 7.8','1833','1833',46,'facing p. 133','O, 1832.851','','Depicts apparently Colonel Gardiner on the ground beside his fallen horse, with Waverley in Highland military dress attempting to help him, with another figure (possibly representing Fergus MacIvor) behind him; in the background Highland infantry attack mounted troopers [Ch. 47]. Imprint reads: London. Published August 1 1832, by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the second of the four stories in the collection.','2009-05-12 09:09:46','2009-08-19 10:24:48'),(1024,'CHT0004',18,'The Novice','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"5\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"70\"\n- \"351\"\n',82,88,3,'9.7 x 7.5','1833','1833',46,'facing p. 165','O, 1832.851','0014463d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56007~100230','Depicts Balfour of Burley in the cavern at the Black Linn holding out his sword and pointing out one of several notches on it to Morton, who stands before him wearing a cloak and hat; a clasped Bible is visible behind on a ledge [Ch. 43]. Imprint reads: London. Published Sep 1 1832, by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the second of the four stories in the collection. Also occurs as a separate print in the Corson Collection (P.2909) (see image).','2009-05-12 09:12:27','2009-09-08 15:42:40'),(1025,'CHT0005',28,'Autobiography of an Old Man','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"466\"\n- \"465\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"102\"\n',110,30,3,'10.8 x 7.7','1833','1833',46,'facing p. 212','O, 1832.851','','Depicts Waverley in Highland military dress, having entered the cottage of Jacob Jopson, after the skirmish at Clifton, with Jopson in nightwear and holding aloft a lighted candle, and with his daughter looking on and holding him timorously; another candle lies on the floor [Ch. 60]. Imprint reads: London. Published Nov. 1 1832 by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the third of the four stories in the collection.','2009-05-12 09:16:01','2009-08-19 10:25:37'),(1026,'CHT0006',23,'The Lawyer\'s Daughter','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"54\"\n- \"371\"\n',25,43,3,'10.3 x 7.8','1833','1833',46,'facing p. 267','O, 1832.851','0014609d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56939~100285','Depicts Nicol Jarvie, surrounded by armed Highlanders, addressing Helen MacGregor, who stands with a long broadsword pointing to the ground [Ch. 31] Imprint reads: London. Published Nov. 1 1832 by Jennings & Chaplin, 62 Cheapside. Introductory statement to the containing work states: \'That the Pictorial Embellishments of the Annuals are derived from subjects originally unconnected with the volumes they adorn, and that, consequently, the Letter-press is adapted to the Plates, is a fact so generally known, that little credit for candour is claimed in the avowal that the Engravings of this Work were made from drawings, by eminent masters, intended to illustrate the Novels of Sir Walter Scott’. Title/caption here matches the title of the last of the four stories in the collection. Same as #WAK0001, but with different title/caption (see image). Exhibited as painting at Royal Academy, no. 610, 1832.','2009-05-12 09:18:50','2009-09-08 15:44:01'),(1027,'ILP0001',20,'The Pirate / The Shipwreck of Cleveland','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"137\"\n',110,10,1,'9.2 x 7','1825','1825',47,'[Plate 1]','O, Dunston B(Scott) 166/1','0014507d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~56380~100252','Depicts Ulla Troil (Norna) attending to the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland, with Mordaunt Mertoun and Bryce the Pedlar looking on [Ch. 7]. Forms (attached) frontispiece to vol. 1 of Bodleian copy of first edition of ‘The Pirate’, 3 vols (1822), interpreted in Todd & Bowden 156Dk as containing the three plates from ‘Illustrations of The Pirate’ (1825). Plate carries imprint of Hurst, Robinson, & Co., London, 1825. The image is identical to that at #NTE0019, and was later printed in ‘The Waverley Album’ (London: Charles Heath, [1832]), facing p. 37 (see image). ','2009-05-12 09:26:05','2009-06-26 13:07:19'),(1028,'ILP0002',20,'Pirate / Vol. 2, p. 129','--- \n- \"264\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7.5','1825','1825',47,'[Plate 2]','O, Dunston B(Scott) 166/2','','Depicts the seated figure of Norna by fireplace, staff in hand, pointing to the fire, with the two sisters standing and listening [Ch. 19]. Forms (attached) frontispiece to vol. 2 of Bodleian copy of first edition of ‘The Pirate’, 3 vols (1822), interpreted in Todd & Bowden 156Dk as containing the three plates from ‘Illustrations of The Pirate’ (1825). The image is identical to that at #LAD0020, from ‘The Lady’s Magazine’, vol. 3, no. 3 (March 1822). Title/caption reference is to original edition. Engraver wrongly identified as Charles Heath in Todd & Bowden.','2009-05-12 09:32:38','2009-08-16 20:12:48'),(1029,'ILP0003',20,'Pirate / Vol. 3, p. 149','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',25,49,1,'10.5 x 7.7','1825','1825',47,'[Plate 3]','O, Dunston B(Scott) 166/3','','Depicts Cleveland in front of a standing stone bowing towards the figure of Minna, who is accompanied by Brenda, with a ship and rowing boat in background [Ch. 40]. Forms (attached) frontispiece to vol. 3 of Bodleian copy of first edition of ‘The Pirate’, 3 vols (1822), interpreted in Todd & Bowden 156Dk as containing the three plates from ‘Illustrations of The Pirate’ (1825). The image is identical to that at #LAD0021, from ‘The Lady’s Magazine’, vol. 3, no. 4 (April 1822). Title/caption reference (inaccurate) is to original edition; pencilled correction to ‘Page 297’ on present Bodleian copy. Engraver wrongly identified as Charles Heath in Todd & Bowden. ','2009-05-12 09:38:46','2009-06-26 13:12:49'),(1030,'LIB0001',28,'Waverley / ‘The Bailie started . . . my poor understanding’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"357\"\n- \"102\"\n',173,137,3,'11.9 x 9.7','1852','1852',48,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Bailie Macwheeble cap in hand ecstatically dancing in celebration after hearing of Waverley\'s engagement to Rose Bradwardine, as Waverley looks on [Ch. 66; EEWN 1, 331.13-20]. ','2009-05-17 14:00:36','2009-06-12 12:29:14'),(1031,'LIB0002',28,'‘The Boat now neared . . . lake seemed to advance’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"314\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"102\"\n',174,130,3,'8.5 x 9','1852','1852',48,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts a rowing boat carrying several Highlanders, one apparently representing Waverley in military uniform, as it enters a cave on the lake by moonlight, as two figures tend to a fire guiding them in on the left [Ch. 17; EEWN 1, 85.14-18].','2009-05-17 14:05:14','2009-06-12 12:31:02'),(1032,'LIB0003',11,'Guy Mannering / ‘Mr. Bertram, paralytic . . . young woman of about seventeen.’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"263\"\n- \"358\"\n- \"108\"\n- \"63\"\n- \"92\"\n- \"178\"\n',173,137,3,'9.5 x 12.3','1852','1852',48,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0007418d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~53625~100088','Depicts the ailing Godfrey Bertram seated in an easy chair outside old Ellangowan, as Lucy Bertram kneels in front of him, Guy Mannering looks on, and Dominie Sampson stands to one side staring into the distance [Ch. 13; EEWN 2, 74.33-40].','2009-05-17 14:08:03','2009-09-11 09:43:51'),(1033,'LIB0004',11,'\'\"No answer! – Well, Julia . . . Still no reply\"\' [4 lines]','--- \n- \"131\"\n- \"63\"\n- \"422\"\n',173,88,3,'10.5 x 8.3','1852','1852',48,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Julia Mannering seated in her dressing room, looking distressed, as her father standing on the right interrogates her about her secret communication with Brown [Ch. 51; EEWN 2, 316.14-16]. ','2009-05-17 14:10:57','2009-06-26 13:14:46'),(1034,'LIB0005',3,'The Antiquary / ‘You indeed know . . . Earl, turning away’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"445\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"366\"\n',87,9,3,'12 x 10','1852','1852',48,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Elspeth in her chair, with a spinning wheel by her side, holding her hands out toward the Earl of Glenallan, who stands facing away from her, with one finger raised heavenwards [Ch. 33; EEWN 3, 226.23-26].','2009-05-17 14:13:43','2009-08-16 20:13:34'),(1035,'LIB0006',3,'‘She gave the signal to Lovel . . . brink of the precipice.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"143\"\n- \"58\"\n- \"76\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"362\"\n- \"361\"\n',174,128,3,'9.8 x 8.5','1852','1852',48,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Isabella Wardour being raised up the cliffs in a chair, as Lovel steadies the rope that is pulling her up, and as Edie Ochiltree (staff in hand) looks on, with Sir Arthur Wardour kneeling and appearing to cling on to Edie\'s coat [Ch 8; EEWN 3, 63.35-39].','2009-05-17 14:16:02','2009-08-16 20:14:08'),(1036,'LIB0007',23,'Rob Roy / ‘At this moment the door opened . . . and locked it.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"423\"\n- \"57\"\n- \"81\"\n- \"80\"\n- \"100\"\n- \"425\"\n',175,137,3,'12.5 x 9.5','1852','1852',48,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Frank Osbaldistone closing the door of the library at Osbaldistone Hall to prevent Andrew Fairservice, who holds a candlestick, from entering, as Diana Vernon and her father Sir Frederick look on with alarm; a portrait of Sir Frederick hangs on the wall behind them [EEWN 5; 330.3-8].','2009-05-17 14:17:45','2009-06-12 12:41:52'),(1037,'LIB0008',23,'‘Rob Roy remained for some time . . . Highland gentleman & Soldier.’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"8\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"61\"\n- \"72\"\n',176,103,3,'11 x 9.2','1852','1852',48,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Rob Roy and a group of Highlanders standing on rocks on the shore of Loch Lomond, waving farewell to the departing Frank Osbladistone and Nicol Jarvie, whose boat is perhaps just visible on the Loch [Ch. 36; EEWN 5, 310.24-28].','2009-05-17 14:24:14','2009-08-16 20:14:38'),(1038,'LIB0009',18,'Old Mortality / ‘So ye’re no thinking . . . a splendid silver dollar.’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"393\"\n- \"375\"\n- \"413\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"96\"\n',177,167,3,'12.1 x 10','1852','1852',48,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Tom Halliday guarding the door to Morton’s prison, with Jenny Dennison and Edith Bellenden attempting to bribe him for entry [Ch. 10; EEWN 4a, 85.21-23].','2009-05-17 14:38:58','2009-06-12 12:45:46'),(1039,'LIB0010',18,'‘A gory figure . . . which thou hast shed’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"412\"\n- \"475\"\n- \"178\"\n',178,103,3,'10.8 x 9','1852','1852',48,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Claverhouse, seated at a table, with a sword hanging from his waist, being confronted by the ghost-like figure of Mucklewrath, who stands with one finger pointing at Claverhouse and one pointing upwards toward heaven [Ch. 34; EEWN 4a, 268-69].','2009-05-17 14:44:54','2009-06-12 12:49:10'),(1040,'LIB0011',16,'Montrose','--- \n- \"432\"\n',122,127,3,'12.6 x 10','1852','1852',48,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of James Graham, Marquis of Montrose, wearing body armour. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 19 in vol. 6, with slightly different shading, engraved by H. Robinson. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels (Edinburgh: Cadell, 1842-47): see #ABB0038.','2009-05-17 14:57:49','2009-06-12 12:50:10'),(1041,'LIB0012',16,'‘She sate down . . . accompanied it with her voice.’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"41\"\n- \"59\"\n- \"65\"\n- \"68\"\n- \"74\"\n',98,103,3,'10.5 x 8.8','1852','1852',48,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Annot Lyle playing her harp to Allan McAulay, who sits by her side with his face hidden in his arm, as Menteith looks on [Ch. 6; EEWN 7b, 49.30-33]. Exhibited as painting, with identical quotation, at Royal Academy, no. 123, 1840.','2009-05-17 15:01:40','2009-06-29 12:06:22'),(1042,'LIB0013',12,'Heart of Mid-Lothian / ‘I never ask what . . . in answer to this pert remark’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"22\"\n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"27\"\n- \"204\"\n',177,167,3,'9.5 x 12.4','1852','1852',48,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','ABD, 823.73 5, vol. 7','','Depicts a scene in the Deans family cottage; Jeanie knits, Effie yawns, David sits at the table holding a book, and the Laird of Dumbiedikes sits on a chest on the left, with a pipe turned downward in his mouth, gazing at Jeanie [Ch. 10; EEWN 6, 87.10-15].','2009-05-17 15:04:20','2009-06-12 12:54:12'),(1043,'LIB0014',12,'‘She delivered to the Duke . . . abashed at the mistake’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"283\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"146\"\n',179,42,3,'8.8 x 9.8','1852','1852',48,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','ABD, 823.73 5, vol. 7','','Depicts Jeanie in tartan shawl having delivered a letter to the Duke of Argyle, who sits reading it at his table [Ch. 36; EEWN 6, 322.13-23]. ','2009-05-17 15:05:53','2009-06-12 12:55:21'),(1044,'LIB0015',6,'Bride of Lammermoor / ‘She was unable . . . concealed in her bosom’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"1\"\n- \"2\"\n- \"3\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"131\"\n- \"86\"\n',113,137,3,'12.2 x 9.6','1852','1852',48,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Lady Ashton having cut the ribbon necklace off Lucy’s neck, and haughtily handing it to Ravenswood, as Lucy sits slumped in a chair looking distraught, and Sir William Ashton looks on from behind [Ch. 33; EEWN 7a, 252.32-35].','2009-05-17 15:09:33','2009-06-12 12:55:51'),(1045,'LIB0016',6,'“’And never shall this leave my bosom . . . acknowledge another love than yours.” [4 lines]','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"86\"\n- \"351\"\n',113,137,3,'10.5 x 8.3','1852','1852',48,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Lucy Ashton and Edgar Ravenswood pledging their love by a small waterfall, as Lucy ties the ribbon necklace around her neck; a raven sits conspicuously on a barren tree behind them [Ch. 20; EEWN 7a, 158.39-43].','2009-05-17 15:11:55','2009-06-12 12:56:50'),(1046,'LIB0017',14,'Ivanhoe / Durrenstein on the Danube / (Place of Coeur-de-Lion’s captivity) / ‘King Richard was absent . . . generality of his subjects.’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"244\"\n',108,69,3,'11.3 x 9.2','1853','1853',48,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts the Castle and mountain of Durrenstein in the Dolomites on the Danube River. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0041.','2009-05-17 15:14:12','2009-05-18 12:07:43'),(1047,'LIB0018',14,'‘He weighed it upon the tip . . . a grain above weight.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"40\"\n- \"51\"\n- \"69\"\n- \"87\"\n',179,167,3,'9.5 x 10.6','1853','1853',48,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Isaac seated at a table with Gurth holding a coin on the tip of his finger, as Rebecca looks on [Ch. 10; EEWN 8, 100.8-12]. ','2009-05-17 15:19:11','2009-06-26 13:18:01'),(1048,'LIB0019',17,'James Stuart / ‘The commanding form . . . awe and reverence.’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"383\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.5 x 10.1','1853','1853',48,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of James Stuart, Earl of Murray, Regent of Scotland with quoted text from Ch. 35; EEWN 9, 322.15-18. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 15 in vol. 2, somewhat larger than in the Library edition, engraved by H. Robinson. Caption includes facsimile signature. Caption indicates original portrait is at Holyrood. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0048.','2009-05-17 15:21:05','2009-06-26 13:18:18'),(1049,'LIB0020',17,'‘As Father Philip came close . . . current of the river.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"341\"\n',58,77,3,'10.2 x 9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Father Philip on horseback, having stopped to speak to the White Lady, depicted as a pale ethereal figure seated under a tree [Ch. 5; EEWN 9, 64.22-25].','2009-05-17 15:27:55','2009-06-12 13:00:44'),(1050,'LIB0021',1,'Marie R / ‘Her dress was a robe . . . in large loose folds.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"122\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.9 x 9.9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of Mary, Queen of Scots, with quoted text from Ch. 21; EEWN 10, 194.26-31. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 10 in vol. 3, engraved by W. T. Fry. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0052.','2009-05-17 15:29:36','2009-06-12 13:01:28'),(1051,'LIB0022',1,'‘And you, too . . . or ported or sloped pike’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"112\"\n- \"324\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"123\"\n',179,168,3,'8.5 x 11.4','1853','1853',48,'vol. 11 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0007329d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52530~100024','Depicts Roland Graeme as a boy shouldering a stick in military style, as the Lady of Avenel, seated on a balcony on the battlements, looks on, with Lilias her attendant behind her [Ch. 3; EEWN 10, 20.11-15]. ','2009-05-17 15:35:19','2009-09-11 09:46:29'),(1052,'LIB0023',15,'Elizabeth I. / ‘After this came Elizabeth . . . striking and commanding physiognomy.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"347\"\n- \"84\"\n',124,127,3,'12.8 x 9.7','1853','1853',48,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of Queen Elizabeth, wearing a dress made out of a fabric featuring images of human eyes and ears, with quoted text from Ch. 15; EEWN 11, 142.5-9. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 1 in vol. 4,  with slightly different background and shading, engraved by W. T. Fry. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0057.','2009-05-18 09:24:57','2009-06-12 13:04:43'),(1054,'LIB0025',20,'The Pirate / ‘Minna inherited the stately form . . . character, taste, and expression.’ [7 lines]','--- \n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',179,137,3,'12.1 x 9.6','1853','1853',48,'vol. 13 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Brenda and Minna Troil standing arm in arm [Ch. 3; EEWN 12, 20-21]. ','2009-05-18 09:27:47','2009-06-12 13:05:42'),(1056,'LIB0024',15,'‘If I put a fresh rose-bud . . . about roses gules’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"476\"\n- \"89\"\n',175,103,3,'9.7 x 10','1853','1853',48,'vol. 12 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts an ideal scene at Lidcote-Hall, with Amy Robsart holding a basket of flowers, and (apparently) her father, the curate, and Master Michael Mumblazen alongside her [see Ch. 22; EEWN 11, 217.39-42].','2009-05-18 09:34:19','2009-06-26 13:22:43'),(1057,'LIB0026',20,'‘Look on me maidens . . . the objects of fond affection?’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',98,103,3,'10.2 x 8.4','1853','1853',48,'vol. 13 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Norna of the Fitful-Head near the end of her nighttime story in the sisters\' bedroom, with Minna looking intently at the speaker, and Brenda seeming aghast [Ch. 19; EEWN 12, 185.10-16].','2009-05-18 09:36:00','2009-06-26 13:23:03'),(1058,'LIB0027',10,'George Heriote / ‘He was a well made man . . . ruddy cheek, and grey hair.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"151\"\n- \"69\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.3 x 10','1853','1853',48,'vol. 14 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of George Heriot (spelled Heriote in the facsimile signature that forms the title), with a ruff collar and what appears to be coin or gold pieces in his hand; with quoted text from Ch. 2 [EEWN 13, 29-30]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0064.','2009-05-18 09:37:32','2009-08-16 20:19:18'),(1059,'LIB0028',10,'‘Dame Ursula drew herself . . . on the kind enquirer’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"150\"\n- \"148\"\n',180,103,3,'10.3 x 8.7','1853','1853',48,'vol. 14 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Margaret Ramsay seated on a chair, in a rather sulky fashion, with Dame Ursula behind her looking concerned [Ch. 8; EEWN 13, 98.3-9].','2009-05-18 09:46:06','2009-08-16 20:20:26'),(1060,'LIB0029',19,'Charles R / ‘He was past the middle age . . . dignified good-humour.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"84\"\n',127,127,3,'12.3 x 10.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 15 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of King Charles II wearing large dark wig with quoted text from Ch. 30 [EEWN 14, 313.10-15]. Caption includes facsimile signature. Also appears in the Abbotsford edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0073.','2009-05-18 09:50:50','2009-06-26 13:23:29'),(1061,'LIB0030',19,'‘Peveril found himself seated . . . prepared under her own eye.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"154\"\n- \"477\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"153\"\n',181,167,3,'11 x 9.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 15 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Julian Peveril seated between Alice Bridgenorth and her father, at a dining table at the Black Fort, with Deborah Debbitch (in a cap) also present [Ch. 14; EEWN 14, 142.17-26].		','2009-05-18 09:54:37','2009-06-12 13:25:48'),(1062,'LIB0031',21,'Louis XI / ‘His countenance was partly attractive . . . at once commanding and sinister.’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"165\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,6,'12.4 x 9.9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 16 (frontispiece)','','','Portrait of Louis XI of France in profile, wearing a fur cap and cloak, with quoted text from Ch. 2 [EEWN 15, 33.17-21]. Caption includes facsimile signature. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0075.','2009-05-18 10:01:59','2009-06-12 13:27:06'),(1063,'LIB0032',21,'‘The publicity of the scene . . . retire into a cloister.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"467\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"146\"\n',182,103,3,'11.2 x 8.8','1853','1853',48,'vol. 16 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts the Countess Isabelle kneeling before the Duke of Burgundy on his throne; the other male and female figures also portrayed are perhaps meant to represent the Count and Countess of Crevecoeur [Ch. 32; EEWN 15, 352.28-31].','2009-05-18 10:08:07','2009-06-12 13:46:35'),(1064,'LIB0033',24,'St Ronan’s Well / ‘They had accomplished their purpose . . . put the sylvan to flight.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"176\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"401\"\n',183,82,3,'9.3 x 12.2','1853','1853',48,'vol. 17 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0014614d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57022~100290','Depicts Clara Mowbray as a young woman, with Hannah Irwin as her companion, being rescued from an unwanted admirer by Frank Tyrrel, in full hunting gear and with gun [Ch. 25; EEWN 16, 236-37]. See image (from 1877 reprint).','2009-05-18 10:11:11','2009-09-11 09:48:09'),(1065,'LIB0034',24,'‘”No nearer – no nearer” . . . entreaties that he would begone.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"478\"\n- \"177\"\n',183,169,3,'10.8 x 8.6','1853','1853',48,'vol. 17 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0014615d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57040~100291','Depicts Clara Mowbray in the breakfast-room of Shaws Castle, rejecting the approaches of the Earl of Etherington, who wears a morning coat and has his hat in hand [Ch. 24; EEWN 16, 224.14-17]. See image (from 1877 reprint).','2009-05-18 10:17:53','2009-09-11 09:49:26'),(1066,'LIB0035',22,'Charles P. / ‘Distinguished by a noble countenance . . . high birth and rank.’ [3 lines]','--- \n- \"301\"\n- \"84\"\n',118,127,3,'12.3 x 9.9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 18 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of Bonnie Prince Charlie wearing armour, with quoted text from Ch. 16 [see EEWN 17, 276]. Caption includes facsimile signature. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0006.','2009-05-18 10:22:51','2009-06-12 13:47:28'),(1067,'LIB0036',22,'‘He agreed to put the pony . . . holding up his forefinger.’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"305\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"428\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"180\"\n',184,170,3,'8.3 x 11','1853','1853',48,'vol. 18 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Joshua Geddes reluctantly putting the care of his pony into the hands of Benjie, with Darsie in a fashionable riding suit alongside [Letter 6; EEWN 17, 48-49].','2009-05-18 10:32:08','2009-06-12 13:50:17'),(1068,'LIB0037',4,'The Bethrothed [sic] / Harlech Castle, Merionethshire / The ‘Garde Doloureuse’ of the Tale','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"244\"\n',130,128,3,'8.6 x 13.1','1853','1853',48,'vol. 19 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0007369d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52852~100043','Depicts Harlech Castle in Merionethshire, north Wales, as the prototype for the fictional castle \'Garde Doloureuse\', with a stream in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text: \'The river, whose stream . . . defenders of the castle\' [4 lines; Ch. 4]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0088. Also appears as a print in the Corson Collection (P. 2383), with attributed date 1891 (see image).','2009-05-18 10:36:28','2009-09-11 09:52:19'),(1069,'LIB0038',4,'‘Eveline fetched a fuller sigh . . . Praised be God!’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"190\"\n- \"192\"\n- \"329\"\n- \"178\"\n',182,103,3,'11 x 8','1853','1853',48,'vol. 19 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0007370d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~52870~100044','Depicts Rose Flammock attending to Eveline, who has fainted and lies stretched out on the floor in the haunted chamber of Baldringham [Ch. 14]. See image (from 1891 reprint)','2009-05-18 10:42:38','2009-09-11 09:53:48'),(1070,'LIB0039',26,'The Talisman / The Dead Sea','--- \n- \"352\"\n- \"124\"\n',83,69,3,'9 x 13.9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 20 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts the Dead Sea with two small figures seated at an oasis with palm trees in the foreground. Accompanying quoted text: \'Beneath these sluggish waves . . . a tribute to the ocean.\' [4 lines; Ch. 1]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0092.','2009-05-18 10:46:30','2009-08-16 20:21:45'),(1071,'LIB0040',26,'‘The robust form . . . to his wife Dejanira.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"421\"\n- \"394\"\n- \"196\"\n',185,137,3,'8.7 x 10.5','1853','1853',48,'vol. 20 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Queen Berengaria kneeling before her husband King Richard, who reclines in his couch in his pavilion as Lady Edith looks on [Ch. 17].','2009-05-18 10:51:16','2009-06-26 13:25:06'),(1072,'LIB0041',29,'Oliver P. ','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"308\"\n- \"84\"\n',1,127,3,'12.5 x 9.9','1853','1853',48,'vol. 21 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Portrait of Oliver Cromwell in military uniform. A reengraving of a portrait published in Edmund Lodge\'s \'Portraits of Illustrious Personages of Great Britain\' (1821), where it appears as no. 9 in vol. 7,  with more background and an additional figure, a child, adjusting the sash around Cromwell\'s waist. Title/caption also supplies facsimile signature of \'Cromwell\'. Accompanying quoted text: \'He was a man of middle stature . . . depth of thought.\' [Ch. 8]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0102.','2009-05-18 10:57:56','2009-06-26 13:25:24'),(1073,'LIB0042',29,'‘”As sure, my dearest Phoebe . . . upon such a trifle.’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"479\"\n- \"205\"\n- \"206\"\n',113,103,3,'11.2 x 8.8','1853','1853',48,'vol. 21 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Jocelin Joliffe, in boots, apparently stealing a kiss from Phoebe Mayflower in the sitting room at Woodstock Lodge, as Tomkins looks on from near a window in the recess [Ch. 3].','2009-05-18 10:59:22','2009-06-12 14:00:29'),(1074,'LIB0043',9,'The Fair Maid of Perth / ‘If pretty Mistress Catherine . . . without further question.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"339\"\n',179,137,3,'9.9 x 11.7','1853','1853',48,'vol. 22 (frontispiece)','ABD, 823.73 5, vol. 22','','Depicts Luckie Shoolbred intervening to keep Catharine Glover and Henry Gow apart, with Catharine seated at her side and Henry departing through the doorway with a backwards glance, wearing armour and helmet [Ch. 12; EEWN 21, 128.16-23].','2009-05-18 11:02:02','2009-06-26 13:25:59'),(1075,'LIB0044',9,'‘At length the hour of noon . . . still there was silence.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"210\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"480\"\n',179,103,3,'11.5 x 8.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 22 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Catharine Glover outside the castle wall at Falkland attempting to supply food for the imprisoned Duke of Rothesay, with the figure of Sir John Ramorny standing behind her, as yet unnoticed [Ch. 32; EEWN 21, 341.35-40].','2009-05-18 11:03:53','2009-06-30 08:19:19'),(1076,'LIB0045',2,'Anne of Geierstein / Lake of Lucerne / ‘Their road lay along the side . . . ditch which defends it.’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"72\"\n',94,21,3,'9.4 x 14.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 23 (frontispiece)','','','Depicts Lake Lucerne, with mountains in the background, with passengers crossing a bridge and mechanical works (possibly part of a mine) to the left; quoted text from Ch. 1 [EEWN 22, 6-7]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0114.','2009-05-18 11:15:04','2009-06-12 14:29:24'),(1077,'LIB0046',2,'‘Hear me, said the youth . . . claim a tear from Anne of Geierstein.’ [5 lines]','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"223\"\n- \"217\"\n',186,136,3,'8.8 x 10.6','1853','1853',48,'vol. 23 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Arthur Philipson holding the arm of Anne of Geierstein, who is seated at a table, and who looks away; a plate of grapes is prominent on the table [Ch. 22; EEWN 22, 251.21-26]. ','2009-05-18 11:36:14','2009-06-12 14:33:08'),(1079,'LIB0047',8,'Count Robert of Paris / Constantinople / ‘It has been allowed . . . beauty, wealth, security, and eminence.’ [4 lines]','--- \n- \"247\"\n- \"354\"\n',3,69,3,'8.8 x 13.5','1853','1853',48,'vol. 24 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts the city of Constantinople from across the water, with several figures sitting in Arabian dress in an open space in the foreground; quoted text from Ch. 1 [EEWN 23a, 4.15-20]. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0116.','2009-05-18 11:50:13','2009-06-12 14:33:51'),(1080,'LIB0048',8,'‘He arranged his fair burden . . . be instantly extinguished.’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"407\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"228\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"351\"\n',187,136,3,'10.5 x 9.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 24 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts Hereward, with helmet and sword, bending over Bertha, who lies on a stone wall, having escaped from Sylvan [Ch. 20; EEWN 23a, 208.1-7].','2009-05-18 11:53:05','2009-06-26 13:28:20'),(1081,'LIB0049',25,'The Surgeon’s Daughter / Selkirk, the supposed “Middlemas” of the tale. ','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"354\"\n',129,69,3,'9 x 12.7','1853','1853',48,'vol. 25 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','','Depicts the town of Selkirk and surrounding countryside. Also appears in the Abbotsford Edition of the Waverley Novels: see #ABB0105.','2009-05-18 11:57:29','2009-05-18 11:57:29'),(1082,'LIB0050',25,'‘Richard Middlemas sunk on one knee . . . sunk down in a swoon.’ [6 lines]','--- \n- \"336\"\n- \"232\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"574\"\n',188,82,3,'10.4 x 8.3','1853','1853',48,'vol. 25 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.a & b','0014617d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57073~100293','Depicts Richard Middlemas on one knee entreating Colonel Witherington, as Zilia stands behind him, flinging off her veil [Ch. 22; EEWN 20, 231.26-27]. ','2009-05-18 12:02:31','2009-09-11 09:55:13'),(1083,'KEP0006',31,'The Magic Mirror','--- \n- \"326\"\n- \"284\"\n- \"468\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"471\"\n- \"472\"\n- \"469\"\n- \"470\"\n',110,77,3,'11.8 x 8.4','1828','1828',50,'facing p. 33','EUL, Corson E.KEE.1 ','','Depicts Lady Bothwell and Lady Forester looking with horror into the \'magic\' mirror of Doctor Damiotti, with the mirror revealing an image of Sir Philip Forester and Captain Falconer about to enter into a fatal duel [Ch. 2].','2009-05-22 09:56:35','2009-06-26 13:29:21'),(1084,'KEP0007',32,'The Tapestried Chamber','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"473\"\n',95,43,3,'8.4 x 10.8','1828','1828',50,'facing p. 136','EUL, Corson E.KEE.1 ','','Depicts General Browne starting upward from his bed at the apparition of a female ghost (\'the accursed hag\') in the tapestried chamber when a guest at Woodville Castle.','2009-05-22 11:10:40','2009-06-19 09:21:43'),(1085,'CEN0001',28,'yours very truly / Walter Scott / Born 1771, Died 1832','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',45,57,3,'10.3 x 8','1870','1870',51,'vol. 1 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Scott seated with cane in hand with his dog Bran in the bottom right corner; a view of the Eildon Hills is to the left. Caption includes facsimile of ‘Yours very truly, Walter Scott’. Disliking a recent portrait by David Wilkie, Robert Cadell commissioned this portrait in March 1830, planning to have it engraved to accompany a volume of the Magnum Opus edition of the novels (see #MAG0065). (See Russell, Francis, \'Portraits of Sir Walter Scott\' (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 55, pp.40-41) (Another engraving of the same portrait, by Henry Robinson, appears as the frontispiece to vol. 1 of the Fisher edition of the Waverley Novels. See #FIS0001). ','2009-05-29 15:08:02','2009-08-19 10:27:30'),(1086,'CEN0002',28,'Waverley Honour','--- \n- \"311\"\n',1,1,6,'8.5 x 6.6','1870','1870',51,'vol. 1 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','0014640d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57206~100301','Depicts Waverley Hall and a small lake in front of it, with Waverley on horseback with his troop, waving goodbye to the occupants of Waverley-Honour. Also appears in 1886 reprinting: see image. ','2009-05-29 15:28:34','2009-09-11 09:57:01'),(1087,'CEN0003',11,'Hatteraick and Meg Merrilees [sic] surprised by Mannering ','--- \n- \"252\"\n- \"42\"\n- \"63\"\n- \"265\"\n- \"142\"\n',189,171,6,'12.5 x 8.1','1870','1870',51,'vol. 2 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Guy Mannering, speaking with Dirk Hatteraick and Meg Merrilies. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Ay, ay, sir, I am Dirk Hatteraick of the Yungfrauw Hagenslaapen. I am not ashamed of my name nor of my vessel.’ [Ch. 4; EEWN 2, 25.26-29].','2009-05-29 15:30:29','2009-06-26 13:36:11'),(1088,'CEN0004',11,'Ellangowan Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"108\"\n- \"129\"\n',1,171,6,'7 x 6.8','1870','1870',51,'vol. 2 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts old Ellangowan Castle by moonlight, with a single figure with a walking stick approaching from the path below. ','2009-05-29 15:32:14','2009-06-12 19:09:53'),(1089,'CEN0005',3,'Sir Arthur and Douterswivel Searching for the Treasure','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"277\"\n- \"98\"\n- \"361\"\n',1,171,6,'12.4 x 8','1871','1871',51,'vol. 3 (frontispiece)','EUL, RB.S.280-302','','Depicts Sir Arthur Wardour and Douterswivel standing in the ruins of St Ruth\'s with a fire and several tools at their feet. Accompanying quoted text: \'\"What was that echo? . . . the terrified adept.\' [Ch. 21; EEWN 3, 172.11-22]. This vol. is missing in NLS copy used for entries elsewhere for this edition; the date 1871 (rather than 1870) for this vol. reflects that the EUL copy was a later reprinting. ','2009-05-29 15:39:01','2009-08-17 17:02:00'),(1090,'CEN0006',3,'Oldbuck at Breakfast','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"66\"\n- \"78\"\n- \"79\"\n',1,171,6,'7 x 6.8','1871','1871',51,'vol. 3 (title-page vignette)','EUL, RB.S.280-302','','Depicts Jonathan Oldbuck reading a book as he drinks tea, with two female figures, one representing his sister Griselda, also sitting at the table, and the other probably his niece Maria, standing behind. This vol. is missing in NLS copy used for entries elsewhere for this edition; the date 1871 (rather than 1870) for this vol. reflects that the EUL copy was a later reprinting. ','2009-05-29 15:44:05','2009-08-17 17:03:27'),(1091,'CEN0007',23,'Rob Roy in the Crypt of Glasgow Cathedral','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"61\"\n',1,171,6,'11.3 x 8.1','1870','1870',51,'vol. 4 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Rob Roy in the foreground, with his back to the viewer, hidden by a pillar from the congregants in the crypt of the Glasgow Cathedral, as he speaks to Frank Osbaldistone who stands facing the preacher. Accompanying quoted text: ‘I had not resumed . . . do not look back.’ [Ch. 20; EEWN 5, 163.41-43].','2009-05-29 15:51:19','2009-06-12 19:27:41'),(1092,'CEN0008',23,'Balquhidder Churchyard','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"245\"\n',1,165,6,'7 x 7','1870','1870',51,'vol. 4 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Balquhidder Churchyard (ruins), with one small figure standing among the graves in the foreground. ','2009-05-29 15:58:02','2009-05-29 15:58:02'),(1093,'CEN0009',18,'Death of Old Mortality','--- \n- \"77\"\n- \"21\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"178\"\n',191,172,6,'11.5 x 8.3','1870','1870',51,'vol. 5 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the dying Old Mortality, lying prostrate on the side of the road, with his white pony standing by his side. Accompanying quoted text: ‘About the beginning . . . his dying master.’ [Ch. 1; EEWN 4a, 12.25.28]','2009-05-29 16:06:52','2009-06-26 13:37:01'),(1094,'CEN0010',18,'Bothwell Brig','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"96\"\n',1,165,6,'6.7 x 7','1870','1870',51,'vol. 5 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts a group of soldiers carrying weapons crossing Bothwell Bridge, with several figures at the rear on horseback.','2009-05-29 16:16:24','2009-06-12 19:16:07'),(1096,'CEN0011',16,'Dalgetty in the Duke’s Chapel','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"272\"\n- \"273\"\n',60,165,6,'12.6 x 8.1','1870','1870',51,'vol. 6 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Dalgetty kneeling on a cushion and peering through a curtain in the Marquis of Argyle\'s private gallery in the chapel at Inverary, with Ranald MacEagh looking on. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Never (although . . . of the audience.’ [Ch. 14; EEWN 7b, 110.37-38]. A reengraving of #NTE0012. ','2009-05-29 16:19:50','2009-06-12 19:28:23'),(1097,'CEN0012',5,'Elshie, the Black Dwarf','--- \n- \"254\"\n',191,1,6,'7.8 x 7.2','1870','1870',51,'vol. 6 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the Black Dwarf, walking with and apparently speaking to a goat.','2009-05-29 16:30:29','2009-05-29 16:30:29'),(1098,'CEN0013',12,'Meeting of the Sisters','--- \n- \"279\"\n- \"23\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"280\"\n',60,1,6,'12.5 x 8','1870','1870',51,'vol. 7 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Jeanie Deans embracing her sister Effie in confinement in the Edinburgh Tolbooth, with Ratcliffe the jailer  holding a key by an open shutter. A reengraving of #NTE0009. Accompanying quoted text: ‘With a gentleness . . . a scene so sorrowful.’ [Ch. 21; EEWN 6, 185.21-23].\r\n\r\n','2009-05-29 16:32:11','2009-06-12 19:28:38'),(1099,'CEN0014',12,'The Old Tolbooth, or Heart of Mid-Lothian','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"109\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"251\"\n- \"85\"\n',1,1,6,'6.9 x 6.9','1870','1870',51,'vol. 7 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts several small figures carrying planks and spears around a flaming barrel, in front of the Old Tolbooth of Edinburgh, with St Giles Cathedral visible on the right. ','2009-05-29 16:41:04','2009-05-29 16:41:04'),(1100,'CEN0015',6,'Ravenswood and Lucy at the Mermaiden’s Well ','--- \n- \"2\"\n- \"191\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"86\"\n',60,165,6,'12.6 x 8.1','1870','1870',51,'vol. 8 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Edgar Ravenswood with arm round Lucy Ashton by the fountain, with dead raven and arrow at feet, as Ravenswood looks around for the marksman. Accompanying quoted text: ‘As they arose . . . an old oak.’ [Ch. 20]. A reengraving of #NTE0011.','2009-05-29 16:48:05','2009-08-16 20:25:37'),(1101,'CEN0016',6,'Wolf’s Crag (Fast Castle)','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"378\"\n',1,1,6,'7 x 7.4','1870','1870',51,'vol. 8 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Fast Castle, with two small figures on horseback riding away, the foremost apparently waving to the other.','2009-05-29 16:51:52','2009-06-12 19:17:56'),(1102,'CEN0017',14,'Richard in the Hermit’s Cell','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"285\"\n',1,165,6,'12.6 x 8','1870','1870',51,'vol. 9 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Friar Tuck (as the Clerk of Copmanhurst) delivering a tray of food to Richard, who sits by a table, as two dogs look on. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Out of the recesses . . . of unusual dimensions.’ [Ch. 16; EEWN 8, 145.12-14].','2009-05-29 16:53:24','2009-06-26 13:37:44'),(1103,'CEN0018',14,'Rebecca on the Turret of Torquilstone','--- \n- \"55\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"87\"\n',1,1,6,'7 x 7','1870','1870',51,'vol. 9 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Rebecca standing in a doorway on the turret of Castle Torquilstone and looking below.','2009-05-29 16:55:00','2009-05-29 16:55:00'),(1104,'CEN0019',17,'Father Philip and Elspeth','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"287\"\n- \"341\"\n',189,171,6,'12.5 x 8','1870','1870',51,'vol. 10 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Father Philip holding a book, speaking to Elspeth, who stands anxiously by. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Now, by mine order . . . this perilous volume.’ [Ch. 5; EEWN 9, 60.29-32].','2009-05-29 17:02:16','2009-06-12 19:19:54'),(1105,'CEN0020',17,'Melrose Abbey','--- \n- \"38\"\n- \"219\"\n- \"288\"\n',1,1,6,'7 x 7','1870','1870',51,'vol. 10 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Melrose Abbey, with a group of monks processing in the foreground.','2009-06-01 20:30:40','2009-06-01 20:30:59'),(1106,'CEN0021',1,'The Queen at Lochleven ','--- \n- \"127\"\n- \"122\"\n',189,171,6,'12.5 x 8','1871','1871',51,'vol. 11 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Queen Mary speaking with Lord Lindesay as others look on. Accompanying quoted text: ‘We fear we have detained you . . . on such ceremonies.—Page 232.’ [Ch. 21; EEWN 10, 194-95].','2009-06-01 20:32:16','2009-06-12 19:20:27'),(1107,'CEN0022',1,'Lochleven Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"124\"\n- \"128\"\n',1,1,6,'6.7 x 7.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 11 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Loch Leven Castle, with a boat approaching it on the lake. 	','2009-06-01 20:34:07','2009-06-01 20:35:11'),(1108,'CEN0023',15,'The Countess Amy and Janet','--- \n- \"32\"\n- \"131\"\n- \"89\"\n',189,1,6,'12.5 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 12 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Amy Robsart admiring her new jewellery in the mirror as Janet Foster looks on. Accompanying quoted text: ‘”Thou sayest true . . . fair and polished surface.’ [Ch. 6; EEWN 11, 49.17-23].','2009-06-01 20:36:57','2009-06-12 19:21:07'),(1109,'CEN0024',15,'Kenilworth Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"257\"\n- \"244\"\n',190,1,6,'6.1 x 7.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 12 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Kenilworth Castle from across a river, with two tiny figures visible on the opposite bank. ','2009-06-01 20:38:38','2009-06-01 20:38:38'),(1110,'CEN0025',20,'The Sisters and Norna','--- \n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',1,171,6,'12.5 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 13 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Minna and Brenda Troil standing over the kneeling figure of Norna. Accompanying quoted text: ‘They knew the voice . . . chimney of the apartment.’ [Ch. 19; EEWN 12, 176.39-42].','2009-06-01 20:40:03','2009-06-12 19:21:46'),(1111,'CEN0026',20,'Roost of Sumburgh','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"140\"\n',190,1,6,'5.9 x 7.5','1871','1871',51,'vol. 13 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts a large rock rising from the sea. Similar but not identical to #NTD0025.','2009-06-01 20:41:16','2009-06-12 19:22:21'),(1112,'CEN0027',10,'Captain Colpepper’s Visit to Nigel in Alsatia.—Chap. XXIII','--- \n- \"295\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"293\"\n- \"294\"\n',189,171,6,'8.2 x 12.4','1871','1871',51,'vol. 14 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Captain Colepepper introducing himself to Nigel, with Trapbois looking on behind a table, on which stands a large tankard [Ch. 33].','2009-06-01 20:42:54','2009-06-12 19:30:36'),(1113,'CEN0028',10,'The Tower of London','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"322\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"244\"\n',192,1,6,'7 x 7.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 14 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the Tower of London from the Thames, with several boats on the river.','2009-06-01 20:48:25','2009-06-01 20:48:25'),(1114,'CEN0029',19,'Sir Geoffrey Peveril arresting Major Bridgenorth in his Pursuit of the Countess of Derby.—Chap. VII.','--- \n- \"154\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"297\"\n',24,1,6,'12.6 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 15 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Sir Geoffrey Peveril mounted on Black Hastings impeding the path of Major Ralph Bridgenorth, who rides a white stallion [Ch. 7]. A reengraving of #NTE0022.','2009-06-01 20:50:12','2009-06-12 19:31:26'),(1115,'CEN0030',19,'Peak Castle, Derbyshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"244\"\n',192,1,6,'7.5 x 6.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 15 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Peveril Castle on the top of a craggy hill, with a stream visible in the foreground.','2009-06-01 20:51:46','2009-06-12 19:32:03'),(1116,'CEN0031',21,'Quentin Rescuing Isabella de Croye.—Chap. XXIIQuentin Rescuing Isabella de Croye.—Chap. XXII','--- \n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"178\"\n',1,171,6,'12.3 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 16 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Quentin Durward carrying the prostrate figure of Isabella de Croye, through a street, as three figures follow behind them.','2009-06-01 20:53:34','2009-06-01 20:53:34'),(1117,'CEN0032',21,'Plessis les Tours','--- \n- \"311\"\n',1,1,6,'6 x 7.5','1871','1871',51,'vol. 16 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Plessis les Tours, with two tiny figures walking on the path in front of it. ','2009-06-01 20:54:26','2009-06-01 20:54:26'),(1118,'CEN0033',24,'The Disappointed Duellists.—Chap. XIII','--- \n- \"481\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"403\"\n- \"482\"\n- \"171\"\n',189,171,6,'8.2 x 12.4','1871','1871',51,'vol. 17 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Sir Bingo Binks waiting at the Buckstane for his duel with Tyrrel, with his second Captain MacTurk pulling out a large pocket-watch to counter the evidence of Dr Quackleben and his watch, and with Mr Winterblossom (hat in hand) also in the party [Ch. 13]. ','2009-06-01 20:55:59','2009-06-26 13:38:46'),(1119,'CEN0034',24,'The Cleikum Inn','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"240\"\n',1,171,6,'6.5 x 7.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 17 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the Cleikum Inn, with several animals in the yard and a woman standing in the doorway, as a figure on horseback enters through the gate.','2009-06-01 20:57:48','2009-06-01 20:57:48'),(1120,'CEN0035',22,'Peter Peebles at Mr. Fairford’s—Page 152','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"302\"\n- \"483\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"187\"\n',189,1,6,'8 x 12.5','1871','1871',51,'vol. 18 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Peter Peebles eating a cold meal at the Fairfords\' home, while Alan Fairford and his father attend to legal papers relating to his case [Letter 13].','2009-06-01 20:59:22','2009-08-16 20:26:03'),(1121,'CEN0036',22,'The Solway','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"140\"\n',1,1,6,'6.8 x 7.4','1871','1871',51,'vol. 18 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the Solway Firth, with boats on the water and two figures riding past on horseback.','2009-06-01 21:00:52','2009-06-01 21:00:52'),(1122,'CEN0037',4,'Eveline Waiting on the Wounded Damian.—Chap. XXVII.','--- \n- \"484\"\n- \"190\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"330\"\n- \"178\"\n',1,171,6,'8.2 x 12.7','1871','1871',51,'vol. 19 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Eveline Bereger attending to the wounded Damian de Lacy, who lies on a bed, as the page Amelot pulls back the curtain [Ch. 27].','2009-06-01 21:02:33','2009-06-12 19:40:42'),(1123,'CEN0038',4,'Harlech Castle, North Wales','--- \n- \"14\"\n',1,1,6,'6.2 x 7.3','1871','1871',51,'vol. 19 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Harlech Castle in North Wales, as the prototype for the fictional castle ‘Garde Doloureuse’, with a stream in the foreground.','2009-06-01 21:03:37','2009-06-01 21:03:37'),(1124,'CEN0039',26,'Sir Kenneth before the King','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"405\"\n- \"196\"\n',1,171,6,'12.6 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 20 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Richard Coeur-de-Lion holding an axe, as Kenneth stands before him looking contrite, as Thomas de Multon and another knight look on. Accompanying quoted text: ‘”A traitor” . . . ever near his bed.’ [Ch. 15].','2009-06-01 21:05:12','2009-06-12 19:41:48'),(1125,'CEN0040',26,'Diamond of the Desert','--- \n- \"352\"\n- \"49\"\n',1,1,6,'7.1 x 7.6','1871','1871',51,'vol. 20 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts a covered well under group of palm trees, on the edge of water, with a single small figure on horseback visible on the left.','2009-06-01 21:10:03','2009-06-30 08:07:54'),(1126,'CEN0041',29,'Sir Henry Lee Receiving the Warrant.—Chap. II.','--- \n- \"188\"\n- \"201\"\n- \"200\"\n- \"206\"\n',189,171,6,'8.1 x 12.6','1871','1871',51,'vol. 21 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Sir Henry and Alice Lee sitting on a bench in the park of Woodstock, confronted by Joseph Tomkins, who presents a scroll [Ch. 2].','2009-06-01 21:11:38','2009-06-01 21:11:38'),(1127,'CEN0042',29,'Town of Woodstock','--- \n- \"354\"\n',193,1,6,'6.7 x 7.3','1871','1871',51,'vol. 21 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the town of Woodstock, through a grove of trees, with a single figure walking along a path on the left.','2009-06-01 21:14:38','2009-06-01 21:14:38'),(1128,'CEN0043',9,'The Fair Maid and her Valentine','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"209\"\n',1,172,6,'12.2 x 8.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 22 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Catharine Glover leaning over Henry Gow as he sleeps in a chair. Accompanying quoted text: ‘”He looks very stern . . . when he awakes.”’ [Ch. 5; EEWN 21, 49.33-34].','2009-06-01 21:16:27','2009-06-12 19:42:31'),(1129,'CEN0044',9,'City of Perth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"243\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"213\"\n- \"354\"\n',1,1,6,'6 x 7.7','1871','1871',51,'vol. 22 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the city of Perth from across water, with a bridge and mountains visible in the distance.','2009-06-01 21:18:12','2009-06-26 13:44:54'),(1130,'CEN0045',2,'Anne of Geierstein in the Dungeon','--- \n- \"223\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"217\"\n- \"85\"\n',1,1,6,'12.5 x 8.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 23 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Anne of Geierstein holding aloft a burning torch, along with the Black Priest of St Paul\'s (actually Count Albert of Geierstein) and the liberated Arthur Philipson. Accompanying quoted text: ‘\"Speak, young man . . . to my instruction.\"’ [Ch. 15; EEWN 23, 156.27-28].','2009-06-01 21:19:35','2009-06-26 13:47:16'),(1131,'CEN0046',2,'Mount Pilatus: Switzerland','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"88\"\n',1,1,6,'8 x 7.3','1871','1871',51,'vol. 23 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts a castle on Mount Pilatus in Switzerland, with two small figures visible, one leading a mule, and another kneeling before an effigy of the crucified Jesus. ','2009-06-01 21:22:05','2009-06-01 21:22:05'),(1132,'CEN0047',8,'The Varangian and Count Robert ','--- \n- \"228\"\n- \"226\"\n',194,1,6,'11.9 x 8.1','1871','1871',51,'vol. 24 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Count Robert and Hereward standing before a doorway. Accompanying quoted text: ‘They stood before . . . was about to knock.—Page 240.’ [Ch. 18; EEWN 23a, 189.21-22].','2009-06-01 21:24:37','2009-06-26 13:47:38'),(1133,'CEN0048',8,'Constantinople','--- \n- \"354\"\n',1,165,6,'5.2 x 7.5','1871','1871',51,'vol. 24 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts the city of Constantinople from across the water.','2009-06-01 21:26:42','2009-06-01 21:26:42'),(1134,'CEN0049',25,'Hartley and Menie Gray','--- \n- \"352\"\n- \"34\"\n- \"236\"\n- \"474\"\n',1,171,6,'12.6 x 8.2','1871','1871',51,'vol. 25 (frontispiece)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Adam Hartley on one knee kissing the hand of Menie Gray, who looks the other way in a distraught posture, as Hartley bids her farewell after their interview at Madame Montreville’s residence in India. Accompanying quoted text: ‘He fell on one knee and pressed to his lips the hand which she held out to him.’ [Ch. 25; EEWN 20, 260.20-21].','2009-06-01 21:28:38','2009-06-12 19:45:07'),(1135,'CEN0050',7,'Castle Douglas : Lanarkshire','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n',193,118,6,'5.8 x 7.5','1871','1871',51,'vol. 25 (title-page vignette)','NLS, Hall.279.b-c','','Depicts Castle Douglas in the background, with two small figures, one on horseback, and a dog visible in the foreground.','2009-06-01 21:30:06','2009-06-01 21:30:06'),(1136,'EPI0001',20,'Sir Walter Scott in his Study (Castle Street, Edinburgh)','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"84\"\n- \"94\"\n',45,136,3,'24.5 x 18.8','1871','1871',52,'frontispiece','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','0030017d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~57925~100345','Depicts Scott consulting a book seated at his desk, with a pen in his right hand, and a sleeping cat at one end of the desk, with his dog Maida beneath on the floor; a map, portrait of his mother, and suit of armour are among objects visible behind him. See Russell, Francis, \'Portraits of Sir Walter Scott\' (London: White Brothers, 1987), Item 67, p. 43).','2009-06-13 10:06:51','2009-09-11 09:59:05'),(1137,'EPI0002',20,'The Udaller’s Home--Magnus Troil and his Family','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"384\"\n- \"134\"\n',135,139,3,'25 x 17.2','1871','1871',52,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','','Depicts Magnus Troil seated on a chair, with Brenda on a footstool beneath him, with a music sheet in one hand, and Minna standing by the fireplace. Accompanying quoted text: ‘He was an honest, plain Zetland gentleman . . . circulated in the evening’ [Chs 1 & 3; EEWN 12, 6, 23]. ','2009-06-13 10:16:55','2009-06-13 10:16:55'),(1138,'EPI0003',20,'The Storm with Mordaunt rescuing Cleveland','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n',143,128,3,'16.7 x 25.2','1871','1871',52,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','0030045d.jpg#http://images.is.ed.ac.uk/luna/servlet/detail/UoEwal~1~1~58393~100373','Depicts Mordaunt Mertoun attending to the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland on the shore, with the figure of Bryce the Pedlar appearing in the distance. Accompanying quoted text: ‘When the vessel split . . . eagerly, but in vain’ [Ch.7; EEWN 12; 69-70]. Matches #BOR0125, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 10:20:16','2009-09-11 10:00:52'),(1139,'EPI0004',20,'Norna performing her curative spell on Minna Troil','--- \n- \"327\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"384\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',195,138,3,'18.4 x 25.3','1871','1871',52,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','','Depicts Ulla Troil standing beside a stone table, pointing towards the various implements placed on it, which include a dish of hot charcoal, with Minna seated at its side and gazing away; Magnus Troil and Brenda, seated at the other end of the table, watch on anxiously. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Minna moved with slow and tremulous step . . . with an intense glow’ [Ch. 28; EEWN 12, 262-63].','2009-06-13 10:24:28','2009-06-13 10:27:16'),(1140,'EPI0005',20,'The altercation between the Pedlar and Cleveland at the Fair of Kirkwall','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"485\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"107\"\n',196,139,3,'18 x 25.3','1871','1871',52,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','','Depicts Cleveland and Bunce, both elegantly dressed, in conversation with Bryce the Pedlar, who stands before his stall, with a leering expression on his face; other market stalls are visible behind. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Purchase! You beggarly vagrant . . . might concern him’ [Ch. 32; EEWN 12, 201-02].','2009-06-13 10:32:53','2009-06-13 10:32:53'),(1141,'EPI0006',20,'Minna taking the pistol from Bunce to defend herself and her sister','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"485\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',150,138,3,'17 x 25.8','1871','1871',52,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.3','','Depicts Bunce on the quarter-deck of his ship handing a pistol to Minna Troil, whose sister Brenda stands behind as they are about to leave. Accompanying quoted text: ‘He offered the minstrel . . . for Cleveland’s sake’ [Ch. 36; EEWN 12, 341-42]. Matches #BOR0131, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 10:37:31','2009-06-28 13:56:34'),(1142,'ESR0001',24,'Meg Dods / ‘And what for no’','--- \n- \"402\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"84\"\n',151,173,1,'22 x 16','1882','1882',53,'frontispiece','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts Meg Dods, with a white cap and hands on hips, against the doorway of the Cleikum Inn [Ch. 1]. Matches #BOR0170, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 10:39:33','2009-06-13 10:43:48'),(1143,'ESR0002',24,'St. Ronan’s Village','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"244\"\n',151,174,3,'22 x 13.5','1882','1882',53,'title-page vignette','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts a small settlement with cottages and a church in a valley beneath an old castle, with a river flowing in front, and hills in the background [Ch. 1]. Matches #BOR0171, but with different engraver. ','2009-06-13 10:46:25','2009-06-28 13:57:40'),(1144,'ESR0003',24,'Shaws Castle (Traquair House, with Gateway)','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"244\"\n',151,139,3,'23.5 x 18','1882','1882',53,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts (fictional) Shaws Castle, and gateway with decorative bears, as modelled on Traquair House in Peeblesshire, with a river flowing in front [Ch. 20]. Matches #BOR0175, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 10:49:41','2009-06-13 10:49:41'),(1145,'ESR0004',24,'Meeting of Tyrrel and Miss Mowbray','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"401\"\n',197,52,3,'17 x 25.7','1882','1882',53,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts Frank Tyrrel standing on a bridle-path in a wooded area and  impeding the progress of Clara Mowbray, who rides side-saddle in a riding-dress and hat, and who clutches her hands together with a look of surprise [Ch. 9].','2009-06-13 10:53:45','2009-06-13 10:53:45'),(1146,'ESR0005',24,'Captain MacTurk, Sir Bingo Binks, WinterBlossom, and Dr. Quackleben, waiting for Tyrrel','--- \n- \"481\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"403\"\n- \"482\"\n- \"171\"\n',155,175,3,'17 x 26.7','1882','1882',53,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts Sir Bingo Binks with his second Captain MacTurk at the Buckstane waiting for Tyrrel’s appearance for a duel; Mr Winterblossom and Dr Quackleben are seated alongside, with case containing duelling pistols (marked with MacTurk’s name) between them, and with Quackleben looking at his watch [Ch. 13]. Matches #BOR0173, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 10:57:49','2009-06-13 10:59:06'),(1147,'ESR0006',24,'Meg Dods arriving in her ‘Whiskey’ at the Lawyer’s','--- \n- \"486\"\n- \"402\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"235\"\n',198,103,3,'18.5 x 24.5','1882','1882',53,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts Meg Dods being escorted into the premises of the lawyer Mr Bindloose, having arrived in her Carriage, which is being led away down the street with the horse by an ostler [Ch. 14].','2009-06-13 11:03:40','2009-06-13 11:05:06'),(1148,'ESR0007',24,'Mr. Touchwood introduces himself to the Rev. W. Cargill in his Study','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"173\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"174\"\n',149,139,3,'18.5 x 25','1882','1882',53,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.23','','Depicts Mr Touchwood, cane in hand, disturbing the Rev. Josiah Cargill, the latter at his desk studying a large folio book [Ch. 17]. Matches #BOR0174, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 11:08:40','2009-06-13 11:08:40'),(1149,'ERE0001',22,'Sunset on the Solway Sands--Storm Rising--Horsemen Hunting Salmon','--- \n- \"29\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"140\"\n',143,130,3,'17.5 x 25','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts the Solway Firth near nightfall, with three horsemen on the sands holding spear. Accompanying quoted text: ‘I mentioned in my last … in the old tapestry’ [Letter 4; EEWN 17, 20.5-24]. Matches #BOR0182, but with different engraver. ','2009-06-13 11:11:24','2009-06-13 11:11:24'),(1150,'ERE0002',22,'The Supper Party at Redgauntlet’s Cottage','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"181\"\n- \"180\"\n- \"183\"\n- \"182\"\n- \"179\"\n',135,139,3,'17.5 x 25.5','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts Redgauntlet (‘Herries of Birrenswork’) at supper alongside Darsie Latimer, with Cristal Nixon and Mabel Moffat also at the table, and ‘Greenmantle’ (Lilias Redgauntlet) entering through a door. Accompanying quoted text: ‘He himself (Redgauntlet) assumed . . . if he had called?’ [Letter 4; EEWN 17, 28].','2009-06-13 11:14:27','2009-06-28 13:58:08'),(1151,'ERE0003',22,'Redgauntlet and Darsie Latimer on their way to Shepherd’s Bush intercepted by Joshua Geddes','--- \n- \"428\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"180\"\n- \"179\"\n- \"244\"\n',147,130,3,'16.2 x 27.2','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts (in the middle distance) Redgauntlet (‘Herries of Birrenswork’) on horseback, with Darsie Latimer on foot, meeting Joshua Geddes who is riding from the other direction; the group appear near the edge of a cliff, with a valley and river below. Accompanying quoted text: ‘My host threw himself . . . calls them, Quakers’ [Letter 6; EEWN 17.41-42].','2009-06-13 11:17:30','2009-06-28 13:58:42'),(1152,'ERE0004',22,'Steenie Steenson demanding a Receipt for his Rent from Sir Robert Redgauntlet / (Wandering Willie’s Tale)','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"185\"\n- \"184\"\n',149,139,3,'16.2 x 27.2','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts Steenie facing Sir Robert Redgauntlet and his roistering comrades, in what appears to him to be the hall at Redgauntlet Castle, having apparently received his rent receipt from Sir Robert. Accompanying quoted text: ‘So saying, he . . . and not to yours’ [Letter 11; EEWN 17.96-98].','2009-06-13 11:19:32','2009-06-13 11:19:32'),(1153,'ERE0005',22,'The Smuggler Brig, ‘Jumping Jenny,’ lying to, delivering her Cargo to boats alongside','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"142\"\n',199,140,3,'17.7 x 24.2','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts a sailing vessel moored in the Solway estuary and surrounded by a number of smaller fishing boats and their occupants. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The vessel accordingly . . . on shore . . . Twenty fishing barks . . . essential regularity’ [Ch. 15; EEWN 17, 263.11-18].','2009-06-13 11:22:56','2009-06-13 11:22:56'),(1154,'ERE0006',22,'Peter Peebles capturing Allan Fairford','--- \n- \"302\"\n- \"428\"\n- \"181\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"187\"\n',148,140,3,'18.5 x 24.8','1876','1876',54,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.9','','Depicts Alan Fairford seated alongside Greenmantle, who wears a riding dress, and being interrupted by Peter Peebles, who holds Alan’s shoulder with one hand and points a finger with the other; the figure at the door is apparently meant to represent Joshua Geddes. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Securing themselves . . . under certification’ [Ch. 23; EEWN 17, 367-68]. Matches #BOR0191, but with different engraver. Exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 205, 1876.','2009-06-13 11:26:40','2009-08-16 20:27:04'),(1155,'EFM0001',9,'The Glee Maiden','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"524\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"84\"\n',135,137,3,'26 x 16.8','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 1]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts The Glee Maiden in a doorway holding a viol and bow with a small dog with her on the doorstep. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Such was the damsel . . . amusement by her art’ [Ch. 11; see EEWN 21, 106.30-37]. Matches #BOR0215, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 11:28:50','2009-06-29 12:26:31'),(1156,'EFM0002',9,'Catherine and Father Clement','--- \n- \"214\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"72\"\n- \"88\"\n',150,139,3,'24.5 x 18.5','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 2]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts Catharine Glover listening devoutly to instructions from Father Clement, besides a rock beneath the hill of Kinnoul. Accompanying quoted text: ‘One of the most beautiful prospects . . . devoutly joined’ [Ch. 14; EEWN 21, 145.30-36].','2009-06-13 11:32:08','2009-06-28 14:00:11'),(1157,'EFM0003',9,'Boat Scene on Loch Tay','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"524\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"215\"\n- \"212\"\n',143,130,3,'17.2 x 26','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 3]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts the sailing barge of the Duke of Rothesay on the River Tay, as it comes close to Henshaw’s boat, in which the Glee Maiden is just visible playing her viol. Accompanying quoted text: ‘The wilful Prince stepped . . . above the Tay’ [Ch. 30; EEWN 21, 27-36].','2009-06-13 11:34:54','2009-06-14 18:12:20'),(1158,'EFM0004',9,'The Gateway, Falkland Palace','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"129\"\n',151,175,3,'24.5 x 18','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 4]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts the towered gateway of Falkland Palace in near darkness, with a group of people assembling near the entrance. Accompanying quoted text: ‘At length the forest of Falkland . . . Duke of Albany’ [Ch. 30; EEWN 21, 325.36-38. Matches #BOR0219, which has the shortened title ‘Falkland Castle’, and different engraver.','2009-06-13 11:38:59','2009-08-16 20:27:51'),(1159,'EFM0005',9,'The Interview between the Gow Chrom and the Highlander, anent the Suit of Armour for the Chief','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"353\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"487\"\n',136,138,3,'17.4 x 26','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 5]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts Henry Gow in his smithy pulling a suit of chain-mail out of a chest to show the bearded Highlander Norman nan Ord who stands before him; several smith’s tools are visible on the floor. Accompanying quoted text: ‘Henry withdrew into his smithy . . . Chief’s upon these terms’ [Ch. 33; EEWN 21, 354-55].','2009-06-13 11:47:02','2009-06-13 11:55:06'),(1160,'EFM0006',9,'Campsie Linn','--- \n- \"244\"\n- \"351\"\n',200,139,3,'17.4 x 26','1878','1878',55,'[Plate 6]','EUL, Corson H.RAPFAS.17','','Depicts the Campsie Linn with rapids in the middle. Accompanying quoted text: ‘It is said, by one account, that the young Captain of Clan Quhele swam safe to shore, so far below the Linns of Campsie’ [Ch. 36; EEWN 21, 38-40].','2009-06-13 11:51:20','2009-08-16 20:28:52'),(1161,'BOR0079',14,'Rebecca and the Templar','--- \n- \"9\"\n- \"87\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Rebecca and Bois-Guilbert at opposite sides of a vaulted chamber, Bois-Guilbert with raised arm and Rebecca apparently shunning him.','2009-06-13 15:23:31','2009-06-13 15:25:00'),(1162,'BOR0080',14,'Rowena and the Palmer','--- \n- \"315\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"90\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16, facing p. 70','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ivanhoe disguised as a Palmer, bowing before the enthroned Lady Rowena in her apartment at Rotherwood [Ch. 6].','2009-06-13 15:27:33','2009-06-13 15:27:33'),(1163,'BOR0081',14,'Gurth in Isaac’s House','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"40\"\n- \"51\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"87\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Gurth heavily muffled in a cloak (and barely discernible), approaching the seated Isaac in his richly-furnished apartment, with the standing Rebecca in a white dress, and servants in attendance [Ch. 10]','2009-06-13 15:30:12','2009-06-29 12:10:36'),(1164,'BOR0082',14,'The Knight and Rowena','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"356\"\n- \"315\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"90\"\n- \"178\"\n',201,163,2,'13.3 x 8.8','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the wounded Ivanhoe after the tournament, lying with his helmet removed, and with Cedric in alarm intervening to keep Rowena apart from him [Ch.12]','2009-06-13 15:34:53','2009-06-13 15:34:53'),(1165,'BOR0083',14,'The Knight and the Hermitage','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"196\"\n',201,163,2,'14.2 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Richard Coeur de Lion in black armour on a white horse pointing his lance towards the door of the hermitage [Ch. 16].','2009-06-13 15:37:10','2009-06-13 15:37:10'),(1166,'BOR0084',14,'Rebecca and Urfried','--- \n- \"204\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"316\"\n',201,163,2,'13.4 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 16, facing p. 288','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ulrica seated at spinning wheel, in her cell, as Rebecca is presented to her, escorted by two men in disguise [Ch. 24].','2009-06-13 15:39:38','2009-06-13 15:39:38'),(1167,'BOR0085',14,'Rebecca and Rowena','--- \n- \"18\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"90\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the dark-haired Rebecca standing before the seated and fair-haired Rowena at their concluding meeting, with both women apparently holding aloft clothing from around their faces [Ch. 44].','2009-06-13 15:42:00','2009-06-13 15:42:00'),(1168,'BOR0086',14,'Ivanhoe Nursed by Rebecca','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"51\"\n- \"327\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"178\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ivanhoe reclining on a bed surrounded by tapestry, with Rebecca approaching to attend him, and a black attendant holding a tray with a phial behind her [Ch. 28].','2009-06-13 15:44:58','2009-06-13 15:44:58'),(1169,'BOR0087',14,'Rowena and de Bracy','--- \n- \"488\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"90\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Rowena on dark horse with the defeated and contrite-seeming De Bracy on foot beneath her, in Sherwood Forest [Ch. 32]','2009-06-13 15:48:51','2009-06-13 15:48:51'),(1170,'BOR0088',14,'Isaac before the Grand Master','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"6\"\n- \"34\"\n- \"51\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"320\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Isaac kneeling and kissing the ground before Lucas de Beaumanoir, the Grand Master of the Templars, in the garden at the Preceptory of Templestowe [Ch. 35]','2009-06-13 15:51:59','2009-06-13 15:51:59'),(1171,'BOR0089',14,'Rebecca and the Grand Master','--- \n- \"6\"\n- \"55\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"87\"\n- \"88\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Grand Master, on his elevated seat, holding a staff in one hand and with the other arm aloft, condemning Rebecca, who stands on a platform beneath him, and holds up what is presumably meant to be a scroll [Ch. 37].','2009-06-13 15:53:53','2009-06-13 15:53:53'),(1172,'BOR0090',14,'Rebecca at the Stake','--- \n- \"9\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"222\"\n- \"87\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 17, facing p. 320','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Rebecca in white dress with arms across her bosom in front of the already-smoking bonfire where she is to be burned, as Bois-Guilbert on horseback and holding lance leans down towards her [Ch. 43].','2009-06-13 15:56:12','2009-06-28 14:02:08'),(1173,'BOR0091',17,'Halbert and Catherine','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"342\"\n- \"323\"\n- \"343\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"96\"\n',169,176,2,'9.2 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 18 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the fully-armed Halbert Glendinning looking towards the body of Julian Avenel on the battlefield, with Catherine leaning over Julian and holding a child in her arms, and with two other bodies lying on the field; cavalry are visible in the distance [Ch. 36]. ','2009-06-13 16:03:05','2009-06-28 14:02:40'),(1174,'BOR0092',17,'Father Philip and the White Lady','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"288\"\n- \"341\"\n- \"244\"\n',169,164,2,'9.1 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 18, facing p. 54','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Father Philip on his mule confronting the white seated figure of the White Lady, who holds her hands to her head and looks towards the river [Ch. 5].','2009-06-13 16:08:49','2009-06-13 16:08:49'),(1175,'BOR0093',17,'Christie and the Sub-Prior','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"120\"\n- \"118\"\n- \"382\"\n- \"287\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"288\"\n',169,157,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 18, facing p. 96','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Christie of Clinthill, mounted in armour and holding out his lance, facing Father Eustace, whose mule is pointing in the opposite direction, outside the Tower of Glendearg, with Elspeth Glendinning watching from the doorway [Ch. 9].','2009-06-13 16:16:01','2009-06-13 16:16:01'),(1176,'BOR0094',17,'The White Lady at the Fountain','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"121\"\n',169,177,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 18, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, sword pointing down to the ground, standing before the White Lady of Avenel at the fountain in the ravine [Ch. 12].','2009-06-13 16:17:47','2009-06-13 16:19:40'),(1177,'BOR0095',17,'The Duel','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"121\"\n- \"242\"\n- \"290\"\n',169,156,2,'9.7 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 18, facing p. 228','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the duel between Sir Piercie Shafton and Halbert Glendinning, with Shafton on the ground clutching his chest [Chs 21 & 22].','2009-06-13 16:22:57','2009-06-13 16:22:57'),(1178,'BOR0096',17,'The Escape of Sir Piercie','--- \n- \"15\"\n- \"318\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"290\"\n',169,142,2,'9.3 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 19 (frontispiece)','','','Depicts Sir Piercie Shafton on horseback, with Mysie Happer seated behind him, galloping away through farm animals from the Tower of Glendearg [Ch. 28].','2009-06-13 16:39:35','2009-06-13 16:39:35'),(1179,'BOR0097',17,'Halbert and The White Lady','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"317\"\n- \"289\"\n- \"121\"\n',169,161,2,'9.2 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 19, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, at the edge of a bed and with sword pointing downwards to the floor, facing the floating figure of the White Lady of Avenel, who points at him with the forefinger of one arm, while his brother Edward lies behind him in the bed [Ch. 20].','2009-06-13 16:41:28','2009-06-13 16:41:28'),(1180,'BOR0098',17,'Halbert and Warden','--- \n- \"121\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"119\"\n',169,144,2,'9 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 19, facing p. 64','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning imploring Henry Warden, who appears like a pilgrim with hat, walking staff, and cloak, to follow him to the glen where he believes the wounded Sir Piercie Shafton lies [Ch. 22].','2009-06-13 16:43:29','2009-06-13 16:43:29'),(1181,'BOR0099',17,'The Sub-Prior and Warden','--- \n- \"120\"\n- \"118\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"88\"\n- \"119\"\n',169,178,2,'9.2 x 14.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 19, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Reformer Henry Warden as a prisoner, hands tied behind his back, before Father Eustace in the Monastery of Saint Mary’s, with Christie of Clinthill standing guard in the doorway [Ch. 31].','2009-06-13 16:46:28','2009-06-13 16:47:12'),(1182,'BOR0100',17,'Halbert Meeting the Army','--- \n- \"121\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"96\"\n',169,142,2,'9.2 x 14.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 19, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Halbert Glendinning, accompanied by his pedlar guide, being questioned as to their identity by the foremost rider in the Earl of Murray’s army [Ch. 35].','2009-06-13 16:49:22','2009-06-13 16:49:22'),(1183,'BOR0101',1,'Roland at Langside','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"123\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"96\"\n',169,176,2,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 20 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme in full armour on horseback, observing the rash attack of the vanguard of Mary’s army at the Battle of Langside, from a track half concealed by bushes [Ch. 37].','2009-06-13 16:51:15','2009-06-13 16:51:15'),(1184,'BOR0102',1,'Roland Dismissed','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"112\"\n- \"324\"\n- \"123\"\n',169,146,2,'14 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 20, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme walking away, hands folded behind back, having been dismissed by the Lady of Avenel, who stands before her chair with Lilias holding onto its back and looking on [Ch. 5].','2009-06-13 16:53:37','2009-06-13 16:53:37'),(1185,'BOR0103',1,'Catherine and Roland','--- \n- \"123\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"325\"\n',169,179,2,'9.13 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 20, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme, seated on a trunk, gazing at Catherine Seyton, who half faces away from him as if attending to her sewing [Chs 10 & 11].','2009-06-13 16:58:40','2009-06-13 16:59:46'),(1186,'BOR0104',1,'Howleglas and the Abbot','--- \n- \"395\"\n- \"33\"\n- \"288\"\n',169,180,2,'9.5 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 20, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the entry of the Abbot of Unreason with other revellers into the Church of Saint Mary, with the true Abbot and the monks looking on [Ch. 14].','2009-06-13 17:01:52','2009-06-13 17:02:53'),(1187,'BOR0105',1,'Roland and the Setons [sic]','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"123\"\n',169,147,2,'9 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 20, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme being confronted in a hall by a group of the Seyton family, with swords drawn [Ch. 17].','2009-06-13 17:04:34','2009-06-28 14:03:24'),(1188,'BOR0106',1,'What Seekest Here?','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"414\"\n- \"123\"\n',169,157,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 21 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme having entered a cottage facing Magdalen Graeme, who crouches in the hearth, wearing a dark cape and peaked cap [Ch. 28].','2009-06-13 17:06:11','2009-06-13 17:06:11'),(1189,'BOR0107',1,'Roland and Douglas','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"489\"\n- \"29\"\n- \"123\"\n',169,156,2,'9 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 21, facing p. 64','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme (with oars) and Gorge Douglas (on feet, trimming fishing-tackle) in a rowing boat on Loch Leven [Ch. 24].','2009-06-13 17:13:55','2009-06-13 17:13:55'),(1190,'BOR0108',1,'Madman, Let Me Go','--- \n- \"123\"\n- \"129\"\n- \"325\"\n',169,181,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 21, facing p. 160','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Roland Graeme confronting and grasping the mantle of the disguised Catherine Seyton at night-time outside Loch Leven Castle [Ch. 30].','2009-06-13 17:16:52','2009-06-13 17:17:37'),(1191,'BOR0109',1,'Dryfesdale and Lady Lochleven','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"490\"\n- \"491\"\n',169,142,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 21, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Lady Douglas in dark clothing listening to the disclosures of Dryfesdale, who stands with arms folded and sword behind his back; Lady Douglas’s Bible lies open at her feet [Ch. 32].','2009-06-13 17:20:13','2009-06-13 17:20:13'),(1192,'BOR0110',1,'Death of Dryfesdale','--- \n- \"491\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"229\"\n- \"292\"\n',169,147,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 21, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Henry Seyton, dressed as a page, standing with dagger drawn over the fallen body of Dryfesdale, as the landlord of the inn looks on in alarm [Ch. 33].','2009-06-13 17:22:03','2009-06-13 17:22:03'),(1193,'BOR0111',15,'Lambourne at the Inn','--- \n- \"49\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"350\"\n',201,163,2,'13.4 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Michael Lambourne on his horse in the courtyard of the inn at Cumnor, with the ostler attending to him [Ch. 1].','2009-06-13 17:23:51','2009-06-13 17:23:51'),(1194,'BOR0112',15,'Tressilian Reproaching Amy','--- \n- \"31\"\n- \"350\"\n- \"89\"\n- \"345\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Amy Robsart shrinking back from the imploring grasp of Tressilian, at Cumnor Place, with Lambourne and Foster rushing into the apartment [Ch. 4].','2009-06-13 17:25:50','2009-06-30 08:17:20'),(1195,'BOR0113',15,'Leicester Departing from Amy','--- \n- \"18\"\n- \"56\"\n- \"89\"\n',201,163,2,'13.4 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Amy Robsart standing between curtains in her bedchamber, with hair loose and in a white silk robe, with Leicester holding out his arms to her [Ch. 7].','2009-06-13 17:27:17','2009-06-13 17:27:17'),(1196,'BOR0114',15,'Tressilian and Wayland','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"95\"\n- \"345\"\n',201,163,2,'13.4 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Tressilian moving with sword drawn towards Wayland Smith, who is holding a hammer as he fixes a horse-shoe, with Sludge (‘Flibbertigibbet’) watching in alarm behind [Ch. 10]. ','2009-06-13 19:28:22','2009-06-13 19:28:22'),(1197,'BOR0115',15,'Raleigh and the Queen','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"130\"\n- \"347\"\n- \"349\"\n- \"348\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22, facing p. 240','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Walter Raleigh laying his cloak on the ground before Queen Elizabeth, who walks on the arm of Lord Hunsdon, with other courtiers and a guardsman in the group [Ch. 15]. Title given as ‘Raleigh Sacrificing His Cloak’ in ‘List of Illustrations’.','2009-06-13 19:30:13','2009-06-13 19:30:13'),(1198,'BOR0116',15,'The Queen Writing on the Glass','--- \n- \"347\"\n- \"492\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 22, facing p. 304','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Queen Elizabeth completing the couplet of verse begun by Walter Raleigh on a window-pane, as she and Lady Paget stand on a staircase in the palace at Greenwich [Ch. 17].','2009-06-13 19:32:28','2009-06-28 14:04:38'),(1199,'BOR0117',15,'Amy and the Pedlar','--- \n- \"18\"\n- \"32\"\n- \"34\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"89\"\n- \"95\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Wayland Smith, as a pedlar, displaying clothing to Amy Robsart and Janet Foster in the garden-house at Cumnor Place [Ch. 20].','2009-06-13 19:34:19','2009-06-13 19:34:19'),(1200,'BOR0118',15,'Foster with the Poison','--- \n- \"31\"\n- \"32\"\n- \"327\"\n- \"89\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23, facing p. 70','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Antony Foster holding a flask of poison, with his daughter Janet and Amy Robsart holding on to each other, while two cups stand on a table [Ch. 22].','2009-06-13 19:35:56','2009-06-13 19:35:56'),(1201,'BOR0119',15,'Tressilian Sheltering Amy','--- \n- \"89\"\n- \"345\"\n',201,163,2,'13.4 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Amy Robsart kneeling on the floor, with Tressilian standing above her with arm extended, in an apartment at Kenilworth Castle [Ch. 26]. Title given as ‘Tressilian Yielding his Room to Amy’ in ‘List of Illustrations’.','2009-06-13 19:37:19','2009-06-13 19:37:19'),(1202,'BOR0120',15,'The Queen Questioning Amy','--- \n- \"347\"\n- \"352\"\n- \"89\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Queen Elizabeth holding out her hand to Amy Robsart, who stands beside a pedestal with a fountain flowing from its foot [Ch. 34].','2009-06-13 19:39:00','2009-06-13 19:39:00'),(1203,'BOR0121',15,'Varney, Leicester and Amy','--- \n- \"56\"\n- \"89\"\n- \"99\"\n',201,163,2,'13.3 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23, facing p. 252','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Amy Robsart, the Earl of Leicester, and Richard Varney together in private apartment at Kenilworth, with Amy standing in front of a large fireplace, and Varney and Leicester behind a table, apparently moving as if to leave [Ch. 35].','2009-06-13 19:41:11','2009-06-13 19:41:11'),(1204,'BOR0122',15,'The Duel Interrupted','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"56\"\n- \"346\"\n- \"345\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 23, facing p. 310','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Earl of Leicester in woodland near Kenilworth standing over the fallen figure of Tressilian, about to deliver the fatal blow, but interrupted by a boy from behind who grasps his sword arm [Ch. 39].','2009-06-13 19:44:33','2009-06-13 19:44:33'),(1205,'BOR0123',20,'Mordaunt in Yellowley\'s Cottage','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"494\"\n- \"493\"\n',162,179,2,'9 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 24 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Mordaunt Mertoun warming and drying himself against a peat hearth in the Yellowley’s cottage, as Triptolemus Yellowley (holding a duck-gun) and his sister Barbara look on in some consternation [Ch. 5].','2009-06-13 19:47:57','2009-06-28 14:05:29'),(1206,'BOR0124',20,'Scalloway Castle','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n',202,182,2,'13.7 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 24, facing p. 42','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Scalloway Castle (ruins) in Shetland, with rude cottages by its side, with two small figures and boats in the foreground [Ch. 4].','2009-06-13 19:50:49','2009-06-13 19:52:38'),(1207,'BOR0125',20,'Mordaunt Rescuing Cleveland','--- \n- \"138\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"135\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"139\"\n- \"178\"\n',143,152,2,'9 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 24, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Mordaunt Mertoun attending to the shipwrecked Clement Cleveland on the shore, with the figure of Bryce the Pedlar appearing in the distance [Ch. 7]. Matches #EPI0003, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 19:55:15','2009-06-13 19:55:15'),(1208,'BOR0126',20,'The Sword Dance','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"395\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"133\"\n',162,142,2,'13.8 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 24, facing p. 234','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts men and women performing the traditional sword-dance, with swords raised together, and with the figure of (apparently) Minna Troil prominent in front; a fiddler can also be seen behind [Ch. 15].','2009-06-13 19:57:33','2009-06-13 19:57:33'),(1209,'BOR0127',20,'Norna Telling Her Tale','--- \n- \"264\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n- \"137\"\n',162,146,2,'9.3 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 24, facing p. 294','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the seated figure of Norna crouching by the fireplace, with a lamp on the floor, and Minna Troil standing upright in front of her with her sister Brenda holding on behind [Ch. 19].','2009-06-13 19:59:14','2009-06-13 19:59:14'),(1210,'BOR0128',20,'Minna on the Cliff','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"143\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"134\"\n',203,180,2,'13.8 x 9.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 25 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Minna Troil on horseback at the edge of a cliff, with arms outstretched in the  direction of sea-birds in the sky [Ch. 27].','2009-06-13 20:02:06','2009-06-13 20:02:06'),(1211,'BOR0129',20,'Norna in the Churchyard','--- \n- \"109\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"495\"\n- \"137\"\n',204,183,2,'9 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 25, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Norna digging out sand from in front of the gravestone of Ribolt Troil, with Basil Mertoun observing from the side [Ch. 25].','2009-06-13 20:04:47','2009-06-13 20:06:01'),(1212,'BOR0130',20,'The Pirates\' Council','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"141\"\n- \"142\"\n',204,183,2,'9.2 x 14.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 25, facing p. 208','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the council of pirates around a table, with Captain Goffe pointing a pistol at the boatswain, while Clement Cleveland stands upright looking on [Ch. 34].','2009-06-13 20:08:41','2009-06-13 20:08:41'),(1213,'BOR0131',20,'Minna Taking the Pistol','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"485\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',150,146,2,'9.2 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 25, facing p. 250','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Bunce on the quarter-deck of his ship handing a pistol to Minna Troil, whose sister Brenda stands behind as they are about to leave [Ch. 36]. Matches #EPI0006, but with different engraver.','2009-06-13 20:13:14','2009-06-28 14:05:56'),(1214,'BOR0132',20,'The Stones of Stennis','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"136\"\n- \"140\"\n- \"133\"\n- \"134\"\n',159,152,2,'9 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 25, facing p. 304','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Minna Troil, accompanied by her sister Brenda, who is seated, standing among large upright stones, as the figure of Cleveland approaches from the direction of the sea, where the sun rises and a boat is seen on the horizon [Ch. 40].','2009-06-13 20:15:25','2009-06-13 20:15:25'),(1215,'BOR0133',10,'The Prentice Fight','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"33\"\n',156,145,2,'9 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 26 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts a well-dressed man, about to take out his weapon, being pulled away by a woman from the fray taking place in the street behind them. No specific incident identified. ','2009-06-13 20:18:12','2009-06-13 20:18:12'),(1216,'BOR0134',10,'Rickie Borne to Ramsay\'s Shop','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"151\"\n- \"145\"\n- \"497\"\n- \"178\"\n- \"496\"\n- \"147\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.6','1893','1893',44,'vol. 26, facing p. 22','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the stunned body of Richie Moniplies being carried into David Ramsay’s shop by his apprentices, with shoppers and onlookers looking at Ramsay’s wares [Ch. 2].','2009-06-13 20:23:00','2009-06-13 20:23:00'),(1217,'BOR0135',10,'Heriot and the King','--- \n- \"151\"\n- \"144\"\n- \"131\"\n',155,145,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 26, facing p. 86','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts George Heriot presenting for inspection a gold plate to the seated King James, with other objects strewn around the room [Ch. 5].','2009-06-14 08:37:23','2009-06-28 14:06:28'),(1218,'BOR0136',10,'Nigel at his Lodgings','--- \n- \"498\"\n- \"18\"\n- \"145\"\n- \"293\"\n- \"244\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.6','1893','1893',44,'vol. 26, facing p. 150','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Nigel dressed in his finest at his lodgings next to the Thames, with Richie Moniplies attending to the stockings, and Nelly Christie with a tray under one arm looking on [Ch. 9].','2009-06-14 08:41:38','2009-06-14 08:41:38'),(1219,'BOR0137',10,'At the Ordinary','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"386\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"293\"\n',155,145,2,'9.3 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 26, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the aftermath of the combat in the courtyard of the Ordinary, the main figures, looking on in the foreground, presumably representing Nigel and Lord Dalgarno [Ch. 12].','2009-06-14 08:44:35','2009-06-14 08:44:35'),(1220,'BOR0138',10,'Alsatia','--- \n- \"322\"\n- \"107\"\n- \"354\"\n',155,145,2,'13.8 x 9.65','1893','1893',44,'vol. 27 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts two ruffians, smoking pipes and wearing jerkins and trunk-breeches, lounging in the street in Alsatia (Whitefriars) in London; a female street-seller is seated opposite in the street [Ch. 17].','2009-06-14 08:45:44','2009-06-28 14:07:15'),(1221,'BOR0139',10,'Margaret and Lady Hermione','--- \n- \"149\"\n- \"69\"\n- \"150\"\n',155,145,2,'14 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 27, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Lady Hermione in her apartment handing over of bag of gold to the kneeling Margaret Ramsay [Ch. 20].','2009-06-14 08:47:25','2009-06-28 14:07:46'),(1222,'BOR0140',10,'Duke Hildebrod','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"499\"\n- \"293\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 27, facing p. 96','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts ‘Duke’ Hildebrod seated at a table the in the Trapbois home, and jovially taking a filed tankard for the charwoman Deborah, as Nigel stands at the other end of the table [Ch. 23].','2009-06-14 08:50:13','2009-06-28 14:08:06'),(1223,'BOR0141',10,'Nigel and the King','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"144\"\n- \"293\"\n- \"146\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 27, facing p. 176','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Nigel Olifaunt pleading with the standing King James, having interrupted the hunt, with two hunting dogs next to the fallen deer [Ch. 27].','2009-06-14 08:52:03','2009-06-14 08:52:03'),(1224,'BOR0142',10,'Death of Dalgarno','--- \n- \"498\"\n- \"500\"\n- \"386\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"229\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 27, facing p. 320','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the body of Lord Dalgarno on he ground in open woodland, half-covered by a blanket, with Nelly Christie kneeling in a distraught state by it, and her aggrieved husband John standing above her [Ch. 36].','2009-06-14 08:54:21','2009-06-14 08:54:21'),(1225,'BOR0143',19,'The Challenge','--- \n- \"154\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"501\"\n- \"502\"\n',205,179,2,'9.2 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 28 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Jasper Cranbourne delivering the challenge of Sir Geoffrey Peveril on the point of his sword, with Major Bridgenorth about to take hold of it as Solsgrace alongside him holds out a hand as if to prevent him [Ch. 9]','2009-06-14 08:57:24','2009-06-14 08:58:39'),(1226,'BOR0144',19,'The Gold Room','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"17\"\n- \"389\"\n- \"153\"\n',156,147,2,'9.1 x 14.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 28, facing p. 64','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril and Alice Bridgenorth as children in the gilded parlour at Martindale Castle, with Julian holding on to a reed stick, as the unknown figure of the Countess of Derby enters through a sliding panel [Ch. 5]. Painting exhibited at the Royal Academy, no. 213, 1887; and Royal Scottish Academy, no. 318, 1888.','2009-06-14 09:06:10','2009-06-28 14:12:52'),(1227,'BOR0145',19,'Sir Geoffrey Stopping Major Bridgenorth','--- \n- \"154\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"297\"\n',206,176,2,'13.7 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 28, facing p. 106','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Geoffrey Peveril mounted on Black Hastings impeding the path of Major Bridgenorth, whose hat lies on the ground, with two other mounted men in the background [Ch. 7].','2009-06-14 09:09:44','2009-06-14 09:09:44'),(1228,'BOR0146',19,'Bridgenorth and Lady Peveril','--- \n- \"154\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"503\"\n',207,181,2,'13.9 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 28, facing p. 160','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Major Bridgenorth on a pathway in conversation with Lady Peveril, with several large trees in front of them [Ch. 10].','2009-06-14 09:15:02','2009-06-14 09:15:02'),(1229,'BOR0147',19,'Alice Dancing','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"152\"\n- \"154\"\n- \"477\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"75\"\n',171,146,2,'14 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 28, facing p. 182','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Alice Bridgenorth demonstrating dancing steps to her father, with Deborah Debbitch her nurse playing a viol; a picture of Colonel Christian hangs on the wall [Ch. 12].','2009-06-14 09:17:28','2009-06-28 14:13:16'),(1230,'BOR0148',19,'The Fight with the Miners','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"152\"\n- \"154\"\n- \"153\"\n',171,157,2,'13.7 x 9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 29 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril appealing to the local miners to desist from their attack on Moultrassie Hall, from which smoke billows, and where Alice Bridgenorth appears on a staircase, with her father standing with some defenders below [Ch. 25].','2009-06-14 09:19:01','2009-06-28 14:13:34'),(1231,'BOR0149',19,'Julian and Fenella','--- \n- \"157\"\n- \"153\"\n',207,180,2,'13.9 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 29, facing p. 22','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril on the steps outside Holm-Peel Castle, with Fenella reaching out to him in an effort to communicate [Ch. 16].','2009-06-14 09:20:33','2009-06-28 14:14:18'),(1232,'BOR0150',19,'Ganlesse Riding with Julian','--- \n- \"158\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"153\"\n',206,157,2,'13.8 x 9.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 29, facing p. 118','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ganlesse (Edward Christian) riding alongside Julian Peveril on the highway [Ch. 21].\r\n\r\n','2009-06-14 09:22:15','2009-06-14 09:22:15'),(1233,'BOR0151',19,'Julian Shooting at Bridgenorth','--- \n- \"154\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"153\"\n',171,156,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1893','1893',44,'vol. 29, facing p. 160','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril firing his pistol at Major Bridgenorth in Peveril Castle, with guards from Bridgenorth’s party also present, and (apparently) Sir Geoffrey and Lady Peveril under restraint at the back of the room [Ch. 23].','2009-06-14 09:24:06','2009-06-14 09:24:06'),(1234,'BOR0152',19,'Fenella Dancing Before the King','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"157\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"395\"\n- \"504\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"246\"\n- \"153\"\n- \"244\"\n',207,176,2,'9 x 14.4','1893','1893',44,'vol. 29, facing p. 278','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Fenella dancing before Charles II in parkland by the Thames, with a member of his retinue (Empson) playing a flageolet, and one of the King’s spaniels at his feet; Julian Peveril looks on from a short distance [Ch. 30].','2009-06-14 09:27:07','2009-06-14 09:27:07'),(1235,'BOR0153',19,'The Attempted Rescue','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"153\"\n- \"85\"\n- \"244\"\n',207,152,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 30 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril standing in bow of the boat in which he is being rowed to the Tower of London, with another boat coming towards them and causing alarm [Ch. 36].','2009-06-14 09:28:48','2009-06-14 09:28:48'),(1236,'BOR0154',19,'Buckingham and the King','--- \n- \"152\"\n- \"156\"\n- \"319\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"153\"\n',171,184,2,'14 x 8.9','1893','1893',44,'vol. 30, facing p. 6','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Duke of Buckingham bowing before Charles II having entered unexpectedly into his presence at Mistress Chiffinch’s; a spaniel watches at the edge of the carpet, while Alice Bridgenorth and Julian Peveril, arms linked for security, are among the figures in the background [Ch. 31].','2009-06-14 09:30:51','2009-06-14 09:32:02'),(1237,'BOR0155',19,'Julian and the Dwarf','--- \n- \"162\"\n- \"161\"\n- \"153\"\n- \"85\"\n',171,142,2,'13.5 x 8.5','1893','1893',44,'vol. 30, facing p. 54','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Julian Peveril having entered his cell at Newgate, along with the Warder, and meeting the diminutive Sir Geoffrey Hudson, who formally bows towards him [Ch. 33].','2009-06-14 09:33:35','2009-06-28 14:14:39'),(1238,'BOR0156',19,'Buckingham and Fenella','--- \n- \"326\"\n- \"156\"\n- \"157\"\n- \"18\"\n',207,179,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1893','1893',44,'vol. 30, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Buckingham in an apartment at his town mansion kneeling before the standing figure of Fenella, richly attired as Zarah the sorceress [Ch. 39].','2009-06-14 10:46:23','2009-06-28 14:15:18'),(1239,'BOR0157',19,'The Countess before the King','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"319\"\n- \"389\"\n- \"146\"\n',171,147,2,'13.8 x 9.1','1893','1893',44,'vol. 30, facing p. 230','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Countess of Derby, dressed in a widow’s costume, appearing before Charles II at Court, who sits with just two attendant courtiers behind him [Ch. 45].','2009-06-14 10:48:00','2009-06-28 14:15:52'),(1240,'BOR0158',21,'Quentin and the Strangers','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"165\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"166\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Quentin Durward at the ford confronting Louis XI and Tristan L’Hermite, both disguised, the former as Maitre Pierre and restraining the latter from drawing his sword. [Ch. 2].','2009-06-14 10:49:41','2009-06-14 10:52:03'),(1241,'BOR0159',21,'The Breakfast','--- \n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"165\"\n',201,163,2,'13.5 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Louis XI, seated, being served by Jacqueline (the Countess of Croye) in the inn, with Quentin Durward offering assistance [Ch. 4].','2009-06-14 10:51:40','2009-08-16 20:29:30'),(1242,'BOR0160',21,'The Boar-Hunt','--- \n- \"164\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"165\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31, facing p. 160','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Louis XI, on the ground and disarmed, with Quentin Durward on horseback pointing his lance at the wild boar which is charging towards Louis [Ch. 9].','2009-06-14 10:53:27','2009-06-14 10:53:27'),(1243,'BOR0161',21,'The Princess Fainting','--- \n- \"506\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"505\"\n- \"507\"\n- \"178\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31, facing p. 200','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Princess Joan having sunk backwards into her chair, with Isabelle de Croye and her aunt Hameline attending to her, as Duke of Orleans sits on the floor; and Quentin Durward patrols the hall as a guard [Ch. 11].','2009-06-14 10:57:11','2009-06-28 14:16:42'),(1244,'BOR0162',21,'Louis Consulting Galeotti','--- \n- \"132\"\n- \"163\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"165\"\n- \"508\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31, facing p. 224','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Louis XI in a kneeling position before the standing figure of Galeotti in his cell (study), with Quentin Durward looking on; books lie on the floor and a globe stands on the table [Ch. 13].','2009-06-14 11:00:13','2009-06-28 14:17:07'),(1245,'BOR0163',21,'Quentin Wounded','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"506\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"178\"\n',201,163,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 31, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Quentin Durward after combat in a sitting position by the roadside, with Isabelle de Croye attending to a head wound, while her aunt Hameline kneels over a trunk in search of a remedy [Ch. 15].','2009-06-14 11:01:52','2009-06-28 14:17:37'),(1246,'BOR0164',21,'Meeting of Louis and Charles','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"467\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"165\"\n- \"354\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 8.9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Duke of Burgundy formally kneeling at the feet of the mounted Louis XI in welcoming him to Peronne, with retinues of followers on either side [Ch. 26].','2009-06-14 11:04:17','2009-06-14 11:04:17'),(1247,'BOR0165',21,'The Countess at the Window','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"506\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"34\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Quentin Durward in the Bishop’s garden at Schonwaldt standing with one arm outstretched beneath a casement window from which a female figure (actually the Countess Hameline) looks out [Ch. 20].','2009-06-14 11:05:41','2009-06-28 14:17:56'),(1248,'BOR0166',21,'Crevecoeur Vowing Vengeance','--- \n- \"396\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n',201,163,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32, facing p. 118','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Count of Crevecoeur at the head of his mounted party with his arms raised upwards in vowing vengeance against the murderers of the Bishop of Liege [Ch. 24].','2009-06-14 11:07:31','2009-06-14 11:07:31'),(1249,'BOR0167',21,'Louis Escorting Galeotti','--- \n- \"132\"\n- \"165\"\n- \"508\"\n',201,163,2,'14 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32, facing p. 214','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Louis XI clutching the arm of the bearded Galeotti and leading him into the hall in the Castle of Peronne and away from his planned execution [Ch. 29].','2009-06-14 11:09:24','2009-06-14 11:09:24'),(1250,'BOR0168',21,'Isabelle at the Grating','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n',201,163,2,'13.9 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Quentin Durward bowing and kissing the hand of the Countess Isabelle, as she extends it from behind a grating partition in the Castle of Peronne [Ch. 31].','2009-06-14 11:10:51','2009-06-14 11:10:51'),(1251,'BOR0169',21,'Quentin Delivering the Letter','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"167\"\n- \"164\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"96\"\n',201,163,2,'13.8 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 32, facing p. 320','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Quentin Durward, on horseback in armour, tilting his lance with a letter on its point up towards a turret outside the gateway at Peronne, where it is received by the Countess Isabelle [Ch. 36].','2009-06-14 11:12:23','2009-06-28 14:19:06'),(1252,'BOR0170',24,'Meg Dods','--- \n- \"402\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"84\"\n',151,176,2,'14 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 33 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Meg Dods, with a white cap and hands on hips, against the doorway of the Cleikum Inn [Ch. 1]. Etching by Boucher exhibited at Royal Academy, no. 1402, 1894. Matches #ESR0001, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 11:14:06','2009-06-28 14:22:14'),(1253,'BOR0171',24,'St. Ronan’s Village','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"20\"\n- \"244\"\n',151,143,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 33, facing p. 6','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts a small settlement with cottages and a church in a valley beneath an old castle, with a river flowing in front, and hills in the background [Ch. 1]. Matches #ESR0002 but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 11:15:57','2009-06-28 14:23:18'),(1254,'BOR0172',24,'The Meeting in the Wood','--- \n- \"30\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"401\"\n',208,176,2,'13.9 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 33, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Frank Tyrrel standing on a bridle-path in a wooded area near the Buckstane, impeding the progress of Clara Mowbray, who rides a dark mount with whip in hand and wearing a riding-hat [Ch. 9].','2009-06-14 11:17:56','2009-06-14 11:19:08'),(1255,'BOR0173',24,'Preparing for the Duel','--- \n- \"481\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"403\"\n- \"482\"\n- \"171\"\n',155,145,2,'9 x 14.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 33, facing p. 198','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Bingo Binks with his second Captain MacTurk at the Buckstane waiting for Tyrrel’s appearance for a duel; Mr Winterblossom and Dr Quackleben are seated alongside, the latter looking at his watch [Ch. 13]. Matches #ESR0005, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 11:22:07','2009-06-14 11:22:07'),(1256,'BOR0174',24,'Touchwood and Cargill','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"173\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"174\"\n',149,185,2,'9 x 14.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 33, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Mr Touchwood, cane in hand, disturbing the Rev. Josiah Cargill, the latter at his desk studying a large folio book [Ch. 17]. Matches #ESR0007, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 11:24:08','2009-06-14 11:24:55'),(1257,'BOR0175',24,'Shaws Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"244\"\n',151,146,2,'13.8 x 9.7','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts (fictional) Shaws Castle, and gateway with decorative bears, the latter especially resembling Traquair House in Peeblesshire, with a river flowing in front [Ch. 20]. Matches #ESR0003, where connection with Traquair is made explicit, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 11:26:51','2009-06-14 11:26:51'),(1258,'BOR0176',24,'Reappearance of Tyrell','--- \n- \"225\"\n- \"402\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"509\"\n- \"401\"\n',208,186,2,'9 x 14.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34, facing p. 128','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Tyrrel entering through the doorway of the kitchen at the Cleikum Inn, with John Ostler, holding a lantern and a  pitchfork, recoiling in consternation, and Meg Dods grasping the ear and hair of the postilion, while one of the maids flees up a stairway in the background [Ch. 28].','2009-06-14 11:30:00','2009-06-14 11:30:52'),(1259,'BOR0177',24,'At Hannah Irwin\'s Bedside','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"478\"\n- \"204\"\n- \"176\"\n- \"510\"\n- \"178\"\n',208,187,2,'9.4 x 14.7','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34, facing p. 208','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Lady Penfeather and Lord Etherington as visitors to the hut where Hannah Irwin lies dying in bed, with hands raised, while a crone-like figure (representing her assistant) sits at a spinning wheel [Ch. 32].','2009-06-14 11:37:45','2009-06-17 20:14:57'),(1260,'BOR0178',24,'Clara and her Brother','--- \n- \"67\"\n- \"177\"\n- \"298\"\n',208,181,2,'13.8 x 8.7','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Clara Mowbray in her parlour at Shaws Castle attempting to restrain her brother, John, who wears a coat and holds his hat as if to leave; a teapot and crockery are visible in the background [Ch. 35].','2009-06-14 11:41:57','2009-06-14 11:41:57'),(1261,'BOR0179',24,'Clara Entering Tyrrel\'s Room','--- \n- \"177\"\n- \"401\"\n',208,156,2,'13.6 x 9.1','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34, facing p. 310','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Clara Mowbray entering into Tyrrel’s room at the Cleikum Inn, holding a candle aloft, as Tyrrel (with his back to her) sits at a writing desk; Clara’s image is reflected back through a mirror above the desk [Ch. 38].','2009-06-14 11:43:40','2009-06-14 11:43:54'),(1262,'BOR0180',22,'Green Mantle','--- \n- \"181\"\n- \"84\"\n',209,142,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 35 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Greenmantle in a floral dress, looking at her face in a hand mirror [Ch. 18].','2009-06-14 12:35:58','2009-06-14 12:35:58'),(1263,'BOR0181',22,'On the Nith','--- \n- \"191\"\n- \"244\"\n',210,188,2,'9.2 x 14.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 34, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the River Nith in Dumfriesshire as it approaches the Solway Firth, with sea birds close to the water and in the sky [Letter 3].','2009-06-14 12:38:39','2009-06-14 12:38:39'),(1264,'BOR0182',22,'Sunset--Solway Firth','--- \n- \"29\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"140\"\n',143,143,2,'9.1 x 14.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 35, facing p. 38','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Solway Firth near nightfall, with three horsemen on the sands holding spears [Letter 4]. Matches #ERE0001, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 12:40:25','2009-06-28 14:23:47'),(1265,'BOR0183',22,'Steenie Getting his Receipt','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"19\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"185\"\n- \"184\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 35, facing p. 166','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Steenie moving away from Sir Robert Redgauntlet and his roistering comrades, in what appears to him to be the Hall at Redgauntlet Castle, having received his rent receipt from Sir Robert [Letter 11]. Distinct from #ERE0004, though by same artist.','2009-06-14 12:42:58','2009-06-14 12:42:58'),(1266,'BOR0184',22,'At the Dance','--- \n- \"20\"\n- \"181\"\n- \"180\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"182\"\n- \"303\"\n',211,176,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 35, facing p. 182','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Darsie Latimer inviting the seated Greenmantle to dance, with (apparently) Cristal Nixon at back of her chair; the figure Wandering Willie, with his fiddle, is visible at the back of the room [Letter 12].','2009-06-14 12:45:25','2009-06-14 12:45:52'),(1267,'BOR0185',22,'Alan Introduced to Peebles','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"302\"\n- \"483\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"187\"\n',167,179,2,'13.9 x 9.4','1894','1894',44,'vol. 35, facing p. 208','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Saunders Fairford presenting Peter Peebles, who enters the room holding a cane in one hand and hat in the other, to his son Alan as a client [Letter 13].','2009-06-14 12:49:22','2009-06-14 12:49:22'),(1268,'BOR0186',22,'Alan Entering Annan','--- \n- \"243\"\n- \"302\"\n- \"116\"\n- \"244\"\n',212,189,2,'9.2 x 14.4','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Alan Fairford at dusk riding over a bridge into the town of Annan near the Solway Firth [Ch. 12].','2009-06-14 12:52:57','2009-06-14 12:52:57'),(1269,'BOR0187',22,'Nanty Ewart','--- \n- \"512\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"84\"\n',209,190,2,'12.7 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36, facing p. 118','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Nanty Ewart in cocked hat lighting his pipe, with a pistol and hanger in his belt; and a beached boat behind him [see Ch. 13]. Illustration plate has date 1886 against artist’s name.','2009-06-14 12:56:24','2009-06-14 12:56:24'),(1270,'BOR0188',22,'Smugglers--Solway Firth','--- \n- \"8\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"142\"\n- \"140\"\n',212,189,2,'9.2 x 14.4','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36, facing p. 150','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts a small vessel landed on the shore of the Solway Firth, with cargo being carried from it to a group of pack-horses [Ch. 15].','2009-06-14 12:58:04','2009-06-28 14:24:30'),(1271,'BOR0189',22,'At Fairladies','--- \n- \"301\"\n- \"302\"\n- \"53\"\n',211,176,2,'13.9 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36, facing p. 182','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the seated Prince Charles Edward (in the guise of Father Buonaventure) startled by the sudden appearance of his mistress (based in the story on Clementina Walkinshaw) through the tapestry covering a side-door, with Alan Fairford looking on [Ch. 16].','2009-06-14 13:00:13','2009-06-14 13:00:13'),(1272,'BOR0190',22,'Nanty Ewart Disarmed','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"512\"\n- \"240\"\n- \"179\"\n',167,152,2,'13.9 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36, facing p. 280','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Redgauntlet (‘Herries of Birrenswork’), sword in hand, standing over the prostrate body of Nanty Ewart, whose own sword lies on the ground; Crackenthorp’s inn and the Solway Firth are in the background [Ch. 21].','2009-06-14 13:01:53','2009-06-28 14:25:04'),(1273,'BOR0191',22,'Peebles Catching Alan','--- \n- \"302\"\n- \"428\"\n- \"181\"\n- \"188\"\n- \"187\"\n',148,147,2,'9.3 x 14.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 36, facing p. 326','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Alan Fairford seated alongside Greenmantle, who wears a riding dress, and interrupted by Peter Peebles, who holds Alan’s shoulder with one hand and points a finger with the other; the figure at the door is apparently meant to represent Joshua Geddes [Ch. 23]. Matches #ERE0006, but with different engraver. Exhibited Royal Scottish Academy, no. 205, 1876.','2009-06-14 13:04:09','2009-06-28 14:25:47'),(1274,'BOR0192',4,'Eveline Seized by the Welsh','--- \n- \"190\"\n- \"191\"\n- \"328\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"142\"\n',166,181,2,'13.7 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 37 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Eveline Berenger being seized by Welsh freebooters, one of whom (probably meant to represent the leader Dawfyd) manhandles her, while a hawk and heron lie tangled up in combat on the ground in the foreground [Ch. 23].','2009-06-14 13:06:12','2009-06-28 14:26:04'),(1275,'BOR0193',4,'We Are Betrayed!','--- \n- \"331\"\n- \"103\"\n- \"190\"\n',166,156,2,'13.7 x 9.4','1894','1894',44,'vol. 37, facing p. 80','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Father Aldrovand dressed in chain mail and with a large sword fastened to his side, with arm stretched outward to Eveline Berenger who stands before him [Ch. 6].','2009-06-14 13:07:33','2009-06-14 13:07:33'),(1276,'BOR0194',4,'Arrival of Damian','--- \n- \"331\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"330\"\n- \"288\"\n',166,157,2,'14 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 37, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Damian de Lacy standing by his horse having arrived through the portcullis into the besieged Garde Doloureuse, with various inmates of the Castle surrounding him, prominent amongst whom is Father Aldrovand in his cassock [Ch. 9].','2009-06-14 13:10:32','2009-06-14 13:10:32'),(1277,'BOR0195',4,'Sir Hugo and the Prelate','--- \n- \"514\"\n- \"175\"\n- \"513\"\n',166,147,2,'13.9 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 37, facing p. 246','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Hugo de Lacy, with arms folded, standing next to the archprelate Baldwin, in ecclesiastical robes, who looks towards him with pointing upwards admonishingly [Ch. 18].','2009-06-14 13:13:18','2009-06-14 13:13:18'),(1278,'BOR0196',4,'It is Wenlock\'s Head','--- \n- \"484\"\n- \"103\"\n- \"516\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"515\"\n- \"229\"\n',166,146,2,'14 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 37, facing p. 368','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Hob Miller on horseback presenting the head of the Englishman Wenlock to the page Amelot and his companion Genvil, who are both mounted in full armour and carry lances [Ch. 27].','2009-06-14 13:16:43','2009-06-28 14:26:35'),(1279,'BOR0197',26,'Conrad Unhorsed','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"195\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"198\"\n',203,180,2,'14 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 38 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Conrade of Montserrat after his unhorsing being held down by Roswal, the hound of Sir Kenneth, in front of King Richard and the other Crusaders [Ch. 24].\r\n\r\n','2009-06-14 13:18:49','2009-06-14 13:18:49'),(1280,'BOR0198',26,'The Fight with the Saracen','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"199\"\n',213,164,1,'13.9 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 38, facing p. 16','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Kenneth, mace in hand, grappling with (the as yet unidentified) Saladin, who holds a shield and scimitar, as Kenneth’s horse rears wildly behind them [Ch. 1].','2009-06-14 13:21:14','2009-06-14 13:23:37'),(1281,'BOR0199',26,'Richard and the Physician','--- \n- \"327\"\n- \"196\"\n- \"199\"\n- \"178\"\n',213,157,2,'14 x 9.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 38, facing p. 150','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts King Richard in bed his tent being tended to by Saladin (as ‘El Hakim’), who is seated, as three of the Crusaders stand and look on [Ch. 9].','2009-06-14 13:25:28','2009-06-14 13:25:28'),(1282,'BOR0200',26,'Kenneth and Edith','--- \n- \"130\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"394\"\n',213,191,2,'14 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 38, facing p. 214','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Kenneth kneeling before Edith, who stands in stately fashion holding open a curtain to her pavilion [Ch. 14].','2009-06-14 13:28:44','2009-06-14 13:28:44'),(1283,'BOR0201',26,'Conrad Wounded','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"197\"\n- \"320\"\n- \"195\"\n- \"178\"\n',213,178,2,'13.9 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 38, facing p. 438','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Conrade of Montserrat lying on the ground mortally wounded after single combat, with the victorious Kenneth sword drawn standing above him, as King Richard, Saladin, and the Christian princes look on [Ch. 28].','2009-06-14 17:39:08','2009-06-28 14:27:18'),(1284,'BOR0202',29,'The Apparitions','--- \n- \"284\"\n- \"332\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 39 (frontsipiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Markham Everard starting from his bed in the chamber at Woodstock Lodge and apparently witnessing the ghost of Victor Lee with upraised staff in one arm and accompanied by a mysterious lady [Ch. 15].','2009-06-14 17:40:23','2009-06-14 17:40:23'),(1285,'BOR0203',29,'Sir Henry Disarmed','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"200\"\n- \"246\"\n- \"206\"\n',149,185,2,'13.6 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 39, facing p. 38','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Henry duelling with Joseph Tompkins in the park of Woodstock, with his own sword flying into the air [Ch. 2].','2009-06-14 17:42:24','2009-06-14 17:42:24'),(1286,'BOR0204',29,'Cromwell and Wildrake','--- \n- \"172\"\n- \"308\"\n- \"309\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 9.1','1894','1894',44,'vol. 39, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Oliver Cromwell, standing and looking intently at a large portrait of Charles I which stands on the floor, with Wildrake behind looking on [Ch. 8].','2009-06-14 17:43:56','2009-06-14 17:43:56'),(1287,'BOR0205',29,'The Commissioners at the Lodge','--- \n- \"519\"\n- \"517\"\n- \"332\"\n- \"518\"\n',149,185,2,'9.4 x 14.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 39, facing p. 198','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the parliamentary commissioners, Desborough, Harrison, and Bletson, seated round an oak table at Woodstock Lodge, with Markham Everard also apparently present [Chs 11 & 12]. ','2009-06-14 17:47:34','2009-06-14 17:47:34'),(1288,'BOR0206',29,'Master Holdenough\'s Story','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"307\"\n- \"202\"\n',149,185,2,'13.9 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 39, facing p. 304','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the onslaught on a fortified house with Doctor Rochecliffe (then Joseph Albany) about to be forced off a tower, and with Nehemiah Holdenough clambering up and witnessing the event [Ch. 17].','2009-06-14 17:49:01','2009-06-14 17:49:01'),(1289,'BOR0207',29,'I am Charles Stuart','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"332\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"201\"\n- \"202\"\n- \"309\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 8.9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 40 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Markham Everard, hand reaching towards his sword, on the point of duelling with Charles II, who at this point reveals his identity, while Doctor Rochecliffe and Roger Wildrake attend to Alice Lee under a tree in the background [Ch. 28].','2009-06-14 17:51:37','2009-06-14 17:51:37'),(1290,'BOR0208',29,'A Health to King Charles','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"67\"\n- \"520\"\n- \"201\"\n- \"200\"\n- \"202\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 8.9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 40, facing p. 32','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Sir Henry Lee with his family at Woodstock Lodge at supper proposing a toast to King Charles, who unbeknown to them is also present at the table under the guise of Louis Kerneguy [Ch. 20].','2009-06-14 17:55:27','2009-06-28 14:28:05'),(1291,'BOR0209',29,'Kerneguy and Alice','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"155\"\n- \"201\"\n',149,185,2,'13.5 x 8.9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 40, facing p. 102','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Alice Lee seated at the window of the parlour, with King Charles (as Louis Kerneguy) leaning on the back of the chair and paying attention to her [Ch. 24].','2009-06-14 17:56:46','2009-06-14 17:56:46'),(1292,'BOR0210',29,'Burial of Tomkins','--- \n- \"163\"\n- \"30\"\n- \"38\"\n- \"479\"\n- \"202\"\n- \"206\"\n',149,185,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 40, facing p. 278','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Jocelin Joliffe at night in woodland kneeling with a shovel in one hand over the body of Tomkins, with Doctor Rochecliffe standing above and  reading the service of the Church from a book [Ch. 33].','2009-06-14 17:58:29','2009-06-28 14:28:31'),(1293,'BOR0211',29,'The Restoration','--- \n- \"319\"\n- \"49\"\n',149,185,2,'13.7 x 9.1','1894','1894',44,'vol. 40, facing p. 358','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Charles II mounted on a dark steed, in full dress and holding a feathered hat by his side in his free hand [Ch. 38].','2009-06-14 17:59:48','2009-06-14 17:59:48'),(1294,'BOR0212',9,'Proudfute Unhorsed','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"49\"\n- \"50\"\n- \"522\"\n- \"521\"\n',155,145,2,'13.7 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 41 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Oliver Proudfute on the ground having been unseated from his horse, having challenged Dick Johnstone (‘the Devil’s Dick of Hellgarth’), who remains on his horse, while hawking proceeds in the background [Ch. 8].\r\n\r\n ','2009-06-14 18:02:35','2009-08-16 20:30:17'),(1295,'BOR0213',9,'St. Valentine\'s Morn','--- \n- \"155\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"209\"\n',155,145,2,'13.8 x 9.6','1894','1894',44,'vol. 41, facing p. 86','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Catharine Glover moving towards Henry Gow, asleep in a chair, with one of her arms raised as if to touch him [Ch. 5].','2009-06-14 18:03:43','2009-06-14 18:03:43'),(1296,'BOR0214',9,'Meeting of Citizens','--- \n- \"353\"\n- \"523\"\n- \"211\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"521\"\n',155,145,2,'9.2 x 14.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 41, facing p. 112','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the meeting of citizens of Perth round a table in Simon Glover’s work-room [Ch. 7].','2009-06-14 18:05:57','2009-06-14 18:05:57'),(1297,'BOR0215',9,'The Glee-Maiden','--- \n- \"26\"\n- \"524\"\n- \"74\"\n- \"84\"\n',135,179,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 41, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts The Glee Maiden Louise in a doorway holding a viol and bow with a small dog with her on the doorstep [Ch. 11]. Matches #EFM0001, but with different engraver.','2009-06-14 18:07:55','2009-06-29 12:26:58'),(1298,'BOR0216',9,'Rothsay and the King','--- \n- \"526\"\n- \"525\"\n- \"215\"\n',155,145,2,'13.8 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 41, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Duke of Rothesay kneeling before his father, King Robert, who stands above him with arms outstretched and an expression of anger, as the Duke of Albany looks on coolly from the side [Ch. 13].','2009-06-14 18:15:50','2009-06-14 18:15:50'),(1299,'BOR0217',9,'Escape of Conachar','--- \n- \"296\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"338\"\n- \"244\"\n- \"212\"\n',155,145,2,'14 x 9.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 42 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Ian Eachin MacIan (‘Conachar’) throwing himself into the River Tay in flight, with Henry Gow standing above him on the bank, and evidence of the aftermath of the larger combat in the background [Ch. 34].','2009-06-14 18:17:22','2009-06-28 14:30:18'),(1300,'BOR0218',9,'Proudfute Dead','--- \n- \"229\"\n- \"213\"\n- \"521\"\n',155,145,2,'9.1 x 14.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 42, facing p. 16','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the body of Oliver Proudfute lying sprawled in the street in Perth, with an onlooker viewing it through the opened shutter of a window [Ch. 18].','2009-06-14 18:18:47','2009-06-28 14:32:02'),(1301,'BOR0219',9,'Falkland Castle','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"311\"\n- \"129\"\n',151,146,2,'13.5 x 9.5','1894','1894',44,'vol. 42, facing p. 144','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the towered gateway of Falkland Palace in near darkness, with a group of people assembling close to the entrance [Ch. 30]. Matches #EMF0004, which has the title ‘The Gateway, Falkland Palace’, and with different engraver.\r\n\r\n','2009-06-14 18:20:21','2009-06-28 14:32:50'),(1302,'BOR0220',9,'Catherine and the Glee-Maiden','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"26\"\n- \"210\"\n- \"524\"\n- \"74\"\n',155,145,2,'14 x 9.3','1894','1894',44,'vol. 42, facing p. 256','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Catharine Glover aided by the Glee Maiden Louise, who stands behind her, administering food with a stick through a chink in the wall of the Castle to the imprisoned Duke of Rothesay; a small dog looks on, and a viol rests against the wall [Ch. 32]. ','2009-06-14 18:22:11','2009-06-28 14:33:09'),(1303,'BOR0221',9,'The Smith and the Highlander','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"353\"\n- \"527\"\n- \"209\"\n- \"46\"\n- \"487\"\n',156,147,2,'9.4 x 13.7','1894','1894',44,'vol. 42, facing p. 288','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Henry Gow in his smithy leaning on his hammer, with Dunter his journeyman alongside him, as he listens the request of the Highlander Norman nan Ord for body armour; several smithy tools are visible against a bench and on the floor [Ch. 33]. Painting exhibited at the Royal Academy, no. 223, 1875. ','2009-06-14 18:26:10','2009-06-28 14:34:08'),(1304,'BOR0222',2,'The Duel','--- \n- \"103\"\n- \"296\"\n- \"528\"\n- \"217\"\n',214,192,2,'13.8 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 43 (frontispiece)','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Arthur Philipson before a gateway in the Castle of Geierstein about to draw his sword to face the challenge of the gigantic Rudolph of Donnerhugel, who himself points to a large two-handed sword which has thrown on the ground for Philipson’s use [Ch. 6].','2009-06-14 18:31:51','2009-06-14 18:31:51'),(1305,'BOR0223',2,'Geierstein','--- \n- \"14\"\n- \"72\"\n',214,192,2,'13.8 x 9','1894','1894',44,'vol. 43, facing p. 48','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts the Castle of Geierstein in a hilly and somewhat barren-lloking landscape, with dead trees in the foreground [Ch. 3].','2009-06-15 08:13:00','2009-06-15 08:13:00'),(1306,'BOR0224',2,'In the Stable','--- \n- \"326\"\n- \"529\"\n- \"530\"\n- \"49\"\n',214,192,2,'13.8 x 9.2','1894','1894',44,'vol. 43, facing p. 192','NLS, Lit.S.22','','Depicts Dannischemend and the Baron of Arnheim in the castle stables, the former pointing to a magnificent steed, while the Baron faces him holding a burning torch [Ch. 11].\r\n\r\n','2009-06-15 08:17:0